Lab.catalog([{"id":"kotlin","title":"Kotlin","description":"The language Android is written in, from val to sealed classes and exceptions.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/kotlin.js","lessons":[{"id":"val","title":"Naming a value with val","tagline":"Give a value a name, then use the name.","level":"beginner","goals":["Store a value under a name with <code>val</code>","Use the name to get the value back","Pick good names for your values"],"remember":"val name = value makes a named box. Use the name without quotes to get the value back.","terms":[{"term":"variable","def":"A named box that holds a value."},{"term":"val","def":"Makes a variable whose value is set once and never replaced."},{"term":"value","def":"A piece of data, such as <code>\"Ada\"</code> or <code>36</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val x = 5</code>. What does <code>println(x * 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>x * 2</code>","<code>10</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>x</code> holds 5, so <code>x * 2</code> is 10."},{"q":"Which is a valid variable name?","options":["<code>first name</code>","<code>firstName</code>","<code>1stName</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Names can’t contain spaces or start with a digit. <code>firstName</code> is the usual Kotlin style."}]},{"id":"var","title":"Values that change with var","tagline":"Some boxes need new contents over time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a value that can change with <code>var</code>","Update a value with <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code> and <code>++</code>","Choose between <code>val</code> and <code>var</code>"],"remember":"var can get a new value, val can’t. Start with val; switch to var only when the value must change.","terms":[{"term":"var","def":"Makes a variable that can be given a new value later."},{"term":"reassign","def":"Put a new value in an existing <code>var</code>: <code>score = 10</code>."},{"term":"+= and -=","def":"Short for adding to or subtracting from a variable: <code>x += 2</code> means <code>x = x + 2</code>."},{"term":"++","def":"Adds 1 to a variable: <code>count++</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>var x = 4</code>, then <code>x += 3</code>, then <code>x++</code>. What is <code>x</code>?","options":["<code>7</code>","<code>8</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"4 + 3 is 7, and ++ adds 1: 8."},{"q":"A person’s birth year in your program. val or var?","options":["<code>val</code>, it never changes","<code>var</code>, just in case"],"a":0,"why":"It doesn’t change, so <code>val</code>. Use <code>var</code> only when the program changes it."}]},{"id":"types","title":"Types","tagline":"Every value is a kind of thing: text, whole number, decimal, yes/no.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the four basic types, including <code>Boolean</code>","Write a type yourself after a colon","See how types catch mistakes before running"],"remember":"Every value has a type: String, Int, Double, Boolean. A box keeps its type, so mistakes show up before running.","terms":[{"term":"type","def":"The kind of a value: text, whole number, decimal, true/false."},{"term":"Boolean","def":"A type with two values: <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>."},{"term":"type inference","def":"Kotlin working out a variable’s type from its first value."},{"term":"type mismatch","def":"The error you get when a value doesn’t fit a variable’s type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What type is <code>val ready = false</code>?","options":["<code>String</code>","<code>Boolean</code>","<code>Int</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>true</code> and <code>false</code> are the two <code>Boolean</code> values."},{"q":"What type is <code>val code = \"42\"</code>?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>String</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The quotes make it text, even though it looks like a number."},{"q":"When does Kotlin report a type mismatch?","options":["Before the program runs","Only when that line runs"],"a":0,"why":"It is a compile error, so you find it straight away."}]},{"id":"templates","title":"Putting values into text","tagline":"Build sentences from variables with $.","level":"beginner","goals":["Put a variable into text with <code>$name</code>","Put a calculation into text with <code>${ }</code>","Join pieces of text with <code>+</code>"],"remember":"\"$name\" puts a variable into text; \"${a + b}\" puts a calculation in. + joins texts.","terms":[{"term":"string template","def":"Text with <code>$name</code> or <code>${...}</code> inside, replaced by values when the program runs."},{"term":"concatenation","def":"Joining texts together with <code>+</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = 3</code>. What does <code>println(\"Items: $n\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Items: $n</code>","<code>Items: 3</code>","<code>Items: n</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>$n</code> is replaced with the value 3."},{"q":"<code>val n = 3</code>. What does <code>println(\"Next: ${n + 1}\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Next: 4</code>","<code>Next: 3 + 1</code>","<code>Next: 31</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The expression inside <code>${ }</code> is calculated first."}]},{"id":"conversion","title":"Converting between types","tagline":"\"5\" is text. Turn it into a number before doing maths.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn text into a number with <code>toInt()</code>","Turn numbers into text with <code>toString()</code>","Know when a conversion crashes"],"remember":"Text that looks like a number is still text. Convert with toInt() / toDouble() before maths; toString() goes the other way.","terms":[{"term":"toInt()","def":"Turns text like <code>\"5\"</code> (or a decimal) into an <code>Int</code>."},{"term":"toDouble()","def":"Turns text or a whole number into a <code>Double</code>."},{"term":"toString()","def":"Turns a value into text."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(\"2\" + 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>22</code>","An error"],"a":1,"why":"Text on the left, so <code>+</code> joins: \"2\" then 2."},{"q":"What is <code>7.8.toInt()</code>?","options":["<code>8</code>","<code>7</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Converting to <code>Int</code> drops the decimals; it doesn’t round."},{"q":"What happens with <code>\"ten\".toInt()</code>?","options":["It gives 10","The program crashes","It gives 0"],"a":1,"why":"There is no number in the text, so conversion fails while running."}]},{"id":"booleans","title":"True or false","tagline":"Comparisons answer yes-or-no questions.","level":"beginner","goals":["Compare values with <code>==</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and friends","Get a <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> answer","Tell <code>=</code> and <code>==</code> apart"],"remember":"Comparisons (== != < > <= >=) give true or false. = stores a value, == compares.","terms":[{"term":"comparison","def":"A question like <code>a &gt; b</code> whose answer is <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>."},{"term":"==","def":"Asks whether two values are equal. Not the same as <code>=</code>, which stores a value."},{"term":"!=","def":"Asks whether two values are different."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>5 != 5</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>!=</code> asks “are they different?”. They are not."},{"q":"Which one compares two values?","options":["<code>a = b</code>","<code>a == b</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>==</code> compares; <code>=</code> stores."},{"q":"What is <code>10 &lt;= 10</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"“Less than or equal”: they are equal, so yes."}]},{"id":"logic","title":"Combining conditions","tagline":"and, or, not.","level":"beginner","goals":["Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code>","Flip a condition with <code>!</code>","Read combined conditions like a sentence"],"remember":"&& is and (both true), || is or (at least one), ! is not (flips it).","terms":[{"term":"&&","def":"“and”: true only if both sides are true."},{"term":"||","def":"“or”: true if at least one side is true."},{"term":"!","def":"“not”: turns true into false and false into true."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>true || false</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"For <b>or</b>, one true side is enough."},{"q":"What is <code>!(5 &gt; 3)</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"5 &gt; 3 is true, and <code>!</code> flips it to false."}]},{"id":"if","title":"Making decisions with if","tagline":"Run some steps only when a condition is true.","level":"beginner","goals":["Run steps only when a condition is true","Handle the other case with <code>else</code>","Check several cases with <code>else if</code>"],"remember":"if (condition) { ... } runs only when the condition is true. else covers the rest. Only one branch runs.","terms":[{"term":"if","def":"Runs a block only when its condition is true."},{"term":"else","def":"The block that runs when the <code>if</code> condition is false."},{"term":"block","def":"Steps grouped between <code>{</code> and <code>}</code>."},{"term":"condition","def":"A true/false question, like <code>age &lt; 6</code>, that decides which way to go."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val x = 5</code>. What prints?<pre class=\"code\"><code>if (x &gt; 10) {\n    println(\"big\")\n} else {\n    println(\"small\")\n}</code></pre>","options":["<code>big</code>","<code>small</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"5 is not greater than 10, so the else block runs."},{"q":"In an <code>if / else if / else</code> chain, how many blocks run?","options":["Exactly one","Every block whose condition is true","None or all"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin runs the first true branch, or <code>else</code>, and skips the rest."}]},{"id":"if-expression","title":"if gives back a value","tagline":"Choose a value, not just an action.","level":"beginner","goals":["Pick one of two values with <code>if</code>","Know why this <code>if</code> needs an <code>else</code>","Explain what an expression is"],"remember":"val x = if (condition) a else b picks one of two values. The else is required.","terms":[{"term":"expression","def":"Code that produces a value, like <code>2 + 3</code> or <code>if (a) x else y</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = 4</code>. What is <code>if (n &gt; 3) \"high\" else \"low\"</code>?","options":["<code>\"high\"</code>","<code>\"low\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"4 is greater than 3, so the first value is chosen."},{"q":"Why does <code>val x = if (c) 1</code> fail?","options":["Missing else: x needs a value either way","if can’t give back numbers"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>else</code>, there is no value when the condition is false."}]},{"id":"when","title":"Choosing among many cases","tagline":"when: a tidy way to write a long if chain.","level":"beginner","goals":["Replace a long <code>if</code> chain with <code>when</code>","Match several values or a range in one case","Get a value back from <code>when</code>"],"remember":"when (x) { value -> result ... else -> fallback } picks the first matching case. a..b is a range, both ends included.","terms":[{"term":"when","def":"Picks the first matching case from a list. Can give back a value."},{"term":"range","def":"<code>a..b</code>: all numbers from a to b, both included."},{"term":"->","def":"Separates a <code>when</code> case from its result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Is 10 in <code>1..10</code>?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"<code>..</code> includes both ends."},{"q":"Two cases match the value. Which one runs?","options":["The first one","The last one","Both"],"a":0,"why":"<code>when</code> stops at the first match."}]},{"id":"while","title":"Repeating with while","tagline":"Do the same steps again and again, as long as a condition is true.","level":"beginner","goals":["Repeat steps with a <code>while</code> loop","Use a counter to end the loop","Spot and avoid an infinite loop"],"remember":"while (condition) { ... } repeats the block as long as the condition is true. Change the counter inside, or it never stops.","terms":[{"term":"loop","def":"Code that repeats a block of steps."},{"term":"while","def":"Repeats a block as long as its condition is <code>true</code>. Checks before every round."},{"term":"loop counter","def":"A <code>var</code> that changes each round, so the loop knows when to stop."},{"term":"infinite loop","def":"A loop whose condition never becomes false, so it never ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>var n = 10</code>, then <code>while (n &lt; 5) { println(n) }</code>. What is printed?","options":["10","Nothing","10, for ever"],"a":1,"why":"The condition is checked first. 10 &lt; 5 is false, so the block never runs."},{"q":"What makes a <code>while</code> loop stop?","options":["Its condition becomes false","It has run 10 times","The block reaches its <code>}</code>"],"a":0,"why":"At <code>}</code> it goes back up and checks again. Only a false condition ends it."}]},{"id":"for-ranges","title":"Counting with for","tagline":"Go through a range of numbers, one at a time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Count through numbers with <code>for</code> and <code>1..5</code>","Count down or skip with <code>downTo</code> and <code>step</code>","Leave out the end with <code>until</code>"],"remember":"for (x in 1..5) runs once per number. until leaves out the end, downTo counts down, step jumps.","terms":[{"term":"for","def":"Runs a block once for each value in a range. The name in brackets holds the current value."},{"term":"until","def":"<code>a until b</code>: from a up to b, leaving b out."},{"term":"downTo","def":"<code>a downTo b</code>: counts down from a to b, both included."},{"term":"step","def":"How far to jump each round: <code>0..10 step 2</code> gives 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times does <code>for (i in 1 until 4)</code> run its block?","options":["3","4","5"],"a":0,"why":"1, 2, 3. <code>until</code> stops before 4."},{"q":"Which numbers does <code>for (n in 0..6 step 3)</code> go through?","options":["0, 3, 6","0, 3","3, 6"],"a":0,"why":"It starts at 0 and jumps by 3. The end, 6, is part of the range."},{"q":"What does <code>for (n in 3..1) { println(n) }</code> print?","options":["3, 2, 1","1, 2, 3","Nothing"],"a":2,"why":"<code>3..1</code> would have to go up from 3 to 1, which is impossible, so the range is empty. Use <code>downTo</code>."}]},{"id":"functions","title":"Writing your own function","tagline":"Give a group of steps a name, then use it as often as you like.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write your own function with <code>fun</code>","Call a function by its name","Reuse steps instead of copying them"],"remember":"fun name() { ... } defines a function. name() calls it: the steps run, then the program continues after the call.","terms":[{"term":"function","def":"A named group of steps you can run whenever you need them."},{"term":"define","def":"Write a function with <code>fun</code>: its name and its steps. This does not run it."},{"term":"call","def":"Run a function by writing its name with brackets: <code>greet()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When do the steps inside a function run?","options":["When the program starts","Each time the function is called","Once, where the function is written"],"a":1,"why":"Defining only describes the steps. Each call runs them."},{"q":"<code>main</code> calls <code>cheer()</code> three times. How many times do <code>cheer</code>’s steps run?","options":["1","3","4"],"a":1,"why":"Once per call."}]},{"id":"parameters","title":"Giving a function inputs","tagline":"Parameters let one function work with different values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give a function inputs with parameters","Pass values to it as arguments","Match arguments to parameters in order and type"],"remember":"Parameters (name: Type) are a function’s inputs. Arguments are the values you pass in a call, in the same order.","terms":[{"term":"parameter","def":"An input a function asks for, written <code>name: Type</code> in its brackets."},{"term":"argument","def":"The value you pass for a parameter when you call: the <code>\"Tea\"</code> in <code>printPrice(\"Tea\", 3)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>fun add(a: Int, b: Int)</code>, what are <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>?","options":["Parameters","Arguments","Types"],"a":0,"why":"The names in the definition are parameters. The values in a call are arguments."},{"q":"How many arguments does a call to <code>fun move(x: Int, y: Int, speed: Int)</code> need?","options":["1","2","3"],"a":2,"why":"One for each parameter, separated by commas."}]},{"id":"return","title":"Getting a result back","tagline":"return hands a value back to whoever called the function.","level":"beginner","goals":["Get a value back from a function with <code>return</code>","Write a function’s return type","Use the short <code>= value</code> form"],"remember":"fun f(...): Type { return value } gives a value back. No return type means Unit. Short form: fun f(...) = value.","terms":[{"term":"return","def":"Ends the function and hands a value back to where it was called."},{"term":"return type","def":"The type of value a function gives back, written after its brackets: <code>): Int</code>."},{"term":"Unit","def":"The return type of a function that gives back no useful value. Used when you leave the return type out."},{"term":"single-expression function","def":"A short form, <code>fun f(n: Int) = n * 2</code>, where the value after <code>=</code> is the result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(next(4))</code> print, with <code>fun next(n: Int) = n + 1</code>?","options":["4","5","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"The short form gives back the value after <code>=</code>: 4 + 1."},{"q":"A function has no return type written. What does it return?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>Unit</code>, no useful value","Whatever it prints"],"a":1,"why":"Leaving the return type out means <code>Unit</code>. Printing is not returning."},{"q":"What happens to the lines after <code>return</code> in the same block?","options":["They run after returning","They never run: return ends the function","They run first"],"a":1,"why":"<code>return</code> leaves the function straight away."}]},{"id":"default-args","title":"Default and named arguments","tagline":"Give parameters a usual value, and name arguments to make calls clear.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give a parameter a default value","Name arguments in a call to make it clear","Skip arguments that have a default"],"remember":"name: Type = value gives a parameter a default. name = value in a call is a named argument: any order, skip what has a default.","terms":[{"term":"default value","def":"A value a parameter uses when the call leaves it out: <code>size: String = \"medium\"</code>."},{"term":"named argument","def":"An argument written with its parameter’s name, like <code>size = \"large\"</code>. Order doesn’t matter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>fun greet(name: String, greeting: String = \"Hello\")</code>, what does <code>greet(\"Ada\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Hello, Ada!</code>","<code>Ada, Hello!</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>greeting</code> uses its default, <code>\"Hello\"</code>."},{"q":"Is <code>greet(greeting = \"Hi\", name = \"Lin\")</code> allowed, with the names in a different order?","options":["Yes, names decide where each value goes","No, arguments must follow the parameter order"],"a":0,"why":"With named arguments, the order doesn’t matter."}]},{"id":"scope","title":"Where a name exists","tagline":"A box made inside a block lives only inside that block.","level":"beginner","goals":["Know where a name can be used","Use outer names from inside a block","Avoid confusing names that hide each other"],"remember":"A name exists from where it is made to the end of its block. Inner blocks can see outer names, not the other way round.","terms":[{"term":"scope","def":"The part of the code where a name exists and can be used."},{"term":"local variable","def":"A box made inside a block or function. It exists only until that block’s <code>}</code>."},{"term":"shadowing","def":"An inner box with the same name as an outer one, hiding it inside the inner block."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A box is made inside a <code>for</code> block. Can you use it after the loop?","options":["Yes","No, it only exists inside the block"],"a":1,"why":"It is local to the block and disappears at its <code>}</code>."},{"q":"Two different functions each make a <code>val message</code>. What happens?","options":["A compile error: the name is taken","Nothing bad: each one is local to its own function","The second replaces the first"],"a":1,"why":"Each <code>message</code> lives only inside its own function, so they never meet."}]},{"id":"null","title":"When there might be no value","tagline":"Sometimes there is nothing to put in the box. Kotlin calls that null.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what <code>null</code> means","Allow a missing value with a <code>?</code> type","Read a number safely with <code>toIntOrNull()</code>"],"remember":"null means “no value”. Only a type with ? (like String? or Int?) may hold it.","terms":[{"term":"null","def":"A special value meaning “no value here”."},{"term":"nullable type","def":"A type with <code>?</code> after it, like <code>String?</code>. Its box may hold null."},{"term":"toIntOrNull()","def":"Turns text into an <code>Int</code>, or gives null if there is no number in it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>val a: Int = null</code>","<code>val a: Int? = null</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Only a nullable type, with <code>?</code>, may hold null."},{"q":"What is <code>\"twelve\".toIntOrNull()</code>?","options":["<code>12</code>","<code>0</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":2,"why":"There are no digits in the text, so it gives back null instead of crashing."},{"q":"<code>val score: Int? = 5</code>. Why does <code>score + 1</code> not compile?","options":["The box is an <code>Int?</code>, so Kotlin must assume it might be null","You can’t add to 5"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin goes by the type. <code>Int?</code> means “a number, or nothing”, and you can’t add 1 to nothing."}]},{"id":"null-safety","title":"Working safely with null","tagline":"Use a value that might be missing, without crashing.","level":"beginner","goals":["Check for <code>null</code> before using a value","Use <code>?.</code> and <code>?:</code> to avoid crashes","Know why <code>!!</code> is risky"],"remember":"Check with if (x != null), call with ?., fall back with ?:. Avoid !!: it crashes when the value is null.","terms":[{"term":"safe call (?.)","def":"Makes the call only when the value is not null; otherwise the result is null."},{"term":"Elvis (?:)","def":"<code>a ?: b</code> gives <code>a</code>, or <code>b</code> when <code>a</code> is null."},{"term":"smart cast","def":"After a check like <code>x != null</code>, Kotlin treats <code>x</code> as not null inside that block."},{"term":"!!","def":"Says “this is not null”. Crashes with a <code>NullPointerException</code> if it is."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n: Int? = null</code>. What is <code>n ?: 5</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The left side is null, so Elvis gives the right side."},{"q":"<code>val s: String? = null</code>. What does <code>println(s?.toIntOrNull())</code> print?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","It crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The safe call is skipped because <code>s</code> is null, and the whole thing becomes null."},{"q":"What does <code>!!</code> do when the value is null?","options":["Crashes the program","Gives back 0","Skips the line"],"a":0,"why":"It promises the value is not null. When that promise is broken, you get a <code>NullPointerException</code>."}]},{"id":"lists","title":"Lists","tagline":"Keep many values together, in order.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep many values in order with <code>listOf</code>","Read an item by its index, starting at 0","Get a list’s length with <code>size</code>"],"remember":"listOf(a, b, c) keeps items in order. Indexes start at 0, so the last one is size - 1.","terms":[{"term":"list","def":"Several values kept in order in one box, made with <code>listOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"index","def":"An item’s position in a list, counting from 0."},{"term":"List<String>","def":"The type of a list whose items are all Strings."},{"term":"out of bounds","def":"Asking for an index the list doesn’t have. The program crashes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = listOf(5, 8, 2)</code>. What is <code>n[1]</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>8</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Index 0 is 5, index 1 is 8."},{"q":"A list has 4 items. What is its last index?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>3</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Counting from 0: 0, 1, 2, 3."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(5, 8, 2).size</code>?","options":["<code>3</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>15</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>size</code> counts the items. It doesn’t add them up."}]},{"id":"list-loop","title":"Going through a list","tagline":"Do something with every item, one by one.","level":"beginner","goals":["Do something with every item using <code>for</code>","Get each item’s position with <code>indices</code>","Get item and position together with <code>withIndex()</code>"],"remember":"for (item in list) runs once per item. Use indices or withIndex() when you also need the position.","terms":[{"term":"for (item in list)","def":"A loop that runs its block once for every item, with <code>item</code> holding the current one."},{"term":"indices","def":"The range of valid indexes of a list, like <code>0..2</code>."},{"term":"withIndex()","def":"Lets a loop get the index and the item together: <code>for ((i, item) in list.withIndex())</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>var total = 0\nfor (n in listOf(2, 3, 5)) {\n    total += n\n}\nprintln(total)</code></pre>","options":["<code>10</code>","<code>5</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The loop adds every item: 2 + 3 + 5."},{"q":"<code>val names = listOf(\"Ada\", \"Sam\", \"Lin\")</code>. What is <code>names.indices</code>?","options":["<code>0..2</code>","<code>1..3</code>","<code>0..3</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Three items have the indexes 0, 1 and 2."},{"q":"In <code>for ((i, name) in names.withIndex())</code>, what is <code>i</code> in the first round?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>1</code>","<code>\"Ada\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>i</code> gets the index, and indexes start at 0. The item goes into <code>name</code>."}]},{"id":"mutable-lists","title":"Lists that change","tagline":"Add and remove items while the program runs.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a list that can change with <code>mutableListOf</code>","Add, remove and replace items","Know when to stay with <code>listOf</code>"],"remember":"mutableListOf can change: add() at the end, remove() an item, list[i] = x to replace. listOf can only be read.","terms":[{"term":"mutable","def":"Can be changed after it is made."},{"term":"mutableListOf","def":"Makes a list you can add to, remove from and change."},{"term":"add() / remove()","def":"<code>add(x)</code> puts <code>x</code> at the end; <code>remove(x)</code> takes the first <code>x</code> out."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val l = mutableListOf(\"a\", \"b\")</code>, then <code>l.add(\"c\")</code>, then <code>l.remove(\"a\")</code>. What is <code>l</code>?","options":["<code>[b, c]</code>","<code>[a, b]</code>","<code>[a, b, c]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"\"c\" goes on the end, then \"a\" is taken out."},{"q":"Can a <code>val</code> hold a mutable list that you add to?","options":["Yes: val stops you replacing the list, not changing what is in it","No: you need var"],"a":0,"why":"The box keeps the same list. Adding items changes the list, not the box."},{"q":"<code>val l = mutableListOf(\"x\")</code>. What does <code>l.remove(\"y\")</code> do?","options":["Nothing changes","The program crashes","It removes \"x\""],"a":0,"why":"There is no \"y\" to remove, so the list stays as it is. (<code>remove</code> gives back <code>false</code> to say so.)"}]},{"id":"sets","title":"Sets","tagline":"A collection where every value appears only once.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep each value once with <code>setOf</code>","Check if a value is there with <code>in</code>","Choose between a set and a list"],"remember":"setOf keeps each value once. Ask contains(x), or x in set; there are no positions.","terms":[{"term":"set","def":"A collection where each value appears at most once, made with <code>setOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"duplicate","def":"The same value a second time."},{"term":"contains()","def":"Gives <code>true</code> if the value is in the collection. <code>x in s</code> means the same."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>setOf(1, 1, 2, 2, 2).size</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Only two different values: 1 and 2."},{"q":"<code>val s = setOf(\"a\", \"b\")</code>. What is <code>\"c\" in s</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"\"c\" was never put in the set."},{"q":"When is a set a better choice than a list?","options":["When each value should appear once and you mainly ask “is it in there?”","When you need items by position"],"a":0,"why":"Sets drop duplicates and answer <code>contains</code>. Positions need a list."}]},{"id":"maps","title":"Maps","tagline":"Look up a value by its key, like a word in a dictionary.","level":"beginner","goals":["Store key–value pairs with <code>mapOf</code>","Look up a value by its key","Handle a missing key without crashing"],"remember":"mapOf(key to value) looks values up by key. A missing key gives null, so use ?: or getOrDefault.","terms":[{"term":"map","def":"A collection of key-value pairs, made with <code>mapOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"key","def":"What you look a value up by. Each key appears once in a map."},{"term":"to","def":"Joins a key and its value into a pair: <code>\"Ada\" to 36</code>."},{"term":"getOrDefault()","def":"Looks up a key, or gives the fallback you pass when the key is missing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val m = mapOf(\"a\" to 1, \"b\" to 2)</code>. What is <code>m[\"b\"]</code>?","options":["<code>2</code>","<code>\"b\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The key \"b\" belongs to the value 2."},{"q":"Same map. What is <code>m[\"z\"]</code>?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"There is no key \"z\", so the lookup gives null."},{"q":"<code>val m = mutableMapOf(\"a\" to 1)</code>, then <code>m[\"a\"] = 5</code>. What does <code>println(m)</code> print?","options":["<code>{a=5}</code>","<code>{a=1, a=5}</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A key appears only once. Setting it again replaces its value."}]},{"id":"lambdas","title":"Functions as values","tagline":"A piece of code you can keep in a box and use later.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep a piece of code in a <code>val</code> as a lambda","Call a lambda and read its type","Use <code>it</code> for a single parameter"],"remember":"{ x: Int -> x * 2 } is a function you can keep in a val and call. (Int) -> Int is its type. With one known parameter, call it it.","terms":[{"term":"lambda","def":"A function without a name, written in <code>{ }</code>, that you can keep in a variable."},{"term":"it","def":"The name you can use for a lambda’s only parameter when Kotlin already knows there is one."},{"term":"function type","def":"The type of a function value, like <code>(Int) -&gt; Int</code> or <code>() -&gt; Unit</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val f = { a: Int -&gt; a + 1 }</code>. What is <code>f(9)</code>?","options":["<code>10</code>","<code>9</code>","<code>a + 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Calling <code>f</code> runs its code with <code>a</code> = 9."},{"q":"What does the type <code>(String) -&gt; Int</code> mean?","options":["Takes a String, gives back an Int","Takes an Int, gives back a String"],"a":0,"why":"Inputs are in the brackets on the left, the result is after the arrow."},{"q":"In <code>val triple: (Int) -&gt; Int = { it * 3 }</code>, what is <code>it</code>?","options":["The one value the lambda receives","A variable you must create first"],"a":0,"why":"The type says there is one Int parameter, so Kotlin lets you call it <code>it</code>."}]},{"id":"function-params","title":"Functions that take functions","tagline":"Hand a function a job to do, written as a lambda.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a function that takes another function","Pass a lambda as an argument","Write a lambda after the brackets"],"remember":"A parameter like action: (Int) -> Int takes a function. Pass a lambda; when it is last, write it after the (); when it is the only argument, drop the ().","terms":[{"term":"function-type parameter","def":"A parameter whose type is a function type, like <code>action: () -&gt; Unit</code>. It takes a lambda."},{"term":"trailing lambda","def":"A lambda written after the round brackets of a call, when it is the last argument. If it is the only one, the empty <code>()</code> can go."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>fun applyTo(n: Int, action: (Int) -&gt; Int) = action(n)</code>, what is <code>applyTo(3) { it + 1 }</code>?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>3</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda runs with <code>it</code> = 3, and 3 + 1 is 4."},{"q":"Which call means the same as <code>twice({ println(\"Hi\") })</code>?","options":["<code>twice { println(\"Hi\") }</code>","<code>twice(\"Hi\")</code>","<code>twice() println(\"Hi\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda is the only argument, so it can go after the call and the empty <code>()</code> can be dropped."},{"q":"In <code>applyTo(5) { it * 2 }</code>, how does Kotlin know the lambda has one parameter called <code>it</code>?","options":["The parameter type <code>(Int) -&gt; Int</code> says it takes one Int","<code>it</code> is always 5","It guesses from the <code>*</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The function you pass it to says what kind of lambda it expects."}]},{"id":"collection-ops","title":"Transforming lists","tagline":"Ask a list questions in one line: filter, map, sum and more.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep matching items with <code>filter</code>","Change every item with <code>map</code>","Answer questions with <code>sum</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>any</code>, <code>all</code>"],"remember":"filter keeps, map changes each item, sum/count/any/all give one answer, sortedBy puts in order. Each gives back something new.","terms":[{"term":"filter","def":"Gives a new list with only the items where the lambda gives true."},{"term":"map (on a list)","def":"Gives a new list where every item has been turned into something else by the lambda."},{"term":"any / all","def":"<code>any</code>: does at least one item match? <code>all</code>: do they all match?"},{"term":"sortedBy","def":"Gives a new list in order, smallest first, by the value the lambda picks for each item."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>listOf(1, 2, 3, 4).filter { it &gt; 2 }</code>?","options":["<code>[3, 4]</code>","<code>[1, 2]</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only 3 and 4 are greater than 2. <code>filter</code> gives back a list, not a count."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(1, 2, 3).map { it + 10 }</code>?","options":["<code>[11, 12, 13]</code>","<code>36</code>","<code>[1, 2, 3, 10]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> adds 10 to each item and keeps them all in a new list."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(5, 8, 12).all { it &gt; 6 }</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"5 is not greater than 6, so not all items match. (<code>any</code> would be true.)"}]},{"id":"classes","title":"Your own types: classes","tagline":"Bundle related values into one thing with its own name.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make your own type with <code>class</code>","Create objects from a class","Read and change an object’s properties"],"remember":"class Name(val a: Type, var b: Type) is the plan; Name(x, y) makes an object; object.a reads a property.","terms":[{"term":"class","def":"A plan for your own type: which properties every object of that type has."},{"term":"object","def":"One real thing made from a class, like <code>Player(\"Ada\", 0)</code>."},{"term":"property","def":"A value that belongs to an object, read with a dot: <code>ada.name</code>."},{"term":"constructor","def":"The parentheses after the class name: the values needed to make an object."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Given <code>class Dog(val name: String)</code>, how do you make a dog?","options":["<code>Dog(\"Rex\")</code>","<code>class Dog(\"Rex\")</code>","<code>Dog.name = \"Rex\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Write the class name and pass the constructor values in parentheses."},{"q":"<code>class Counter(var count: Int)</code>. After <code>val a = Counter(0)</code>, <code>val b = Counter(0)</code> and <code>a.count += 1</code>, what is <code>b.count</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>1</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are separate objects, each with its own <code>count</code>."},{"q":"<code>class Car(val color: String, var km: Int)</code> and <code>val car = Car(\"red\", 0)</code>. Which line compiles?","options":["<code>car.km = 100</code>","<code>car.color = \"blue\"</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"<code>km</code> is a <code>var</code>, so it can change. <code>color</code> is a <code>val</code>."}]},{"id":"methods","title":"Functions inside a class","tagline":"Objects can do things, not only hold values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a function inside a class","Call it on an object with a dot","Use <code>this</code> to mean the object itself"],"remember":"A function inside a class works on the object it is called on: object.doSomething(). this means that object.","terms":[{"term":"member function","def":"A function written inside a class and called on an object: <code>ada.win(10)</code>. Also called a method."},{"term":"this","def":"Inside a class: the object the function was called on."},{"term":"state","def":"The current values of an object’s properties. Each object keeps its own."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Cup(var ml: Int) { fun fill() { ml = 250 } }</code>. After <code>val a = Cup(0)</code>, <code>val b = Cup(0)</code> and <code>a.fill()</code>, what is <code>b.ml</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>250</code>","<code>500</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>fill</code> ran on <code>a</code>, so only <code>a.ml</code> changed."},{"q":"Inside a member function, what does <code>this</code> mean?","options":["The object the function was called on","The class itself, for every object at once","The <code>main</code> function"],"a":0,"why":"In <code>ada.win(10)</code>, <code>this</code> is <code>ada</code>."},{"q":"How do you call <code>fill</code> on the cup <code>a</code>?","options":["<code>a.fill()</code>","<code>fill(a)</code>","<code>Cup.fill()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The object, a dot, then the function call."}]},{"id":"encapsulation-basics","title":"Hiding the inside","tagline":"private: let a class guard its own data.","level":"beginner","goals":["Hide a class’s data with <code>private</code>","Let others read but not change with <code>private set</code>","Change data only through checking functions"],"remember":"private: only the class itself can use it. private set under a var: everyone can read it, only the class can change it.","terms":[{"term":"private","def":"Only code inside the same class can use this property or function."},{"term":"private set","def":"Written under a <code>var</code> property: anyone can read it, only the class can change it."},{"term":"encapsulation","def":"Keeping an object’s inside hidden, so outside code goes through its functions."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>class Phone</code> there is <code>private var pin = 0</code>. Where can <code>pin</code> be used?","options":["Only inside <code>Phone</code>","Anywhere in the program","Only in <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>private</code> limits it to the class it is written in."},{"q":"<code>var level = 1</code> with <code>private set</code> under it, in <code>class Game</code>. Outside, what does <code>println(game.level)</code> do?","options":["Prints <code>1</code>","Compile error","Prints nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Only changing is private. Reading is open to everyone."},{"q":"Why make a property private?","options":["So every change goes through the class’s own functions","It makes the program run faster","Kotlin requires it for every property"],"a":0,"why":"The class can check each change and keep its data valid."}]},{"id":"data-classes","title":"Data classes","tagline":"One word gives you printing, comparing and copying.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a class that only holds values with <code>data</code>","Print and compare objects by their values","Make a changed copy with <code>copy</code>"],"remember":"data class: readable printing (toString), == compares values (equals), and copy(name = value) for a changed copy.","terms":[{"term":"data class","def":"A class for holding values. Kotlin writes <code>toString</code>, <code>equals</code> and <code>copy</code> for it."},{"term":"toString","def":"Turns an object into text. <code>println</code> uses it."},{"term":"equals","def":"Decides whether two objects are equal. <code>==</code> uses it."},{"term":"copy","def":"Makes a new object with the same values, except the ones you name."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>data class Cat(val name: String, val age: Int)</code>. What does <code>println(Cat(\"Tom\", 2))</code> print?","options":["<code>Cat(name=Tom, age=2)</code>","<code>Cat@1b6d3586</code>","<code>Tom 2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A data class prints its name and each property with its value."},{"q":"<code>val a = Cat(\"Tom\", 2)</code>, then <code>val b = a.copy(age = 3)</code>. What is <code>a.age</code> now?","options":["<code>2</code>","<code>3</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>copy</code> makes a new object. The original keeps its values."},{"q":"With the data class <code>Cat</code>, what is <code>Cat(\"Tom\", 2) == Cat(\"Tom\", 2)</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Same values in every property, so they are equal."}]},{"id":"enums","title":"A fixed set of choices","tagline":"enum class: a type with a short, fixed list of values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a type with a fixed list of values","Use an entry with a dot, like <code>Light.GREEN</code>","Cover every entry in a <code>when</code> without <code>else</code>"],"remember":"enum class Name { A, B, C } is a type with only those values. A when that covers every entry needs no else.","terms":[{"term":"enum class","def":"A type with a fixed list of values, such as <code>enum class Light { RED, YELLOW, GREEN }</code>."},{"term":"entry","def":"One value of an enum class, used with a dot: <code>Light.RED</code>."},{"term":"exhaustive","def":"A <code>when</code> that covers every possible value, so it needs no <code>else</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>enum class Coin { HEADS, TAILS }</code>. Which line is valid?","options":["<code>val c = Coin.HEADS</code>","<code>val c = Coin(\"HEADS\")</code>","<code>val c = HEADS</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Entries are used with the type name and a dot. You don’t create new ones."},{"q":"A <code>when</code> that gives back a value covers every entry of an enum. Does it need <code>else</code>?","options":["No","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin can see that every possible value is handled."},{"q":"What does <code>println(Coin.TAILS)</code> print?","options":["<code>TAILS</code>","<code>Coin.TAILS</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An entry prints as its name."}]},{"id":"inheritance","title":"Building on a class","tagline":"A new class can start from an existing one and change what it needs.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build a class on another with <code>open</code> and <code>:</code>","Change a parent’s function with <code>override</code>","Run the parent’s version with <code>super</code>"],"remember":"open class Parent, then class Child : Parent(). Mark a function open in the parent to override it in the child; super calls the parent’s version.","terms":[{"term":"inheritance","def":"Making a new class from an existing one, so it gets the parent’s properties and functions."},{"term":"open","def":"Allows a class to be built on, or a function to be overridden."},{"term":"override","def":"Replaces an open function from the parent with the child’s own version."},{"term":"super","def":"Inside the child: the parent’s version, as in <code>super.greet()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Base</code> has no <code>open</code>. Does <code>class Child : Base()</code> compile?","options":["No: classes are closed unless marked open","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin classes are final by default."},{"q":"Dog overrides <code>sound()</code> from Animal. <code>val d = Dog(\"Rex\")</code>. Which version does <code>d.sound()</code> run?","options":["Dog’s","Animal’s","Both"],"a":0,"why":"The object is a Dog, so its own override runs."},{"q":"Inside an override, what does <code>super.greet()</code> do?","options":["Runs the parent’s <code>greet</code>","Runs the override again","Makes a new parent object"],"a":0,"why":"<code>super</code> means the parent’s version of the function."}]},{"id":"interfaces","title":"Promises a class keeps","tagline":"interface: different classes, one shared promise.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a promise as an <code>interface</code>","Make a class keep it with <code>override</code>","Treat different classes the same through one interface"],"remember":"interface lists what a class promises to do. class X : TheInterface keeps it with override. Code that needs the interface accepts any class that keeps it.","terms":[{"term":"interface","def":"A list of functions a class promises to have, without saying how they work."},{"term":"implement","def":"To keep an interface’s promise: <code>class Dog : Speaker</code> plus an <code>override</code> for each function."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does an interface hold?","options":["Functions a class promises to have","A finished object, ready to use","Only text values"],"a":0,"why":"It says what a class can do. Each class writes how."},{"q":"<code>Square</code> and <code>Circle</code> both keep the <code>Shape</code> interface. Which can you pass to <code>fun report(shape: Shape)</code>?","options":["Both","Only <code>Square</code>","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Any class that keeps the promise counts as a <code>Shape</code>."},{"q":"Which line makes Dog keep the <code>Speaker</code> interface?","options":["<code>class Dog : Speaker</code>","<code>class Dog : Speaker()</code>","<code>class Dog(Speaker)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An interface has no constructor, so there are no parentheses after its name."}]},{"id":"objects","title":"One-of-a-kind objects","tagline":"When there should be exactly one: object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make exactly one of something with <code>object</code>","Use it by its name","Put shared things in a <code>companion object</code>"],"remember":"object Name { ... } makes exactly one, used by its name. A companion object holds what belongs to the class itself.","terms":[{"term":"object declaration","def":"<code>object Name { ... }</code>: a type with exactly one object, made by Kotlin and used by its name."},{"term":"companion object","def":"A block inside a class for things that belong to the class itself. Reached with the class name: <code>Player.guest()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Five different functions all use <code>object Scoreboard</code>. How many Scoreboards are there?","options":["Exactly one","Five","None until someone writes <code>Scoreboard()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An <code>object</code> always has exactly one, and every use reaches that same one."},{"q":"<code>class Player(val name: String) { companion object { fun guest() = Player(\"Guest\") } }</code>. How do you call <code>guest</code>?","options":["<code>Player.guest()</code>","<code>guest.Player()</code>","<code>Player(\"Ada\").guest</code>"],"a":0,"why":"What is in the companion object belongs to the class, so you reach it through the class name."},{"q":"Which is a good fit for <code>object</code>?","options":["The app’s settings","Each song in a playlist"],"a":0,"why":"There is one set of settings. There are many songs, so a song is a class."}]},{"id":"sealed","title":"A closed family of types","tagline":"sealed: every possible case, known in advance.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make a closed family of types with <code>sealed</code>","Check which type a value is with <code>is</code>","Handle every case in a <code>when</code> without <code>else</code>"],"remember":"sealed closes a family of types. when over it uses is Type -> and needs no else when every member is handled.","terms":[{"term":"sealed","def":"Marks a class or interface whose members are all known: they are written next to it (same package and module; in this course, the same file)."},{"term":"is","def":"Checks whether a value is of a type: <code>is Done</code>. After it, Kotlin treats the value as that type."},{"term":"exhaustive","def":"A <code>when</code> that covers every possible case, so it needs no <code>else</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a <code>when</code> over a sealed type leave out <code>else</code>?","options":["Kotlin knows every member, so it can check all are handled","<code>else</code> is never allowed in <code>when</code>","Sealed types can’t be null"],"a":0,"why":"All members are written next to the sealed type, so Kotlin can see whether each one has a branch."},{"q":"You add <code>class Paused : Download</code>. What happens to <code>show</code>, which has no <code>else</code>?","options":["It stops compiling until it gets a Paused branch","Paused values print nothing","Nothing changes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>when</code> is no longer exhaustive, and Kotlin points to it."},{"q":"In <code>is Done -&gt; \"${d.size} MB\"</code>, why does <code>d.size</code> work?","options":["After <code>is Done</code>, Kotlin knows <code>d</code> is a Done","Every Download has a size","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>is</code> check works like a null check: inside that branch, Kotlin treats <code>d</code> as a Done."}]},{"id":"exceptions","title":"When something goes wrong at run time","tagline":"Catch a problem instead of crashing.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Catch a problem with <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code>","Run steps either way with <code>finally</code>","Report a problem yourself with <code>throw</code>"],"remember":"try { risky } catch (e: Exception) { handle } stops a crash. finally always runs. throw reports a problem yourself.","terms":[{"term":"exception","def":"An object describing a problem that happened while the program ran."},{"term":"throw","def":"Report a problem: stops the current code and hands the exception up to the caller."},{"term":"try / catch","def":"<code>try</code> runs risky code; if it throws, <code>catch</code> runs instead of crashing."},{"term":"finally","def":"A block after <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> that runs every time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>try {\n    println(\"A\")\n    throw Exception(\"oops\")\n    println(\"B\")\n} catch (e: Exception) {\n    println(\"C\")\n}</code></pre>","options":["<code>A</code> then <code>C</code>","<code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, <code>C</code>","Only <code>C</code>"],"a":0,"why":"\"A\" prints first. The throw skips the rest of <code>try</code>, so \"B\" never prints, and <code>catch</code> prints \"C\"."},{"q":"When does a <code>finally</code> block run?","options":["Every time","Only when something went wrong","Only when nothing went wrong"],"a":0,"why":"<code>finally</code> runs whether an exception happened or not."},{"q":"An exception is thrown and no <code>catch</code> handles it. What happens?","options":["The program crashes","Kotlin ignores it","The line is tried again"],"a":0,"why":"An exception that is never caught stops the program: a crash."}]},{"id":"extensions","title":"Adding functions to existing types","tagline":"Give Int or String a new function of your own.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add your own function to <code>Int</code> or <code>String</code>","Use <code>this</code> for the value before the dot"],"remember":"fun Type.name() adds a function to an existing type. Inside, this is the value before the dot.","terms":[{"term":"extension function","def":"A function added to an existing type from outside it: <code>fun Int.isEven()</code>."},{"term":"receiver","def":"The value an extension is called on, the one before the dot. Inside, it is <code>this</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>fun Int.plusOne() = this + 1</code>. What is <code>5.plusOne()</code>?","options":["<code>6</code>","<code>5</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>this</code> is 5, so 5 + 1 is 6."},{"q":"Does an extension function change the <code>String</code> type itself?","options":["No, it is a function you call with a dot","Yes, it is added inside String"],"a":0,"why":"String stays as it is. You call your function as if it were part of it."},{"q":"In <code>\"Oslo\".exclaim()</code>, what is the receiver?","options":["<code>\"Oslo\"</code>","<code>exclaim</code>","<code>String</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The receiver is the value before the dot."}]},{"id":"scope-functions","title":"let, apply, also, run","tagline":"Four small helpers that run a block on one object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Run a block on one object with <code>let</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>also</code>, <code>run</code>","Tell <code>it</code> from <code>this</code> in each one","Know which ones give back the object"],"remember":"let and also use it; apply and run use this. apply and also give back the object; let and run give back the block’s result.","terms":[{"term":"let","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>it</code> and gives back the block’s result. Often used with <code>?.</code>."},{"term":"apply","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>this</code> and gives back the object. Good for setting up."},{"term":"also","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>it</code> and gives back the object. Good for an extra step, like printing."},{"term":"run","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>this</code> and gives back the block’s result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which scope function uses <code>this</code> and gives back the object itself?","options":["<code>apply</code>","<code>let</code>","<code>run</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>apply</code>: <code>this</code> inside, the object out. Ideal for setting things up."},{"q":"What is <code>val x = 10.let { it * 2 }</code>?","options":["<code>20</code>","<code>10</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>let</code> gives back the block’s result: 10 * 2."},{"q":"<code>name</code> is <code>null</code>. What does <code>name?.let { println(it) }</code> do?","options":["Nothing: the block doesn’t run","Prints <code>null</code>","Crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>?.</code> stops at null, so <code>let</code> is never called."}]},{"id":"properties","title":"Smarter properties","tagline":"Properties that check new values or work themselves out.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Check new values with a setter","Work a value out on every read with a getter","Use <code>field</code> to reach the property’s own box"],"remember":"set(value) { field = ... } checks new values; get() = ... works a value out on every read.","terms":[{"term":"setter","def":"Code that runs whenever a property gets a new value: <code>set(value) { ... }</code>."},{"term":"getter","def":"Code that runs whenever a property is read: <code>get() = ...</code>."},{"term":"backing field","def":"The box that really holds a property’s value. In a setter or getter it is called <code>field</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>set(value) { ... }</code>, what is <code>field</code>?","options":["The box that really holds the property’s value","The new value being assigned","Another name for the class"],"a":0,"why":"<code>value</code> is the new value; <code>field</code> is where the property keeps it."},{"q":"<code>val area: Int get() = width * height</code>. When is the area worked out?","options":["Every time <code>area</code> is read","Once, when the object is made"],"a":0,"why":"A getter runs on every read, so it always matches the current values."},{"q":"Does <code>val isAlive: Boolean get() = health &gt; 0</code> keep a box of its own?","options":["No: the getter works the answer out on every read","Yes: it stores true or false once"],"a":0,"why":"A property with only a getter that never uses <code>field</code> has no backing field. It follows <code>health</code> automatically."}]},{"id":"late-init","title":"Values that arrive later","tagline":"lateinit and by lazy: no value yet, and that’s fine.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Fill a property later with <code>lateinit var</code>","Work a value out on first use with <code>by lazy</code>","Choose between the two"],"remember":"lateinit var: set it before you read it. val x by lazy { ... }: worked out on first read, then remembered.","terms":[{"term":"lateinit","def":"Marks a <code>var</code> that gets its value after the object is made, before anyone reads it."},{"term":"by lazy","def":"<code>val x by lazy { ... }</code>: the block runs on the first read, and its value is remembered."},{"term":"initialize","def":"Give a variable or property its first value."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You read a <code>lateinit</code> property before giving it a value. What happens?","options":["The program crashes","You get <code>null</code>","You get an empty text"],"a":0,"why":"It throws <code>UninitializedPropertyAccessException</code>, and if nothing catches it, the program stops."},{"q":"A <code>by lazy</code> property is read three times. How many times does its block run?","options":["Once","Three times","Never"],"a":0,"why":"It runs on the first read; the next reads get the remembered value."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>lateinit var name: String</code>","<code>lateinit var age: Int</code>","<code>lateinit val name: String</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>lateinit</code> needs a <code>var</code>, and a type like <code>String</code>, not <code>Int</code>."}]}]},{"id":"git","title":"Git and the command line","description":"Save, share and undo your work: the terminal, commits, branches, merging, rebasing, GitHub and pull requests, from zero.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/git.js","lessons":[{"id":"terminal","title":"The terminal","tagline":"A window where you type commands instead of clicking.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open a terminal and run a command","Find out where you are with <code>pwd</code> and <code>ls</code>","Move between folders with <code>cd</code> and make one with <code>mkdir</code>"],"remember":"pwd says where you are, ls shows what is here, cd moves you, cd .. goes up one level.","terms":[{"term":"terminal, command","def":"The terminal is a window where you type commands, such as <code>pwd</code>, run them with Enter, and read what they print."},{"term":"prompt","def":"The sign, here <code>$</code>, where the terminal waits for your next command."},{"term":"working directory","def":"The folder the terminal is standing in. Commands act on it."},{"term":"path","def":"The folders that lead to a place, separated by <code>/</code>, such as <code>/home/ada/projects</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>cd ..</code> do?","options":["Moves up one level, to the folder that holds this one","Runs the previous command again","Deletes the current folder"],"a":0,"why":"<code>..</code> always means the folder one level up."},{"q":"<code>cd projects</code> printed nothing. What happened?","options":["It failed","It worked: many commands print nothing when they succeed","The terminal froze"],"a":1,"why":"When something goes wrong, you get a message. Silence usually means it worked."},{"q":"In the Windows Command Prompt, which command lists what is in a folder?","options":["<code>pwd</code>","<code>dir</code>","<code>mkdir</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The Command Prompt uses <code>dir</code> where macOS, Linux and PowerShell use <code>ls</code>."}]},{"id":"what-git","title":"What version control is","tagline":"Keep every version of your project without a pile of copies.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what version control keeps for you","Say why copies named like <code>Main_final_v2.kt</code> stop working","Check that Git is installed"],"remember":"Version control keeps one copy of your files plus a list of saved versions, each with a note, an author and a time.","terms":[{"term":"version control","def":"A tool that saves versions of a project and keeps the full history."},{"term":"version","def":"A saved snapshot of the whole project, with a note about what changed."},{"term":"Git","def":"The most used version control tool. You run it in the terminal with <code>git</code>."},{"term":"option","def":"A word starting with <code>-</code> or <code>--</code> that changes what a command does, like <code>--version</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a saved version in Git hold?","options":["Only the one file you changed","A snapshot of the project, plus what changed, who and when","A copy with <code>_v2</code> in its name"],"a":1,"why":"A version saves the whole project as it was, together with a note and its author and time."},{"q":"You run <code>git --version</code> and see <code>git version 2.43.0</code>. What does it mean?","options":["Git is installed and ready","Your project has 2.43 versions","Git must be updated first"],"a":0,"why":"The command only asks Git for its own version number. Getting one back means Git is there."}]},{"id":"init","title":"Starting a repository","tagline":"Tell Git which folder to keep a history for.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell Git your name and email, once per computer","Turn a folder into a repository with <code>git init</code>","Ask Git what it sees with <code>git status</code>"],"remember":"git init once per project folder, then git status any time you want to know what Git sees.","terms":[{"term":"repository","def":"A project folder that Git keeps a history for. Short: repo."},{"term":".git","def":"The hidden folder <code>git init</code> creates, where Git stores the history."},{"term":"git status","def":"Asks Git what it sees in the repository right now."},{"term":"git config","def":"Sets Git options, such as your name. <code>--global</code> means for every project on this computer."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Git keep a repository’s history?","options":["In a hidden <code>.git</code> folder inside the project","In your home folder","In a file named history.txt"],"a":0,"why":"<code>git init</code> creates <code>.git</code> inside the project folder, and everything Git saves goes there."},{"q":"After <code>git init</code>, <code>ls</code> shows nothing. Did it work?","options":["No, run it again","Yes: <code>.git</code> is hidden, and <code>ls -a</code> shows it"],"a":1,"why":"Names that start with a dot are hidden from a plain <code>ls</code>."},{"q":"You set your name with <code>git config --global</code>. When do you need to do it again?","options":["For every new project","Only on a new computer","Every day"],"a":1,"why":"<code>--global</code> saves it for every project on this computer."}]},{"id":"commits","title":"Saving a snapshot","tagline":"Pick changes with git add, save them with git commit.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell the working folder, the staging area and commits apart","Put a file in the staging area with <code>git add</code>","Save a commit with a clear message"],"remember":"Edit, git add what belongs together, git commit -m \"what it does\".","terms":[{"term":"commit","def":"A saved snapshot of the project, with a message, an author and an id like <code>14d1672</code>."},{"term":"staging area","def":"The waiting list for the next commit. <code>git add</code> puts changes on it."},{"term":"working tree","def":"Git’s name for the working folder: your project as you see and edit it. Git prints it in messages like <code>working tree clean</code>."},{"term":"untracked","def":"A file Git sees but does not save, because it was never added."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git add Main.kt</code> do?","options":["Saves a commit","Puts Main.kt in the staging area for the next commit","Creates a file named Main.kt"],"a":1,"why":"<code>add</code> only fills the waiting list. Nothing is saved until <code>git commit</code>."},{"q":"Which commit message will help you most a month from now?","options":["<code>\"stuff\"</code>","<code>\"Greet Oslo too\"</code>","<code>\"changes\"</code>"],"a":1,"why":"A good message says what the commit does."},{"q":"<code>git status</code> lists <code>todo.txt</code> under “Untracked files”. What does that mean?","options":["Git sees it but won’t save it until you add it","The file is broken","It is already in a commit"],"a":0,"why":"Untracked files have never been added, so no commit holds them."}]},{"id":"history","title":"Looking at history","tagline":"See what changed, when, and why.","level":"beginner","goals":["List your commits with <code>git log</code>","See changes you have not added yet with <code>git diff</code>","Open one commit with <code>git show</code>"],"remember":"git log lists commits, git diff shows what you have not added yet, git show opens one commit.","terms":[{"term":"hash","def":"A commit’s unique id, a long code like <code>7e085e9...</code>. The first 7 characters are usually enough."},{"term":"diff","def":"A list of changed lines: <code>+</code> for added, <code>-</code> for removed."},{"term":"git log","def":"Lists the commits, newest first."},{"term":"HEAD","def":"Marks the commit you are on now. In <code>git log</code> it shows as <code>(HEAD -&gt; main)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>git log</code>, which commit is listed first?","options":["The oldest","The newest"],"a":1,"why":"Newest first, so the latest work is at the top."},{"q":"A line in <code>git diff</code> starts with <code>-</code>. What does it mean?","options":["The line was removed","The line was added","The line has an error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>-</code> marks removed lines and <code>+</code> marks added ones."},{"q":"You changed <code>Main.kt</code> and ran <code>git add</code>. Now <code>git diff</code> prints nothing. Why?","options":["The change was lost","<code>git diff</code> only shows changes you have not added yet","Git is broken"],"a":1,"why":"The change is safe in the staging area. <code>git diff</code> shows only what is not added yet."}]},{"id":"ignore","title":"Ignoring files","tagline":"Keep secrets and generated files out of your history.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell which files should never be committed","Write a <code>.gitignore</code> file","Check with <code>git status</code> that Git skips them"],"remember":"Secrets and generated files go in .gitignore, and .gitignore goes in a commit.","terms":[{"term":".gitignore","def":"A file listing what Git should skip, one pattern per line."},{"term":"pattern","def":"A line in <code>.gitignore</code> that matches names. <code>*</code> means any text, a trailing <code>/</code> means a folder."},{"term":"generated file","def":"A file a tool makes, such as compiled output or a log. It can always be made again."},{"term":"secret","def":"A password or private key. It must never be in a commit."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these belongs in <code>.gitignore</code>?","options":["<code>Main.kt</code>","<code>secrets.txt</code> with a private key","<code>.gitignore</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Secrets must never be saved. Your code and the <code>.gitignore</code> itself should be."},{"q":"What does the pattern <code>*.log</code> match?","options":["Only a file literally named *.log","Every file whose name ends in .log","Every folder"],"a":1,"why":"<code>*</code> stands for any text, so <code>debug.log</code> and <code>run.log</code> both match."},{"q":"Should you commit the <code>.gitignore</code> file?","options":["Yes, so everyone on the project skips the same files","No, it is private to you"],"a":0,"why":"Once it is committed, the rules come with the project."}]},{"id":"branches","title":"Branches","tagline":"Try an idea on its own line while the working version stays safe.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain a branch as a label on a commit","Make a branch and move to it with <code>git switch -c</code>","Read <code>HEAD</code> to see which branch you are on"],"remember":"A branch is a label on a commit. git switch -c makes one and moves you to it; HEAD shows where you are.","terms":[{"term":"branch","def":"A label that points to a commit and moves forward when you commit on it."},{"term":"HEAD","def":"Marks where you are: <code>HEAD -&gt; main</code> means you are on the branch main."},{"term":"main","def":"The usual name of a project’s first branch, where the working version lives."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a branch in Git?","options":["A full copy of the project folder","A label that points to a commit","A backup on another computer"],"a":1,"why":"A branch is only a label, which is why making one is instant."},{"q":"<code>git branch</code> prints <code>* forecast</code> and <code>main</code>. What does the <code>*</code> mean?","options":["forecast is the branch you are on","forecast has unsaved files","forecast is the oldest branch"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>*</code> marks your current branch, the same one <code>HEAD</code> points to."},{"q":"You switch from <code>forecast</code> to <code>main</code>. What happens to <code>Forecast.kt</code>, which exists only on forecast?","options":["It is deleted for good","It leaves the folder, and comes back when you switch to forecast","It stays in the folder"],"a":1,"why":"Git makes the folder match the branch you are on. The file is safe in the forecast commit."}]},{"id":"merging","title":"Merging","tagline":"Bring the work from one branch into another.","level":"beginner","goals":["Bring a finished branch into <code>main</code> with <code>git merge</code>","Tell a fast-forward from a merge commit","Read what Git prints after a merge"],"remember":"Be on the branch that receives the work, then git merge the other one: a fast-forward if only one side moved, a merge commit if both did.","terms":[{"term":"merge","def":"<code>git merge name</code> brings the commits of branch <code>name</code> into the branch you are on."},{"term":"fast-forward","def":"A merge where Git only moves the branch label forward, because the branch you are on has no new commits of its own."},{"term":"merge commit","def":"A commit with two parents that joins two branches. Git makes one when both branches have new commits."},{"term":"parent","def":"The commit right before a commit. A merge commit has two."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does <code>git merge</code> do a fast-forward?","options":["When the branch you are on has no new commits of its own","When none of the files changed","Every time"],"a":0,"why":"Then the other branch already contains everything, so Git only has to move the label forward."},{"q":"How many parents does a merge commit have?","options":["One","Two","None"],"a":1,"why":"It joins two branches, so it points back to the last commit of each."},{"q":"You are on <code>main</code> and run <code>git merge units</code>. Which branch moves?","options":["<code>main</code>","<code>units</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"A merge changes only the branch you are on. <code>units</code> stays where it was."}]},{"id":"conflicts","title":"Merge conflicts","tagline":"When both branches changed the same line, Git asks you to decide.","level":"beginner","goals":["Spot a conflict in what <code>git merge</code> and <code>git status</code> print","Read the conflict markers and write the right result","Finish the merge with <code>git add</code> and <code>git commit</code>, or cancel it"],"remember":"Conflict: edit the file to the result you want, delete the markers, git add it, git commit. git merge --abort cancels.","terms":[{"term":"merge conflict","def":"Both branches changed the same lines in different ways. Git stops the merge and asks you to choose."},{"term":"conflict markers","def":"The lines <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> that Git writes around the two versions."},{"term":"resolve","def":"Edit the file into its final form, remove the markers, and <code>git add</code> it."},{"term":"--abort","def":"<code>git merge --abort</code> cancels a merge that stopped, and puts your files back as they were."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What sits between <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD</code> and <code>=======</code>?","options":["The version from the branch you are on","The version from the branch being merged in","The version from before both changes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>HEAD</code> is where you are. The side being merged in comes after <code>=======</code>."},{"q":"You edited the conflicted file into its final form. Which step tells Git it is fixed?","options":["<code>git add Main.kt</code>","<code>git status</code>","Saving the file"],"a":0,"why":"Saving changes the file, but Git keeps it marked as conflicted until you <code>git add</code> it."},{"q":"The conflict is too big to fix right now. What gets you back to before the merge?","options":["<code>git merge --abort</code>","Deleting <code>Main.kt</code>","<code>git commit</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>--abort</code> cancels the merge and puts your files back as they were."}]},{"id":"remotes","title":"Working with GitHub","tagline":"Keep a copy of your repository online, and stay in sync with it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Put a repository on GitHub with <code>git remote add</code> and <code>git push</code>","Copy a repository from GitHub with <code>git clone</code>","Bring in other people’s commits with <code>git fetch</code> and <code>git pull</code>"],"remember":"push uploads your commits; clone copies a whole repository; fetch downloads; pull downloads and merges.","terms":[{"term":"remote, origin","def":"A copy of your repository somewhere else, such as GitHub. <code>origin</code> is the usual name for your main one."},{"term":"push","def":"<code>git push</code> uploads your new commits to the remote."},{"term":"clone","def":"<code>git clone</code> copies a whole repository from a remote into a new folder."},{"term":"fetch, pull","def":"<code>git fetch</code> downloads new commits without touching your files. <code>git pull</code> fetches, then merges them into your branch."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git push</code> do?","options":["Uploads your new commits to the remote","Downloads commits from the remote","Saves a snapshot on your laptop"],"a":0,"why":"Push sends your commits up. Fetch and pull bring commits down; commit saves a snapshot."},{"q":"What is <code>origin</code>?","options":["A short name for the remote’s address","The first commit","A branch on your laptop"],"a":0,"why":"<code>git remote add origin</code> saved the GitHub address under that name."},{"q":"Which command updates your files with commits from GitHub?","options":["<code>git pull</code>","<code>git fetch</code>","<code>git push</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>pull</code> downloads and merges. <code>fetch</code> only downloads."}]},{"id":"pull-requests","title":"Pull requests and review","tagline":"Ask your team to look at a branch before it joins main.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open a pull request on GitHub from a branch you pushed","Review one: comment, approve or request changes","Merge it on GitHub and bring the result to your laptop"],"remember":"Push a branch, open a pull request, fix review comments with more commits on that branch, merge on GitHub, then pull main.","terms":[{"term":"pull request","def":"A page on GitHub that asks for a branch to be reviewed and merged into another branch, usually <code>main</code>."},{"term":"review","def":"A teammate reads the changes, comments, and then chooses Comment, Approve or Request changes."},{"term":"base, compare","def":"In a pull request, <b>base</b> is the branch that receives the work (<code>main</code>); <b>compare</b> is your branch."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a pull request?","options":["A request on GitHub to review a branch and merge it","A command that downloads commits","A copy of a repository"],"a":0,"why":"It is a page on GitHub around your branch. <code>git pull</code> is a different thing, despite the name."},{"q":"A reviewer chose <b>Request changes</b>. What do you do?","options":["Commit the fix on the same branch and push","Open a new pull request","Merge anyway"],"a":0,"why":"The pull request follows its branch, so new commits appear in it."},{"q":"The pull request was merged on github.com. How does your laptop get it?","options":["<code>git switch main</code>, then <code>git pull</code>","It arrives on its own","<code>git push</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Your laptop changes only when you download. Pull brings GitHub’s new <code>main</code> into yours."}]},{"id":"rebase","title":"Rebase","tagline":"Move your branch to the end of main, for a straight history.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Replay your branch on top of <code>main</code> with <code>git rebase</code>","Compare a rebased history with a merged one","Know when not to rebase: commits others already have"],"remember":"git rebase main replays your branch on top of main as new commits. Rebase only commits nobody else has.","terms":[{"term":"rebase","def":"<code>git rebase main</code> replays the commits of your branch, one by one, on top of <code>main</code>."},{"term":"replayed commit","def":"A copy of a commit made by rebase: the same change on a new parent, so it gets a new id."},{"term":"shared branch","def":"A branch that is on GitHub and that others may have pulled, like <code>main</code>. Don’t rebase its commits."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is different about your commits after a rebase?","options":["They have new ids","They lose their messages","They are uploaded to GitHub"],"a":0,"why":"Rebase makes each commit again on a new parent, so each gets a new id."},{"q":"Which branch is safe to rebase?","options":["Your own branch that nobody else has pulled","<code>main</code>, which the whole team uses","Any branch, at any time"],"a":0,"why":"Rebase replaces commits. Only do that with commits nobody else has."},{"q":"You rebased <code>rain</code> onto <code>main</code>. On <code>main</code>, what does <code>git merge rain</code> make?","options":["A fast-forward","A merge commit","A conflict"],"a":0,"why":"<code>rain</code> now starts at the end of <code>main</code>, so Git only moves the label."}]},{"id":"undo","title":"Undoing things","tagline":"Throw away an edit, take back a commit, or cancel one that is already shared.","level":"beginner","goals":["Throw away changes you haven’t committed with <code>git restore</code>","Take back your last commit with <code>git reset HEAD~1</code>","Undo a pushed commit safely with <code>git revert</code>"],"remember":"Not committed: git restore. Committed but not pushed: git reset HEAD~1. Already pushed: git revert.","terms":[{"term":"restore","def":"<code>git restore file</code> throws away uncommitted edits. With <code>--staged</code> it only unstages the file."},{"term":"reset","def":"<code>git reset HEAD~1</code> moves your branch back one commit and keeps its changes in your files."},{"term":"revert","def":"<code>git revert id</code> makes a new commit that does the opposite of commit <code>id</code>. Safe for pushed commits."},{"term":"HEAD~1","def":"The commit one before <code>HEAD</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A wrong commit is already on GitHub. Which command undoes it without rewriting history?","options":["<code>git revert</code>","<code>git reset HEAD~1</code>","<code>git restore</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Revert adds a new commit that undoes the old one, so history only grows."},{"q":"What does <code>git restore Main.kt</code> do to your uncommitted edits in that file?","options":["Throws them away","Commits them","Stages them"],"a":0,"why":"The file goes back to the way it was when you last added or committed it. Those edits can’t be brought back."},{"q":"What does <code>HEAD~1</code> mean?","options":["The commit one before <code>HEAD</code>","The very first commit","The next commit"],"a":0,"why":"<code>~1</code> means one step back from <code>HEAD</code>."}]}]},{"id":"kdeep","title":"Kotlin, deeper","description":"More of the Kotlin that libraries are built on: generic types, delegates, sequences, builders, Result and calling Java.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/kotlin-deep.js","lessons":[{"id":"generics","title":"Generic types","tagline":"One class or function that works for any type you pick.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a type like <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> as “list of String”","Write a class with a type parameter <code>&lt;T&gt;</code>","Write a function that works for any type"],"remember":"class Box<T>(val item: T): T stands in for a type, picked each time a Box is made. fun <T> name(...) does the same for a function.","terms":[{"term":"type parameter","def":"A stand-in for a type, in angle brackets: the <code>T</code> in <code>class Box&lt;T&gt;</code>. It gets a real type each time the class or function is used."},{"term":"generic","def":"A class or function with a type parameter, so one piece of code works for many types, like <code>List</code> or <code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code>."},{"term":"angle brackets","def":"<code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> around a type, as in <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code>: they say which type is used."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val b = Box(3.5)</code>. What type is <code>b</code>?","options":["<code>Box&lt;Double&gt;</code>","<code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code>","<code>Box&lt;Int&gt;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"3.5 is a Double, so Kotlin picks Double for T."},{"q":"Which line is a correct generic function?","options":["<code>fun &lt;T&gt; first(items: List&lt;T&gt;): T</code>","<code>fun first(items: List&lt;T&gt;): T</code>","<code>fun first(&lt;T&gt; items: List): T</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The type parameter is named in angle brackets after <code>fun</code>, before the function name."},{"q":"<code>fun unpack(box: Box&lt;String&gt;)</code>. Does <code>unpack(Box(5))</code> compile?","options":["No: 5 is not a String","Yes: any box fits"],"a":0,"why":"The parameter wants a box of Strings, and 5 is an Int."}]},{"id":"variance","title":"in and out","tagline":"When a Shelter<Cat> may stand in for a Shelter<Animal>.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> isn’t always a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code>","Mark a type parameter <code>out</code> when a class only gives T out","Mark it <code>in</code> when a class only takes T in"],"remember":"out T: T only comes out, so a Shelter<Cat> fits where a Shelter<Animal> is wanted. in T: T only goes in, so it works the other way round.","terms":[{"term":"variance","def":"The rule for whether a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> may be used where a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code> is wanted. Set with <code>out</code>, <code>in</code>, or neither."},{"term":"out","def":"On a type parameter: T only comes out (read or returned). Then a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> fits where a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code> is wanted."},{"term":"in (on a type parameter)","def":"T only goes in, as a parameter. Then a <code>Feeder&lt;Animal&gt;</code> fits where a <code>Feeder&lt;Cat&gt;</code> is wanted."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>fun countAll(animals: List&lt;Animal&gt;)</code>. Can you pass a <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code>?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>List</code> can only be read, so Kotlin declares it with <code>out</code>."},{"q":"<code>fun adopt(animals: MutableList&lt;Animal&gt;)</code>. Can you pass a <code>MutableList&lt;Cat&gt;</code>?","options":["No","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>adopt</code> could add a Dog to your list of cats. A <code>MutableList</code> takes items in too, so it has no <code>out</code>."},{"q":"<code>interface Printer&lt;___ T&gt; { fun print(item: T) }</code>. Which word fits the gap?","options":["<code>in</code>","<code>out</code>"],"a":0,"why":"T is only a parameter here: it goes in."}]},{"id":"delegation","title":"Delegation","tagline":"Hand a property’s work to a helper with by.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what <code>by</code> means in <code>val x by lazy { }</code>","Run code every time a property changes, with <code>Delegates.observable</code>","Read properties from a map with <code>by map</code>"],"remember":"by hands a property to a delegate: by lazy, by Delegates.observable(start) { _, old, new -> }, by map.","terms":[{"term":"delegate","def":"A helper object that a property hands its work to, named after <code>by</code>: <code>lazy</code>, <code>Delegates.observable</code>, a map."},{"term":"delegated property","def":"A property written with <code>by</code>, whose reading (and setting, for a <code>var</code>) is done by its delegate."},{"term":"import","def":"A line at the top of a file that brings in a name from a library: <code>import kotlin.properties.Delegates</code>."},{"term":"_","def":"Used as a lambda parameter name, it means “there is a value here, but I don’t need it”."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>val books by lazy { ... }</code>, what does <code>by</code> mean?","options":["Reading <code>books</code> is handled by the lazy helper","<code>books</code> is a copy of <code>lazy</code>","The block runs on every read"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by</code> hands the property to a delegate, here <code>lazy</code>."},{"q":"With <code>Delegates.observable(0) { _, old, new -> ... }</code>, when does the lambda run?","options":["After every new value is set","Every time the property is read","Once, when the object is made"],"a":0,"why":"It observes changes: each time the property is set, it runs with the old and the new value."},{"q":"<code>class Settings(map: Map&lt;String, String&gt;) { val theme: String by map }</code>. What is <code>Settings(mapOf(\"theme\" to \"dark\")).theme</code>?","options":["<code>\"dark\"</code>","<code>\"theme\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The property reads the map’s value under the key with its own name, <code>\"theme\"</code>."}]},{"id":"inline","title":"Inline functions","tagline":"Copy a function into the place that calls it, and get two new powers.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what <code>inline</code> does to a function that takes a lambda","Use <code>return</code> inside such a lambda to leave the outer function","Check a type parameter while the program runs, with <code>reified</code>"],"remember":"inline pastes a function and its lambda where it is called. That allows return inside the lambda, and reified T for is T checks.","terms":[{"term":"inline function","def":"A function marked <code>inline</code>: its body and its lambda are pasted where it is called."},{"term":"non-local return","def":"A <code>return</code> inside the lambda of an inline function. It leaves the function where the call is written."},{"term":"reified","def":"Marks a type parameter of an inline function whose real type is still known while the program runs, so <code>is T</code> works."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>inline</code> do to a function?","options":["Pastes its body, with the lambda, where it is called","Makes its lambda run twice","Lets it be called before it is written"],"a":0,"why":"The program does the same, without packing the lambda into an object first."},{"q":"Inside the lambda of an inline function, <code>return</code> leaves...","options":["the function where the call is written","only the lambda","the inline function, which carries on"],"a":0,"why":"It works like a <code>return</code> written right there, because after pasting, that’s where the code is."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>inline fun &lt;reified T&gt; isA(x: Animal) = x is T</code>","<code>fun &lt;reified T&gt; isA(x: Animal) = x is T</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The second gives <code>Only type parameters of inline functions can be reified.</code>"}]},{"id":"operators","title":"Operator overloading","tagline":"Teach your own class what +, [ ] and ( ) mean.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make <code>+</code> work on your own class with <code>operator fun plus</code>","Read items with <code>[ ]</code> through <code>operator fun get</code>","Call an object like a function with <code>invoke</code>"],"remember":"a + b calls a.plus(b), a[i] calls a.get(i), a(x) calls a.invoke(x), once the function is marked operator.","terms":[{"term":"operator overloading","def":"Giving a symbol like <code>+</code> or <code>[ ]</code> a meaning for your own class."},{"term":"operator","def":"Marks a function with one of the fixed names (<code>plus</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>invoke</code>...) so the matching symbol calls it."},{"term":"invoke","def":"The operator behind round brackets on an object: <code>hello(\"Ada\")</code> runs <code>hello.invoke(\"Ada\")</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"On your own class, <code>a + b</code> calls...","options":["<code>a.plus(b)</code>","<code>b.plus(a)</code>","<code>a.add(b)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The left side’s <code>plus</code> runs, with the right side as its argument."},{"q":"<code>shelf[0]</code> calls...","options":["<code>shelf.get(0)</code>","<code>shelf.invoke(0)</code>","<code>shelf.first()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Square brackets are the <code>get</code> operator."},{"q":"<code>Greeter</code> has <code>operator fun invoke(name: String)</code> and <code>val hi = Greeter(\"Hi\")</code>. Which call works?","options":["<code>hi(\"Lin\")</code>","<code>Greeter.hi(\"Lin\")</code>","<code>hi[\"Lin\"]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Round brackets on the object call <code>invoke</code>."}]},{"id":"value","title":"Value classes","tagline":"A separate type around one value, at no extra cost.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Wrap one value in its own type with <code>value class</code>","Let Kotlin catch swapped arguments for you","Know the rules: one <code>val</code>, and <code>@JvmInline</code> above it"],"remember":"@JvmInline value class Meters(val value: Int): its own type while you code, usually a plain Int while it runs.","terms":[{"term":"value class","def":"A class around exactly one <code>val</code>. It is its own type while you write code, and the plain value while the program runs."},{"term":"annotation","def":"A note for the compiler, written with <code>@</code> before a name, like <code>@JvmInline</code>."},{"term":"@JvmInline","def":"The annotation a value class needs on Android: store it as the plain value inside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why use <code>value class Meters(val value: Int)</code> instead of a plain <code>Int</code>?","options":["Kotlin catches a <code>Minutes</code> passed where <code>Meters</code> is wanted","It makes the number bigger","It lets the value change"],"a":0,"why":"Each value class is its own type, so mixing them up doesn’t compile."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>@JvmInline value class Grams(val amount: Int)</code>","<code>@JvmInline value class Point(val x: Int, val y: Int)</code>","<code>@JvmInline value class Grams(var amount: Int)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One property, and a <code>val</code>. With two, Kotlin says <code>Inline class must have exactly one primary constructor parameter.</code> (an older name for a value class)."},{"q":"While the program runs, what is a <code>Meters(5000)</code> usually stored as?","options":["The plain Int 5000","An object holding one Int","A String"],"a":0,"why":"That’s the point of a value class: the safety is checked while compiling, and no extra object is made."}]},{"id":"sequences","title":"Sequences","tagline":"Do the work one item at a time, and only as much as needed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how a chain on a sequence differs from one on a list","Turn a list into a sequence with <code>asSequence</code>, and back with <code>toList</code>","Tell when a sequence saves work"],"remember":"A list chain does each step for all items; a sequence takes one item through all steps, and only when a final step like first() or toList() asks.","terms":[{"term":"Sequence","def":"A chain of steps that takes one item through all of them before the next item, and only when a result is asked for."},{"term":"lazy","def":"Waiting to do work until the result is needed, like <code>by lazy</code> and sequences."},{"term":"final step","def":"A call like <code>first()</code>, <code>sum()</code> or <code>toList()</code> that asks a sequence for a result and makes the work happen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A sequence chain has <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> but no final step. How many times does <code>map</code> run?","options":["0","Once per item","Once"],"a":0,"why":"A sequence is lazy: nothing runs until a final step asks for a result."},{"q":"On a list, <code>listOf(1, 2, 3).map { ... }.filter { ... }</code>: what happens first?","options":["<code>map</code> runs on all three items, then <code>filter</code>","<code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> on item 1, then on item 2..."],"a":0,"why":"A list chain finishes each step for the whole list before the next step. The other order is what a sequence does."},{"q":"When does <code>asSequence()</code> help most?","options":["Many items, several steps, and you need only the first few results","A list of three items","When you want the items in reverse order"],"a":0,"why":"Then the sequence can stop early and skip most of the work."}]},{"id":"generators","title":"Building sequences","tagline":"Write a sequence step by step with sequence { } and yield.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build your own sequence with <code>sequence { }</code> and <code>yield</code>","Explain how the block pauses after each <code>yield</code>","Take a few items from a sequence that never ends, with <code>take</code>"],"remember":"sequence { yield(x) } hands out x and pauses until the next item is asked for. take(n) gets at most n items.","terms":[{"term":"sequence { }","def":"Builds a sequence from a block of code that hands out items with <code>yield</code>."},{"term":"yield","def":"Inside <code>sequence { }</code>: hand out one item, then pause until the next one is asked for."},{"term":"take","def":"<code>take(n)</code> gives at most the first n items, so even an endless sequence can end."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens right after <code>yield(x)</code> inside <code>sequence { }</code>?","options":["The block pauses until the next item is asked for","The block ends","The block starts again from the top"],"a":0,"why":"It remembers its place and its variables, and carries on from there next time."},{"q":"<code>evens</code> is the endless sequence 0, 2, 4, 6... What is <code>evens.take(3).toList()</code>?","options":["<code>[0, 2, 4]</code>","<code>[2, 4, 6]</code>","It never finishes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>take(3)</code> asks for the first three items, then stops."},{"q":"Which line never finishes, on the endless <code>evens</code>?","options":["<code>evens.toList()</code>","<code>evens.take(3).toList()</code>","<code>evens.first()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>first()</code> needs one item and <code>take(3)</code> three, but <code>toList()</code> wants them all."}]},{"id":"dsl","title":"Building a DSL","tagline":"Lambdas with a receiver let code read like a description.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a function type with a receiver, like <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code>","Write a builder function that sets up an object from a lambda","Explain why such a builder is type-safe"],"remember":"Type.() -> Unit is a lambda with receiver: inside it, the object is this. A builder makes the object, runs the lambda on it, and gives it back.","terms":[{"term":"lambda with receiver","def":"A lambda of a type like <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code>. Inside it, the object it runs on is <code>this</code>."},{"term":"builder","def":"A function that makes an object, lets a lambda with receiver fill it in, and gives it back."},{"term":"DSL","def":"Domain-specific language: a small language for one job, here made of Kotlin builders."},{"term":"type-safe","def":"Checked by Kotlin before the program runs: a wrong type or an unknown function doesn’t compile."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside a <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code> lambda, what is <code>this</code>?","options":["The Menu the lambda runs on","The lambda itself","Nothing: <code>this</code> only works inside a class"],"a":0,"why":"That’s what the receiver is: the object before the dot in <code>Menu.()</code>."},{"q":"Which scope function you know takes a lambda with receiver?","options":["<code>apply</code>","<code>let</code>","<code>also</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Inside <code>apply</code> (and <code>run</code>) the object is <code>this</code>. <code>let</code> and <code>also</code> use <code>it</code>."},{"q":"<code>Menu</code> has no <code>dessert</code> function. What happens with <code>menu { dessert(\"Cake\") }</code>?","options":["It doesn’t compile","It runs and skips that line","It crashes while running"],"a":0,"why":"The builder is type-safe: Kotlin says <code>Unresolved reference 'dessert'.</code>"}]},{"id":"errors","title":"Result and error handling","tagline":"A failure you expect can be an ordinary value.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Turn an exception into a value with <code>runCatching</code>","Read a <code>Result</code> with <code>getOrDefault</code>, <code>onSuccess</code> and <code>onFailure</code>","Choose between throwing an exception and returning a <code>Result</code>"],"remember":"runCatching { } turns a throw into a Result. Get the value out with getOrDefault or getOrNull, or react with onSuccess / onFailure.","terms":[{"term":"Result","def":"A box holding either a success with a value, or a failure with an exception: <code>Result&lt;Int&gt;</code>."},{"term":"runCatching","def":"Runs a block and gives back a <code>Result</code>: a success with its value, or a failure if it threw."},{"term":"success / failure","def":"The two cases of a Result. <code>onSuccess</code> runs only for a success, <code>onFailure</code> only for a failure."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>runCatching { \"abc\".toInt() }</code> give?","options":["A failure holding the exception","A crash","<code>0</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The exception is caught and kept inside the Result. Printed: <code>Failure(java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: \"abc\")</code>."},{"q":"Which gives the number inside <code>r</code>, or <code>-1</code> if it failed?","options":["<code>r.getOrDefault(-1)</code>","<code>r.getOrNull(-1)</code>","<code>r.onFailure(-1)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>getOrNull()</code> takes no argument and gives null on failure; <code>onFailure</code> takes a lambda, not a number."},{"q":"Your own code calls <code>setAge(-5)</code>, which should never happen. Throw an exception or return a Result?","options":["Throw: it is a bug, not a normal outcome","Return a Result","Neither: print a message and carry on"],"a":0,"why":"A Result is for failures the caller should expect and handle. A bug should stop loudly so you find it."}]},{"id":"java-interop","title":"Calling Java","tagline":"Kotlin and Java share one app. Mind the nulls at the border.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Call Java code from Kotlin","Spot a platform type and give it a safe Kotlin type","Make a companion function easy to call from Java with <code>@JvmStatic</code>"],"remember":"A value from Java has a platform type (String!): give it a Kotlin type, String? if it might be null. @JvmStatic makes companion functions plain to call from Java.","terms":[{"term":"Java","def":"The older language Android was first written in. Kotlin and Java code can call each other in one app."},{"term":"platform type","def":"The type of a value from Java, shown as <code>String!</code>: Kotlin doesn’t know if it can be null."},{"term":"@Nullable / @NotNull","def":"Annotations in Java code that tell Kotlin a value may be null (<code>String?</code>) or never is (<code>String</code>)."},{"term":"@JvmStatic","def":"On a function in a <code>companion object</code>: Java can call it as <code>Settings.load()</code> instead of <code>Settings.Companion.load()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Java method without annotations returns a String. What type does Kotlin see?","options":["<code>String!</code>: might be null, might not","<code>String</code>: never null","<code>String?</code>: always nullable"],"a":0,"why":"That’s a platform type. Kotlin can’t know, so it leaves the choice to you."},{"q":"A Java method is marked <code>@Nullable</code>. Its String arrives in Kotlin as...","options":["<code>String?</code>","<code>String</code>","<code>String!</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With the annotation, Kotlin knows it may be null and checks your code for it."},{"q":"Without <code>@JvmStatic</code>, how does Java call <code>load()</code> in <code>Settings</code>’s companion object?","options":["<code>Settings.Companion.load()</code>","<code>Settings.load()</code>","It can’t"],"a":0,"why":"Java sees the companion object as an object named <code>Companion</code> inside the class."}]}]},{"id":"oop","title":"OOP basics","description":"How to design with objects: modelling real things, identity, encapsulation, composition and inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction, cohesion and domain modelling, all in plain Kotlin.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/oop.js","lessons":[{"id":"objects","title":"Objects model things","tagline":"Read the problem: things become classes, actions become functions.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a short description into classes (the nouns) and functions (the verbs)","Decide which class a rule belongs in: the one that holds its data","Spot a rule that is repeated outside its object"],"remember":"Nouns in the description become classes, verbs become functions. Each rule goes in the class that holds the data it is about.","terms":[{"term":"modelling","def":"Describing real things in code: the things become classes, what they know becomes properties, what they do becomes functions."},{"term":"behaviour","def":"What an object can do: its member functions, like <code>lend()</code>. A rule about the object’s data belongs in its behaviour."}],"quiz":[{"q":"“A <em>lamp</em> can be <em>switched</em> on.” Which word most likely becomes a class?","options":["lamp","switched","on"],"a":0,"why":"A lamp is a thing (a noun), so it becomes a class. <em>Switched</em> is something done to it, so it becomes a function."},{"q":"A shop app says: “A customer adds items to a basket.” Which word most likely becomes a function?","options":["adds","customer","basket"],"a":0,"why":"Verbs are things an object does. Customer and basket are things, so they are more likely classes."},{"q":"Where should the rule “a book that is out can’t be lent” live?","options":["Inside <code>Book</code>, in <code>lend()</code>","In every screen that lends books","In <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Next to the data it is about, written once. Every screen then calls <code>book.lend()</code>."}]},{"id":"identity","title":"Same object or equal object","tagline":"A variable points to an object, and two names can point to the same one.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what <code>val b = a</code> copies","Ask “same object?” with <code>===</code> and “equal?” with <code>==</code>","Make a real copy of a data class object with <code>copy()</code>"],"remember":"val b = a copies the arrow, not the object. === asks “same object?”; == asks “equal?”.","terms":[{"term":"reference","def":"What a variable holds for an object: an arrow to it, not the object itself. <code>val b = a</code> copies the arrow."},{"term":"identity","def":"Being one particular object. <code>a === b</code> is <code>true</code> only when both point at the same object."},{"term":"equality","def":"Meaning the same thing, asked with <code>==</code>. A data class compares property values; a plain class compares identity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"After <code>val a = Book(\"Rain\")</code> and <code>val b = a</code>, how many Book objects exist?","options":["One","Two","None"],"a":0,"why":"Only one constructor call. <code>b</code> is a second arrow to the same book."},{"q":"<code>data class P(val x: Int)</code>. What do <code>P(1) == P(1)</code> and <code>P(1) === P(1)</code> give?","options":["<code>true</code> and <code>false</code>","<code>true</code> and <code>true</code>","<code>false</code> and <code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Equal values, but each <code>P(1)</code> is a separate object."},{"q":"<code>fun refill(c: Cup) { c.tea = \"mint\" }</code>. After <code>refill(mine)</code>, what is <code>mine.tea</code>?","options":["<code>\"mint\"</code>","What it was before","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"The function got an arrow to the same cup, so its change is the caller’s change."}]},{"id":"encapsulation","title":"Encapsulation: keeping data valid","tagline":"Write each rule once, and let only the class change its data.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name a rule an object must always keep (its invariant)","Check the rule in every function that changes the state","Share a list without letting others change it"],"remember":"Name the invariant, let only the class change its state, and check the rule in every function that changes it.","terms":[{"term":"invariant","def":"A rule about an object that must always be true, like “booked is never more than seats”."},{"term":"valid","def":"An object is valid while all its invariants hold."},{"term":"encapsulation","def":"Keeping state private so only the class’s own functions change it, and those functions keep the rules."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is an invariant?","options":["A rule about an object that must always be true","A property that is a <code>val</code>","A function that never changes"],"a":0,"why":"Like “booked seats never go over the seats on the bus”."},{"q":"<code>val items = mutableListOf&lt;String&gt;()</code> is a public property. Can outside code add to it?","options":["Yes: <code>val</code> only stops it pointing to another list","No: <code>val</code> makes the list unchangeable"],"a":0,"why":"The arrow is fixed, but the list it points at can still change. Keep it private."},{"q":"The app says 43 of 40 seats are booked. With good encapsulation, where do you look first?","options":["In Bus’s own functions","Everywhere in the program","In <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only Bus’s functions can change <code>booked</code>, so the bug has to be there."}]},{"id":"composition","title":"Composition","tagline":"Build a bigger object from smaller parts it has.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a class from parts it keeps as properties (“has a”)","Let an object do its work by asking its parts","Reuse one part class in several bigger classes"],"remember":"Composition: build an object from parts it has (“has a”), and let it do its work by asking those parts.","terms":[{"term":"composition","def":"Building an object from other objects it keeps as properties, and doing the work by asking them."},{"term":"has a","def":"The test for composition: “a phone has a battery”, so Battery is a part of Phone."},{"term":"part","def":"An object kept inside another as a property, like the <code>battery</code> in a Phone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which pair fits composition best?","options":["A car and its engine","A dog and an animal","A square and a shape"],"a":0,"why":"“A car has an engine.” The other two are “is a” sentences."},{"q":"In <code>class Phone { private val battery = Battery() }</code>, how should Phone use power?","options":["Call <code>battery.use(30)</code>","Change <code>battery.level</code> directly","Copy the Battery code into Phone"],"a":0,"why":"The part keeps its own rules. The phone asks; the battery decides."},{"q":"Why hand a part in through the constructor, as in <code>Drone(Battery())</code>?","options":["The code making the drone picks its battery","It makes the drone faster","Kotlin requires it for every part"],"a":0,"why":"The drone works with any Battery it is given. Making it yourself inside the class also works, but then the class always picks."}]},{"id":"inheritance","title":"Inheritance, carefully","tagline":"Build on a class only when “is a” is always true, and know what that ties you to.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Use the “is a” test before writing <code>: Parent()</code>","Explain how a change inside a parent can break a child","Call the parent’s version with <code>super</code>, not your own function again"],"remember":"Inherit only when “X is a Y” is always true, from a class made to be a parent. A child depends on its parent’s insides, so a change there can break it.","terms":[{"term":"is a","def":"The test for inheritance: “a savings account is an account” must be true always, not only sometimes."},{"term":"base class","def":"Another name for a parent class, the class others build on."},{"term":"fragile base class","def":"When a change inside a parent breaks a child that didn’t change, because the child relied on how the parent worked inside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which sentence passes the “is a” test?","options":["A savings account is an account","A car is an engine","A phone is a battery"],"a":0,"why":"It is true always. The other two are “has a”: composition."},{"q":"What is the fragile base class problem?","options":["A change inside a parent breaks a child that didn’t change","A parent class can’t be changed at all","A child class has too many functions"],"a":0,"why":"The child relied on how the parent worked inside, and that changed."},{"q":"Why does Kotlin ask for <code>open</code> before a class can be a parent?","options":["So being built on is a choice the class’s author made","To make the class run faster","Because open classes can’t have properties"],"a":0,"why":"A class meant to be a parent is written and changed with its children in mind."}]},{"id":"composition-vs-inheritance","title":"Choosing between them","tagline":"Composition or inheritance? Count what each one costs.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot when inheritance multiplies into too many classes","Swap a part while the program runs, using composition","Pick composition or inheritance for a new case"],"remember":"Start with composition: parts combine freely and can be swapped. Use inheritance for a true, lasting “is a” in a small family.","terms":[{"term":"trade-off","def":"A choice where each option gives you something and costs you something, like less code (inheritance) against free combining (composition)."},{"term":"combination","def":"One pick from each group, like wheels with a laser. With inheritance each combination needs its own class."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Robots have 3 kinds of mover and 2 kinds of tool. With composition, how many part classes do you write?","options":["5","6","9"],"a":0,"why":"3 movers plus 2 tools. With one class per combination you would need 6 at the bottom alone."},{"q":"Which one can change while the program runs?","options":["A <code>var tool: Tool</code> part","The parent class of an object","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"A part is a property, so a <code>var</code> can point to a new part. The parent is fixed when the object is made."},{"q":"When is inheritance a good fit?","options":["A small family where “is a” is always true","Whenever two classes share one function","When you need many combinations"],"a":0,"why":"Many combinations and shared bits of code are both better handled with parts."}]},{"id":"polymorphism","title":"Polymorphism","tagline":"One call, many behaviours, or one when over every kind: where each kind’s code lives.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Compare a <code>when</code> over kinds with one override per class","Explain how the real object, not the variable’s type, picks the code that runs","Choose by what grows: new kinds favour overrides, new operations favour sealed and <code>when</code>"],"remember":"Polymorphism: one call, and the real object decides which override runs. Overrides make new kinds cheap; sealed and when make new operations cheap. Pick by what grows.","terms":[{"term":"polymorphism","def":"One call that behaves differently depending on the object behind it, like <code>d.cost(km)</code> on a Bike or a Drone."},{"term":"dynamic dispatch","def":"Picking which override runs from the real object, while the program runs. The variable’s type only decides what you may call."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val d: Delivery = Van()</code>. Whose <code>cost</code> runs for <code>d.cost(5)</code>?","options":["Van’s","Delivery’s","Bike’s"],"a":0,"why":"The real object decides, and it is a Van. Delivery only promises <code>cost</code>; it has no body to run."},{"q":"A delivery app gets a new kind of vehicle every month. Which style keeps each change small?","options":["An interface with one class per kind","A <code>when</code> over kinds in every function","One big class with a Boolean per kind"],"a":0,"why":"New kinds are what grows, so each one should be a single new class. The callers stay the same."},{"q":"A sealed family of shapes is fixed, but keeps getting new operations: area, outline, a label. Which style keeps each change small?","options":["A <code>when</code> with no <code>else</code> per operation","A new override in every shape class","A <code>when</code> with an <code>else</code> branch"],"a":0,"why":"A new operation is then one new function. With no <code>else</code>, Kotlin also checks that every shape is handled."}]},{"id":"abstraction","title":"Abstraction","tagline":"Show a simple surface, and hide the detail behind it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Design a class whose public functions say what, not how","Keep the steps private so they can change freely","Tell abstraction apart from encapsulation"],"remember":"Keep the public surface small and named after what the caller wants. Keep the how private, so it can change.","terms":[{"term":"abstraction","def":"Giving something a simple surface and hiding the detail behind it, so callers only need to know what it does."},{"term":"surface","def":"Everything other code can use from a class: its public functions and properties."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is the better surface for a music player class?","options":["<code>play()</code> and <code>next()</code>","<code>setIndex()</code>, <code>readFile()</code> and <code>startSpeaker()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"They say what the caller wants. The other set exposes how it is done."},{"q":"Playlist changes how it stores its songs inside. What must callers of <code>next()</code> change?","options":["Nothing","Every call to <code>next()</code>","Only the first call"],"a":0,"why":"They only ever used the surface, and the surface stayed the same."},{"q":"Which question does abstraction answer?","options":["How little does the caller need to know?","How do I keep this data valid?","Which class is the parent?"],"a":0,"why":"Keeping data valid is encapsulation. The two often work together."}]},{"id":"interface-or-abstract","title":"Interface or abstract class","tagline":"Two ways to describe a family. Pick by what the family shares.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write an <code>abstract class</code> with an <code>abstract fun</code>","Explain why you can’t make an object of an abstract class","Choose between an interface and an abstract class"],"remember":"Interface: a promise of what a class can do; a class can keep many. Abstract class: shared values and code for close relatives, with abstract functions the children fill in; one parent only, and no objects of its own.","terms":[{"term":"abstract class","def":"A class that can’t be made into an object itself. It can hold values and finished functions, plus abstract functions for its children to write."},{"term":"abstract function","def":"A function with no body, like <code>abstract fun sound(): String</code>. Every child must <code>override</code> it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which one can hold a stored value with a starting value, like <code>var meals = 0</code>?","options":["An abstract class","An interface","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"An interface only promises. An abstract class is a class, so it can store values."},{"q":"A Duck must be both a <code>Swimmer</code> and a <code>Flyer</code>. Which choice is sure to compile?","options":["Both are interfaces","Both are abstract classes"],"a":0,"why":"A class can keep many interfaces but has only one parent class."},{"q":"Animal is an abstract class. What happens with <code>Animal(\"Cat\")</code>?","options":["It doesn’t compile","It makes an animal with no sound","It crashes when <code>sound()</code> is called"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin stops it before the program runs: <code>Cannot create an instance of an abstract class.</code>"}]},{"id":"delegation","title":"Delegation in design","tagline":"Hand the work to a helper object, and let Kotlin write the forwarding.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pass calls on to a helper object instead of inheriting from a class","Use <code>by</code> to let Kotlin write the forwarding functions","Override only the functions that should behave differently"],"remember":"Delegation: keep a helper and pass calls on to it. class X(private val inner: I) : I by inner forwards every function you don’t override.","terms":[{"term":"delegation","def":"Handing a job to a helper object instead of doing it yourself or inheriting it."},{"term":"delegate","def":"The helper object that receives the calls, like <code>inner</code> in <code>Logbook by inner</code>."},{"term":"forwarding","def":"A function that only passes the call on to the delegate, like <code>override fun size() = inner.size()</code>. <code>by</code> writes these for you."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>class A(private val b: Speaker) : Speaker by b</code>, A doesn’t override <code>bye()</code>. Whose <code>bye()</code> runs?","options":["<code>b</code>’s","A’s own, empty one","None: it doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by b</code> forwards every function A doesn’t write itself."},{"q":"Why does the counting logbook built with <code>by</code> count 3, not 6?","options":["The helper’s <code>addAll</code> calls the helper’s own <code>add</code>","<code>by</code> skips <code>addAll</code>","The count is reset after each call"],"a":0,"why":"There is no parent whose code calls back into ours. The helper only ever calls itself."},{"q":"Which line forwards the Storage interface to a helper called <code>inner</code>?","options":["<code>class Logged(private val inner: Storage) : Storage by inner</code>","<code>class Logged : Storage(inner)</code>","<code>class Logged by Storage</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The helper comes in through the constructor; <code>: Storage by inner</code> keeps the interface by forwarding to it."}]},{"id":"cohesion-coupling","title":"Cohesion and coupling","tagline":"Code is easy to change when each class has one focus and classes are linked loosely.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that does unrelated jobs (low cohesion)","Spot code that reaches into another class’s insides (tight coupling)","Count which classes a change touches, and bring that number down"],"remember":"High cohesion: each class has one focus. Loose coupling: classes use each other only through their surfaces. Then a change stays in one place.","terms":[{"term":"cohesion","def":"How well the things inside one class belong together. High cohesion: one clear focus."},{"term":"coupling","def":"How much one class depends on another. Tight: it reaches into the other’s insides. Loose: it uses only the other’s surface."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A class adds up prices, sends emails and formats dates. What is the problem?","options":["Low cohesion: three unrelated jobs","Tight coupling to Cart","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Each job is a separate reason to change. They belong in separate classes."},{"q":"Which line is loosely coupled to Cart?","options":["<code>cart.total()</code>","<code>cart.prices.sum()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It uses Cart’s surface. The other depends on how Cart stores its prices inside."},{"q":"How do private properties help keep coupling loose?","options":["Other classes can only use the surface, so they can’t depend on the insides","Private code runs faster","They make classes shorter"],"a":0,"why":"What others can’t reach, they can’t depend on, so it is free to change."}]},{"id":"domain-modeling","title":"Modelling a domain","tagline":"Turn a description of a problem into types that make mistakes hard to write.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pick the right tool for each part of a description: class, data class, enum or sealed","Put each rule in the class that owns the data","Let the compiler point to every place a new case must be handled"],"remember":"Turn the words of the problem into types: data class for values, classes for things with rules, enum for simple choices, sealed for cases with their own data. Put each rule where its data lives.","terms":[{"term":"domain","def":"The part of the world a program is about, like members, books and loans for a library."},{"term":"domain model","def":"The types that describe a domain in code, with each rule placed in the class that owns its data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A café has cup sizes small, medium and large, with nothing else to store. Which tool fits?","options":["An enum class","A sealed interface","A data class"],"a":0,"why":"A fixed list of simple choices, none carrying its own data."},{"q":"Where does the rule “a book on loan can’t be lent again” belong?","options":["In <code>Book.lendTo()</code>","In every screen with a Lend button","In the Member class"],"a":0,"why":"In the class that owns the book’s state, written once."},{"q":"You add <code>object Repairing : BookState</code>. What does Kotlin do?","options":["Every <code>when</code> over BookState with no <code>else</code> stops compiling until it handles Repairing","Nothing, until the program runs","The program crashes at the start"],"a":0,"why":"A sealed family is known in full, so Kotlin can check every <code>when</code> before the program runs."}]}]},{"id":"solid","title":"SOLID principles","description":"Five rules of thumb that keep your classes easy to change, one lesson each, with small Kotlin programs you can run and fix.","requires":["oop"],"file":"tracks/solid.js","lessons":[{"id":"srp","title":"Single responsibility","tagline":"One class, one reason to change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that does two different jobs","Ask “who would ask me to change this?”","Split it into two classes with one job each"],"remember":"One class, one job. If two different people would ask you to change a class, split it into two.","terms":[{"term":"design principle","def":"A rule of thumb for arranging code so it stays easy to change. It is advice, not a rule the compiler checks."},{"term":"SOLID","def":"Five design principles, one per letter: Single responsibility, Open/closed, Liskov substitution, Interface segregation, Dependency inversion."},{"term":"single responsibility","def":"A class has one job, so it has one reason to change. Ask: who would ask me to change this?"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “single responsibility” mean for a class?","options":["It has one job, so one reason to change","It has only one function","Only one object can be made from it"],"a":0,"why":"A class can have several functions, as long as they all serve the same job."},{"q":"A <code>Profile</code> class loads the user’s name and also picks the colors of the profile screen. Who might ask you to change it?","options":["Two different people: whoever owns the data and the designer","Nobody, it works","Only the person who wrote it"],"a":0,"why":"Two different askers means two jobs. Split it into a class for the data and one for the look."},{"q":"You split <code>Cart</code> into <code>Cart</code> and <code>Receipt</code>. The designer changes the wording. Which class do you edit?","options":["<code>Receipt</code> only","<code>Cart</code> only","Both"],"a":0,"why":"The wording is <code>Receipt</code>’s one job, so <code>Cart</code> stays untouched."}]},{"id":"open-closed","title":"Open/closed","tagline":"Add new behaviour without editing code that already works.","level":"intermediate","goals":["See why a growing <code>when</code> must be edited again and again","Add a new case as a new class behind an interface","Leave working code untouched when the app grows"],"remember":"Open for extension, closed for modification: add a new class that keeps the interface instead of editing code that already works.","terms":[{"term":"open/closed principle","def":"Code should be open for extension (you can add behaviour) and closed for modification (you don’t edit what already works)."},{"term":"extension","def":"In open/closed: adding new behaviour, for example a new class that keeps an existing interface. Not the same as a Kotlin extension function."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “closed for modification” mean?","options":["You don’t edit code that already works to add something new","Nobody may ever read the code","The class is <code>private</code>"],"a":0,"why":"New behaviour goes into new code. The old code stays as it is."},{"q":"The shop wants a 20% off discount. With the <code>Discount</code> interface, what do you write?","options":["A new class that keeps <code>Discount</code>","A new branch inside <code>checkout</code>","A second <code>checkout</code> function"],"a":0,"why":"One new class, whose <code>newTotal</code> returns <code>total * 8 / 10</code>. <code>checkout</code> already works with any <code>Discount</code>."}]},{"id":"liskov","title":"Liskov substitution","tagline":"A class that keeps an interface must keep its promise too.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell the difference between matching an interface and keeping its promise","Spot a class that compiles but surprises the code using it","Fix it so any class that keeps the interface can stand in for it"],"remember":"Matching the interface is not enough: every class that keeps it must do what it promises, so code can use any of them without surprises.","terms":[{"term":"Liskov substitution principle","def":"Wherever code expects a type, any subtype must work there without surprises."},{"term":"subtype","def":"A class that keeps an interface, or a child class of a parent. A <code>ListStore</code> is a subtype of <code>NoteStore</code>."},{"term":"substitution","def":"Putting one thing in the place of another, like passing a <code>DemoStore</code> where a <code>NoteStore</code> is expected."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the compiler check when a class keeps an interface?","options":["The shape: names, parameters, return types","That every function does what the comment promises","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"The meaning is up to you. That is why the promise belongs in a comment, and why this principle exists."},{"q":"<code>StuckCounter</code> keeps <code>Counter</code>, but its <code>add()</code> does nothing. What does the principle say?","options":["It breaks Liskov substitution: it can’t stand in for a <code>Counter</code>","It is fine, because it compiles","It is fine, because no exception is thrown"],"a":0,"why":"Code that trusts the promise gets <code>0</code> after three taps. Compiling is not the same as keeping a promise."}]},{"id":"isp","title":"Interface segregation","tagline":"Small interfaces, so no class promises what it can’t do.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot an interface that forces a class to fake a function","Split one big interface into small ones","Let a class keep only the interfaces it really can"],"remember":"Several small interfaces beat one big one: each class keeps only the promises it can really keep.","terms":[{"term":"interface segregation principle","def":"No class should be forced to promise functions it can’t keep. Split a big interface into small ones."},{"term":"segregation","def":"Splitting apart. Here: splitting one big interface into several small ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A class has to write <code>override fun download() = \"\"</code> because its interface forces it. What is the better fix?","options":["Split the interface so the class doesn’t have to promise <code>download</code>","Keep the empty version, it compiles","Add even more functions to the interface"],"a":0,"why":"A forced fake function is the sign of an interface that is too big."},{"q":"How does a class keep two interfaces?","options":["<code>class Song : Playable, Downloadable</code>","<code>class Song : Playable : Downloadable</code>","It can’t: only one interface per class"],"a":0,"why":"A class can keep several interfaces, separated by commas."},{"q":"Can a <code>Radio</code> (only <code>Playable</code>) go in a <code>List&lt;Downloadable&gt;</code>?","options":["No, it doesn’t keep <code>Downloadable</code>","Yes, every class fits every list","Yes, but only once"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin refuses it with a type mismatch. That is what keeps the Save button away from radios."}]},{"id":"dip","title":"Dependency inversion","tagline":"Ask for an interface and let someone pass the real thing in.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that builds the helper it needs inside itself","Ask for an interface in the constructor instead","Pass in a real or a made-up helper from outside"],"remember":"Don’t build your helpers inside. Ask for an interface in the constructor, and let whoever makes the object pass the real one in.","terms":[{"term":"dependency","def":"A helper a class needs to do its job. <code>WeatherScreen</code> depends on a weather source."},{"term":"dependency inversion principle","def":"The class that does the main work (like the screen) depends on an interface, not on one exact helper class. The helpers keep that interface."},{"term":"dependency injection","def":"Passing a dependency in from outside, usually through the constructor: <code>WeatherScreen(FakeWeather(\"Sunny\"))</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a dependency?","options":["A helper a class needs to do its job","A class that has no functions","A line that has a bug"],"a":0,"why":"The weather source is a dependency of the screen; the sender is a dependency of the reminder."},{"q":"Which version lets you pass made-up weather in?","options":["<code>class WeatherScreen(private val source: WeatherSource)</code>","<code>class WeatherScreen { private val source = OnlineWeather() }</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"Only a constructor parameter opens a way in. Built inside, the helper is fixed."},{"q":"In the after picture, which way does <code>OnlineWeather</code>’s arrow point?","options":["Up, at the <code>WeatherSource</code> interface it keeps","Down, at nothing","At <code>WeatherScreen</code>, which builds it"],"a":0,"why":"Both the screen and the helper point at the interface. That flip is what “inversion” means."}]}]},{"id":"patterns","title":"Design patterns","description":"Named answers to problems that keep coming back, written the Kotlin way, and when to leave them out.","requires":["solid"],"file":"tracks/patterns.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-patterns","title":"What a design pattern is","tagline":"Good answers to problems that keep coming back, with names.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a design pattern is, and what it is not","Spot a pattern you already used in Kotlin","Decide when plain code is the better choice"],"remember":"A design pattern is a named answer to a common problem. Use it when you have that problem, not just in case.","terms":[{"term":"design pattern","def":"A named, well-tested shape of code for a problem that keeps coming up. You adapt it to your program; it is not a library."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a design pattern?","options":["A named, well-tested shape of code for a problem that keeps coming up","A library you download and add to your program","A rule every program must follow"],"a":0,"why":"It is an idea with a name. You write the code yourself, shaped to your program."},{"q":"When is a pattern worth adding?","options":["When the problem it solves is really in your code","Always, just in case","Only in programs with more than ten classes"],"a":0,"why":"A pattern adds classes and names. That only pays off when it removes a real problem."},{"q":"Which Kotlin keyword already gives you the Singleton pattern?","options":["<code>object</code>","<code>class</code>","<code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>object</code> makes exactly one, reachable by its name from everywhere."}]},{"id":"singleton","title":"Singleton","tagline":"Exactly one, shared by everyone, and the dangers that come with it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot the bug that two copies of one thing cause","Make a singleton with <code>object</code>","Name the dangers of global state, and when to pass an object in instead"],"remember":"object makes a singleton: one object for everyone. Handy, but any code can change it, so keep it for things that truly exist once.","terms":[{"term":"singleton","def":"A design pattern: a type with exactly one object, which every part of the program reaches. In Kotlin: <code>object</code>."},{"term":"global state","def":"Values that any part of the program can read and change, like the <code>var</code> properties of a singleton."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The menu and the game each write <code>Sound()</code> for a <code>class Sound</code>. How many Sound objects are there?","options":["Two","One","None"],"a":0,"why":"Every <code>Sound()</code> makes a new object. That is how the mute bug happens."},{"q":"What is the biggest danger of a singleton with <code>var</code> properties?","options":["Any function can change it without saying so","It makes the program slower","It can’t have functions"],"a":0,"why":"It is global state. A change can come from anywhere, which makes bugs hard to find."},{"q":"A chat app shows many messages. Should <code>Message</code> be an <code>object</code>?","options":["No: there are many messages, so it is a class","Yes: every app needs singletons"],"a":0,"why":"A singleton is for something that exists once. Many messages means many objects."}]},{"id":"factory","title":"Factory","tagline":"One function decides which object to make.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Recognise creation code that is copied in many places","Write a factory function that gives back an interface type","Add a new kind of object by changing one place"],"remember":"A factory is one function that decides which class to make and gives back the interface. New kind: one class, one line.","terms":[{"term":"factory","def":"A function (often in a companion object) whose job is to create objects, so the code that uses them doesn’t need to know which class to make."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>makeEnemy</code> return <code>Enemy</code> and not <code>Bat</code>?","options":["So callers can use any enemy without knowing its class","Because Bat is private","It makes the program faster"],"a":0,"why":"Callers only rely on the interface. Which class is behind it is the factory’s business."},{"q":"You add a Ghost enemy. With a factory, what changes?","options":["One new class and one new line in the factory","Every place in the game that makes enemies","Nothing: Kotlin finds Ghost on its own"],"a":0,"why":"All the creating lives in one place, so that is the only place to update."},{"q":"<code>Player.guest()</code> in a companion object gives back a ready-made Player. What is it?","options":["A small factory","A singleton","A copy of every player"],"a":0,"why":"It is a function whose job is making an object, so it is a factory."}]},{"id":"builder","title":"Builder","tagline":"Put an object together step by step, then build it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build an object step by step with a builder class","Use named arguments and <code>apply</code>, Kotlin’s lighter builders","Choose between the three"],"remember":"A builder collects parts step by step and build() makes the object. In Kotlin, named arguments and apply often do the job with less code.","terms":[{"term":"builder","def":"A design pattern: a helper object that collects the parts step by step, then makes the finished object with <code>build()</code>."},{"term":"chaining","def":"Calling one function after another with dots, <code>a.size(25).add(\"olives\")</code>, where each call works on what the previous one gave back."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Alarm</code> has a default for every setting except <code>hour</code>. To make <code>Alarm(hour = 6, daily = true)</code>, do you need a builder class?","options":["No: named and default arguments already do the job","Yes: any class with more than two settings needs one"],"a":0,"why":"In Kotlin, named and default arguments cover most of what a builder does for a fixed set of settings."},{"q":"What does <code>Profile().apply { name = \"Lin\" }</code> give back?","options":["The Profile itself, with <code>name</code> set","The text <code>\"Lin\"</code>","<code>Unit</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>apply</code> gives back the object it was called on. That is why it works as a builder."},{"q":"Why does each builder step end with <code>return this</code>?","options":["So the next step can be chained with a dot","To make the finished object","To make the builder private"],"a":0,"why":"Giving back the builder lets the next call work on it. <code>build()</code> makes the object."}]},{"id":"observer","title":"Observer","tagline":"Subscribe once, and hear about every change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep a list of listeners and notify them all","Subscribe and unsubscribe with lambdas","Know when a direct call is simpler"],"remember":"Observer: keep a list of listener lambdas, subscribe adds one, and every change calls them all.","terms":[{"term":"observer","def":"A design pattern: one object keeps a list of listeners and calls them all when something changes."},{"term":"listener","def":"A function handed in to be called later, when something happens. Here a lambda such as <code>(Int) -&gt; Unit</code>."},{"term":"subscribe","def":"To add yourself to the list of listeners. <code>unsubscribe</code> takes you off it again."},{"term":"notify","def":"To call every listener in the list with the news."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two listeners have subscribed. <code>addItem()</code> is called once. How many listener calls happen?","options":["Two: each listener once","One: only the first listener","None until you call them by name"],"a":0,"why":"The loop calls every listener in the list."},{"q":"What does the Observer pattern buy the cart?","options":["It doesn’t need to know who is listening","It turns the cart into a singleton","It sorts the listeners by name"],"a":0,"why":"New parts subscribe themselves. The cart’s code stays the same."},{"q":"A part of the screen goes away but never unsubscribes. What happens?","options":["The cart keeps calling its listener","Its listener is removed on its own"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda stays in the list until someone removes it."}]},{"id":"strategy","title":"Strategy","tagline":"Hand in the way a job is done, and swap it when needed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pull a changing rule out into its own class or lambda","Swap a strategy while the program runs","Tell when a plain <code>if</code> is enough"],"remember":"Strategy: put each way of doing a job behind one interface or function type, hand one in, swap it when needed.","terms":[{"term":"strategy","def":"A design pattern: one job with several ways to do it. Each way is a separate object or lambda that you hand in and can swap."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A new rule arrives: shipping costs 2 on Sundays. With the Strategy pattern, what do you change?","options":["Write one new Shipping class (or lambda)","Edit the <code>pay</code> function in Checkout","Edit every existing rule"],"a":0,"why":"The checkout only knows the interface. A new rule is new code, not an edit to old code."},{"q":"Which of these is a strategy you have already used?","options":["The lambda in <code>sortedBy { it.price }</code>","<code>listOf(1, 2, 3)</code>","<code>println(\"Hi\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"You hand <code>sortedBy</code> the rule for what to sort by. Swap the lambda, get a different order."},{"q":"There are exactly two ways to charge shipping, and that will never change. What is the best choice?","options":["An <code>if</code>","An interface and two classes"],"a":0,"why":"A pattern earns its extra classes when rules keep coming. For two fixed choices, an <code>if</code> is clearer."}]},{"id":"adapter","title":"Adapter","tagline":"Make a class you can’t change fit the interface you need.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Recognise when two pieces of code don’t fit together","Write an adapter class that translates between them","Know when changing the code is better than wrapping it"],"remember":"An adapter keeps the interface your code expects on the outside and calls the class you can’t change on the inside.","terms":[{"term":"adapter","def":"A design pattern: a class that keeps the interface your code expects on the outside, and calls a different class on the inside, translating between them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You can’t edit OldSensor, and <code>report</code> needs a Thermometer. What does the adapter do?","options":["Keeps the Thermometer promise and calls OldSensor inside","Changes OldSensor’s code","Copies OldSensor’s code into report"],"a":0,"why":"It sits between the two and translates, so neither side has to change."},{"q":"Both classes are yours, and both are easy to change. Do you need an adapter?","options":["Usually not: change one to fit the other","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"An extra class in between only pays off for code you can’t or shouldn’t edit."},{"q":"With <code>km() = counter.miles * 1.6</code>, what is <code>MilesAdapter(MilesCounter(5.0)).km()</code>?","options":["<code>8.0</code>","<code>5.0</code>","<code>3.1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"5.0 × 1.6 is 8.0."}]},{"id":"decorator","title":"Decorator","tagline":"Wrap an object to add something, layer by layer.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add behaviour by wrapping instead of making subclasses","Stack wrappers in any order","Forward the rest with <code>by</code>"],"remember":"A decorator is the same interface wrapped around another object: it adds its part and passes the call on. by inner forwards the rest.","terms":[{"term":"decorator","def":"A design pattern: a class that keeps the same interface as the object it holds, adds something, and passes the call on."},{"term":"wrap","def":"To hold another object inside and stand in front of it, so calls go through you first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Coffee costs 250 and Milk adds 50. What does <code>Milk(Milk(Coffee())).price()</code> give?","options":["<code>350</code>","<code>300</code>","<code>250</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Two Milk layers, each adding 50 to what is inside: 250 + 50 + 50."},{"q":"What is the difference between a decorator and an adapter?","options":["A decorator keeps the same interface and adds behaviour; an adapter changes the interface to fit","There is no difference","A decorator needs inheritance; an adapter doesn’t"],"a":0,"why":"Both wrap an object. The adapter translates, the decorator adds."},{"q":"In <code>class HappyHour(private val inner: Drink) : Drink by inner</code>, HappyHour doesn’t write <code>name()</code>. What does <code>name()</code> do?","options":["It calls <code>inner.name()</code>","It doesn’t compile","It gives back an empty text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by inner</code> forwards every function you don’t override to <code>inner</code>."}]},{"id":"facade","title":"Facade","tagline":"One simple front door for a job that needs many parts.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot the same sequence of calls repeated in many places","Write a facade class with one simple function","Keep a facade small, and the parts still usable"],"remember":"A facade is one simple function in front of several parts, calling them in the right order. The parts stay usable on their own.","terms":[{"term":"facade","def":"A design pattern: one class with a simple function that runs a common job across several parts, in the right order."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a facade give its callers?","options":["One simple call for a job that needs several parts","A copy of every part","A list of listeners"],"a":0,"why":"The facade knows the steps and their order, so callers don’t have to."},{"q":"MovieNight is in place. Can other code still use <code>Lights</code> on its own?","options":["Yes: the parts are still there","No: the facade hides them for good"],"a":0,"why":"A facade is a shortcut for the common job, not a wall."},{"q":"A facade has grown to 40 functions covering the whole app. What is the risk?","options":["It has become one big class with many reasons to change","None: a bigger facade is a better facade"],"a":0,"why":"That breaks single responsibility. Keep a facade to one job, or split it."}]},{"id":"state-machine","title":"State machine","tagline":"Always in exactly one state, and only allowed moves between them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Replace contradicting Booleans with one state","Write the transitions in one function with <code>when</code>","Use a sealed interface when a state carries data"],"remember":"A state machine is in exactly one state at a time. Events move it only along the transitions you wrote, all in one when.","terms":[{"term":"state machine","def":"A design where something is always in exactly one of a fixed set of states, and only the listed transitions move it to another."},{"term":"event","def":"Something that happens and may change the state, such as someone pressing Lock."},{"term":"transition","def":"A rule: in state A, event E leads to state B."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A player has <code>var isPlaying</code> and <code>var isPaused</code>. How many combinations can the two make?","options":["Four, and one of them makes no sense","Three","Two"],"a":0,"why":"Two Booleans give 2 × 2 = 4 combinations. Playing and paused at once is the impossible one."},{"q":"With the door rules above, the door is LOCKED and the event OPEN arrives. What is the state after?","options":["<code>LOCKED</code>","<code>OPEN</code>","<code>CLOSED</code>"],"a":0,"why":"From LOCKED, only UNLOCK leads anywhere. Any other event leaves the state as it is."},{"q":"One state needs its own data, like a percent while loading. Which Kotlin tool fits?","options":["A sealed interface with a class for that state","An enum plus a few Booleans","A plain String"],"a":0,"why":"Each member of a sealed family can carry its own properties, and <code>when</code> still checks you handled them all."}]}]},{"id":"android","title":"Android fundamentals","description":"Your first real app: install Android Studio, run it on a virtual phone, see how a screen lives, add resources and languages, then open screens, ask for permissions and handle Back.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/android.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-android","title":"What Android is","tagline":"The program that runs the phone, and the apps that run on top of it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an operating system does for apps","Tell an app, Android and Google Play apart","Match an Android version to its API level"],"remember":"Android is the operating system that runs the phone and its apps. Each version has an API level: Android 16 is 36.","terms":[{"term":"operating system","def":"The main program a device runs. It starts apps, shares the screen and the hardware between them, and keeps them apart."},{"term":"app","def":"A program that runs on top of Android, like a weather app or your own."},{"term":"Google Play","def":"The app store on most Android phones. People download apps from it, and Android installs them."},{"term":"API level","def":"The number of an Android version: Android 15 is 35, Android 16 is 36, Android 17 is 37."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Android?","options":["The operating system that runs the phone and its apps","An app you download from Google Play","A programming language"],"a":0,"why":"Android is the main program on the phone. Apps, including yours, run on top of it."},{"q":"An app supports API level 30 and newer. Which phone can install it?","options":["A phone on API level 33","A phone on API level 29","Both"],"a":0,"why":"33 is newer than 30. A phone on 29 is older than the oldest version the app supports."},{"q":"An app wants to show text on the screen. How does it happen?","options":["The app tells Android what to show, and Android draws it","The app switches the screen’s pixels on by itself","Google Play draws it"],"a":0,"why":"Apps never touch the hardware directly. They ask Android, which shares the screen between apps."}]},{"id":"studio","title":"Installing Android Studio","tagline":"One program to write, build and try your apps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Install Android Studio with the Android SDK","Create a virtual phone to test your apps on","Say what the IDE, the SDK and the emulator each do"],"remember":"Android Studio is where you write and run apps; the SDK builds them; the emulator is a phone in a window.","terms":[{"term":"IDE","def":"Integrated development environment: a code editor that can also build and run your code. Android Studio is one."},{"term":"Android SDK","def":"Software development kit: the tools and Android libraries that turn your code into an app."},{"term":"emulator","def":"A program that pretends to be an Android phone, in a window on your computer. Each virtual phone is also called an AVD (Android Virtual Device)."},{"term":"system image","def":"A copy of one Android version for the emulator to run."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Android SDK give you?","options":["The tools and libraries that build Android apps","An app store","A keyboard for typing code"],"a":0,"why":"SDK means software development kit: what you need to turn code into an Android app."},{"q":"What is a system image?","options":["A copy of one Android version for the emulator to run","A screenshot of your app","The app’s icon"],"a":0,"why":"The emulator needs an Android to run. The system image you pick decides which version."},{"q":"Where do you create a virtual phone?","options":["In Device Manager","In Google Play","In the Android SDK"],"a":0,"why":"Device Manager, in Android Studio, lists your virtual phones. <b>+</b> and <b>Create Virtual Device</b> make a new one."}]},{"id":"first-app","title":"Your first app","tagline":"Create a project, run it on the emulator, change one word.","level":"beginner","goals":["Create a project from the Empty Activity template","Run the app on the emulator","Change the code and see the change after Run"],"remember":"Code changes reach the phone only when you click Run.","terms":[{"term":"template","def":"A ready-made project Android Studio writes for you, like Empty Activity."},{"term":"package name","def":"A unique name for the app, written like a web address backwards: <code>com.example.hello</code>."},{"term":"Gradle","def":"The build tool Android Studio uses to turn the project into an app."},{"term":"Run","def":"The ▶ button: builds the app, installs it on the chosen phone and opens it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the package name <code>com.example.hello</code> for?","options":["It is a unique name for the app","It is the text on the screen","It is the Android version"],"a":0,"why":"Android tells apps apart by this name, so no two apps on a phone share one."},{"q":"What does Minimum SDK set?","options":["The oldest Android version the app runs on","The newest Android version","How big the app can be"],"a":0,"why":"It is an API level. Phones older than it can’t install the app."},{"q":"You click Run. What happens?","options":["The app is built, installed on the phone and opened","The code is sent to Google Play","Only the spelling is checked"],"a":0,"why":"Run does the whole trip: build, install, open."}]},{"id":"project-files","title":"What’s in a project","tagline":"Four groups of files, and what each one is for.","level":"beginner","goals":["Find the manifest, the Kotlin code, the resources and the Gradle files","Say what each one is for","Read the app’s Android versions in build.gradle.kts"],"remember":"manifests: what the app is. java: the code. res: everything else. Gradle Scripts: how it is built.","terms":[{"term":"AndroidManifest.xml","def":"The app’s ID card for Android: its name, icon and screens."},{"term":"resources","def":"Everything in an app that isn’t code, in the <b>res</b> folder: pictures, the icon, text, colours."},{"term":"build.gradle.kts","def":"Build settings written in Kotlin. The app’s one holds <code>applicationId</code>, <code>minSdk</code> and <code>targetSdk</code>."},{"term":"minSdk / targetSdk","def":"<code>minSdk</code>: the oldest API level the app runs on. <code>targetSdk</code>: the API level it was tested on."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do pictures and the app icon go?","options":["In res","In java","In Gradle Scripts"],"a":0,"why":"res holds everything that isn’t code. Pictures go in drawable, the icon in mipmap."},{"q":"What does the manifest tell Android?","options":["What the app is: its name, icon and screens","How to turn Kotlin into an app","Which apps the user likes"],"a":0,"why":"Android reads the manifest to know which screen to open when the icon is tapped."},{"q":"Hello has <code>minSdk = 24</code>. A phone is on API level 23. What happens?","options":["It can’t install Hello","It installs Hello, which then crashes","It installs Hello and runs it"],"a":0,"why":"API level 23 is older than the oldest version Hello supports."}]},{"id":"activity","title":"Activities","tagline":"One screen, one class, and the function Android calls first.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an Activity is","Read MainActivity line by line","Put text on the screen with <code>setContent</code> and <code>Text</code>"],"remember":"An Activity is one screen. Android creates it and calls onCreate, where setContent puts your UI on the screen.","terms":[{"term":"Activity","def":"One screen of an app: an object Android creates and calls. Yours builds on <code>ComponentActivity</code>."},{"term":"onCreate","def":"The function Android calls once, right after it creates the Activity. You set up the screen here."},{"term":"Bundle","def":"A small bag of values Android can hand back to a screen. <code>savedInstanceState</code> is <code>null</code> when the screen opens fresh."},{"term":"setContent","def":"Puts a UI on the screen: whatever its block describes, such as <code>Text(\"Hello\")</code>, which shows one line of text."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is an Activity?","options":["One screen of an app, which Android creates and calls","A function you call from <code>main</code>","A file in res"],"a":0,"why":"Android makes the Activity object and calls its functions, such as <code>onCreate</code>."},{"q":"Who creates the <code>MainActivity</code> object?","options":["Android","Your code, with <code>MainActivity()</code>","Gradle"],"a":0,"why":"Android reads the manifest and creates the Activity itself when the screen opens."},{"q":"What does <code>setContent { Text(\"Hi\") }</code> do?","options":["Shows a screen with the text Hi","Prints Hi below the code","Saves Hi in a Bundle"],"a":0,"why":"<code>setContent</code> puts its block on the screen, and <code>Text</code> shows a line of text."}]},{"id":"lifecycle","title":"The activity lifecycle","tagline":"Android tells your screen when it appears, goes away and comes back.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the three states an open screen moves through","Know which callback Android calls at each step","Watch the callbacks in Logcat with <code>Log.d</code>"],"remember":"Up: onCreate, onStart, onResume. Down: onPause, onStop, onDestroy. Home stops the screen; it doesn’t destroy it.","terms":[{"term":"lifecycle","def":"The states a screen moves through, from created to destroyed, and the order Android moves it in."},{"term":"callback","def":"A function you write and Android calls when something happens, like <code>onStart</code> when the screen becomes visible."},{"term":"Created, Started, Resumed","def":"The object exists; the screen is visible; the screen is in front and gets taps."},{"term":"Logcat","def":"Android Studio’s log window. <code>Log.d(\"Hello\", \"onStart\")</code> writes the message <code>onStart</code> with the tag <code>Hello</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Hello is in front. Ada presses Home. Which callbacks run?","options":["<code>onPause</code>, then <code>onStop</code>","<code>onPause</code> only","<code>onPause</code>, <code>onStop</code>, <code>onDestroy</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The screen leaves the front, then leaves view. It isn’t destroyed, because Ada can come back to it."},{"q":"In which state can the user tap things on your screen?","options":["Resumed","Started","Created"],"a":0,"why":"Started means visible. Resumed means in front, receiving taps."},{"q":"Why does your <code>onStart</code> call <code>super.onStart()</code>?","options":["<code>ComponentActivity</code> does its own work there, and the app crashes if it is skipped","It makes the log line appear","It runs <code>onStart</code> a second time"],"a":0,"why":"Like <code>super.onCreate</code>, each <code>super</code> call lets <code>ComponentActivity</code> do its part. Android checks that it ran."}]},{"id":"config-changes","title":"Rotation and configuration changes","tagline":"Turn the phone, and you get a brand-new Activity.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name changes that make Android recreate an Activity","Predict the callbacks when the phone rotates","Explain why values kept in the Activity are lost"],"remember":"A configuration change, like rotation or dark theme, destroys the Activity and creates a new one. Its properties start over.","terms":[{"term":"configuration","def":"The settings a screen depends on: screen shape and size, light or dark theme, language, font size."},{"term":"configuration change","def":"One of those settings changing while the app is open, such as rotating the phone."},{"term":"recreation","def":"What Android does on a configuration change: destroy the Activity, then create a new one for the new configuration."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a configuration change?","options":["Switching the phone to dark theme","Getting a new message","Tapping text in the app"],"a":0,"why":"Dark theme changes how every screen should look, so Android recreates the Activity."},{"q":"Why does Android create a new Activity on a configuration change?","options":["So the new screen starts with the right layout, text and colours","To free memory","Because the app crashed"],"a":0,"why":"A fresh object is built for the new settings from the start."},{"q":"<code>MainActivity</code> has <code>private var name = \"Ada\"</code>, and the app changed it to <code>\"Lin\"</code>. The phone rotates. What is <code>name</code> in the new <code>MainActivity</code>?","options":["<code>\"Ada\"</code>","<code>\"Lin\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The new object starts with the starting value. The <code>\"Lin\"</code> was in the old object, which is gone."}]},{"id":"resources","title":"Resources","tagline":"Keep words, colours and pictures in res/, and reach them through R.","level":"beginner","goals":["Move the text your app shows into <code>strings.xml</code>","Name a colour and add a picture to <code>res/</code>","Read a resource in code through its id in <code>R</code>"],"remember":"Text, colours and pictures live in res/. R gives each one an id, and getString(R.string.name) reads a text.","terms":[{"term":"resource","def":"Something your app uses that is not code: a text, a colour, a picture. Resources live in <code>res/</code>."},{"term":"strings.xml","def":"The file in <code>res/values/</code> that holds the app’s texts, each with a name."},{"term":"R","def":"A class Android generates when the app builds, with an <code>Int</code> id for every resource, like <code>R.string.greeting</code>."},{"term":"drawable","def":"A picture resource in <code>res/drawable/</code>. Its id is <code>R.drawable.</code> plus the file name without the ending."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>R.string.greeting</code>?","options":["An <code>Int</code> id that points at the text","The text “Hello, Ada!” itself","The file <code>strings.xml</code>"],"a":0,"why":"R holds ids. <code>getString(R.string.greeting)</code> turns the id into the text."},{"q":"Where does a picture called <code>logo.png</code> go, and what is its id?","options":["<code>res/drawable/</code>, <code>R.drawable.logo</code>","<code>res/values/</code>, <code>R.string.logo</code>","Next to <code>MainActivity.kt</code>, <code>R.logo</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Pictures are drawables. The id is the file name without <code>.png</code>."},{"q":"How does an XML file, like the manifest, point at the text <code>app_name</code>?","options":["<code>@string/app_name</code>","<code>R.string.app_name</code>","<code>getString(app_name)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"XML uses <code>@</code> plus the type and name. <code>R.string.app_name</code> is the Kotlin way."}]},{"id":"localization","title":"Other languages","tagline":"Add a folder per language, and Android picks the right one for each phone.","level":"beginner","goals":["Translate your app with a <code>values-es</code> style folder","Write counts like “1 visit” and “3 visits” with <code>plurals</code>","Avoid the dotted and dotless i bug when changing case"],"remember":"values/ has every string; values-xx overrides some. Counts use plurals with the count twice. Compare words with uppercase(), no locale.","terms":[{"term":"language folder","def":"A folder like <code>res/values-es/</code>. The code after the dash names the language its strings are for."},{"term":"default resources","def":"The folders without a dash, like <code>values/</code>. Android falls back to them, so they must have every string."},{"term":"plurals","def":"A string with one <code>&lt;item&gt;</code> per form, such as <code>one</code> and <code>other</code>. Android picks the form from the count and the language."},{"term":"Locale","def":"A description of a language (and maybe a region) for code, like <code>Locale.forLanguageTag(\"tr\")</code>. Case changes and number formats follow it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Hello has <code>values/</code> and <code>values-es/</code>. Where does a Spanish phone get a string that is only in <code>values/</code>?","options":["From <code>values/</code>","Nowhere: the text is empty","From Google Translate"],"a":0,"why":"A string missing from the language folder falls back to the default folder."},{"q":"On an English phone, what does <code>resources.getQuantityString(R.plurals.visit_count, 1, 1)</code> return, with the plurals from this lesson?","options":["<code>\"1 visit\"</code>","<code>\"1 visits\"</code>","<code>\"%d visit\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The first <code>1</code> picks <code>quantity=\"one\"</code>, the second replaces <code>%d</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>\"title\".uppercase()</code> return on a phone set to Turkish?","options":["<code>\"TITLE\"</code>","<code>\"TİTLE\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With no locale, <code>uppercase()</code> uses the same rules on every phone. Only <code>uppercase(Locale.forLanguageTag(\"tr\"))</code> gives the dotted <code>İ</code>."}]},{"id":"context","title":"Context","tagline":"The object your code uses to reach Android, and which one to keep.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a <code>Context</code> is and where you already have one","Choose between the Activity and <code>applicationContext</code>","Avoid keeping a closed screen in memory by mistake"],"remember":"An Activity is a Context for its own screen. Anything that lives longer keeps applicationContext.","terms":[{"term":"Context","def":"The object your code uses to reach Android: resources, files, messages, other screens. An Activity is one."},{"term":"applicationContext","def":"One <code>Context</code> for the whole app. It lives as long as the app runs, so long-lived objects may keep it."},{"term":"toast","def":"A short message at the bottom of the screen that goes away by itself: <code>Toast.makeText(this, \"Saved\", Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()</code>."},{"term":"memory leak","def":"Memory that can’t be freed because something still points to an object nobody needs, like a closed screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can <code>MainActivity</code> call <code>getString(R.string.greeting)</code> with nothing in front?","options":["An Activity is a <code>Context</code>, and <code>getString</code> belongs to <code>Context</code>","<code>getString</code> works anywhere in Kotlin","Android Studio adds it while the app runs"],"a":0,"why":"The Activity inherits <code>getString</code> from <code>Context</code>."},{"q":"An <code>object</code> keeps the first Activity it was given. The phone rotates. What happens to that old Activity?","options":["It stays in memory, because the object still points to it","The object switches to the new Activity by itself","The app stops at once"],"a":0,"why":"Android frees only what nothing points to. The object still points to the old screen."},{"q":"A cache lives as long as the app and needs a <code>Context</code>. What should it keep?","options":["<code>applicationContext</code>","The Activity that created it","A new Activity for each call"],"a":0,"why":"Keep the context that lives as long as the cache: the app’s."}]},{"id":"intents","title":"Intents","tagline":"Name the screen you want, or describe the job and let Android find an app.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open your own second screen with an <code>Intent</code>","Send a value to that screen with <code>putExtra</code>","Open a web link in another app"],"remember":"An explicit Intent names your screen; an implicit Intent names a job. startActivity sends it; catch ActivityNotFoundException.","terms":[{"term":"Intent","def":"A message that asks Android to start something, like another screen."},{"term":"explicit intent","def":"An Intent that names the exact class to start, for screens in your own app: <code>Intent(this, DetailActivity::class.java)</code>."},{"term":"implicit intent","def":"An Intent that describes a job, like <code>ACTION_VIEW</code> with a web address, so Android finds an app that can do it."},{"term":"extra","def":"A value carried by an Intent under a key: <code>putExtra(\"title\", \"Shopping\")</code>, read with <code>getStringExtra(\"title\")</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse(\"https://example.com\"))</code> is","options":["implicit: it describes a job","explicit: it names a class"],"a":0,"why":"It names an action and an address, not a class. Android picks the app."},{"q":"In <code>DetailActivity</code>, what type does <code>intent.getStringExtra(\"title\")</code> have?","options":["<code>String?</code>","<code>String</code>","<code>Int</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The key might be missing, so the result can be <code>null</code>. Use <code>?:</code> to give a default."},{"q":"To open your own <code>SettingsActivity</code>, which intent fits?","options":["An explicit one: <code>Intent(this, SettingsActivity::class.java)</code>","An implicit one with <code>ACTION_VIEW</code>"],"a":0,"why":"For your own screens, name the class. Then no other app can receive it."}]},{"id":"permissions","title":"Permissions","tagline":"The camera, location and microphone need the user’s yes, asked at the right moment.","level":"beginner","goals":["Declare a permission in the manifest","Check a permission and ask for it while the app runs","Keep the app working when the answer is no"],"remember":"List it in the manifest, check it, ask when the user wants the feature, and keep working if the answer is no.","terms":[{"term":"permission","def":"Android’s permission for an app to use something protected, listed in the manifest with <code>&lt;uses-permission&gt;</code>."},{"term":"runtime permission","def":"A permission the user must also grant while the app runs, through a system dialog. Camera, location and microphone are examples."},{"term":"launcher","def":"The object <code>registerForActivityResult</code> returns. Its <code>launch(...)</code> shows the request, and the answer arrives in your lambda."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Is the manifest line enough to use the camera?","options":["No: the user must also say yes while the app runs","Yes: the manifest line grants it","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"The camera is a runtime permission. The manifest line makes it possible to ask; the user decides."},{"q":"When is the best moment to ask for the camera?","options":["When the user taps “Add photo”","In <code>onCreate</code>, as the app opens","After the app has been open for a week"],"a":0,"why":"Asked in context, the reason is clear to the user."},{"q":"The user tapped “Don’t allow” twice. What can still turn the camera on?","options":["Only the Settings app","Calling <code>launch</code> again","Restarting the phone"],"a":0,"why":"After two denials Android doesn’t show the dialog any more. The user can change it in Settings."}]},{"id":"manifest","title":"The manifest in depth","tagline":"One file tells Android what your app contains, what it needs, and what it can do for other apps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read the main parts of <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>","Let other apps share text into a screen with an <code>intent-filter</code>","Use <code>exported</code> and <code>queries</code> correctly"],"remember":"Every component is listed in the manifest. An intent-filter says which jobs a screen accepts, and a screen with a filter must set exported.","terms":[{"term":"component","def":"A part of an app Android can start: an activity, service, receiver or provider. Each one is listed in the manifest."},{"term":"intent filter","def":"A list in the manifest of the implicit intents a component accepts, such as <code>SEND</code> with <code>text/plain</code>."},{"term":"exported","def":"<code>android:exported=\"true\"</code> lets other apps start the component; <code>\"false\"</code> keeps it for your app only."},{"term":"share sheet","def":"The list of apps Android shows when you tap Share. A screen appears in it when its intent filter accepts <code>SEND</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which component does work without a screen, like playing music?","options":["<code>&lt;service&gt;</code>","<code>&lt;activity&gt;</code>","<code>&lt;provider&gt;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An activity is a screen, and a provider shares data with other apps. A service works without a screen."},{"q":"What does <code>android:exported=\"false\"</code> mean on an Activity?","options":["Only your own app can start it","It can’t be started at all","It is hidden from the home screen only"],"a":0,"why":"Other apps can’t start it; your app still can."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 14 and opens a web link with <code>startActivity</code>. Does it need <code>&lt;queries&gt;</code>?","options":["No: starting another app works without it","Yes: without it the link never opens"],"a":0,"why":"<code>&lt;queries&gt;</code> is only for checking in advance. Starting an app is not filtered; catch <code>ActivityNotFoundException</code>."}]},{"id":"backstack","title":"The back stack and tasks","tagline":"Each new screen goes on top, and Back takes the top one off.","level":"beginner","goals":["Predict where Back goes after several screens","Close a screen from code with <code>finish()</code>","Use a launch mode to avoid two copies of one screen"],"remember":"startActivity puts a screen on top; Back and finish() take it off. singleTop reuses the top screen through onNewIntent.","terms":[{"term":"task","def":"The screens the user opened for one job, kept in order. The Recents screen shows tasks."},{"term":"back stack","def":"The order of the screens in a task, newest on top. Back takes the top one off."},{"term":"finish()","def":"Closes the Activity it is called on, like Back does. The screen below comes back."},{"term":"launch mode","def":"A manifest setting that says what <code>startActivity</code> does when the screen may already exist: <code>standard</code>, <code>singleTop</code>, <code>singleTask</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The stack is List › Detail. The user does Back. What happens to Detail?","options":["It is taken off and destroyed; List comes back","It moves under List","It stays on top, paused"],"a":0,"why":"Back takes the top screen off and destroys it."},{"q":"DetailActivity is <code>standard</code> and on top. The app starts DetailActivity again. What is the stack?","options":["List › Detail › Detail","List › Detail","Detail"],"a":0,"why":"<code>standard</code> always makes a new copy, even of the screen on top."},{"q":"With <code>launchMode=\"singleTop\"</code>, the same case: where does the new Intent arrive?","options":["In <code>onNewIntent</code> of the Detail on top","In <code>onCreate</code> of a new Detail","Nowhere: it is dropped"],"a":0,"why":"<code>singleTop</code> reuses the copy on top and hands it the new Intent."}]},{"id":"predictive-back","title":"Predictive back","tagline":"Show where Back leads before the user lets go, and step in only when you must.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe what the predictive back gesture shows","Run your own code on Back with <code>addCallback</code>","Turn that code on only while it is needed"],"remember":"Register a back callback in advance and keep it enabled only while Back must do something else; otherwise Android shows the preview.","terms":[{"term":"predictive back","def":"The back gesture that shows, while the finger is still down, the screen Back will lead to."},{"term":"OnBackPressedCallback","def":"Code you register to run on Back. It runs only while its <code>isEnabled</code> is <code>true</code>."},{"term":"onBackPressedDispatcher","def":"The Activity’s list of back callbacks. <code>addCallback</code> adds one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can’t predictive back work with code that decides after Back happens?","options":["Android must know when the swipe starts whether your app handles Back","The animation is too fast for Kotlin","Back is only for the home screen"],"a":0,"why":"The preview is drawn during the swipe, so the answer is needed before the user lets go."},{"q":"No back callback is enabled. The user swipes back on the second screen. What happens?","options":["The previous screen peeks out, and letting go closes this one","Nothing: Back needs a callback","The app closes"],"a":0,"why":"With no enabled callback, Android handles Back and shows the preview."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 16 and overrides <code>onBackPressed()</code>. On an Android 16 phone, is it called?","options":["No","Yes, as before","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"For apps that target Android 16 and run on it, <code>onBackPressed()</code> is not called. Use a callback."}]}]},{"id":"compose","title":"Jetpack Compose","description":"Build Android UI with Kotlin functions that redraw themselves when state changes.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/compose.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-compose","title":"What Compose is","tagline":"Describe the screen in Kotlin functions, and Compose draws it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what “declarative” means: describe the result, not the steps","Write a function marked <code>@Composable</code> that shows text","See a composable in the Preview without starting the emulator"],"remember":"A composable is a @Composable function that describes what to show for its inputs. @Preview draws one in Android Studio.","terms":[{"term":"annotation","def":"A word starting with <code>@</code> above a function, like <code>@Composable</code>, that gives it extra meaning."},{"term":"declarative","def":"Describing what the screen should show, instead of giving step-by-step changes to make."},{"term":"@Composable","def":"An annotation that marks a function as a composable: a function that describes part of the UI."},{"term":"@Preview","def":"An annotation on a composable with no parameters. Android Studio draws it in the editor."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “declarative” mean in Compose?","options":["You describe what the screen should show, and Compose draws it","You give step-by-step orders to change each part of the screen","The screen is drawn once and never changes"],"a":0,"why":"You write what the result looks like for the current data. Compose does the drawing."},{"q":"Which function can call <code>Text(\"Hi\")</code>?","options":["A function marked <code>@Composable</code>","Any Kotlin function","Only <code>fun main()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Composables can only be called from other composables, or inside <code>setContent { }</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>@Preview</code> do?","options":["Android Studio draws that composable in the editor","It runs the app on the emulator","It hides the composable from the real app"],"a":0,"why":"It lets you see a composable while you write it. The app itself doesn’t change."}]},{"id":"text-and-modifiers","title":"Text and modifiers","tagline":"Make text bigger and bolder, and give it room.","level":"beginner","goals":["Change how text looks with <code>fontSize</code> and <code>fontWeight</code>","Add space and set a size with a <code>Modifier</code>","Tell <code>dp</code> and <code>sp</code> apart"],"remember":"Text styles with fontSize (in sp) and fontWeight. A Modifier sets space and size (in dp), and more.","terms":[{"term":"Modifier","def":"A chain of instructions about size, space and more, passed to a composable as <code>modifier = Modifier...</code>."},{"term":"padding","def":"Empty space inside a composable’s edges: <code>Modifier.padding(16.dp)</code>."},{"term":"dp","def":"The unit for sizes and spaces. It looks about the same size on every screen."},{"term":"sp","def":"The unit for text size. It also grows with the text size someone picks in the phone’s settings."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which unit do you use for <code>fontSize</code>?","options":["<code>sp</code>","<code>dp</code>","Pixels"],"a":0,"why":"<code>sp</code> is for text, and it grows with the phone’s text size setting."},{"q":"What does <code>Modifier.fillMaxWidth()</code> do?","options":["Makes the composable as wide as the space it is in","Makes the letters wider","Adds space on the left and right"],"a":0,"why":"The area of the composable grows. The letters keep their size."},{"q":"Where does <code>Modifier.padding(16.dp)</code> add space?","options":["On all four sides","Only on the left","Only above"],"a":0,"why":"With one value, padding adds the same space on every side."}]},{"id":"layouts","title":"Arranging things","tagline":"Put composables in a column, in a row, or on top of each other.","level":"beginner","goals":["Stack composables top to bottom with <code>Column</code> and side by side with <code>Row</code>","Center things and put even space between them","Layer composables on top of each other with <code>Box</code>"],"remember":"Column stacks down, Row goes across, Box layers on top. Alignment centers, Arrangement.spacedBy adds space between.","terms":[{"term":"Column","def":"Puts its children from top to bottom."},{"term":"Row","def":"Puts its children side by side."},{"term":"Box","def":"Puts its children on top of each other."},{"term":"children","def":"The composables called inside a layout’s braces."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which layout puts its children on top of each other?","options":["<code>Box</code>","<code>Column</code>","<code>Row</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A Box layers its children. Column and Row put them next to each other."},{"q":"In a <code>Column</code>, which argument centers the children across the screen?","options":["<code>horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally</code>","<code>verticalArrangement = Arrangement.spacedBy(8.dp)</code>","<code>Modifier.fillMaxSize()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A Column stacks children downward; alignment decides where each one sits across."},{"q":"A Column of three Texts has <code>Arrangement.spacedBy(8.dp)</code>. Where is the space?","options":["8 dp between each pair: two gaps","8 dp above, between and below every Text","8 dp only below the last Text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>spacedBy</code> only adds space between children, not at the edges."}]},{"id":"images","title":"Images and icons","tagline":"Show pictures and small symbols from your drawables.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show a picture from <code>res/drawable</code> with <code>Image</code>","Describe a picture for people who can’t see it with <code>contentDescription</code>","Add a small symbol with <code>Icon</code>"],"remember":"Image(painter = painterResource(R.drawable.x), contentDescription = ...) shows a picture. Use null only for decoration.","terms":[{"term":"painterResource","def":"Loads a drawable from <code>res/drawable</code> so <code>Image</code> or <code>Icon</code> can draw it."},{"term":"contentDescription","def":"The words a screen reader says for an image. <code>null</code> means “skip it, it’s decoration”."},{"term":"screen reader","def":"A tool that reads the screen aloud for people who can’t see it well. On Android it is TalkBack."},{"term":"Icon","def":"A small symbol in the color of the content around it. It takes the drawable’s own size; icons from Vector Asset are 24 dp."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>painterResource(R.drawable.glass)</code> do?","options":["Loads the glass drawable so it can be drawn","Draws a new picture of a glass","Saves a picture into <code>res/drawable</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It reads the drawable you added to the project and gives Compose a painter for it."},{"q":"A picture is pure decoration. Which <code>contentDescription</code> fits?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>\"image\"</code>","<code>\"\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>null</code> tells the screen reader to skip it. Any text would be read aloud."},{"q":"You add a star with <b>New &gt; Vector Asset</b> and show it with <code>Icon</code>, with no size. How big is it?","options":["24 dp","The whole screen width","1 dp"],"a":0,"why":"Vector Asset saves icons at 24 dp, and an <code>Icon</code> takes its drawable’s own size. <code>Modifier.size(...)</code> changes it."}]},{"id":"buttons","title":"Buttons and clicks","tagline":"Run your own code when someone taps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a tappable button with <code>Button</code> and <code>onClick</code>","Put text and an icon inside a button","Pick a button style, and switch a button off with <code>enabled = false</code>"],"remember":"Button(onClick = { ... }) { Text(\"...\") }: the first lambda runs on each tap, the second is what the button shows.","terms":[{"term":"Button","def":"A tappable composable. <code>onClick</code> says what happens, the trailing lambda what it shows."},{"term":"onClick","def":"The lambda a button runs each time it is tapped."},{"term":"enabled","def":"When <code>false</code>, the button turns grey and ignores taps."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does the code inside <code>onClick = { ... }</code> run?","options":["Each time the button is tapped","Once, when the screen appears","Never, unless you call it yourself"],"a":0,"why":"Compose keeps the lambda and runs it for every tap."},{"q":"Which style fits a less important action?","options":["<code>TextButton</code>","<code>Button</code>","Both are the same"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Button</code> is filled and stands out, for the main action. <code>TextButton</code> is plain text."},{"q":"In <code>Button(onClick = { ... }) { ... }</code>, what goes in the second pair of braces?","options":["What the button shows","The code to run on a tap","The button’s color"],"a":0,"why":"The trailing lambda holds the content, like a <code>Text</code>. The code to run on a tap goes in <code>onClick</code>."}]},{"id":"state","title":"State","tagline":"Values that update the screen when they change.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a plain <code>var</code> doesn’t update the screen","Hold a changing value with <code>remember { mutableStateOf(...) }</code>","Change it in <code>onClick</code> and watch the screen follow"],"remember":"var x by remember { mutableStateOf(start) }: change x, and every composable that reads it runs again.","terms":[{"term":"state","def":"A value Compose watches, made with <code>mutableStateOf</code>. Changing it updates the screen."},{"term":"recomposition","def":"Compose running a composable again because a state it read has changed."},{"term":"remember","def":"Keeps a value between runs of a composable, instead of making it again each time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>mutableStateOf(0)</code> give you?","options":["A box holding 0 that Compose watches","A plain number 0","A list of numbers"],"a":0,"why":"When its value changes, Compose runs the composables that read it again."},{"q":"Why wrap it in <code>remember { }</code>?","options":["So the same box is kept each time the composable runs again","So the value is saved to a file","So the button can be tapped"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, each run would make a fresh box with the starting value."},{"q":"What is recomposition?","options":["Compose running a composable again after a state it read changed","Restarting the whole app","Building the project in Android Studio"],"a":0,"why":"It is how the screen catches up with new state."}]},{"id":"recomposition","title":"How Compose redraws","tagline":"Only the parts that read a changed state run again.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell which composables run again after a state change","Explain why writing the same value changes nothing","Keep composables quick and free of side effects, because they can run often"],"remember":"When a state changes, only the composables that read it, or get a changed input, run again. The rest are skipped.","terms":[{"term":"skipping","def":"Compose leaving a composable out of a recomposition because none of its inputs changed."},{"term":"side effect","def":"Work beyond describing the screen, like writing to Logcat or saving a file. It belongs in <code>onClick</code>, not in a composable’s body."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>glasses</code> is 0. You run <code>glasses = 0</code>. What runs again?","options":["Nothing","Every composable","Only <code>WaterScreen</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The value didn’t change, so there is nothing to redraw."},{"q":"Where should code that saves the count to a file go?","options":["In <code>onClick</code>","In the body of <code>GlassCount</code>","In <code>Title</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A composable body can run many times or be skipped. <code>onClick</code> runs once per tap."},{"q":"<code>Title()</code> takes no parameters and reads no state. What happens to it when <code>glasses</code> changes?","options":["It is skipped","It runs again","It disappears from the screen"],"a":0,"why":"Skipped composables stay on the screen as they were. Their code just doesn’t run."}]},{"id":"hoisting","title":"State hoisting","tagline":"Move state up, so several composables can share it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a composable with its own state into one that takes a value and an event","Pass state down and events up between composables","Preview a stateless composable with any value"],"remember":"Hoist state to the composable that calls everyone who needs it. Values go down as parameters, events go up as lambdas.","terms":[{"term":"state hoisting","def":"Moving state up into a calling composable, and passing the value and an event function down instead."},{"term":"stateless","def":"A composable that holds no state of its own: it shows what it is given."},{"term":"stateful","def":"A composable that holds state with <code>remember</code>."},{"term":"event","def":"Something that happened, like a tap, reported upward by calling a lambda such as <code>onAdd()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two composables need the same <code>glasses</code>. Where should the state live?","options":["In the composable that calls both of them","Inside one of them","In both, one copy each"],"a":0,"why":"Hoisting it to the common caller gives one place for the value, shared by both."},{"q":"In <code>GlassCounter(glasses: Int, onAdd: () -&gt; Unit)</code>, what is <code>onAdd</code> for?","options":["Reporting a tap upward","Holding the number","Drawing the button"],"a":0,"why":"Events go up as lambdas. The owner of the state decides what to do."},{"q":"Why are stateless composables easy to preview?","options":["You can pass in any value, like 8 glasses","They don’t need <code>@Composable</code>","They have no parameters"],"a":0,"why":"The preview calls them with whatever value you want to see."}]},{"id":"saveable","title":"Surviving rotation","tagline":"Keep state when the Activity is created again.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>remember</code> loses its value when the phone rotates","Keep a value through rotation with <code>rememberSaveable</code>","Know which values it keeps, and when the app starts fresh"],"remember":"remember forgets when the Activity is recreated; rememberSaveable puts simple values in the saved state and gets them back.","terms":[{"term":"rememberSaveable","def":"Like <code>remember</code>, but the value also survives rotation and Android closing the app in the background."},{"term":"saved state","def":"A small bag of values Android keeps for an Activity while it is recreated: the <code>Bundle</code> that <code>onCreate</code> receives as <code>savedInstanceState</code>."},{"term":"Recents screen","def":"The phone’s list of recently used apps. Swiping an app away there closes it for good."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>remember</code> lose its value when the phone rotates?","options":["The Activity is destroyed and created again","Rotation clears the phone’s memory","Compose resets every value once a minute"],"a":0,"why":"What <code>remember</code> kept belonged to the old Activity."},{"q":"Android closes your app in the background, and the person comes back. Which value is still there?","options":["One kept with <code>rememberSaveable</code>","One kept with <code>remember</code>","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"The saved state is restored in this case too."},{"q":"The person swipes the app away from Recents and opens it again. What does <code>rememberSaveable</code> give back?","options":["The starting value: a fresh start","The last saved value"],"a":0,"why":"Closing an app on purpose throws its saved state away."}]},{"id":"text-input","title":"Text fields","tagline":"Let people type, and use what they typed.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add a box people can type in with <code>TextField</code>","Keep what they type with <code>rememberTextFieldState</code>","Read the typed text and turn it into a number"],"remember":"val s = rememberTextFieldState() and TextField(state = s): read the typed text with s.text.toString().","terms":[{"term":"TextField","def":"A box people can type in. It brings up the keyboard when tapped."},{"term":"TextFieldState","def":"Holds a text field’s text. Read it with <code>.text.toString()</code>."},{"term":"rememberTextFieldState","def":"Makes a <code>TextFieldState</code> and keeps it, through rotation too."},{"term":"label","def":"A small composable inside a text field that says what to type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why call <code>.toString()</code> on <code>goalState.text</code> before <code>toIntOrNull()</code>?","options":["It holds typed characters, but isn’t a String yet","To delete the text","To show the keyboard"],"a":0,"why":"<code>toIntOrNull()</code> works on a String, so turn the characters into one first."},{"q":"The person types 12 and rotates the phone. What does the field show?","options":["12","Nothing","The starting text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>rememberTextFieldState</code> uses <code>rememberSaveable</code> inside."},{"q":"What is <code>label = { Text(\"Daily goal\") }</code> for?","options":["Telling people what to type in the field","Setting the starting text","Choosing the keyboard"],"a":0,"why":"The label is shown inside the field as a hint about its purpose."}]},{"id":"lists","title":"Long lists","tagline":"Show hundreds of rows, but build only the ones on screen.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show a scrolling list with <code>LazyColumn</code>","Add rows with <code>items</code> and a single row with <code>item</code>","Explain why a long list should not be a <code>Column</code>"],"remember":"For a long list use LazyColumn { items(list) { value -> ... } }. It builds only the rows on screen.","terms":[{"term":"LazyColumn","def":"A scrolling column that builds only the rows on screen. <code>LazyRow</code> is the same, side to side."},{"term":"lazy","def":"Doing work only when it is needed. A lazy list builds a row when it scrolls into view."},{"term":"items","def":"Inside a <code>LazyColumn</code>: <code>items(list) { value -> ... }</code> adds one row for every value. <code>item { }</code> adds a single row."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why use <code>LazyColumn</code> instead of <code>Column</code> for two years of history?","options":["It builds only the rows on screen, and it scrolls","It sorts the days","It makes the text bigger"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>Column</code> builds all 730 rows at once. <code>LazyColumn</code> builds what you can see."},{"q":"In <code>items(days) { day -> Text(day) }</code>, what is <code>day</code>?","options":["One value from the list, a different one for each row","The whole list","The number of rows"],"a":0,"why":"Like the variable in a <code>for</code> loop, it is the current value."},{"q":"Which one shows a list that scrolls from left to right?","options":["<code>LazyRow</code>","<code>LazyColumn</code>","<code>Row</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LazyRow</code> is the side-to-side version. A plain <code>Row</code> builds everything and doesn’t scroll."}]},{"id":"lazy-keys","title":"Keys in lists","tagline":"Give every row a name tag so its state stays with it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a row’s remembered state can jump to another row","Give list rows a <code>key</code>","Choose a key that is unique and never changes"],"remember":"items(list, key = { it.id }): a unique id that never changes keeps each row’s state with its item.","terms":[{"term":"key","def":"A value that names one row in a lazy list, given with <code>key = { ... }</code>. Remembered state follows the key."},{"term":"unique","def":"Not shared with anything else. No two rows may have the same key."},{"term":"mutableStateListOf","def":"Makes a list you can <code>add</code> to and <code>remove</code> from, which Compose watches like state."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Without keys, how does a lazy list match a row’s remembered state?","options":["By the row’s position","By the row’s text","It doesn’t remember anything"],"a":0,"why":"By position, so state can land on the wrong item when rows move."},{"q":"Which is the best key for today’s drinks?","options":["The drink’s id number","The drink’s size in ml","The row’s position"],"a":0,"why":"An id is unique and stays the same. Sizes repeat, and positions change."},{"q":"Two rows get the same key. What happens?","options":["The app crashes","Compose picks one at random","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Keys must be unique. A repeated key throws <code>IllegalArgumentException: Key \"...\" was already used.</code>"}]},{"id":"scaffold","title":"Screen structure","tagline":"A top bar, the content and an add button, each in its place.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a screen with <code>Scaffold</code>","Add a top bar and a floating action button","Keep content clear of the bars with <code>innerPadding</code>"],"remember":"Scaffold(topBar = { ... }, floatingActionButton = { ... }) { innerPadding -> ... }, and always use innerPadding.","terms":[{"term":"Scaffold","def":"A ready-made screen layout with slots for a top bar, a floating action button and the content."},{"term":"slot","def":"A parameter that takes a piece of UI as a lambda, like <code>topBar = { ... }</code>."},{"term":"FAB","def":"Floating action button: a square button with rounded corners for the screen’s main action, made with <code>FloatingActionButton</code>."},{"term":"innerPadding","def":"The space the bars take, status bar included, handed to the content. Apply it with <code>Modifier.padding(innerPadding)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>innerPadding</code> tell you?","options":["How much room the bars take","The size of the FAB","The space between list rows"],"a":0,"why":"Apply it to the content so nothing hides behind the bars."},{"q":"Where does a <code>FloatingActionButton</code> go?","options":["In the <code>floatingActionButton</code> slot","Inside the <code>TopAppBar</code>","Anywhere in the content"],"a":0,"why":"The slot puts it in the bottom corner, above the content."},{"q":"Inside a Scaffold, do you still need <code>safeDrawingPadding()</code> on the content?","options":["No: <code>innerPadding</code> already includes the status bar","Yes, always","Only in dark mode"],"a":0,"why":"Scaffold works out the room for the bars and the status bar, and hands it to you as <code>innerPadding</code>."}]},{"id":"theming","title":"Theming","tagline":"Pick colors and text styles once; the whole app follows, even in dark mode.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give the app its own colors with <code>lightColorScheme</code> and <code>darkColorScheme</code>","Read colors and text styles from <code>MaterialTheme</code>","Make a screen that really turns dark in dark mode"],"remember":"Read colors and text styles from MaterialTheme, put the screen in a Scaffold or Surface, and dark mode works.","terms":[{"term":"theme","def":"The colors and text styles of the whole app, set once with <code>MaterialTheme</code> and read everywhere inside it."},{"term":"color role","def":"A color named by its job, such as <code>primary</code> or <code>onSurface</code> (“text on a surface”), not by its shade."},{"term":"Surface","def":"A composable that paints a theme color behind its content and sets the text color to match. <code>Scaffold</code> does the same for a whole screen."},{"term":"dynamic color","def":"On Android 12 and newer, colors taken from the phone’s wallpaper. The template turns it on with <code>dynamicColor = true</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You set <code>primary</code> to water blue, but on your Android 14 phone the buttons are green. Why?","options":["<code>dynamicColor</code> is on, so the colors come from the wallpaper","Android 14 can’t show blue","The hex number is wrong"],"a":0,"why":"The template turns on dynamic color for Android 12 and newer. Set <code>dynamicColor</code> to <code>false</code> to see your own colors."},{"q":"What is <code>onPrimary</code> for?","options":["Text and icons drawn on top of the primary color","A brighter primary color","The color of the top bar only"],"a":0,"why":"“On” roles are for content that sits on top of the matching color, so it stays readable."},{"q":"Dark mode is on, but your screen stays white. What is missing?","options":["A <code>Scaffold</code> or <code>Surface</code> to paint the background","A call to <code>isSystemInDarkTheme()</code> in every Text","Nothing: screens are always white"],"a":0,"why":"The theme only holds the colors. <code>Scaffold</code> or <code>Surface</code> paints them behind your content."}]},{"id":"overlays","title":"Dialogs and sheets","tagline":"Ask before resetting, offer choices from the bottom.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show an <code>AlertDialog</code> controlled by a Boolean state","Close it from its buttons and from <code>onDismissRequest</code>","Show a <code>ModalBottomSheet</code> with a few choices"],"remember":"A dialog or sheet is on screen while you call it: if (show) { AlertDialog(...) }, and every way out sets show = false.","terms":[{"term":"dialog","def":"A small box over the screen that asks a question, made with <code>AlertDialog</code>."},{"term":"bottom sheet","def":"A panel that slides up from the bottom of the screen, made with <code>ModalBottomSheet</code>."},{"term":"onDismissRequest","def":"Runs when the user tries to close the dialog or sheet without a button: tapping outside, Back, or swiping a sheet down."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you close an <code>AlertDialog</code>?","options":["Set the state that controls it to false","Call <code>close()</code> on it","Compose closes it after any tap"],"a":0,"why":"It is on screen while the <code>if</code> calls it. Change the state and it is gone."},{"q":"When does <code>onDismissRequest</code> run?","options":["When the user taps outside or presses Back","When the user taps the confirm button","When the dialog first appears"],"a":0,"why":"Buttons have their own <code>onClick</code>. <code>onDismissRequest</code> is for the other ways out."},{"q":"Which line must sit above a function that uses <code>ModalBottomSheet</code>?","options":["<code>@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)</code>","<code>@Preview</code>","None"],"a":0,"why":"The bottom sheet is marked experimental, so you opt in."}]},{"id":"gestures","title":"Gestures","tagline":"Make anything tappable, and let the user drag it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make any element respond to taps with <code>clickable</code>","Follow a finger with <code>draggable</code>","Move an element with <code>offset</code>"],"remember":"clickable { } for taps on anything; draggable reports how far the finger moved, and offset moves the element.","terms":[{"term":"clickable","def":"A modifier that runs a lambda when the element is tapped, with a ripple and screen reader support."},{"term":"draggable","def":"A modifier that reports finger movement in one direction, as <code>delta</code> pixels."},{"term":"offset","def":"A modifier that shifts an element by a number of pixels, with <code>IntOffset(x, y)</code>."},{"term":"Float","def":"A decimal number type like <code>Double</code>, but smaller. Written with an <code>f</code>: <code>0f</code>, <code>2.5f</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>delta</code> mean in <code>rememberDraggableState { delta -> ... }</code>?","options":["How far the finger moved since the last call","The total distance from the start","Whether the finger is down"],"a":0,"why":"It is a small step. Adding them up gives the whole distance."},{"q":"You want a whole card to open its details when tapped. What do you use?","options":["<code>Modifier.clickable { ... }</code>","<code>Modifier.draggable(...)</code>","<code>Modifier.offset { ... }</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>clickable</code> is for taps. <code>draggable</code> is for movement, <code>offset</code> moves things."},{"q":"What is <code>2.7f.roundToInt()</code>?","options":["<code>3</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>2.7</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It rounds to the nearest whole number, and 2.7 is closer to 3."}]},{"id":"effects","title":"Side effects","tagline":"Do something when a screen appears, not on every redraw.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why waiting doesn’t belong in a composable’s body","Run code when a composable appears with <code>LaunchedEffect</code>","Restart it by changing its key"],"remember":"LaunchedEffect(key) { ... } runs when it appears, starts over when the key changes, and stops when it leaves the screen.","terms":[{"term":"side effect","def":"Something a composable does besides describing the screen: waiting, saving, sending."},{"term":"LaunchedEffect","def":"Runs a block when it appears on screen, starts it over when its key changes, and stops it when it leaves."},{"term":"key (of an effect)","def":"The value in <code>LaunchedEffect(key)</code>. A new value restarts the block."},{"term":"delay","def":"<code>delay(ms)</code> waits that many milliseconds without freezing the screen. It is a <code>suspend</code> function, so it runs inside a coroutine such as the block of <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The screen recomposes because some other state changed. Does a <code>LaunchedEffect(Unit)</code> block start again?","options":["No","Yes, every time","Only if it already finished"],"a":0,"why":"Recomposition doesn’t restart it. Only a new key, or leaving and coming back, does."},{"q":"What happens to a running <code>LaunchedEffect</code> block when its key changes?","options":["It is stopped and starts again from the top","It keeps going and a second copy starts","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"One block per effect: the old one is stopped, a new one starts."},{"q":"The user leaves the screen during <code>delay(5000)</code>. What happens to the block?","options":["It is stopped","It finishes the wait in the background","The app waits before leaving"],"a":0,"why":"The effect left the screen, so its block is stopped."}]},{"id":"disposable-effect","title":"Cleaning up","tagline":"Start listening when a screen appears, stop when it goes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pair a start with a stop using <code>DisposableEffect</code>","Put the cleanup in <code>onDispose</code>","Predict when the cleanup runs"],"remember":"DisposableEffect(key) { start; onDispose { stop } }: the stop runs when the screen leaves or the key changes.","terms":[{"term":"DisposableEffect","def":"An effect for start and stop pairs. Its block runs when it appears; <code>onDispose</code> runs when it leaves or its key changes."},{"term":"onDispose","def":"The cleanup block that must end every <code>DisposableEffect</code>."},{"term":"dispose","def":"To clean something up when you are done with it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does <code>onDispose</code> run?","options":["When the composable leaves the screen, or the key changes","Every time the screen recomposes","Only when the app closes"],"a":0,"why":"Those are the two moments when the old effect is no longer wanted."},{"q":"Which job fits <code>DisposableEffect</code> better than <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["Add a listener now and remove it later","Wait 5 seconds, then hide a tip","Show a dialog"],"a":0,"why":"It is made for start and stop pairs. Waiting belongs in <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."},{"q":"The user switches from the Home bottle to the Office bottle. How many listeners from this screen does the Home bottle have afterwards?","options":["None","One","Two"],"a":0,"why":"The key changed, so <code>onDispose</code> removed the Home listener before the block added one to Office."}]},{"id":"snapshot-flow","title":"Watching state over time","tagline":"React each time a state changes, even one your code doesn’t change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a list’s scroll position with <code>rememberLazyListState</code>","Turn state changes into a stream of values with <code>snapshotFlow</code>","Handle each new value with <code>collect</code>"],"remember":"snapshotFlow { state } hands out a new value each time the state changes; collect { } handles each one, inside LaunchedEffect.","terms":[{"term":"snapshotFlow","def":"Watches the state read inside its braces and hands out a new value each time the result changes."},{"term":"flow","def":"A stream of values that arrive one by one over time."},{"term":"collect","def":"Runs a lambda for every value of a flow, and keeps waiting for more. Lives inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."},{"term":"firstVisibleItemIndex","def":"On a list state: the index of the row at the top of the screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The top row stays index 4 while the user scrolls a little. How many new values does <code>snapshotFlow</code> hand out?","options":["None","One per scroll movement","One per second"],"a":0,"why":"It hands out a value only when the result is different from the previous one."},{"q":"Why does the <code>collect</code> go inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["It keeps waiting for values, and must stop when the screen leaves","So it runs only once","It doesn’t need to"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LaunchedEffect</code> is the place that may wait, and it stops when the screen goes. <code>collect</code> runs for every new value, not once."},{"q":"What is <code>listState.firstVisibleItemIndex</code>?","options":["The index of the row at the top of the screen","How many rows the list has","The last row the user tapped"],"a":0,"why":"It changes as the user scrolls, which is why it is worth watching."}]},{"id":"navigation","title":"Moving between screens","tagline":"One Activity, many screens, and a back stack that Compose keeps for you.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe screens as routes with <code>@Serializable</code>","Set up a <code>NavHost</code> and move with <code>navigate</code>","Pass a value to the next screen and read it with <code>toRoute</code>"],"remember":"Routes are @Serializable types; NavHost maps each route to a screen; navigate(route) pushes, Back or popBackStack() pops.","terms":[{"term":"route","def":"A type that names a screen, and may carry its data, like <code>History(val goal: Int)</code>."},{"term":"NavHost","def":"Shows the screen for the current route. Each <code>composable&lt;Route&gt; { }</code> inside it says what to show."},{"term":"navigate","def":"<code>navController.navigate(route)</code> puts that route’s screen on top of the back stack."},{"term":"@Serializable","def":"Marks a class or object so it can be stored and restored. Navigation needs it on every route."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>navController.navigate(History(goal = 6))</code> do?","options":["Puts the History screen, with goal 6, on top of the back stack","Replaces the whole app with a new Activity","Sets today’s goal to 6"],"a":0,"why":"Navigate pushes a screen. Back pops it."},{"q":"Which route should carry data?","options":["<code>@Serializable data class History(val goal: Int)</code>","<code>@Serializable object History</code>","Neither can"],"a":0,"why":"A data class has properties for the data. An <code>object</code> is for screens that need none."},{"q":"The user makes the Back gesture on the start screen. What happens?","options":["They leave the app","Nothing","The start screen opens again"],"a":0,"why":"There is nothing left to pop, so Back leaves the app, as on any app’s first screen."}]}]},{"id":"tools","title":"Android Studio and debugging","description":"The tools you use every day on your Water tracker: find your way around Android Studio, read Logcat, pause code in the debugger, look inside the screen, install with adb, check with lint, and let GitHub check every pull request.","requires":["android","compose"],"file":"tracks/tools.js","lessons":[{"id":"studio-tour","title":"A tour of Android Studio","tagline":"Find your way around the window: where the files, the code, the running app and the messages are.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the main parts of the Android Studio window","Open a tool window such as Logcat or Terminal, and get a closed one back","Use three shortcuts: Search Everywhere, quick fixes and comment line"],"remember":"Toolbar to run, Project for files, editor for code, tool windows for the rest. Shift twice finds anything; Alt+Enter fixes.","terms":[{"term":"tool window","def":"A panel on the edge of the Android Studio window for one job, such as Build, Logcat or Terminal. Find them under View &gt; Tool Windows."},{"term":"Running Devices","def":"The tool window that shows the emulator’s screen while your app runs."},{"term":"Search Everywhere","def":"Press Shift twice and type to find a file, a function or a menu action."},{"term":"quick fix","def":"A fix Studio offers for the marked code under the cursor. Alt+Enter (Option+Enter on macOS) opens the list."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you choose which device your app runs on?","options":["The target device menu in the toolbar","The Project window","The status bar"],"a":0,"why":"The toolbar holds what to run, where to run it, and the Run and Debug buttons."},{"q":"A word is underlined in red and the cursor is on it. What does <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> do?","options":["Deletes the line","Shows quick fixes for it","Runs the app"],"a":1,"why":"Alt+Enter (Option+Enter on macOS) lists the fixes Studio knows for the marked code."},{"q":"You closed the Terminal window. How do you get it back?","options":["Restart Android Studio","View &gt; Tool Windows &gt; Terminal","Create a new project"],"a":1,"why":"Every tool window is listed under View &gt; Tool Windows. Its icon on the window edge works too."}]},{"id":"logcat","title":"Logcat","tagline":"Write messages from your app, find yours among thousands, and read a crash.","level":"beginner","goals":["Pick a log level, and keep the tag in one <code>TAG</code> constant","Filter Logcat to your app, a tag or a level","Find a crash and read which line caused it"],"remember":"Log.d(TAG, \"...\") writes a line. package:mine finds yours, level:WARN the serious ones, is:crash the crash.","terms":[{"term":"log level","def":"How serious a message is: Verbose, Debug, Info, Warn or Error, written with <code>Log.v</code>, <code>Log.d</code>, <code>Log.i</code>, <code>Log.w</code> or <code>Log.e</code>."},{"term":"query","def":"What you type in the box at the top of Logcat to keep only some lines, such as <code>package:mine level:WARN</code>."},{"term":"stack trace","def":"The list of <code>at</code> lines under a crash: the function calls that led to it, newest first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which query keeps only crashes?","options":["<code>level:ERROR</code>","<code>is:crash</code>","<code>tag:crash</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>is:crash</code> matches crash entries. <code>level:ERROR</code> also keeps every other error line."},{"q":"In a crash, which line usually points at the code to fix?","options":["The last line of the block","The first <code>at</code> line that names your own package and file","The <code>FATAL EXCEPTION</code> line"],"a":1,"why":"The stack trace lists calls newest first. The first one in your own code is where your code was when it failed."},{"q":"Which call writes a warning?","options":["<code>Log.d(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>","<code>Log.w(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>","<code>Log.v(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>w</code> is Warn. <code>d</code> is Debug and <code>v</code> is Verbose, both less serious."}]},{"id":"debugger","title":"The debugger","tagline":"Pause your running app on a line and look at the real values inside.","level":"beginner","goals":["Set a breakpoint and start the app with Debug","Run your code one line at a time with Step Over","Read values in the Variables pane to find a bug"],"remember":"Breakpoint on the line, Debug instead of Run, then Step Over and watch Variables until a value surprises you.","terms":[{"term":"debugger","def":"A tool that pauses your running app so you can look at its values and run it one line at a time."},{"term":"breakpoint","def":"A red dot in the gutter next to a line. When the app was started with Debug, it pauses before that line runs."},{"term":"Step Over, Step Into, Step Out","def":"Run the current line and pause on the next; go inside the function called on this line; finish the current function and go back to its caller."},{"term":"Variables pane","def":"The part of the Debug window that lists every value the paused function can see."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You are paused on a line that calls <code>priceOf(item)</code>, and you want to see what happens inside <code>priceOf</code>. Which button?","options":["Step Over","Step Into","Resume Program"],"a":1,"why":"Step Into goes inside the called function. Step Over would run the whole call as one step."},{"q":"What does a red dot in the gutter mean?","options":["A line that crashed","A breakpoint: the app pauses before this line runs","A line that was never run"],"a":1,"why":"Clicking the gutter sets a breakpoint. It works when the app was started with Debug."},{"q":"You have seen enough and want the app to run on normally. What do you press?","options":["Step Over","Resume Program","Step Into"],"a":1,"why":"Resume Program lets the app run until it reaches a breakpoint again."}]},{"id":"layout-inspector","title":"Layout Inspector","tagline":"Look inside your running app’s screen: which composables it is made of, and what each one received.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open Layout Inspector on a running app","Find a composable in the Component Tree and read its values in Attributes","See how often each composable recomposes or skips"],"remember":"Running Devices, Toggle Layout Inspector: the tree shows what was drawn, Attributes shows what it received. Reset, tap once, read the counts.","terms":[{"term":"Layout Inspector","def":"An Android Studio tool that shows the composables of your running app’s screen and the values they received."},{"term":"Component Tree","def":"The Layout Inspector pane that lists the composables, nested the way they are drawn."},{"term":"Attributes","def":"The Layout Inspector pane that shows the values of the selected composable, such as its text."},{"term":"recomposition count","def":"How many times a composable ran again, next to how many times Compose skipped it. Turn them on under View Options."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you start Layout Inspector?","options":["In the Running Devices window, with Toggle Layout Inspector","In Logcat, with a query","Under File &gt; New"],"a":0,"why":"Run the app, then use Toggle Layout Inspector in the Running Devices window."},{"q":"You select a Text in the Component Tree. Where do you read the text it received?","options":["Attributes","Logcat","The status bar"],"a":0,"why":"Attributes shows the values of the selected composable."},{"q":"You tap once, and a composable’s skip count goes up by 1. What happened?","options":["It ran again","Compose skipped it: nothing it received changed","It crashed"],"a":1,"why":"The first column counts recompositions, the second counts skips."}]},{"id":"adb","title":"adb","tagline":"Talk to the phone or emulator from the terminal: install apps, run commands on it, read its log.","level":"beginner","goals":["List connected devices with <code>adb devices</code>","Build an APK with <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code>, then install and remove it with <code>adb install</code> and <code>adb uninstall</code>","Run a command on the device with <code>adb shell</code>, and read its log with <code>adb logcat</code>"],"remember":"adb devices to see, ./gradlew assembleDebug to build the APK, adb install to add, adb uninstall to remove, adb shell to run a command on the device, adb logcat to read the log.","terms":[{"term":"adb","def":"Android Debug Bridge: a command line tool that installs apps on a device, runs commands on it and reads its log."},{"term":"APK","def":"One file that holds a whole Android app, ready to install, such as <code>app-debug.apk</code>."},{"term":"serial","def":"The name adb gives each device, such as <code>emulator-5554</code>. Pick one with <code>-s</code> right after <code>adb</code>."},{"term":"gradlew","def":"A small file in the top folder of a project that runs Gradle, the build tool. <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code> builds the debug APK."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>adb devices</code> show?","options":["Every app on the phone","The emulators and phones adb can talk to","The files in your project"],"a":1,"why":"It lists each connected device with its serial and state."},{"q":"Which command runs <code>pm</code> on the device?","options":["<code>pm list packages</code>","<code>adb shell pm list packages</code>","<code>adb pm shell list packages</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>adb shell</code> followed by a command runs that command on the device. Typed alone, <code>pm</code> would run on your computer, which has no such tool."},{"q":"<code>adb logcat -s Water</code> keeps printing lines. How do you stop it?","options":["Close Android Studio","Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>","Uninstall the app"],"a":1,"why":"Ctrl+C stops the command that is running in the terminal."}]},{"id":"lint","title":"Lint","tagline":"A tool that reads your code and warns you about likely bugs before anyone runs it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Run lint with <code>./gradlew lint</code> and find its report","Fix a lint error, such as state made without <code>remember</code>","Use a baseline so old warnings don’t hide new ones"],"remember":"Run never runs lint: ./gradlew lint does. Fix the errors, and baseline old warnings so new ones stand out.","terms":[{"term":"lint","def":"A tool that reads your code, resources and manifest without running them, and reports likely bugs."},{"term":"issue id, severity","def":"Each lint issue has an id, such as <code>UnrememberedMutableState</code>, and a severity, such as warning or error. Errors make <code>./gradlew lint</code> fail."},{"term":"baseline","def":"A file, <code>lint-baseline.xml</code>, that records known issues, so lint reports only new ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Does pressing Run also run a full lint check?","options":["Yes, every time","No: you run it yourself, for example with <code>./gradlew lint</code>","Only when the app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Lint is not part of the normal build. Run it yourself, or let a server run it for you."},{"q":"What does a lint baseline do?","options":["Fixes old issues automatically","Records today’s issues, so later runs report only new ones","Turns lint off"],"a":1,"why":"Old issues are filtered out, so a new one stands out instead of hiding among them."},{"q":"Lint finds one warning and no errors. What does <code>./gradlew lint</code> do?","options":["It fails","It succeeds, and the warning is in the report","It deletes the code"],"a":1,"why":"By default only errors make the command fail. Warnings are reported."}]},{"id":"ci","title":"Continuous integration","tagline":"Let GitHub build and check every pull request for you, on a clean machine.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what continuous integration is and why teams use it","Read a minimal GitHub Actions workflow line by line","Find a failed check on a pull request and see which step failed"],"remember":"A workflow in .github/workflows/ runs on every pull request: check out, set up Java and Gradle, then ./gradlew lint assembleDebug.","terms":[{"term":"continuous integration (CI)","def":"A server builds and checks every change automatically, on a clean machine, before it is merged."},{"term":"workflow","def":"A YAML file in <code>.github/workflows/</code> that says when GitHub Actions runs and what it does."},{"term":"job, step","def":"A job runs on one machine. Its steps run in order; <code>uses:</code> runs a ready-made action, <code>run:</code> runs a command."},{"term":"check","def":"The result a workflow shows on a pull request: a green tick when it passed, a red cross when it failed."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where must the workflow file be?","options":["In the <code>app</code> folder","In <code>.github/workflows/</code>","Anywhere, if it ends in <code>.yml</code>"],"a":1,"why":"GitHub only discovers workflows in <code>.github/workflows</code>."},{"q":"The <code>run:</code> step with lint fails. What happens next?","options":["The steps after it run anyway","The steps after it don’t run, and the pull request shows a red cross","GitHub fixes the code"],"a":1,"why":"A failed step stops the job. Fix the code and push again to rerun it."},{"q":"Why does the job start with <code>actions/checkout</code>?","options":["To choose a branch name","The machine starts empty, so it first downloads your repository","To install Java"],"a":1,"why":"Every run gets a fresh machine. Without checkout, there is no code to build."}]}]},{"id":"coroutines","title":"Coroutines","description":"Keep the app responsive during slow work: start coroutines, run things at the same time, pick the thread, cancel, handle errors, share values safely and pass values through channels.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/coroutines.js","lessons":[{"id":"why-async","title":"Why apps must not wait","tagline":"One thread draws the screen. Make it wait, and the whole app freezes.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what the main thread of an app does","Explain why a long wait on the main thread freezes the screen","Recognise an ANR and what causes it"],"remember":"The main thread draws the screen and runs every tap, one job at a time. Never make it wait.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One line of steps that the phone works through, one step at a time."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The thread that draws an app’s screen and runs the code for taps."},{"term":"blocking","def":"Making a thread stop and wait, so it can do nothing else meanwhile. <code>Thread.sleep(3000)</code> blocks for 3 seconds."},{"term":"ANR","def":"“Application Not Responding”: Android’s dialog when the main thread hasn’t responded to a tap for 5 seconds."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the main thread of an app do?","options":["Draws the screen and runs the code for taps","Only talks to servers","Nothing while the app is open"],"a":0,"why":"All screen work happens on the main thread, one job at a time."},{"q":"The main thread is stuck in a long job and hasn’t responded to a tap for 5 seconds. What does Android do?","options":["Shows a dialog saying the app isn’t responding","Runs the tap on another thread","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"That dialog is an ANR. The user can close the app from it."},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>Thread.sleep(1000)</code> and <code>delay(1000)</code> inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["<code>sleep</code> stops the whole thread; <code>delay</code> waits without holding the thread up","None","<code>delay</code> waits 1000 seconds"],"a":0,"why":"Both wait 1 second, but only <code>delay</code> leaves the main thread free to draw and handle taps."}]},{"id":"basics","title":"Your first coroutine","tagline":"Start work that waits, while the rest of the program keeps going.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a <code>suspend</code> function that waits with <code>delay</code>","Start a coroutine with <code>launch</code>","Predict the order in which lines print"],"remember":"suspend fun can pause; delay pauses the coroutine, not the thread; launch { } starts a new coroutine and returns at once.","terms":[{"term":"coroutine","def":"A piece of work that can pause and carry on later, leaving its thread free while it waits."},{"term":"suspend","def":"Marks a function that may pause, like <code>suspend fun sync()</code>. It can only be called from a coroutine or another <code>suspend</code> function."},{"term":"launch","def":"<code>launch { ... }</code> starts a new coroutine that runs the block, and returns at once."},{"term":"runBlocking","def":"Keeps <code>main()</code> running until the coroutines inside finish. Fine in small programs, never in an app."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>delay(1000)</code> do in a coroutine?","options":["Pauses the coroutine for 1 second, leaving the thread free","Stops the whole thread for 1 second","Waits 1000 seconds"],"a":0,"why":"The coroutine waits; meanwhile the thread can run other coroutines."},{"q":"Inside <code>runBlocking</code>: <code>launch { println(\"A\") }</code>, then <code>println(\"B\")</code>. What prints first?","options":["<code>B</code>","<code>A</code>","Either, at random"],"a":0,"why":"<code>launch</code> returns at once, so <code>B</code> prints. The new coroutine runs when the thread is free. Every time."},{"q":"Where can you call a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["In a coroutine or in another <code>suspend</code> function","Anywhere","Only in <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only a coroutine can pause, so a function that may pause needs one around it."}]},{"id":"structured","title":"Structured concurrency","tagline":"Every coroutine has a parent, and a parent waits for its children.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a scope is and how parents and children relate","Wait for several coroutines with <code>coroutineScope</code>","Start a coroutine from a button with <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>"],"remember":"Coroutines live in a scope. A parent waits for its children, and stopping it stops them. coroutineScope { } returns when all its children are done.","terms":[{"term":"scope","def":"The owner that coroutines are started in. It waits for them, and stopping it stops them."},{"term":"structured concurrency","def":"Every coroutine has a parent: a parent waits for its children, and stopping a parent stops its children."},{"term":"coroutineScope","def":"<code>coroutineScope { ... }</code> makes a new scope inside a <code>suspend</code> function and returns when all its children have finished."},{"term":"rememberCoroutineScope","def":"In a composable, gives a scope that belongs to the screen, for starting coroutines from <code>onClick</code>. It is stopped when the screen leaves."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A parent coroutine is stopped. What happens to its children?","options":["They are stopped too","They keep running on their own","They start again"],"a":0,"why":"Children belong to their parent. That is what keeps work from running on after nobody needs it."},{"q":"The block of <code>coroutineScope { }</code> has reached its end, but one child is still running. What happens?","options":["It waits for the child, then returns","It returns at once","It stops the child"],"a":0,"why":"A scope is finished only when all its children are."},{"q":"Why does a Sync button need <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>?","options":["<code>onClick</code> is not a coroutine; the scope lets it start one that stops when the screen leaves","It makes the button faster","Buttons can only call <code>suspend</code> functions"],"a":0,"why":"<code>scope.launch</code> starts the coroutine, and the scope belongs to the screen."}]},{"id":"async","title":"Doing things at the same time","tagline":"Start two jobs together and wait for both answers.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start work that gives back a result with <code>async</code>","Get the result with <code>await</code>","Explain why two waits at once take half the time"],"remember":"async { } starts work and returns a Deferred; await() gives the result, pausing until it is ready. Start them all, then await.","terms":[{"term":"async","def":"<code>async { ... }</code> starts a coroutine and returns a <code>Deferred</code> that will hold the block’s result."},{"term":"Deferred","def":"The box <code>async</code> gives back. It holds the result once the work is done."},{"term":"await","def":"<code>deferred.await()</code> gives the result, pausing the coroutine until it is ready."},{"term":"System.currentTimeMillis","def":"<code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code> gives the current time in milliseconds, handy for measuring how long something took."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>async { loadGoal() }</code> give back?","options":["A <code>Deferred</code>: a box that will hold the result","The goal, right away","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"It returns at once with the box; the result goes in when the load is done."},{"q":"What happens at <code>goal.await()</code> when the goal hasn’t arrived yet?","options":["The coroutine pauses until it has","It gives back 0","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>await</code> is a <code>suspend</code> function: it pauses until the value is ready."},{"q":"Two loads take 1 second each. Which order of lines takes about 1 second?","options":["async, async, then await, await","async, await, then async, await"],"a":0,"why":"Starting both before awaiting lets the two waits overlap."}]},{"id":"dispatchers","title":"Which thread runs it","tagline":"Send heavy work to a background thread, then come back with the result.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name <code>Dispatchers.Main</code>, <code>IO</code> and <code>Default</code> and what each is for","Move work off the main thread with <code>withContext</code>","Explain why <code>suspend</code> alone doesn’t move work to another thread"],"remember":"A dispatcher picks the thread: Main for the screen, IO for files and network, Default for heavy work. withContext(...) { } runs a block there and comes back; launch(Dispatchers.Default) { } starts a whole coroutine there.","terms":[{"term":"dispatcher","def":"Decides which thread runs a coroutine. <code>Dispatchers.Main</code> is the main thread of an app."},{"term":"Dispatchers.IO","def":"Background threads for work that waits on files or the network."},{"term":"Dispatchers.Default","def":"Background threads for heavy calculations, such as adding up or sorting a long list."},{"term":"withContext","def":"<code>withContext(dispatcher) { ... }</code> runs the block on that dispatcher, then gives back its last value on the original thread."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which dispatcher fits reading a file?","options":["<code>Dispatchers.IO</code>","<code>Dispatchers.Main</code>","<code>Dispatchers.Default</code>"],"a":0,"why":"IO is for work that waits on files or the network."},{"q":"A <code>suspend</code> function adds up a huge list, with no <code>withContext</code>. It is called from <code>LaunchedEffect</code>. Which thread does the adding?","options":["The main thread","A background thread","No thread at all"],"a":0,"why":"<code>suspend</code> doesn’t switch threads. The work runs where the caller is: the main thread."},{"q":"After <code>withContext(Dispatchers.Default) { ... }</code> finishes, where does the coroutine carry on?","options":["On the thread it was on before","On a Default thread from then on"],"a":0,"why":"Only the block moves. The rest of the coroutine stays where it was."}]},{"id":"cancellation","title":"Cancelling work","tagline":"Stop a coroutine you no longer need, or one that takes too long.","level":"beginner","goals":["Stop a coroutine with its <code>Job</code> and <code>cancel()</code>","Make a busy loop stop when cancelled with <code>ensureActive()</code>","Give up on slow work with <code>withTimeoutOrNull</code>"],"remember":"job.cancel() stops a coroutine at its next suspend call. Busy loops need ensureActive(). withTimeoutOrNull gives null when time runs out.","terms":[{"term":"Job","def":"The handle <code>launch</code> gives back. Use it to cancel the coroutine."},{"term":"cancel","def":"<code>job.cancel()</code> asks a coroutine to stop. At its next check, such as a <code>delay</code>, a <code>CancellationException</code> is thrown: cancelling is cooperative."},{"term":"ensureActive","def":"<code>ensureActive()</code> stops the coroutine right there if it has been cancelled. Use it in loops that never pause."},{"term":"withTimeoutOrNull","def":"<code>withTimeoutOrNull(ms) { ... }</code> cancels the block if it takes longer than <code>ms</code> milliseconds, and then gives back <code>null</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>launch</code> give back?","options":["A <code>Job</code>, a handle to stop the coroutine","The result of the block","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Keep the <code>Job</code> in a <code>val</code> to cancel the coroutine later."},{"q":"A coroutine runs a long loop with no <code>suspend</code> calls and no <code>ensureActive()</code>. You call <code>cancel()</code>. What happens?","options":["It keeps going until the loop ends","It stops at once"],"a":0,"why":"Cancelling is cooperative. Without a check, the loop never notices."},{"q":"What does <code>withTimeoutOrNull(500) { delay(2000); \"done\" }</code> give back?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>\"done\"</code>","It crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The block needs 2 seconds but has 0.5, so it is cancelled and the answer is <code>null</code>."}]},{"id":"exceptions","title":"Errors in coroutines","tagline":"Catch a failure where it happens, and keep one failure from stopping the rest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Catch an error from a <code>suspend</code> function with <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>","Explain how a failed child cancels its parent and siblings","Keep the other children running with <code>supervisorScope</code>"],"remember":"Catch with try/catch inside the coroutine. An uncaught failure cancels the parent and siblings; supervisorScope lets a child fail alone.","terms":[{"term":"propagation","def":"An uncaught error travelling up: the failed child cancels its parent, which cancels the other children."},{"term":"supervisorScope","def":"Like <code>coroutineScope</code>, but a failed child cancels neither the scope nor its siblings."},{"term":"SupervisorJob","def":"The kind of job behind <code>supervisorScope</code>: one whose children can fail alone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>try { scope.launch { throw Exception(\"x\") } } catch (e: Exception) { ... }</code>. Does the <code>catch</code> run?","options":["No: <code>launch</code> returned before the exception","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>try</code> only covers starting the coroutine. Put the <code>try</code> inside it."},{"q":"In <code>coroutineScope</code>, one child fails and nobody catches it. What happens to the other children?","options":["They are cancelled","They keep running","They fail with their own errors"],"a":0,"why":"The error goes to the parent, which cancels the rest and then fails."},{"q":"In <code>supervisorScope</code>, an <code>async</code> child fails. Where do you see the error?","options":["When you call <code>await()</code> on it","Nowhere","In the other children"],"a":0,"why":"The error is kept in the <code>Deferred</code> until <code>await()</code> throws it."}]},{"id":"shared-state","title":"Shared state","tagline":"When coroutines on several threads change one value, guard it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>glasses++</code> from several threads loses updates","Protect a value with <code>Mutex</code> and <code>withLock</code>","Avoid the problem by changing a value on one thread only"],"remember":"glasses++ from several threads can lose updates. Guard it with mutex.withLock { }, or change it on one thread only.","terms":[{"term":"race condition","def":"A bug where the result depends on which thread happens to go first, like two <code>glasses++</code> losing an update."},{"term":"Mutex","def":"A lock for coroutines: <code>val mutex = Mutex()</code>. Only one coroutine at a time can hold it."},{"term":"withLock","def":"<code>mutex.withLock { ... }</code> runs the block while holding the lock. Others pause until it is free."},{"term":"confinement","def":"Changing a value on one thread only, such as the main thread, so no lock is needed."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can <code>glasses++</code> lose updates when several threads run it?","options":["It is three steps (read, add, write), and two threads can read the same old value","Kotlin rounds big numbers","Threads can’t change a <code>var</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Both read 5, both write 6, and one update is lost."},{"q":"Another coroutine is inside <code>mutex.withLock { }</code>. What does yours do when it gets there?","options":["Pauses until the lock is free, without blocking its thread","Skips the block","Crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>withLock</code> waits like <code>delay</code> does: the coroutine pauses, and the thread is free."},{"q":"Every coroutine that changes <code>glasses</code> runs on the main thread, and the change is a plain <code>glasses++</code>. Do you need a <code>Mutex</code>?","options":["No: only one thread ever touches it","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"That is confinement. One thread runs one <code>glasses++</code> at a time."}]},{"id":"channels","title":"Channels","tagline":"Pass values from one coroutine to another, one at a time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Send and receive values through a <code>Channel</code>","Predict when <code>send</code> and <code>receive</code> pause","End a channel with <code>close</code> and read it with a <code>for</code> loop"],"remember":"A Channel passes values between coroutines: send waits for a receiver, receive waits for a value, close ends the for loop.","terms":[{"term":"Channel","def":"A pipe between coroutines: values go in with <code>send</code> and come out, in order, with <code>receive</code>."},{"term":"send","def":"<code>channel.send(value)</code> puts a value in. On a <code>Channel()</code>, it pauses until a receiver takes it."},{"term":"receive","def":"<code>channel.receive()</code> takes the next value out, pausing while the channel is empty."},{"term":"close","def":"<code>channel.close()</code> says no more values are coming, so a <code>for</code> loop over the channel ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two coroutines receive from one channel. The sender sends 4 values. How many receivers get each value?","options":["Exactly one","Both","It depends on the order"],"a":0,"why":"Receiving takes the value out of the channel, so each value goes to one receiver."},{"q":"What does <code>receive()</code> do when the channel is empty?","options":["Pauses until a value is sent","Gives back <code>null</code>","Throws"],"a":0,"why":"It waits, like <code>delay</code>, without blocking the thread."},{"q":"When does <code>for (drink in drinks) { }</code> end?","options":["When the channel is closed and empty","After one value","Never"],"a":0,"why":"<code>close()</code> marks the end; the loop still gets every value sent before it."}]}]},{"id":"flow","title":"Flow","description":"How Flow streams values to your UI, from cold flows and operators to flows of flows, errors, fast producers, hot flows, sharing, lifecycle-aware collection and wrapping callbacks.","requires":["coroutines"],"file":"tracks/flow.js","lessons":[{"id":"cold","title":"A stream of values","tagline":"A flow hands out values one by one, and does nothing until someone collects it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a flow with <code>flow { }</code> and <code>emit</code>","Get its values one by one with <code>collect</code>","Explain why a flow is cold: each <code>collect</code> runs it from the top"],"remember":"flow { emit(x) } is a recipe; collect runs it from the top, again for every collector.","terms":[{"term":"Flow<Int>","def":"A stream of Ints that arrive one by one over time. Read “flow of Int”."},{"term":"flow { }","def":"Makes a flow. Its block runs only when someone collects the flow."},{"term":"emit","def":"<code>emit(value)</code> hands one value from the flow’s block to the collector."},{"term":"cold","def":"A cold flow does nothing until it is collected, and runs again from the top for each collector."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A flow’s block starts with <code>println(\"hi\")</code>. The program makes the flow but never calls <code>collect</code>. What is printed?","options":["Nothing","<code>hi</code>","<code>hi</code>, then the values"],"a":0,"why":"The block runs only when the flow is collected."},{"q":"The <code>glasses</code> flow above is collected twice. How many times is “Counting starts” printed?","options":["Once","Twice"],"a":1,"why":"A cold flow runs its block again from the top for every <code>collect</code>."},{"q":"When does <code>glasses.collect { ... }</code> return?","options":["Right away","After the first value","When the flow’s block reaches its end"],"a":2,"why":"<code>collect</code> waits for every value, and returns once the block has finished."}]},{"id":"operators","title":"Transforming flows","tagline":"map, filter and onEach work on each value as it arrives.","level":"beginner","goals":["Change each value of a flow with <code>map</code>","Drop values with <code>filter</code>","Do something with each value on the way with <code>onEach</code>"],"remember":"map changes, filter drops, onEach looks; each value goes through the whole chain on its own, and only when you collect.","terms":[{"term":"operator","def":"A function like <code>map</code> or <code>filter</code> that takes a flow and gives back a new flow."},{"term":"onEach","def":"Runs a lambda for each value and passes the value on unchanged."},{"term":"ml","def":"Millilitres: 1,000 ml is one litre. A glass is about 200 to 250 ml."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A flow emits 1, 2 and 3. What does <code>.map { it * 10 }</code> hand to <code>collect</code>?","options":["10, 20 and 30","60","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> changes each value on its own, as it arrives."},{"q":"<code>.onEach { println(it) }</code> comes before <code>.filter { it &gt; 5 }</code>. Is the value 3 printed?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"<code>onEach</code> is above <code>filter</code> in the chain, so it sees 3 before <code>filter</code> drops it."},{"q":"With <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> and no <code>collect</code>, what runs?","options":["Nothing","The flow, but nothing is printed","Only <code>map</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Operators make a new recipe. Nothing runs until <code>collect</code>."}]},{"id":"flatmap","title":"Flows of flows","tagline":"When each value starts a flow of its own, flatMapLatest keeps only the newest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start a flow for each value with <code>flatMapLatest</code>","Explain why it cancels the old inner flow","Choose <code>flatMapConcat</code> or <code>flatMapMerge</code> when old work must go on"],"remember":"flatMapLatest cancels the old inner flow, flatMapConcat waits its turn, flatMapMerge runs them all at once.","terms":[{"term":"inner flow","def":"The flow that each value starts, like <code>search(text)</code> for each typed text."},{"term":"flatMapLatest","def":"Starts an inner flow for each value, and cancels the previous one when a new value arrives."},{"term":"flatMapConcat","def":"Runs the inner flows one after another, in order, each to its end."},{"term":"flatMapMerge","def":"Runs the inner flows at the same time and passes values on as they arrive."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>flatMapLatest</code>, a new value arrives while the inner flow for the old one is still running. What happens to it?","options":["It is cancelled","It finishes first","It runs alongside the new one"],"a":0,"why":"A new value cancels the inner flow still running for the previous one."},{"q":"Photos must upload one at a time, in order, and every upload must finish. Which operator fits?","options":["<code>flatMapConcat</code>","<code>flatMapLatest</code>","<code>flatMapMerge</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>flatMapConcat</code> starts the next inner flow only after the previous one has ended."},{"q":"What does <code>map { text -&gt; search(text) }</code> give, when <code>search</code> returns a flow?","options":["A flow of flows","A flow of results","A list"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> only wraps each value; it doesn’t collect the flows it makes."}]},{"id":"errors-retry","title":"Errors and retries","tagline":"retry tries again; catch turns a failure into a value.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn an exception in a flow into a value with <code>catch</code>","Try a failing flow again with <code>retry</code>","Put <code>retry</code> and <code>catch</code> in the right order"],"remember":"retry first, catch after it: try again, then show a fallback. catch only sees errors from above it.","terms":[{"term":"catch { } (on a flow)","def":"An operator that handles an exception thrown above it in the chain, and can <code>emit</code> a value instead."},{"term":"retry(n)","def":"When the flow above it fails, runs it again from the top, up to <code>n</code> more times."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The chain is <code>.retry(2)</code> then <code>.catch</code>, and the server fails every time. How many tries are made in all?","options":["3","2","1"],"a":0,"why":"The first try, plus 2 more. Then the exception reaches <code>catch</code>."},{"q":"An exception is thrown inside <code>collect { }</code>. Does a <code>.catch</code> earlier in the chain handle it?","options":["No","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>catch</code> only sees errors from above it. <code>collect</code> is below it."},{"q":"What does <code>catch</code> do after it has emitted its fallback value?","options":["The flow ends normally","The flow starts again","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The exception is handled, so the flow simply ends, and <code>collect</code> returns."}]},{"id":"backpressure","title":"Fast producers","tagline":"When values come faster than you can handle them: buffer, conflate or collectLatest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a slow collector slows the flow down","Keep every value with <code>buffer</code>, or only the newest with <code>conflate</code>","Cancel old work with <code>collectLatest</code>"],"remember":"buffer keeps every value, conflate keeps the newest, collectLatest cancels the old work.","terms":[{"term":"producer and collector","def":"The producer is the side that makes values (the flow’s block). The collector is the side that handles them (<code>collect</code>)."},{"term":"buffer","def":"A queue between producer and collector. <code>buffer()</code> lets the producer run ahead; every value is still handled."},{"term":"conflate","def":"Keeps only the newest value while the collector is busy; the ones in between are dropped."},{"term":"collectLatest","def":"Like <code>collect</code>, but a new value cancels the lambda still working on the old one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"No operator. The producer is faster than the collector. What does the producer do?","options":["Waits in <code>emit</code> until the collector is done","Keeps going at full speed","Drops values"],"a":0,"why":"By default they take turns: <code>emit</code> returns only when the collector’s lambda has finished."},{"q":"With <code>collectLatest</code>, value 2 arrives while the lambda is still handling 1. What happens?","options":["The work on 1 is cancelled, and the lambda starts with 2","2 waits until 1 is done","2 is dropped"],"a":0,"why":"A new value cancels the old work and starts the lambda again."},{"q":"With <code>buffer()</code>, how many values are lost?","options":["None","All but the newest","Half"],"a":0,"why":"The values wait in the queue and every one is handled, in order."}]},{"id":"hot","title":"Hot flows","tagline":"A StateFlow holds the current value; a SharedFlow announces events to whoever is listening.","level":"beginner","goals":["Hold a value that screens can watch with <code>MutableStateFlow</code>","Announce one-off events with <code>MutableSharedFlow</code>","Explain what makes a flow hot"],"remember":"StateFlow: always one value, handed to every new collector. SharedFlow: events for whoever is collecting right now.","terms":[{"term":"hot flow","def":"A flow that exists and takes values with nobody collecting. Collecting it starts no code, and it never ends."},{"term":"StateFlow","def":"A hot flow that always holds one value, <code>.value</code>, and hands it to every new collector."},{"term":"SharedFlow","def":"A hot flow that hands each value to the collectors there at that moment, and keeps nothing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A StateFlow holds 4. A new screen starts collecting it. What does the screen get first?","options":["4, right away","Nothing until the value changes","Every value it ever held"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow hands its current value to every new collector at once."},{"q":"<code>messages.emit(\"Hi\")</code> runs on a <code>MutableSharedFlow</code> while nobody collects. What happens to “Hi”?","options":["It is lost","It waits for the first collector","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"A SharedFlow made with <code>MutableSharedFlow()</code> keeps nothing. Only the collectors there at that moment get a value."},{"q":"<code>glasses.value</code> is 3, and the code sets it to 3 again. What do the collectors get?","options":["Nothing","3 once more","0"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow hands out a value only when it is different from the current one."}]},{"id":"statein-sharein","title":"Sharing a flow","tagline":"stateIn and shareIn run one cold flow once and share it with every collector.","level":"beginner","goals":["Share one run of a cold flow with <code>stateIn</code>","Say when it runs with <code>SharingStarted</code>","Choose between <code>stateIn</code> and <code>shareIn</code>"],"remember":"stateIn and shareIn run a cold flow once, in a scope, for every collector. stateIn has a current value; shareIn is for events.","terms":[{"term":"stateIn","def":"Runs a cold flow once in a scope and shares it as a <code>StateFlow</code>, which starts with the value you give."},{"term":"shareIn","def":"Like <code>stateIn</code>, but gives a <code>SharedFlow</code>: no starting value, and a new collector gets only what comes next."},{"term":"SharingStarted","def":"When the shared cold flow runs: <code>WhileSubscribed(5000)</code> while someone collects, plus 5 seconds; <code>Eagerly</code> at once, until the scope ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>stateIn(this, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5000), 0)</code>. A screen collects before the sensor has sent anything. What does it get?","options":["0","Nothing yet","An error"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow always has a value. Until the cold flow emits, it is the starting value you passed, 0."},{"q":"Three screens collect one <code>stateIn</code> StateFlow. How many times does the cold flow run?","options":["Once","Three times"],"a":0,"why":"That is the point of sharing: one run, its values handed to every collector."},{"q":"The screen should show the latest sips at once when it opens. Which one fits?","options":["<code>stateIn</code>","<code>shareIn</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>stateIn</code> makes a StateFlow, which always holds a value and hands it to a new collector right away. <code>shareIn</code> makes a SharedFlow, which keeps nothing: a new collector waits for the next value."}]},{"id":"lifecycle","title":"Collecting safely in the UI","tagline":"Collect while the screen is visible, stop when it isn’t.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read a flow as state in Compose with <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code>","Collect only while the screen is started with <code>repeatOnLifecycle</code>","Explain why collecting in the background wastes battery"],"remember":"In Compose, collectAsStateWithLifecycle(); elsewhere, repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) inside lifecycleScope.launch. Both collect only while the screen is visible.","terms":[{"term":"collectAsStateWithLifecycle","def":"Collects a flow into state a composable reads, only while the screen is at least started."},{"term":"initialValue","def":"For a plain <code>Flow</code>, which has no current value: <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle(initialValue = 0)</code> shows 0 until the first value arrives."},{"term":"lifecycleScope","def":"A scope that belongs to an Activity. Its coroutines are cancelled when the Activity is destroyed."},{"term":"repeatOnLifecycle","def":"<code>repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) { }</code> runs its block each time the screen is started and cancels it each time it is stopped."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user presses Home. What does <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code> do?","options":["Stops collecting","Keeps collecting","Destroys the StateFlow"],"a":0,"why":"Home stops the screen, and collecting stops with it. The StateFlow itself keeps its value."},{"q":"The user comes back. What does the screen show first?","options":["The StateFlow’s current value","The value it showed before Home","0"],"a":0,"why":"Collecting starts again, and a StateFlow hands its current value at once."},{"q":"When are the coroutines in <code>lifecycleScope</code> cancelled?","options":["When the Activity is destroyed","At every <code>onStop</code>","Never"],"a":0,"why":"The scope belongs to the Activity. Stopping and starting inside it is the job of <code>repeatOnLifecycle</code>."}]},{"id":"callbackflow","title":"Wrapping callbacks","tagline":"callbackFlow turns a listener into a flow, and removes the listener when collecting stops.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a listener into a flow with <code>callbackFlow</code>","Send values from the listener with <code>trySend</code>","Remove the listener in <code>awaitClose</code>"],"remember":"In callbackFlow: subscribe a listener that calls trySend, then awaitClose { unsubscribe }.","terms":[{"term":"callbackFlow","def":"Makes a cold flow from a listener: subscribe it in the block, and unsubscribe in <code>awaitClose</code>."},{"term":"trySend","def":"Puts a value into a <code>callbackFlow</code> without waiting, so a plain listener can call it."},{"term":"awaitClose","def":"Keeps a <code>callbackFlow</code> open while someone collects; its lambda runs when collecting stops."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the listener call <code>trySend</code> and not <code>emit</code>?","options":["The listener is a plain function and can’t wait","<code>trySend</code> is faster","<code>emit</code> only works with numbers"],"a":0,"why":"The bottle calls the listener like any function. Only <code>trySend</code> works there, because it never waits."},{"q":"When does the lambda of <code>awaitClose</code> run?","options":["When collecting stops","Right after <code>subscribe</code>","After every sip"],"a":0,"why":"<code>awaitClose</code> waits while someone collects. When the collecting coroutine is cancelled, its lambda runs."},{"q":"Two screens collect <code>sipsOf(bottle)</code>. How many listeners does the bottle have?","options":["Two","One"],"a":0,"why":"<code>callbackFlow</code> is cold: each collector runs the block and subscribes its own listener."}]},{"id":"suspend-callback","title":"One-shot callbacks","tagline":"suspendCancellableCoroutine turns a single answer into a suspend function.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a one-time callback into a suspend function with <code>suspendCancellableCoroutine</code>","Hand back the answer with <code>resume</code>, or an error with <code>resumeWithException</code>","Stop the request with <code>invokeOnCancellation</code>"],"remember":"One answer: suspendCancellableCoroutine, resume once, and stop the request in invokeOnCancellation. Many answers: callbackFlow.","terms":[{"term":"suspendCancellableCoroutine","def":"Pauses the calling coroutine and hands you a continuation; what you pass to <code>resume</code> becomes its result."},{"term":"continuation","def":"A handle to a paused coroutine. <code>resume(value)</code> wakes it with a value, <code>resumeWithException(e)</code> makes it throw <code>e</code>."},{"term":"invokeOnCancellation","def":"Sets a lambda that runs if the waiting coroutine is cancelled, to stop the request."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A library calls your callback once with a result. Which tool fits?","options":["<code>suspendCancellableCoroutine</code>","<code>callbackFlow</code>","<code>MutableStateFlow</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One answer becomes a suspend function. A callback that fires many times becomes a <code>callbackFlow</code>."},{"q":"When does the lambda of <code>invokeOnCancellation</code> run?","options":["When the waiting coroutine is cancelled","After every <code>resume</code>","Right away"],"a":0,"why":"It is there for cancelling only, so you can stop the request."},{"q":"The callback calls <code>continuation.resumeWithException(e)</code>. What happens in the caller?","options":["The call throws <code>e</code>","It gets 0","It waits forever"],"a":0,"why":"The paused coroutine wakes up with the exception, which a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> around the call can handle."}]}]},{"id":"arch","title":"App architecture","description":"How a real Android app splits into layers, keeps its data straight, and wires itself together.","requires":["android","flow"],"file":"tracks/architecture.js","lessons":[{"id":"layers","title":"Layers: UI, domain, data","tagline":"Each layer knows only the one below it.","level":"beginner","remember":"UI → (domain) → data. Arrows point down, never up.","terms":[{"term":"UI layer","def":"The part of the app that draws the screen and holds screen state, made of Composables and ViewModels."},{"term":"Data layer","def":"The part of the app that owns the data, made of repositories and the data sources they call."},{"term":"Use case","def":"A small domain-layer class with one job that sits between ViewModels and repositories."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which dependency is allowed?","options":["<code>NewsViewModel</code> uses <code>NewsRepository</code>","<code>NewsRepository</code> uses <code>NewsViewModel</code>","Both, as long as it compiles"],"a":0,"why":"Dependencies point from the UI layer down to the data layer. A repository that knows a ViewModel can’t be reused or tested on its own."},{"q":"Does every app need a domain layer?","options":["Yes, every ViewModel must call a use case","No, add it when logic is shared or a ViewModel gets too big"],"a":1,"why":"Google marks the domain layer optional. A ViewModel may call a repository directly."},{"q":"A button is tapped. Who should start the network call?","options":["The Composable, with <code>api.getLatest()</code>","The ViewModel, through a use case or repository"],"a":1,"why":"The UI reports the tap. The ViewModel asks the layer below and turns the result into UiState."},{"q":"A repository parses a 5 MB JSON string before returning it. The ViewModel calls it from viewModelScope. Who should move the parsing off the main thread?","options":["The ViewModel, by wrapping every repository call in withContext","The repository, with withContext inside the function that parses","The composable, by calling it in LaunchedEffect"],"a":1,"why":"The class that does the slow work makes itself main-safe. Then every caller can use it from the main thread without thinking about it."}]},{"id":"udf","title":"ViewModel and UiState","tagline":"Events go up. State comes down.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Events up, state down. One immutable UiState; change it with copy().","terms":[{"term":"Unidirectional data flow","def":"A pattern where the UI sends events up to a state holder and only redraws from the state that comes back down."},{"term":"UiState","def":"An immutable data class that describes everything one screen shows at a given moment."},{"term":"UI event","def":"A message from the screen to the ViewModel that says what the user did, such as a tap on a filter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How should the screen switch the filter to \"Tech\"?","options":["<code>state.filter = \"Tech\"</code>","<code>onEvent(SelectFilter(\"Tech\"))</code>","Keep the filter in a <code>var</code> inside the Composable"],"a":1,"why":"The screen reports what happened. The ViewModel owns the state and makes the change."},{"q":"After <code>_uiState.update { it.copy(filter = \"Tech\") }</code>, what happened to the old state object?","options":["Its filter field changed to \"Tech\"","Nothing. A new object replaced it in the StateFlow."],"a":1,"why":"<code>copy()</code> builds a new object. With <code>val</code> fields the old one can’t change."},{"q":"Why expose <code>StateFlow</code> instead of the <code>MutableStateFlow</code>?","options":["So only the ViewModel can write state","StateFlow uses less memory"],"a":0,"why":"The screen gets a read-only view, so every change has to go through an event."},{"q":"The user picks the “Tech” filter, sends the app to the background, and Android kills the process. Where must the filter live to come back?","options":["In the UiState StateFlow","In SavedStateHandle","In a companion object"],"a":1,"why":"Process death destroys the ViewModel and everything in memory. SavedStateHandle is saved by the system and handed to the new ViewModel."},{"q":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\">init {\n    viewModelScope.launch {\n        val list = repo.observeNews().first()\n        _uiState.update { it.copy(items = list) }\n    }\n}</pre>A new article is saved to the database later. What does the screen show?","options":["The new article","The old list, it never updates","An empty list"],"a":1,"why":"The list was copied in once. Keep each input as a flow, combine them into UiState and use stateIn, so the state follows every change."}]},{"id":"mvvm-mvi","title":"MVVM vs MVI","tagline":"A function per action, or one stream of intents.","level":"intermediate","remember":"MVVM: a function per action. MVI: one intent type, one reducer, every change in one place.","terms":[{"term":"MVVM","def":"Model-View-ViewModel: the screen observes state from a ViewModel and calls one of its public functions for each user action."},{"term":"MVI","def":"Model-View-Intent: the screen sends every action as a value of one sealed type, and a single reducer turns state plus intent into the next state."},{"term":"MVI intent","def":"A value that describes one user action in MVI, such as CartIntent.Increment; it has nothing to do with android.content.Intent."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In MVI, how does the screen apply a coupon?","options":["<code>vm.applyCoupon(\"SAVE10\")</code>","<code>vm.onIntent(ApplyCoupon(\"SAVE10\"))</code>","<code>state.coupon = \"SAVE10\"</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Every action is a value sent to the one entry point. The reducer decides what it does to the state."},{"q":"What does an MVI reducer take and return?","options":["The current state and an intent in, the next state out","An intent in, a network result out","Nothing; it changes the state in place"],"a":0,"why":"It is a pure function. That is why a log of intents can rebuild the same state again."},{"q":"A settings screen has two toggles that never affect each other. What fits best?","options":["Plain MVVM functions","Full MVI with a reducer and an intent log"],"a":0,"why":"MVI’s extra types pay off when many actions touch shared state. Two independent toggles don’t need them."},{"q":"In MVI the user taps Checkout. Which order is right?","options":["Reducer returns an “order in progress” state → ViewModel launches the call → result comes back as OrderPlaced → reducer returns the final state","Reducer calls the API → reducer returns the final state","ViewModel calls the API → ViewModel sets the state directly → reducer logs it"],"a":0,"why":"A reducer stays pure, so the effect runs outside it. The result re-enters as one more intent, so every change still goes through the reducer."},{"q":"Typing “kot” sends QueryChanged three times. The answer for “k” arrives last and replaces the results for “kot”. What fixes it?","options":["Sort the answers by length","Cancel the previous search when a new query arrives, for example with mapLatest","Use tryEmit for the intents"],"a":1,"why":"Requests finish in any order. Cancelling the old one means only the latest query can ever write results."}]},{"id":"usecases","title":"Use cases and interactors","tagline":"One job per class, and only when the job is real.","level":"intermediate","remember":"A use case holds logic worth sharing. If it only forwards a call, delete it.","terms":[{"term":"Interactor","def":"Another name for a use case: a domain-layer class that runs one piece of business logic."},{"term":"Pass-through use case","def":"A use case whose invoke only forwards to one repository function and adds no logic of its own."},{"term":"zip","def":"A Flow operator that pairs the nth value of one flow with the nth value of another, so it waits until both emit again."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which use case is worth its own class?","options":["<code>invoke(id) = repo.article(id)</code>","One that combines news with followed topics and is used by two screens"],"a":1,"why":"It holds a real rule and removes duplicate code. The other only adds a hop."},{"q":"What does <code>operator fun invoke</code> give you?","options":["Callers can write <code>getFeed()</code> instead of <code>getFeed.execute()</code>","It runs the use case on a background thread","It lets Hilt inject the class"],"a":0,"why":"It is a calling convention. Threading comes from suspend functions and Flow; injection comes from <code>@Inject</code>."},{"q":"May a use case depend on another use case?","options":["Yes, and on repositories, but never on the UI layer","No, only on repositories"],"a":0,"why":"Use cases can build on each other. Like everything in the domain layer, they never point up at the UI."},{"q":"A use case combines 5,000 articles with followed topics and sorts them. The screen stutters each time a topic changes. What fixes it?","options":["Make the Room query suspend","Add flowOn(defaultDispatcher) at the end of the use case","Call stateIn with SharingStarted.Eagerly"],"a":1,"why":"The combine lambda runs where the flow is collected, which is the main thread through viewModelScope. flowOn moves everything above it to another dispatcher."},{"q":"Free users may keep three bookmarks, and two screens can save one. Where does the limit check go?","options":["In each ViewModel","In one SaveArticleUseCase that both screens call","In the Room DAO query"],"a":1,"why":"Two copies of a rule drift apart. One use case keeps the business rule in one place for every screen."}]},{"id":"ui-state-patterns","title":"Loading, error and empty states","tagline":"Draw every look the screen can have, and show messages once.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Draw loading, content, empty and error. Keep snackbar text in state and clear it once shown.","terms":[{"term":"Empty state","def":"The look a screen shows when loading worked but there is nothing to list, such as a short message and an action."},{"term":"One-off event","def":"Something the UI should react to once, like a snackbar or a navigation, which still has to survive rotation."},{"term":"Impossible state","def":"A combination of fields that should never happen together, such as isLoading = true with an old error still set."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Pull-to-refresh should keep the list visible while it reloads. Which model makes that easy?","options":["A sealed type where <code>Loading</code> replaces <code>Success</code>","A data class with <code>isLoading</code> next to <code>items</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Flags can hold the old items and a loading flag at once. A single sealed value can only be one of them."},{"q":"Why give an empty list its own look?","options":["So the screen says “No stories yet” instead of looking broken","Because <code>emptyList()</code> can’t be collected"],"a":0,"why":"Empty is a real result. Users need to see that loading worked and there is nothing there."},{"q":"A delete shows a snackbar. Where should its text live?","options":["In UiState, cleared when the UI calls <code>userMessageShown()</code>","In a <code>var</code> inside the Composable","In a Boolean <code>showSnackbar</code> that is never reset"],"a":0,"why":"State survives rotation and the acknowledgement clears it, so it shows once. A flag that is never reset shows again after every rotation."},{"q":"Most refreshes finish in about 150 ms, and the spinner blinks on and off. What fits best?","options":["Remove the spinner","Show the spinner only after a short delay, and cancel that when the load finishes","Always keep the spinner up for 2 s"],"a":1,"why":"Fast loads then show no spinner at all, and slow loads still show progress."},{"q":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\">val state = repo.stories()\n    .map { UiState.Content(it) }\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(5_000), UiState.Empty)</pre>What does the user see on every open?","options":["A spinner","A flash of “No stories yet” before the stories appear","Nothing until the stories arrive"],"a":1,"why":"The screen draws the initial value before the first result. Start with UiState.Loading instead of Empty."}]},{"id":"ssot","title":"Repository as source of truth","tagline":"The UI reads the database. The network writes to it.","level":"advanced","remember":"UI ← database ← network. The network writes to the database, never to the screen.","terms":[{"term":"Single source of truth","def":"The one place that owns a piece of data; everything else reads from it and sends changes through it."},{"term":"Offline-first","def":"A design where the app reads from local storage first and uses the network only to refresh it."},{"term":"Repository","def":"A data-layer class that hides where data comes from and gives the rest of the app one API for it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In an offline-first repository, where does the screen get its list?","options":["From the network response","From the database Flow","From whichever answers first"],"a":1,"why":"The database is the single source of truth. The screen observes it."},{"q":"<code>refresh()</code> fails with an <code>IOException</code>. What does the screen show?","options":["An empty list","The rows already in Room, plus an offline note","It crashes"],"a":1,"why":"The failure stops the write. Room still holds the old rows and its Flow keeps them on screen."},{"q":"New articles arrive from the API. How do they reach the screen?","options":["The repository writes them to Room, and the Flow emits","The repository passes them to the ViewModel directly"],"a":0,"why":"Writing to the database is the only path. The observing Flow does the rest."},{"q":"The user stars an article with no connection. Which order is right?","options":["Write the star to Room marked pending → Room’s Flow updates the screen → WorkManager sends it when online → clear the pending flag","Send to the server → write to Room → update the screen","Update the UiState → send to the server → write to Room"],"a":0,"why":"The database is the source of truth for writes too. The screen updates at once, and the background job syncs later with retries."},{"q":"A refresh calls <code>dao.upsertAll(serverRows)</code> while one row has a pending edit. What happens to the edit?","options":["It is kept, upsert skips pending rows","It is overwritten by the server’s older copy and never sent","The refresh fails"],"a":1,"why":"A plain upsert replaces the row and its pending flag. Merge in one transaction and skip server rows whose local copy is still pending."}]},{"id":"hilt","title":"Dependency injection with Hilt","tagline":"Ask for a ViewModel. Hilt builds everything under it.","level":"intermediate","remember":"@Inject constructor says how to build it; @Provides and @Binds fill the gaps; the scope says how many exist.","terms":[{"term":"@Inject","def":"An annotation on a constructor that tells Hilt how to build the class and what it needs."},{"term":"@Provides","def":"A Hilt module function that builds an object Hilt can’t construct itself, like Retrofit or a Room database."},{"term":"@Binds","def":"An abstract Hilt module function that tells Hilt which implementation to use for an interface."},{"term":"@InstallIn","def":"A Hilt annotation that puts a module in a component, such as SingletonComponent, which sets where its bindings are available."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>@Inject constructor(...)</code> tell Hilt?","options":["How to build this class, and what it needs","To keep one instance for the whole app","To build it lazily on a background thread"],"a":0,"why":"It marks the constructor Hilt calls. Scope is a separate annotation like <code>@Singleton</code>."},{"q":"When do you reach for <code>@Binds</code>?","options":["To build a third-party object like Retrofit","To tell Hilt which implementation to use for an interface"],"a":1,"why":"<code>@Binds</code> maps an interface to an implementation Hilt can already build. <code>@Provides</code> runs your code to build an object."},{"q":"Two screens each get their own <code>NewsViewModel</code>. <code>NewsRepository</code> is <code>@Singleton</code>. How many repositories exist?","options":["1","2"],"a":0,"why":"Each screen gets a new ViewModel, but both receive the same singleton repository."},{"q":"You add two @Provides functions that both return CoroutineDispatcher: one for IO, one for Default. What happens?","options":["Hilt picks the first one","The build fails with a duplicate binding error until you add qualifiers","Hilt injects a random one"],"a":1,"why":"Hilt finds a binding by type. A qualifier annotation becomes part of the key, so @IoDispatcher and @DefaultDispatcher are different bindings."},{"q":"Formatter is unscoped. A class calls <code>get()</code> twice on a <code>Lazy&lt;Formatter&gt;</code> and twice on a <code>Provider&lt;Formatter&gt;</code>. How many Formatters are built?","options":["1 from Lazy, 2 from Provider","2 from each","1 from each"],"a":0,"why":"A Lazy builds once and returns that same object after. A Provider asks Hilt on every get(), and an unscoped type is built each time."}]},{"id":"hilt-scopes","title":"Hilt components and scopes","tagline":"A scope ties one instance to one Android lifetime.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Scope state to the shortest lifetime that needs it; leave stateless classes unscoped.","terms":[{"term":"Hilt component","def":"A generated container of bindings tied to an Android lifetime, such as SingletonComponent or ViewModelComponent."},{"term":"Scope annotation","def":"An annotation like @Singleton or @ViewModelScoped that makes Hilt keep one instance per component instead of building a new one per request."},{"term":"@ActivityRetainedScoped","def":"The scope of ActivityRetainedComponent, which survives configuration changes and ends when the Activity finishes."},{"term":"Unscoped binding","def":"The default: Hilt builds a new instance for every injection point that asks for it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user rotates the phone. Which scoped object is built again?","options":["@Singleton","@ActivityRetainedScoped","@ViewModelScoped","@ActivityScoped"],"a":3,"why":"Rotation recreates the Activity object, so ActivityComponent is rebuilt. The retained and ViewModel components survive configuration changes."},{"q":"An unscoped <code>PriceFormatter</code> is injected into two classes. How many instances exist?","options":["1","2"],"a":1,"why":"Unscoped is the default, and it means a new instance per request."},{"q":"A <code>@HiltViewModel</code> asks for an <code>@ActivityScoped</code> helper. What happens?","options":["It gets the current Activity’s helper","The build fails","It gets a new helper each time"],"a":1,"why":"ViewModelComponent is not a child of ActivityComponent. The ViewModel outlives every Activity object, so Hilt refuses the binding at compile time."},{"q":"A module has <code>@InstallIn(ViewModelComponent::class)</code> and a <code>@Singleton @Provides</code> function. What happens?","options":["It works, @Singleton wins","The build fails because the scope does not match the component","The object is rebuilt on every injection"],"a":1,"why":"A scoped binding must use its component’s scope, here @ViewModelScoped. Hilt reports a mismatch at build time."},{"q":"A binding lives in a module installed in ActivityComponent, with no scope. A @HiltViewModel asks for it. What happens?","options":["It works, unscoped bindings are visible everywhere","The build fails, ViewModelComponent cannot see ActivityComponent bindings","It works only after rotation"],"a":1,"why":"A component sees bindings from itself and its parents. ActivityComponent is not a parent of ViewModelComponent, so the binding is invisible there."}]},{"id":"modularization-arch","title":"Modularizing by feature and layer","tagline":"Features talk through small api modules, and only :app sees everything.","level":"advanced","remember":"Impl depends on api, never on another impl. Only :app sees every impl and wires the navigation.","terms":[{"term":"Feature module","def":"A Gradle module that holds one screen or user flow, such as news or bookmarks, separate from other features."},{"term":"API module","def":"The small public half of a feature, with routes and interfaces that other features may depend on."},{"term":"Impl module","def":"The private half of a feature, with its screens, ViewModels and DI bindings, which only the app module depends on."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>:feature:bookmarks:impl</code> needs to open an article. What should it depend on?","options":["<code>:feature:news:impl</code>","<code>:feature:news:api</code>","<code>:app</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The api module has the route. The screen behind it stays private to news:impl."},{"q":"Who builds the NavHost that connects every feature?","options":["<code>:app</code>, which depends on every impl module","Each feature builds its own NavHost and they find each other at run time","<code>:core:ui</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only <code>:app</code> sees every impl, so it calls each feature’s <code>NavGraphBuilder</code> extension and passes callbacks between them."},{"q":"What belongs in an api module?","options":["Routes and small interfaces other features use","The screens and ViewModels","The Room database"],"a":0,"why":"Keep it small and stable. Everything that changes often lives in impl, where only the feature and <code>:app</code> recompile."},{"q":"Bookmarks needs to know which articles News has downloaded. Where do the pieces go?","options":["An interface in :feature:news:api, bound with @Binds in :feature:news:impl","Bookmarks depends on :feature:news:impl","Copy the download code into Bookmarks"],"a":0,"why":"Bookmarks injects the interface and compiles without the implementation. Hilt checks that the binding exists when it builds the graph in :app."},{"q":"Every feature depends on one :core:common. You change a date formatter in it. What does Gradle do?","options":["Recompiles only the features that format dates","Reruns the compile task of every feature","Recompiles only :core:common"],"a":1,"why":"Every feature depends on the changed module, so all of them compile again. Split core by purpose so a change reaches only its users."}]},{"id":"clean","title":"Clean Architecture in practice","tagline":"Dependencies point inward, and each ring keeps its own model.","level":"advanced","remember":"Source code points inward. Each ring has its own model, and mappers translate at the borders.","terms":[{"term":"Clean Architecture","def":"A way to arrange code in rings, with business rules in the center and frameworks outside, where dependencies only point inward."},{"term":"Dependency rule","def":"The Clean Architecture rule that inner code never names anything from an outer ring, such as a domain model importing Room."},{"term":"Domain model","def":"A plain Kotlin class, like Article, that describes business data without any database, network or UI annotations."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which import breaks the dependency rule in the domain module?","options":["<code>kotlinx.coroutines.flow.Flow</code>","<code>androidx.room.Entity</code>","<code>java.time.Instant</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Room is a framework in the outer ring. Flow and Instant are general-purpose types most teams allow in the domain."},{"q":"The API renames <code>\"title\"</code> to <code>\"headline\"</code>. With a DTO and mappers, what changes?","options":["The DTO: <code>@SerialName(\"headline\") val title</code>","Every model from the DTO to the UI","The domain model and every use case"],"a":0,"why":"The DTO absorbs the JSON shape. Its Kotlin property keeps its name, so the mapper, the domain and the UI don’t change."},{"q":"You are building a one-screen app with one API call. Is the full set of rings worth it?","options":["Yes, every app needs every ring and mapper","Usually not: one model and a repository are enough until the app grows"],"a":1,"why":"Each ring adds models and mappers to maintain. Start small and split when a second data source or team shows up."},{"q":"Where does <code>fun ArticleEntity.toDomain(): Article</code> belong?","options":["In the domain module, next to Article","In the data layer, next to ArticleEntity","In the UI layer"],"a":1,"why":"A mapper names both models, so it sits in the outer ring. In the domain it would make the center import the Room entity."},{"q":"The ViewModel catches Retrofit’s HttpException to tell a 401 from a 503. What is the problem, and what fixes it?","options":["Nothing, that is the right place","The UI now depends on Retrofit; map failures to a small DomainError in the data layer","HttpException is never thrown, catch IOException"],"a":1,"why":"Errors cross borders like models do. Translating them in the data layer keeps library types out of the UI."}]}]},{"id":"data","title":"Data and networking","description":"Build a Recipes app that keeps its data: settings in DataStore, recipes in a Room database, documents in files, then recipes from a server with Retrofit, kept offline, loaded in pages and updated live.","requires":["android","compose","coroutines","flow"],"file":"tracks/data.js","lessons":[{"id":"choose","title":"Where to keep data","tagline":"Settings, lists, documents and shared data each have their own place.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why data in a variable is gone after the app closes","Name four places an app can keep data","Pick the right place for a setting, a long list, a document and shared data"],"remember":"A few settings: DataStore. Many records: a database. One document: a file. Shared with other phones: a server. Memory forgets.","terms":[{"term":"storage","def":"The phone’s long-term memory. What an app writes there is still there after the app closes."},{"term":"DataStore","def":"A Jetpack library that keeps a few small settings, as keys and values, in one file."},{"term":"database","def":"A program that keeps many records of the same shape, and finds, sorts and changes them one at a time."},{"term":"server","def":"A computer on the internet that many phones talk to, so they can share data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada’s recipes were only in a <code>var</code>. She swipes the app away and opens it again. What does she see?","options":["An empty list","Her twelve recipes","The recipes from yesterday morning"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>var</code> lives in memory, and the memory is cleared when the app stops."},{"q":"Which place fits 300 recipes that the user sorts by cooking time?","options":["A database","DataStore","The app’s memory"],"a":0,"why":"Many records of the same shape, sorted and searched: that is what a database is for."},{"q":"Lin wants her friend Sam to see her recipes on his own phone. What does that need?","options":["A server","A file on Lin’s phone","DataStore on Lin’s phone"],"a":0,"why":"Everything on Lin’s phone stays on Lin’s phone. Both phones must talk to a server."}]},{"id":"datastore","title":"Small settings","tagline":"Remember the user’s choices after the app closes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep an on/off setting in DataStore","Read it as a <code>Flow</code> and show it on screen","Save a change from a button with <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>"],"remember":"preferencesDataStore once at the top level; read with data (a Flow), write with edit inside a coroutine.","terms":[{"term":"DataStore","def":"A Jetpack library that keeps small settings in a file. <code>data</code> reads them as a <code>Flow</code>; <code>edit</code> changes them."},{"term":"preferences key","def":"A typed name for one setting, like <code>booleanPreferencesKey(\"favorites_only\")</code>."},{"term":"edit","def":"A suspend function of DataStore: <code>store.edit { prefs -&gt; prefs[KEY] = value }</code> changes values and writes the file."},{"term":"rememberCoroutineScope","def":"Gives a composable its own scope. <code>scope.launch { }</code> starts a coroutine from a click; it is cancelled when the composable leaves the screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does <code>val Context.dataStore by preferencesDataStore(name = \"settings\")</code> go?","options":["Once, at the top level of a Kotlin file","Inside the composable that uses it","Inside every Activity"],"a":0,"why":"At the top level it is made once, so the app has exactly one store for the file."},{"q":"What does <code>store.data</code> give you?","options":["A <code>Flow</code> that emits the saved values now and after every change","The values once, then nothing","A list of every change ever made"],"a":0,"why":"That is why the screen updates by itself after <code>edit</code>."},{"q":"Why does the button use <code>scope.launch</code>?","options":["<code>edit</code> is a suspend function, and <code>onClick</code> is not a coroutine","It makes the button draw faster","Compose needs it to show <code>Text</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A suspend function runs only in a coroutine or another suspend function."}]},{"id":"room","title":"A local database","tagline":"Describe a table of recipes, and the questions you ask it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a table, a row, a primary key and a query are","Describe the recipes table with <code>@Entity</code>","Write its reads and writes as a DAO with <code>@Query</code> and <code>@Insert</code>"],"remember":"A table has rows (records) and columns (properties); @Entity describes the table, @Dao holds its queries.","terms":[{"term":"table","def":"Rows and columns in a database. Each row is one record (a recipe); each column is one property (its title)."},{"term":"primary key","def":"A column whose value no other row has, like the id. <code>@PrimaryKey</code> marks it."},{"term":"query","def":"A question to the database, written in SQL, like <code>\"SELECT * FROM recipes ORDER BY minutes\"</code>."},{"term":"DAO","def":"Data access object: an interface marked <code>@Dao</code> with the functions that read and write the tables."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In the recipes table, what is one row?","options":["One recipe","One property of every recipe, like all the titles","The whole database"],"a":0,"why":"Rows are records; columns are properties."},{"q":"What does <code>\"SELECT * FROM recipes ORDER BY minutes\"</code> give back?","options":["Every recipe, shortest cooking time first","Only the minutes column","The recipe that takes longest"],"a":0,"why":"<code>*</code> means every column; <code>ORDER BY minutes</code> sorts the rows, smallest first."},{"q":"Why are <code>getAll</code> and <code>insert</code> <code>suspend</code> functions?","options":["Reading and writing the file takes time, so Room does it on a background thread while the screen keeps working","Room needs them to be private","So they run only once"],"a":0,"why":"The caller waits in a coroutine; the screen does not freeze."}]},{"id":"room-setup","title":"Opening the database","tagline":"Make one database for the whole app, and put its recipes on screen.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List the tables of a database with <code>@Database</code>","Open it once for the whole app with <code>Room.databaseBuilder</code>","Add Room to the build, with a saved schema for every version"],"remember":"@Database lists the tables and the version; open it once for the whole app with Room.databaseBuilder; the Room plugin saves a schema per version.","terms":[{"term":"database class","def":"An <code>abstract</code> class marked <code>@Database</code> that extends <code>RoomDatabase</code>. It lists the tables and hands out the DAOs."},{"term":"version","def":"The number of the database’s shape in <code>@Database(..., version = 1)</code>. It goes up when a table changes."},{"term":"schema","def":"The description of a database: its tables, their columns and types. The Room plugin saves one file per version."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the square brackets in <code>entities = [Recipe::class]</code> hold?","options":["The list of tables in the database","The rows of the recipes table","The DAO functions"],"a":0,"why":"Each entity listed there becomes one table."},{"q":"Why does the app keep one database in <code>RecipesDb</code>?","options":["Opening a database is expensive, and the app needs only one","Room allows only one table per database","So the database is deleted when the app closes"],"a":0,"why":"One instance for the whole life of the app, as the Room guide says."},{"q":"What does the Room Gradle plugin save at every build?","options":["A schema file describing the tables of this version","A copy of every recipe","The SQL of every query you ran"],"a":0,"why":"You need the schema files of the old and new version when the database changes."}]},{"id":"room-flow","title":"Live queries","tagline":"Let the list update itself whenever the table changes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Return a <code>Flow</code> from a DAO query","Show it on screen with <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code>","Change a row with <code>@Update</code> and watch the list follow"],"remember":"A DAO query that returns Flow emits again after every change to its table; the screen only collects, the clicks only write.","terms":[{"term":"observable query","def":"A DAO function that returns a <code>Flow</code>. Room emits its result, and emits again after every change to the table."},{"term":"Update","def":"<code>@Update</code> marks a DAO function that replaces the row with the same primary key."}],"quiz":[{"q":"After <code>dao.insert(...)</code>, what must a screen that collects <code>getAll()</code> do?","options":["Nothing: Room emits the new list","Call <code>getAll()</code> again","Restart the app"],"a":0,"why":"The table changed, so Room runs the query again and emits."},{"q":"Why is <code>getAll()</code> no longer a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["It returns a Flow at once; the query runs while the Flow is collected","Flow functions must run on the main thread","Room doesn’t allow suspend functions"],"a":0,"why":"Making the Flow is quick. The work happens during <code>collect</code>."},{"q":"How does <code>@Update</code> find the row to change?","options":["By its primary key, the id","By its title","By its place on the screen"],"a":0,"why":"That is why <code>copy</code> keeps the id and changes only <code>favorite</code>."}]},{"id":"relations","title":"Related tables","tagline":"Give every recipe its steps, and load both together.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Link two tables with a column that holds the other row’s id","Load a recipe with its steps using <code>@Embedded</code> and <code>@Relation</code>","Explain why the query needs <code>@Transaction</code>"],"remember":"The child table keeps the parent’s id; @Embedded plus @Relation builds the nested object; mark the query @Transaction.","terms":[{"term":"one-to-many relation","def":"One row in a table (a recipe) belongs with many rows in another (its steps), linked by a column like <code>recipeId</code>."},{"term":"Embedded","def":"<code>@Embedded</code> puts all the columns of an entity into a plain class, as one property."},{"term":"Relation","def":"<code>@Relation(parentColumns = [...], entityColumns = [...])</code> fills a list with the rows of another table whose column matches."},{"term":"Transaction","def":"<code>@Transaction</code> makes all the queries of one DAO function act as a single step on the database."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is the link between a step and its recipe stored?","options":["In the steps table, in the column <code>recipeId</code>","In the recipes table, as a list of steps","In DataStore"],"a":0,"why":"The “many” side keeps the id of the “one” side."},{"q":"In <code>WHERE id = :id</code>, what is <code>:id</code>?","options":["The function’s parameter <code>id</code>, filled in by Room","A column named “:id”","A comment"],"a":0,"why":"A colon and a parameter name put that value into the query."},{"q":"Why does <code>getWithSteps</code> need <code>@Transaction</code>?","options":["Room runs two queries, and both must read the same moment of the database","Without it the steps come back in the wrong order","It makes the query faster"],"a":0,"why":"One query for the recipe, one for its steps: <code>@Transaction</code> keeps them together."}]},{"id":"migrations","title":"Changing the database","tagline":"Update the app without losing anyone’s recipes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a changed table needs a new version number","Let Room write a simple migration with <code>AutoMigration</code>","Write a migration yourself with <code>Migration</code>"],"remember":"Change a table: raise version, and give Room a migration, AutoMigration for simple changes or Migration with your own SQL.","terms":[{"term":"migration","def":"The steps that change a database file from one version to the next while keeping its rows."},{"term":"schema","def":"The description of a database: its tables, their columns and types. Room saves one file per version."},{"term":"AutoMigration","def":"<code>AutoMigration(from = 1, to = 2)</code> in <code>@Database</code>: Room compares the two schemas and writes the migration."},{"term":"Migration","def":"A class whose <code>migrate</code> function runs your own SQL to go from one version to another."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When must you raise <code>version</code>?","options":["Whenever a table or column changes","At every release of the app","Only when you add a whole new table"],"a":0,"why":"Any change to the shape of the database needs a new version and a migration."},{"q":"Why does the new column need <code>@ColumnInfo(defaultValue = \"0\")</code>, and not only <code>= 0</code> in Kotlin?","options":["The rows already in the file get their value from the database, and a Kotlin default lives only in your code","Kotlin defaults are not allowed in entities","It makes the column the primary key"],"a":0,"why":"The migration runs in the database; it never sees your Kotlin code."},{"q":"What does <code>AutoMigration</code> compare to write the migration?","options":["The saved schema files of the two versions","The recipes on the user’s phone","The old and the new app"],"a":0,"why":"That is why the schema files must exist for both versions."}]},{"id":"files","title":"Files","tagline":"Write a whole document into your app’s own folder, and read it back.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Find your app’s private folders with <code>filesDir</code> and <code>cacheDir</code>","Write and read a text file away from the main thread","Say what happens to those files on uninstall and when space runs low"],"remember":"filesDir keeps files until uninstall; cacheDir may be emptied by Android; read and write inside withContext(Dispatchers.IO).","terms":[{"term":"filesDir","def":"A property of every <code>Context</code>: the app’s private folder for files it keeps. Deleted on uninstall."},{"term":"cacheDir","def":"The app’s private folder for files it can make again. Android may delete them when space runs low."},{"term":"readText","def":"<code>file.readText()</code> reads the whole file as one <code>String</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which apps can read <code>File(context.filesDir, \"recipes.txt\")</code>?","options":["Only your app","Every app with a storage permission","Every app"],"a":0,"why":"<code>filesDir</code> is private to your app."},{"q":"What happens to files in <code>filesDir</code> when the user uninstalls the app?","options":["They are deleted","They stay for the next install","They move to Downloads"],"a":0,"why":"The whole private folder goes with the app."},{"q":"Why does <code>writeText</code> run inside <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code>?","options":["Writing to disk can be slow, and the main thread must keep drawing the screen","<code>writeText</code> works only there","It keeps the file private"],"a":0,"why":"On the main thread a slow write would freeze the screen."}]},{"id":"share-files","title":"Sharing files","tagline":"Hand one file to another app without opening your whole folder.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why another app can’t open a file in your <code>filesDir</code>","Set up <code>FileProvider</code> and get a <code>content://</code> address for one file","Share the file with <code>ACTION_SEND</code> and a temporary read permission"],"remember":"FileProvider turns one file in a listed folder into a content:// Uri; send it with ACTION_SEND and FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION.","terms":[{"term":"FileProvider","def":"A ready-made provider from Jetpack’s core library that gives other apps a <code>content://</code> Uri for one of your files."},{"term":"authority","def":"The unique name of a provider, like <code>com.example.recipes.fileprovider</code>. It is the first part of its Uris."},{"term":"content Uri","def":"An address like <code>content://…/exports/recipes.txt</code> that names a file through a provider, without its real place."},{"term":"FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION","def":"A flag on an Intent that lets the receiving app read the Uri it carries, until that app’s task is finished."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>file_paths.xml</code> decide?","options":["Which folders FileProvider may give Uris for","Where Room keeps the database","Which apps appear in the share sheet"],"a":0,"why":"Only files inside the listed folders can be shared."},{"q":"How long can the mail app read the file?","options":["Until its task is finished: the permission is temporary","Forever","Until your app closes"],"a":0,"why":"A permission from the Intent’s flag ends by itself."},{"q":"Why not send the file’s real path?","options":["Other apps can’t open your <code>filesDir</code>, so the path is no use to them","Paths are too long for an Intent","Intents can’t carry text"],"a":0,"why":"FileProvider opens the file for them, one Uri at a time."}]},{"id":"http-basics","title":"Talking to a server","tagline":"What a server, an HTTP request and JSON are, and how JSON becomes a Kotlin object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a server, an HTTP request and a response are","Read JSON: lists, objects and their keys","Turn JSON text into a Kotlin object with <code>@Serializable</code> and <code>Json</code>"],"remember":"A server answers HTTP requests with a status code and a JSON body; @Serializable and Json.decodeFromString turn that JSON into Kotlin objects.","terms":[{"term":"server","def":"A computer on the internet that keeps data and answers requests from apps."},{"term":"HTTP","def":"How apps and servers talk. A request has a method (<code>GET</code>, …) and a URL; a response has a status code (200 means OK) and a body."},{"term":"JSON","def":"A text format for data: <code>[ ]</code> holds a list, <code>{ }</code> holds an object made of <code>\"key\": value</code> pairs."},{"term":"DTO","def":"Data transfer object: a class with the shape the server sends, like <code>RecipeDto</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server answers with status code 200. What does that mean?","options":["OK: the body has what you asked for","The server is busy","200 recipes are on the way"],"a":0,"why":"200 is the code for success. The data is in the body."},{"q":"In JSON, what do square brackets <code>[ ]</code> hold?","options":["A list","One object","A comment"],"a":0,"why":"Square brackets hold a list; curly braces hold one object."},{"q":"Reading <code>{\"id\":1,\"title\":\"Tomato soup\",\"minutes\":25}</code>, which property does the key <code>\"title\"</code> fill?","options":["<code>title</code>, the property with the same name","The first property, <code>id</code>","None: keys are only labels"],"a":0,"why":"Each key fills the property with the same name."}]},{"id":"retrofit","title":"Calling a server with Retrofit","tagline":"Describe the server’s requests as an interface, and let Retrofit send them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe a server call as a Retrofit interface with <code>@GET</code>","Build one <code>RecipeApi</code> with a base URL and a JSON converter","Call it from <code>LaunchedEffect</code> and show the recipes"],"remember":"Retrofit turns an interface into HTTP requests: @GET names the path, baseUrl ends with /, and the converter reads the JSON.","terms":[{"term":"Retrofit","def":"A library that turns an interface with <code>@GET</code> and friends into HTTP requests."},{"term":"base URL","def":"The start of every address, like <code>https://recipes.example.com/</code>. Retrofit puts each path after it, so it must end with <code>/</code>."},{"term":"converter","def":"The part of Retrofit that turns a JSON body into Kotlin objects, here with <code>Json</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is <code>getRecipes()</code> a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["So the coroutine can wait for the server without freezing the screen","So the request travels faster","Because it returns a list"],"a":0,"why":"A server answer can take seconds. A suspend function lets the coroutine wait while the screen keeps working."},{"q":"What does <code>create(RecipeApi::class.java)</code> do?","options":["Writes the code behind the interface and gives you an object to call","Sends the first request","Downloads the recipes"],"a":0,"why":"You only write the interface. Retrofit makes the object that sends the requests."},{"q":"Does the user see a dialog for the <code>INTERNET</code> permission?","options":["No: it is granted when the app is installed","Yes, the first time the app goes online","Yes, every time the app opens"],"a":0,"why":"It is a normal permission. One line in the manifest is enough."}]},{"id":"net","title":"Requests and errors","tagline":"No signal, a broken server, odd JSON: show a message, not a crash.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three ways a server call fails","Catch them and show a message instead of crashing","Let the user try again"],"remember":"Catch IOException (no connection), HttpException (a failed status code) and SerializationException (JSON that doesn’t fit), and show each as a state.","terms":[{"term":"IOException","def":"Thrown when the request gets no answer: no connection, server not found, or no answer in time."},{"term":"HttpException","def":"Thrown by Retrofit when the server answers with a status code outside the 200s. <code>e.code()</code> gives the code."},{"term":"SerializationException","def":"Thrown when the JSON doesn’t fit the class, for example a missing or unknown key."},{"term":"status code","def":"The number at the start of a response: 200s success, 400s a wrong request (404 not found), 500s a server problem."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server answers 404. What does the suspend Retrofit call do?","options":["Throws an <code>HttpException</code>","Throws an <code>IOException</code>","Returns an empty list"],"a":0,"why":"An answer arrived, but its status code isn’t in the 200s, so Retrofit throws an <code>HttpException</code>."},{"q":"Which status codes mean success?","options":["The 200s","The 400s","The 500s"],"a":0,"why":"200s are success, 400s a wrong request, 500s a server problem."},{"q":"Ada taps Try again, which runs <code>attempt++</code>. Why does the call run again?","options":["<code>attempt</code> is the key of <code>LaunchedEffect</code>, and a new key restarts its block","The button calls <code>getRecipes()</code> itself","Every recomposition restarts <code>LaunchedEffect</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A new key stops the old block and starts it again from the top."}]},{"id":"auth","title":"Signing in","tagline":"Sign in once, then let a short-lived token prove who you are.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what an access token and a refresh token are","Send the token with every request and refresh it when it runs out","Keep tokens where other apps can’t read them"],"remember":"Sign in once; send the access token in the Authorization header; on 401 refresh once and repeat; keep tokens in DataStore, never the password.","terms":[{"term":"token","def":"A long random-looking text the server hands out after sign-in. It proves who is asking, without the password."},{"term":"access token","def":"The short-lived token sent with every request, in the header <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>."},{"term":"refresh token","def":"A longer-lived token that only gets a new access token when the old one has run out."},{"term":"header","def":"An extra named line in a request or response, like <code>Authorization</code>. <code>@Header</code> fills one from a parameter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the app keep a token instead of the password?","options":["A token soon runs out and only works for this server; a password may open many accounts","Tokens are shorter to type","Servers can’t read passwords"],"a":0,"why":"A leaked token does little harm for a short time. A leaked password does much more."},{"q":"The refresh request gets a 401 too. What now?","options":["Show the sign-in screen","Refresh again and again","Keep using the old access token"],"a":0,"why":"Both tokens are no good any more, so Ada has to sign in again."},{"q":"Where is a good place to keep the tokens?","options":["DataStore, in the app’s own storage","In the code, as a <code>const val</code>","In a Logcat message"],"a":0,"why":"Android lets only your app read its own storage. Code and logs can be read by others."}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching","tagline":"Show the saved recipes at once, and let the network only refresh them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show saved recipes at once, even offline","Make the Room table the one place the screen reads from","Decide when the saved copy is too old"],"remember":"The screen reads only from Room; the network only refreshes Room, and only when the copy is too old or the user asks.","terms":[{"term":"cache","def":"A copy of data kept close by, here server recipes saved in Room, so they can be shown without asking again."},{"term":"offline first","def":"The screen reads only from the local database; the network only refreshes that database."},{"term":"@Upsert","def":"A Room DAO function that updates the row with the same primary key, or inserts the row if there is none."},{"term":"cache invalidation","def":"Deciding when the saved copy is too old and must be refreshed, for example after 15 minutes or when the user asks."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In an offline-first app, where does the recipe screen read its list from?","options":["The Room table","The server, and Room only when offline","Both, one after the other"],"a":0,"why":"One source for the screen: the table. The network only writes into it."},{"q":"A refresh brings a recipe whose id is already in the table. What does <code>@Upsert</code> do?","options":["Updates that row","Adds a second row with the same id","Throws an error"],"a":0,"why":"Same primary key: update. A new key: insert."},{"q":"The last refresh was 5 minutes ago and <code>MAX_AGE</code> is 15 minutes. The screen opens. What happens?","options":["No call: the saved recipes are shown","A full refresh","The table is emptied first"],"a":0,"why":"The copy is younger than <code>MAX_AGE</code>, so <code>refreshRecipes</code> returns early."}]},{"id":"paging","title":"Loading in pages","tagline":"Twenty thousand recipes, twenty at a time, as the user scrolls.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a long list loads in pages","Write a <code>PagingSource</code> that loads one page from the server","Show the pages in a <code>LazyColumn</code> with <code>collectAsLazyPagingItems</code>"],"remember":"A PagingSource loads one page per key and returns nextKey (null at the end); Pager plus collectAsLazyPagingItems loads more as the user scrolls.","terms":[{"term":"page","def":"One slice of a long list that the server sends at a time, such as recipes 21 to 40."},{"term":"PagingSource","def":"Your class that loads one page for a key and says which keys come before and after it."},{"term":"LoadResult","def":"What <code>load</code> returns: <code>LoadResult.Page(data, prevKey, nextKey)</code> on success, <code>LoadResult.Error(e)</code> on failure."},{"term":"Pager","def":"Makes a Flow of pages from a <code>PagingConfig</code> and a PagingSource. <code>collectAsLazyPagingItems()</code> shows it in a <code>LazyColumn</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>nextKey = null</code> tell Paging?","options":["There are no more pages","Load page 1 again","This page failed"],"a":0,"why":"<code>null</code> means nothing comes after this page, so Paging stops loading."},{"q":"In <code>PagingSource&lt;Int, RecipeDto&gt;</code>, what is the <code>Int</code>?","options":["The key that names a page, here the page number","How many rows a page has","The recipe id"],"a":0,"why":"The first type is the key, the second the item."},{"q":"Page 4 fails because the signal is gone. What should <code>load</code> return?","options":["<code>LoadResult.Error(e)</code>","An empty <code>LoadResult.Page</code>","Nothing: let the exception crash the app"],"a":0,"why":"An error result keeps the loaded rows and lets <code>retry()</code> load that page again. An empty page would end the list."}]},{"id":"realtime","title":"Live updates","tagline":"Keep a connection open and let the server tell you when something changes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why asking again and again is slow and wasteful","Turn WebSocket messages into a Flow with <code>callbackFlow</code>","Save each live change into Room so the screen updates by itself"],"remember":"A WebSocket stays open so the server can send changes at once; wrap it in callbackFlow, write each change into Room, and retry when it drops.","terms":[{"term":"polling","def":"Asking the server again and again, at fixed times, whether anything changed."},{"term":"WebSocket","def":"A connection that stays open, so the app and the server can each send messages at any time."},{"term":"server-sent events","def":"SSE: a response that never ends, in which the server writes one event after another. One way only, server to app."},{"term":"OkHttp","def":"The library Retrofit uses to send requests. It also opens WebSockets with <code>newWebSocket</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is polling every 10 seconds a poor fit for live changes?","options":["Most requests find nothing new, they cost battery, and changes still arrive up to 10 s late","Servers can’t answer that often","HTTP allows only one request a minute"],"a":0,"why":"Polling pays for every request but only learns about a change on the next one."},{"q":"An app only receives live scores and never sends anything. Which is the lighter choice?","options":["Server-sent events","A WebSocket","Polling every second"],"a":0,"why":"SSE is one way, server to app, over a normal HTTP response. A WebSocket is for both directions."},{"q":"A live change is upserted into Room. How does the recipe screen update?","options":["Room’s Flow sends the new list, and the screen redraws","The WebSocket code redraws the screen","Only after the app restarts"],"a":0,"why":"The screen reads Room. Any write to the table, from a refresh or a live message, reaches it through the Flow."}]}]},{"id":"views","title":"Views","description":"Read and change older screens built with Views: XML layouts, RecyclerView, Fragments, and mixing them with Compose.","requires":["android","compose"],"file":"tracks/views.js","lessons":[{"id":"xml","title":"XML layouts","tagline":"How older screens are written down in a file, then built as objects.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read a layout file from <code>res/layout</code>","Build its views with view binding and show them","Change a view that is already on screen"],"remember":"A layout file describes views; inflate builds them; setContentView(binding.root) shows them; you change a view instead of drawing it again.","terms":[{"term":"layout file","def":"An XML file in <code>res/layout</code> that describes the views of a screen as nested tags."},{"term":"inflate","def":"Read a layout file and build the view objects it describes."},{"term":"view binding","def":"A build feature that makes a class per layout, like <code>ActivityProfileBinding</code>, with one property for each view that has an id."},{"term":"findViewById","def":"The older way to get a view: look it up by id, like <code>findViewById(R.id.title)</code>. Mistakes only show up when the app runs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which views in <code>activity_profile.xml</code> get a property in <code>ActivityProfileBinding</code>?","options":["Only views that have an <code>android:id</code>","Every view in the file","Only the outer view"],"a":0,"why":"The binding has one property per view with an id, plus <code>root</code> for the outer view."},{"q":"You rename <code>@+id/title</code> to <code>@+id/heading</code>. What happens to <code>binding.title.text = \"Hi\"</code>?","options":["It stops compiling: <code>Unresolved reference 'title'.</code>","The app crashes when it runs that line","It still works"],"a":0,"why":"The binding class is made again with a <code>heading</code> property, so <code>title</code> no longer exists."},{"q":"A <code>TextView</code> is on screen. How do you show new text in it?","options":["<code>binding.title.text = \"New\"</code>","Inflate the layout again","Call <code>Text(\"New\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A view is an object that stays on screen. You change its properties, and it updates itself."}]},{"id":"passes","title":"Measure, layout, draw","tagline":"Three passes turn a tree of views into pixels.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three passes and what each one decides","Choose between <code>invalidate()</code> and <code>requestLayout()</code>","Read a small custom view"],"remember":"Measure (how big), layout (where), draw (paint). Look changed: invalidate(). Size changed: requestLayout().","terms":[{"term":"measure, layout, draw","def":"The three passes of a frame: work out every view’s size, then its position, then paint it."},{"term":"invalidate","def":"<code>invalidate()</code> asks for the view to be drawn again in the next frame, without measuring or placing it again."},{"term":"requestLayout","def":"<code>requestLayout()</code> asks for a new measure and layout pass in the next frame, because a size or position may change."},{"term":"custom view","def":"Your own class that extends <code>View</code> and paints itself in <code>onDraw</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In which order do the passes run?","options":["Measure, layout, draw","Draw, measure, layout","Layout, measure, draw"],"a":0,"why":"Sizes come first, positions use those sizes, and drawing uses the final size and position."},{"q":"A custom view changes only its color. Which call fits?","options":["<code>invalidate()</code>","<code>requestLayout()</code>","Neither: nothing is needed"],"a":0,"why":"The size and position stay the same, so a redraw is enough. Without any call, the old color stays on screen."},{"q":"You set <code>binding.title.text</code> on a built-in <code>TextView</code>. Do you call <code>requestLayout()</code> yourself?","options":["No: TextView asks for the passes it needs","Yes, every time","Only when the text gets longer"],"a":0,"why":"Built-in views ask for their own passes when their properties change. You only do it in your own custom views."}]},{"id":"recycler","title":"RecyclerView","tagline":"A long list built from a handful of reused rows.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a list reuses its row views","Write an adapter with <code>onCreateViewHolder</code> and <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","Avoid rows that show the wrong data after scrolling"],"remember":"RecyclerView makes a screenful of rows and refills them. onBindViewHolder sets every part of the row, every time.","terms":[{"term":"RecyclerView","def":"A view that shows a long list by making only a screenful of rows and reusing them as you scroll."},{"term":"adapter","def":"The class that connects your data to RecyclerView: it makes rows (<code>onCreateViewHolder</code>), fills them (<code>onBindViewHolder</code>) and counts items (<code>getItemCount</code>)."},{"term":"ViewHolder","def":"An object that holds one row view so it can be filled again and again."},{"term":"layout manager","def":"The part that places the rows, like <code>LinearLayoutManager</code> for one row under another."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which part decides that rows go one under another?","options":["The layout manager","The adapter","The ViewHolder"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LinearLayoutManager</code> places the rows. The adapter only makes and fills them."},{"q":"When a reused row is shown for a new contact, which function runs for it?","options":["Only <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","<code>onCreateViewHolder</code>, then <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","Only <code>onCreateViewHolder</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The row already exists, so it only needs to be filled with the new contact."},{"q":"The bind code is <code>if (contact.starred) { holder.binding.star.isVisible = true }</code>. What goes wrong after scrolling?","options":["Stars show up on contacts who are not starred","Starred contacts lose their star","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"A reused row keeps the star from its last contact, because nothing ever hides it. Write <code>star.isVisible = contact.starred</code>."}]},{"id":"view-types","title":"Many item types","tagline":"Headers and rows in one list, each with its own layout.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Give each kind of row its own view type","Create and fill the right holder for each kind","Explain why holders of different kinds are never mixed"],"remember":"One kind of row, one view type. getItemViewType names it, onCreateViewHolder builds it, and holders are only reused within their kind.","terms":[{"term":"view type","def":"An <code>Int</code> that names one kind of row, like 0 for a header and 1 for a person. Each kind has its own layout and its own spare holders."},{"term":"getItemViewType","def":"The adapter function that returns the view type for a position. If you don’t override it, every position gets 0."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>getItemViewType</code> return if you don’t override it?","options":["0 for every position","The position","A different number for each layout"],"a":0,"why":"By default the adapter assumes one kind of row. Override it once rows look different."},{"q":"Only a header holder is free, and a person row scrolls in. What does RecyclerView do?","options":["Makes a new person holder","Fills the header holder as a person","Shows an empty row"],"a":0,"why":"Holders are reused only within their view type, so the bind code never gets a holder of the wrong kind."},{"q":"Inside <code>if (holder is HeaderHolder) { }</code>, why does <code>holder.binding.letter</code> work?","options":["After the <code>is</code> check, Kotlin knows <code>holder</code> is a HeaderHolder","Every holder has a <code>letter</code>","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"It is a smart cast: inside that branch, Kotlin treats <code>holder</code> as a HeaderHolder."}]},{"id":"diff","title":"Efficient updates","tagline":"Hand over a new list, and only the rows that changed are redrawn.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell a <code>ListAdapter</code> how to compare items with <code>DiffUtil</code>","Update a list with <code>submitList</code>","Tell “the same item” from “the same contents”"],"remember":"Same id: same item. Same fields: same contents. Give submitList a new list every time.","terms":[{"term":"ListAdapter","def":"An adapter that keeps the list itself and, when you call <code>submitList</code>, updates only the rows that changed."},{"term":"DiffUtil","def":"The tool that compares an old and a new list and works out the inserts, removals, moves and changes. You answer its questions in a <code>DiffUtil.ItemCallback</code>."},{"term":"submitList","def":"<code>submitList(newList)</code> hands a ListAdapter its next list to compare and show."},{"term":"notifyDataSetChanged","def":"The old, blunt update: “everything may have changed”. Every visible row is filled again, with no animation."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A contact changes name but keeps its id. What does DiffUtil report?","options":["A change at that position","A removal and an insertion","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Same id, so the same item. Different contents, so that one row is filled again."},{"q":"Why avoid <code>notifyDataSetChanged()</code> when only one contact changed?","options":["It fills every row again and plays no animation","It crashes on long lists","It deletes the list"],"a":0,"why":"RecyclerView can’t tell what changed, so it treats everything as changed."},{"q":"You add Dee to the same <code>mutableListOf</code> you submitted before, then call <code>submitList</code> with that same list. What happens?","options":["Nothing: it is the same list object","Dee’s row slides in","The whole list is filled again"],"a":0,"why":"ListAdapter sees the very same list it already has, so it has nothing to compare. Submit a new list instead."}]},{"id":"fragments","title":"Fragments","tagline":"A piece of a screen whose view can come and go while it stays.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say what a Fragment is and where you meet one","Tell the Fragment’s lifecycle from its view’s lifecycle","Clear a binding in <code>onDestroyView</code>"],"remember":"A Fragment can outlive its view. Build the binding in onViewCreated and set it to null in onDestroyView.","terms":[{"term":"Fragment","def":"A piece of UI with its own layout and lifecycle, shown inside an Activity. Older apps often build each screen as one."},{"term":"view lifecycle","def":"The shorter life of a Fragment’s view, from <code>onCreateView</code> to <code>onDestroyView</code>. It can end and start again while the Fragment stays."},{"term":"onDestroyView","def":"The callback where a Fragment’s view is torn down, for example when it goes onto the back stack. Clear view references here."},{"term":"addToBackStack","def":"Records a Fragment change so that pressing Back undoes it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The inbox is replaced by a message, with <code>addToBackStack</code>. What happens to the inbox?","options":["Its view is destroyed; the Fragment stays","The Fragment is destroyed completely","Nothing: it stays resumed under the message"],"a":0,"why":"It gets <code>onDestroyView</code> but not <code>onDestroy</code>. Back gives it a new view."},{"q":"Which callback runs again on the same Fragment when you press Back to it?","options":["<code>onViewCreated</code>","<code>onCreate</code>","<code>onDestroy</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The Fragment object was kept, so only its view lifecycle starts again: <code>onCreateView</code>, then <code>onViewCreated</code>."},{"q":"Why set <code>_binding = null</code> in <code>onDestroyView</code>?","options":["So a waiting Fragment doesn’t keep its old view in memory","Because the app won’t compile otherwise","To make Back faster"],"a":0,"why":"The binding points at every view in the old layout. While the Fragment waits on the back stack, those views are useless."}]},{"id":"viewpager","title":"Paging screens","tagline":"Swipe between pages, and know which one is really on screen.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show swipeable Fragment pages with <code>ViewPager2</code>","Write a <code>FragmentStateAdapter</code>","React when a page is the one on screen"],"remember":"ViewPager2 plus a FragmentStateAdapter (getItemCount, createFragment). Only the page on screen is resumed.","terms":[{"term":"ViewPager2","def":"A view that shows pages the user swipes between. It is built on RecyclerView."},{"term":"FragmentStateAdapter","def":"The adapter for Fragment pages: <code>getItemCount</code> says how many, <code>createFragment</code> makes the Fragment for a page."},{"term":"offscreenPageLimit","def":"How many pages the pager keeps built on each side of the current page."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Sports is on screen, with News and Weather built on each side. Which page is resumed?","options":["Only Sports","All three","None: pager pages stop at onStart"],"a":0,"why":"Neighbour pages stop at <code>onStart</code>. Only the current page gets <code>onResume</code>."},{"q":"Where should a page count “this page was viewed”?","options":["<code>onResume</code>","<code>onViewCreated</code>","<code>createFragment</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>onViewCreated</code> also runs for neighbour pages nobody has seen. <code>onResume</code> runs when the page becomes the current one."},{"q":"The pager opens on News with <code>offscreenPageLimit = 1</code>. Which callbacks has the Sports page had?","options":["<code>onViewCreated</code> and <code>onStart</code>, not <code>onResume</code>","All of them, including <code>onResume</code>","None yet"],"a":0,"why":"Sports is built ahead so a swipe shows it at once, but it is not on screen, so it is not resumed."}]},{"id":"compose-in-views","title":"Mixing Compose and Views","tagline":"Compose inside an old screen, and an old view inside Compose.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show composables inside a View screen with <code>ComposeView</code>","Show a View inside a composable with <code>AndroidView</code>","Know what <code>factory</code> and <code>update</code> each do"],"remember":"ComposeView: Compose inside a View. AndroidView: a View inside Compose, where factory runs once and update runs whenever what it reads changes.","terms":[{"term":"ComposeView","def":"A View that shows composables. Fill it with <code>setContent { }</code>."},{"term":"AndroidView","def":"A composable that shows a View: <code>factory</code> creates it once, <code>update</code> copies state into it."},{"term":"ViewCompositionStrategy","def":"The rule for when a ComposeView ends its Compose content. In a Fragment, use <code>DisposeOnViewTreeLifecycleDestroyed</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>ComposeView</code>?","options":["A View that shows composables","A composable that shows a View","A kind of Fragment"],"a":0,"why":"It is a View, so it fits anywhere a View fits: in XML, in code, or as a Fragment’s view."},{"q":"In an <code>AndroidView</code>, state that <code>update</code> reads changes. What runs again?","options":["Only <code>update</code>","<code>factory</code>, then <code>update</code>","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"The View already exists, so <code>factory</code> is not called again. <code>update</code> copies the new state into it."},{"q":"A ComposeView is a Fragment’s whole view. Which strategy fits?","options":["<code>DisposeOnViewTreeLifecycleDestroyed</code>","None: Compose content never ends","Call <code>setContent</code> again in <code>onDestroyView</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It ends the Compose content together with the Fragment’s view, in step with <code>onDestroyView</code>."}]}]},{"id":"compose2","title":"Compose, advanced","description":"Make Compose fast and expressive: skipping, phases, drawing, locals, animation, shared elements, custom layouts and navigation.","requires":["compose"],"file":"tracks/compose-advanced.js","lessons":[{"id":"stability","title":"Stability and skipping","tagline":"Why a child skips, or runs again, when its parent recomposes.","level":"advanced","remember":"Stable params skip on equals(). Unstable params skip only on the same instance.","terms":[{"term":"Stability","def":"Whether the Compose compiler can trust a type’s equals() and be told about every change to it; stable parameters let a composable skip on equal inputs."},{"term":"Strong skipping mode","def":"The Compose compiler default since Kotlin 2.0.20: every restartable composable can skip, unstable parameters are compared with ===, and lambdas are remembered automatically."},{"term":"@Immutable","def":"An annotation promising that no public property of a type ever changes after construction, so Compose treats the type as stable."},{"term":"ImmutableList","def":"A read-only list from kotlinx.collections.immutable that the Compose compiler treats as stable."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Strong skipping is on. <code>Tags(tags: List&lt;String&gt;)</code> receives the same list instance as last time. What happens?","options":["It skips","It runs, because <code>List</code> is unstable","It fails to compile"],"a":0,"why":"Unstable parameters are compared with <code>===</code>. The same instance passes, so the composable skips."},{"q":"Which parameter type is stable?","options":["<code>List&lt;String&gt;</code>","<code>ImmutableList&lt;String&gt;</code>","<code>class User(var name: String)</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The compiler knows kotlinx immutable collections can’t change. A plain <code>List</code> might be mutable, and a <code>var</code> property can change without notice."},{"q":"What does <code>@Immutable</code> promise?","options":["No public property changes after the object is built","The class copies itself on every write","Compose will deep-copy it before each recomposition"],"a":0,"why":"It is a promise only. The compiler trusts it and compares with <code>equals()</code>, so breaking it shows stale UI."},{"q":"Strong skipping is on. Which <code>ShareButton</code> call can skip when <code>PostActions</code> recomposes with the same <code>related</code> list?<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><code>val ids = related.map { it.id }\nShareButton(onClick = { vm.share(ids) })          // A\n\nval stable = remember(related) { related.map { it.id } }\nShareButton(onClick = { vm.share(stable) })       // B</code></pre>","options":["Only B: A captures a new list each run, so its lambda is new each time","Both, because lambdas are always remembered","Only A, because B reads <code>remember</code> during composition"],"a":0,"why":"The remembered lambda is keyed on its captures. A fresh list is a new instance, so A gets a new lambda and ShareButton sees a changed parameter."},{"q":"A <code>SearchBarState</code> class holds <code>var query by mutableStateOf(\"\")</code>. Which annotation fits it?","options":["<code>@Immutable</code>, because the instance never changes","None: classes with a <code>var</code> can never skip","<code>@Stable</code>: it changes, but every change goes through Compose State"],"a":2,"why":"@Immutable promises nothing ever changes. @Stable allows change as long as Compose is told through State, like <code>LazyListState</code>."}]},{"id":"derived","title":"derivedStateOf","tagline":"Recompose when the answer changes, not every time the input does.","level":"intermediate","remember":"derivedStateOf turns a busy state into a calm one. Readers hear only when the result flips.","terms":[{"term":"derivedStateOf","def":"Creates a State computed from other State objects that notifies its readers only when the computed result changes."},{"term":"LazyListState","def":"The state holder of a LazyColumn or LazyRow, exposing the scroll position such as firstVisibleItemIndex and functions such as animateScrollToItem."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The list scrolls from item 0 to item 30. How many times does the reader of <code>showScrollToTop</code> recompose because of it?","options":["30","1","0"],"a":1,"why":"The result flips from false to true once, at the first item. The other 29 index changes produce the same value."},{"q":"Why wrap <code>derivedStateOf</code> in <code>remember</code>?","options":["Otherwise every recomposition builds a new derived object and loses its caching","It only works on the main thread otherwise","remember moves the calculation to a background thread"],"a":0,"why":"The derived object must survive recompositions to compare old and new results. Lint flags it when it is not remembered."},{"q":"Where is <code>derivedStateOf</code> the wrong tool?","options":["A full name built from first and last name fields","Showing a button once a list scrolls past item 0","Collapsing a toolbar once the scroll passes a threshold"],"a":0,"why":"The full name changes every time either field does. Derived state adds overhead without saving a single recomposition."}]},{"id":"phases","title":"Phases and deferred reads","tagline":"Read state as late as possible and skip whole phases.","level":"advanced","remember":"A read reruns its own phase and everything after. Read in a lambda to skip composition.","terms":[{"term":"Compose phases","def":"The three steps of every frame: composition decides what UI exists, layout measures and places it, drawing paints it."},{"term":"Deferred state read","def":"Reading State inside a lambda that runs in a later phase, such as Modifier.offset { } or graphicsLayer { }, so a change skips the earlier phases."},{"term":"Modifier.graphicsLayer","def":"A modifier that applies scale, rotation, translation, alpha and clipping on its own draw layer; its lambda form reads state only while drawing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>Modifier.graphicsLayer { alpha = fade() }</code>, and <code>fade()</code> reads changing state. Which phases rerun?","options":["Drawing only","Layout and drawing","All three"],"a":0,"why":"The read happens while drawing, so only drawing has to run again."},{"q":"Which modifier keeps a moving offset out of composition?","options":["<code>Modifier.offset(y = offsetY.dp)</code>","<code>Modifier.offset { IntOffset(0, offsetY) }</code>","<code>Modifier.padding(top = offsetY.dp)</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The lambda runs during layout. The other two read the value while composing."},{"q":"Why take <code>scrollProvider: () -&gt; Int</code> instead of <code>scroll: Int</code>?","options":["The child can read it later, inside layout or draw, so composition is skipped","Lambdas are cheaper to copy than Ints","<code>Int</code> parameters are unstable"],"a":0,"why":"An <code>Int</code> parameter must be read by the caller during composition. A lambda defers the read to wherever the child calls it."},{"q":"A header needs another child’s height. It stores <code>onSizeChanged { h = it.height }</code> in a <code>mutableIntStateOf</code> and reads it in composition. What does the user see?","options":["The right layout on the first frame","Nothing, because <code>onSizeChanged</code> runs before composition","One frame with the old layout, then a jump, because layout wrote state that composition already read"],"a":2,"why":"Phases run composition, layout, draw. A layout-phase write to state read in composition can only be acted on in the next frame. Measure them together in a custom Layout instead."},{"q":"<code>ArticleScreen</code> passes <code>CollapsingHeader(scroll = scroll.value)</code>. What recomposes on each scrolled frame?","options":["Only the header’s offset modifier","Both <code>ArticleScreen</code> and the header, because the read happens in the screen’s composition","Nothing, because Int parameters are stable"],"a":1,"why":"The caller reads the value to pass it. Passing <code>{ scroll.value }</code> moves the read into the header’s layout block, and both composables skip."}]},{"id":"drawing","title":"Drawing with Canvas and draw modifiers","tagline":"Paint shapes yourself with DrawScope, behind or on top of any composable.","level":"intermediate","remember":"(0, 0) is the top-left, units are pixels, and later draw calls paint on top.","terms":[{"term":"DrawScope","def":"The receiver of Canvas and the draw modifiers. It knows the drawing area’s size and offers drawRect, drawCircle, drawPath and the transforms."},{"term":"Canvas (Compose)","def":"A composable that gives you a DrawScope to paint in. It is a wrapper around Modifier.drawBehind."},{"term":"drawWithContent","def":"A draw modifier where you call drawContent() yourself, so you choose what is drawn before and after the composable’s own content."},{"term":"drawWithCache","def":"A draw modifier that keeps objects such as brushes and paths between frames until the size or a state it read changes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In a <code>DrawScope</code>, where is <code>Offset(0f, 0f)</code>?","options":["Top-left corner","Center","Bottom-left corner"],"a":0,"why":"x grows to the right and y grows down from the top-left pixel."},{"q":"What is <code>Canvas(modifier) { }</code> built on?","options":["<code>Modifier.drawBehind</code>","An Android <code>View</code> with <code>onDraw</code>","<code>SubcomposeLayout</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Canvas is a convenient wrapper around <code>Modifier.drawBehind</code>."},{"q":"You wrap drawing in <code>rotate(45f) { }</code>. What happens to the composable’s layout size?","options":["Nothing, transforms only affect drawing","It grows to fit the rotated shape","Its parent measures it again"],"a":0,"why":"DrawScope transforms run in the draw phase only. Layout never sees them."},{"q":"You want a dark scrim drawn over an image without an extra Box. Which modifier fits?","options":["<code>drawBehind { drawRect(scrim) }</code>","<code>drawWithContent { drawContent(); drawRect(scrim) }</code>","<code>background(scrim)</code> after the image modifiers"],"a":1,"why":"drawBehind always paints under the content. drawWithContent lets you call <code>drawContent()</code> first and then paint on top."},{"q":"A gradient Brush is built inside <code>drawBehind { }</code> on a box that animates every frame. What is the better choice and why?","options":["<code>drawWithCache</code>: build the brush in the outer block, draw it in <code>onDrawBehind</code>, so it is rebuilt only when the size or a read State changes","<code>Canvas</code>, because Canvas caches every object","<code>graphicsLayer</code>, because it draws brushes on the GPU"],"a":0,"why":"The draw lambda can run every frame, so allocating there creates garbage. drawWithCache keeps the objects until the cache is invalidated."}]},{"id":"locals","title":"CompositionLocal","tagline":"Pass a value down the tree without threading it through every call.","level":"intermediate","remember":"LocalX.current walks up the tree and takes the nearest provider. None? The default.","terms":[{"term":"CompositionLocal","def":"A value passed implicitly down the composition tree and read with LocalX.current by any composable below its provider."},{"term":"CompositionLocalProvider","def":"A composable that binds values to CompositionLocals for all content inside it, overriding any provider further up."},{"term":"staticCompositionLocalOf","def":"Creates a CompositionLocal that does not track readers, so changing its value recomposes the whole provided subtree; best for values that rarely change."},{"term":"LocalContentColor","def":"The CompositionLocal that Material Text and Icon use as their default color; Surface and many components provide it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Purple is provided outside, Orange inside, and an <code>Icon</code> sits inside both. What tint does it get?","options":["Orange, the nearest provider","Purple, the outermost provider","The default, because the providers conflict"],"a":0,"why":"A read walks up the tree and stops at the first provider it meets."},{"q":"When does <code>staticCompositionLocalOf</code> fit better than <code>compositionLocalOf</code>?","options":["For a value that almost never changes, like a spacing scale","For a value that changes every frame","Whenever the value is a data class"],"a":0,"why":"Static locals skip reader tracking, which is cheaper, but a change recomposes the whole subtree."},{"q":"Where can you read <code>LocalContext.current</code>?","options":["In a composable function body","In an <code>onClick</code> lambda","In a ViewModel"],"a":0,"why":"Locals are part of the composition. Only composable code can read them."},{"q":"<code>SettingsActivity</code> calls <code>setContent { SettingsScreen() }</code>. <code>MainActivity</code> provides <code>LocalAnalytics</code> in <code>AppTheme</code>. What does <code>LocalAnalytics.current</code> return in SettingsScreen?","options":["The factory default, because a second <code>setContent</code> starts a new root","The value from MainActivity’s provider","The value of the last provider created anywhere in the app"],"a":0,"why":"A local only reaches code inside the same composition. A throwing default such as <code>error(\"No Analytics provided\")</code> makes the missing provider fail at once."}]},{"id":"animation","title":"Animations","tagline":"Set a target. Compose animates toward it.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Change the target, not the frames. Springs keep their velocity when the target moves.","terms":[{"term":"animate*AsState","def":"A family of functions such as animateDpAsState and animateColorAsState that return a State animating toward the latest target value."},{"term":"AnimatedContent","def":"A composable that animates from old content to new whenever targetState changes, using a transitionSpec built with togetherWith."},{"term":"updateTransition","def":"Creates a Transition that animates several child values from one target state, so they run in sync."},{"term":"spring","def":"A physics-based AnimationSpec set by dampingRatio and stiffness that preserves velocity when interrupted."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A number should slide out while the new number slides in. Which API?","options":["<code>AnimatedContent</code>","<code>animateFloatAsState</code>","<code>AnimatedVisibility</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>AnimatedContent</code> keeps the old and new content on screen during the swap."},{"q":"A spring animation is halfway when the target flips back. What happens?","options":["It keeps its velocity and curves toward the new target","It jumps to the end, then starts over","It finishes the old animation first"],"a":0,"why":"Springs carry their velocity into the new target. That is why interrupted spring motion looks continuous."},{"q":"Why use <code>updateTransition</code> for a chip’s color, corner and scale?","options":["All values follow one state and stay in sync","It is the only API that can animate a color","It skips recomposition"],"a":0,"why":"One transition drives every child animation from the same target state."},{"q":"Which line is the bug?<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><code>@Composable\nfun Shake(trigger: Int) {\n    val offset = Animatable(0f)\n    LaunchedEffect(trigger) { offset.animateTo(12f); offset.animateTo(0f) }\n    Box(Modifier.offset { IntOffset(offset.value.roundToInt(), 0) })\n}</code></pre>","options":["<code>LaunchedEffect(trigger)</code>: the key should be <code>Unit</code>","<code>val offset = Animatable(0f)</code>: it needs <code>remember</code>","<code>Modifier.offset { … }</code>: use <code>offset(x = …)</code> instead"],"a":1,"why":"Each recomposition builds a new Animatable at 0 while the effect animates the old one, so the box stops following. <code>remember { Animatable(0f) }</code> keeps one."},{"q":"The user turns on Remove animations in accessibility settings. What happens to a Compose <code>tween(500)</code> animation?","options":["It jumps to its end, because the animator duration scale is 0","It still runs for 500 ms","It is replaced by a spring"],"a":0,"why":"Compose scales durations by the system animator scale. At 0, animations end at once, so meaning must never live only in motion."}]},{"id":"shared-elements","title":"Shared element transitions","tagline":"One element travels from the old screen to the new one.","level":"advanced","remember":"SharedTransitionLayout outside, AnimatedContent inside, the same unique key on both screens.","terms":[{"term":"SharedTransitionLayout","def":"The outer composable for shared element transitions. It provides the SharedTransitionScope that the shared modifiers need."},{"term":"Modifier.sharedElement","def":"Marks a composable as the same element on two screens. Only the target content is drawn while its bounds animate."},{"term":"Modifier.sharedBounds","def":"Shares only the container bounds between two visually different pieces of content, fading one out and the other in."},{"term":"rememberSharedContentState","def":"Remembers the SharedContentState that holds the key used to match elements across screens."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does the <code>AnimatedVisibilityScope</code> come from?","options":["From the <code>AnimatedContent</code>, <code>AnimatedVisibility</code> or <code>NavHost</code> that switches the screens","From <code>SharedTransitionLayout</code>","From <code>rememberSharedContentState</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>SharedTransitionLayout</code> gives the shared scope. The thing that animates the screens in and out gives the visibility scope."},{"q":"The list shows a title in small grey text. The detail shows it large and bold. Which modifier fits?","options":["<code>sharedBounds</code>","<code>sharedElement</code>","<code>animateContentSize</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>sharedBounds</code> is for content that looks different on each side. The guide prefers it for text, so font and color can change."},{"q":"When do two shared elements animate together?","options":["When their keys are equal","When they are the same composable function","When they have the same size"],"a":0,"why":"Matching is by key only. Same key on both screens, one element moves."},{"q":"You put a NavHost inside <code>SharedTransitionLayout</code>. Where does each screen get its <code>AnimatedVisibilityScope</code>?","options":["From the <code>composable&lt;Route&gt; { }</code> block itself, which runs in an <code>AnimatedContentScope</code>","From a new <code>AnimatedVisibility</code> you wrap around each screen","From <code>rememberSharedContentState()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Navigation Compose switches screens with AnimatedContent, so each destination block already is the visibility scope. Pass <code>this@composable</code> down."},{"q":"A shared photo does not animate between grid and detail. <code>isMatchFound</code> is false. What is the likely cause?","options":["ContentScale is different on the two sides","The image is inside a <code>Column</code>","The keys differ, for example a typo, or one side was not composed"],"a":2,"why":"Matching is by key only. ContentScale differences just snap at the end; they do not stop the match."}]},{"id":"layout","title":"Custom layouts","tagline":"Constraints go down, sizes come up, then the parent places.","level":"advanced","remember":"Measure each child once, choose your size, then place. Down, up, place.","terms":[{"term":"Layout (composable)","def":"The low-level composable for custom layouts; its measure block receives the children and constraints and must call layout(width, height) to place them."},{"term":"Constraints","def":"The minimum and maximum width and height a parent allows a child to take during measurement."},{"term":"Measurable","def":"A child in a layout that has not been measured yet; calling measure(constraints) on it returns a Placeable."},{"term":"Placeable","def":"A measured child with a known width and height that the parent positions with place or placeRelative inside layout { }."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times may you call <code>measure()</code> on one child in a layout pass?","options":["Once","Twice: once for width, once for height","As often as you need"],"a":0,"why":"Single-pass measurement keeps deep trees fast. A second call throws."},{"q":"In what order does a layout pass run?","options":["Constraints down, sizes up, then placement","Placement first, then measurement","Sizes down, constraints up"],"a":0,"why":"A parent constrains its children, reads their sizes, picks its own, then places them."},{"q":"Why call <code>placeRelative</code> rather than <code>place</code>?","options":["It mirrors x in right-to-left layouts","It animates the move","It allows a second measure"],"a":0,"why":"<code>placeRelative</code> flips the x position when the layout direction is RTL."},{"q":"A custom <code>Layout</code> on a <code>fillMaxWidth()</code> parent measures each chip with the incoming <code>constraints</code>. What happens?","options":["Chips flow side by side as expected","It throws, because constraints must be copied first","Every chip is forced to full width and lands on its own row"],"a":2,"why":"fillMaxWidth sets minWidth equal to maxWidth. Loosen with <code>constraints.copy(minWidth = 0, minHeight = 0)</code> before measuring the children."},{"q":"A header should show a compact version below 400 dp and a wide version above. Which tool fits?","options":["A custom <code>Layout</code> that measures both versions and places one","<code>BoxWithConstraints</code>, built on SubcomposeLayout, which composes after the constraints are known","<code>Modifier.layout</code> on the compact header"],"a":1,"why":"Here the content depends on the space, so it must be composed during measure. A plain Layout composes first and cannot measure a child twice."}]},{"id":"navigation","title":"Type-safe navigation","tagline":"Routes are Kotlin types. The compiler checks every argument.","level":"intermediate","remember":"@Serializable route in, navigate(Route(args)) to go, toRoute<Route>() to read it back.","terms":[{"term":"Type-safe route","def":"A @Serializable object or class used as a navigation destination, so the compiler checks its arguments."},{"term":"NavHost","def":"The composable that shows the current destination of a NavController’s back stack and declares the navigation graph."},{"term":"composable<T>()","def":"The NavGraphBuilder function that adds a destination for route type T and its screen content."},{"term":"toRoute()","def":"Rebuilds the typed route object, with its arguments, from a NavBackStackEntry or a SavedStateHandle."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>composable&lt;Product&gt; { entry -&gt; }</code>, how do you get the typed argument?","options":["<code>entry.toRoute&lt;Product&gt;()</code>","<code>entry.arguments?.getInt(\"id\")</code>","<code>navController.currentDestination</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>toRoute</code> deserializes the whole route object, with every argument typed."},{"q":"Stack: Home, Product(7), Cart. You call <code>navigate(Cart) { launchSingleTop = true }</code>. Result?","options":["Home, Product(7), Cart","Home, Product(7), Cart, Cart","Home, Cart"],"a":0,"why":"Cart is already on top, so single-top reuses it instead of pushing a copy."},{"q":"What should a route class carry?","options":["Ids and small values, like <code>Product(id = 42)</code>","The whole <code>Product</code> object with its image bytes","A reference to the ViewModel"],"a":0,"why":"Routes are saved in the back stack state. Keep them small and load the data with the id."},{"q":"<code>@Serializable data class Product(val id: Int, val ref: String? = null)</code> with <code>navDeepLink&lt;Product&gt;(basePath = \"https://shop.example/product\")</code>. Which URI opens it?","options":["<code>https://shop.example/product?id=42</code>","<code>https://shop.example/product/42?ref=mail</code>","<code>https://shop.example/product/abc</code>"],"a":1,"why":"A property without a default becomes a path segment, one with a default an optional query. <code>abc</code> cannot be parsed into an Int, so it does not match."}]},{"id":"nav3","title":"Navigation 3: you own the back stack","tagline":"The back stack is a list you hold. Add to go forward, remove to go back.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Navigation 3: the back stack is your list. NavDisplay draws it, entryProvider turns keys into screens.","terms":[{"term":"NavDisplay","def":"The Navigation 3 composable that observes a back stack list and shows the content for its entries."},{"term":"NavEntry","def":"A Navigation 3 destination: a key plus the composable content to show for it, with optional metadata."},{"term":"entryProvider","def":"The function, or entryProvider { entry<Key> { } } DSL, that turns a back stack key into a NavEntry."},{"term":"NavKey","def":"A marker interface for @Serializable keys, so rememberNavBackStack can save the back stack."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In Navigation 3, how do you open a screen?","options":["Add its key to the back stack list","Call <code>navController.navigate()</code>","Add a destination to the graph"],"a":0,"why":"There is no controller. <code>NavDisplay</code> shows what is in the list, so adding a key is navigating."},{"q":"Which back stack survives process death?","options":["<code>rememberNavBackStack(Home)</code> with @Serializable keys that implement NavKey","<code>remember { mutableStateListOf(Home) }</code>","Any list, NavDisplay saves it"],"a":0,"why":"<code>rememberNavBackStack</code> saves the keys, which is why they must be serializable. Plain <code>remember</code> is lost on rotation too."},{"q":"Stack: Home, Product(1), Product(2), Cart. You run <code>backStack.removeAll { it is Product }</code>. What is shown?","options":["Cart, with Home below it","Product(2)","Home"],"a":0,"why":"The list becomes Home, Cart. The top is still Cart, and back now goes straight to Home."},{"q":"Two Product screens are on a Navigation 3 back stack, and each calls <code>viewModel()</code>. No entry decorators are set. What do they get?","options":["One ViewModel each, cleared when the key leaves","A crash, because <code>viewModel()</code> needs a NavController","The same ViewModel, scoped to the Activity"],"a":2,"why":"Without the ViewModel store decorator from lifecycle-viewmodel-navigation3, the owner is the Activity. The decorator gives each entry its own store."},{"q":"After login, the back stack is Login. Which code is right to land on Home with no way back to Login?","options":["<code>backStack.add(Home)</code> in the composable body","<code>LaunchedEffect(loggedIn) { if (loggedIn) { backStack.clear(); backStack.add(Home) } }</code>","<code>backStack.removeLastOrNull()</code> after adding Home in <code>onClick</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Changing the list is a side effect, so do it in an effect, not the body. Clearing first means back does not return to Login."}]}]},{"id":"a11y","title":"Accessibility and adaptive UI","description":"Make screens that work with TalkBack, large fonts, big windows, foldables and edge-to-edge.","requires":["compose"],"file":"tracks/a11y.js","lessons":[{"id":"why","title":"Why accessibility","tagline":"A screen reader walks your layout as one long line.","level":"beginner","remember":"TalkBack reads your screen one stop at a time. Every stop needs words, and decoration needs none.","terms":[{"term":"TalkBack","def":"Android’s built-in screen reader, which speaks the focused element and moves focus with swipe gestures."},{"term":"Switch Access","def":"An Android accessibility service that lets people control the device with one or more switches instead of the touch screen."},{"term":"contentDescription","def":"The text an accessibility service reads for an Icon or Image; null marks it as decorative so it is skipped."},{"term":"Accessibility Scanner","def":"A Google app that inspects the current screen and suggests fixes for labels, touch target size and contrast."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In what order does a swipe right in TalkBack move through a screen?","options":["Top to bottom, start to end, through the elements in order","Largest element first","Only through buttons"],"a":0,"why":"TalkBack follows the semantics order, which by default is reading order. Lesson 3 shows how to change it."},{"q":"A heart icon sits next to the text \"Favorite\" inside one button. What should the icon’s <code>contentDescription</code> be?","options":["\"Heart\"","\"Favorite\"","<code>null</code>"],"a":2,"why":"The text already names the button. A description on the icon would make TalkBack say it twice."},{"q":"A user sets the largest font size in Settings. What changes in your app?","options":["Text sized in sp grows, dp sizes stay","Everything doubles in size","Nothing unless you opt in"],"a":0,"why":"Font scale multiplies sp only. Lesson 4 covers what that does to your layouts."}]},{"id":"semantics","title":"Semantics in Compose","tagline":"Decide what each focus stop says, and how many there are.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Put the action on the row, not on the switch. One row, one stop, one sentence.","terms":[{"term":"mergeDescendants","def":"A semantics flag that makes a node and its children one focus stop, reading their content together."},{"term":"clearAndSetSemantics","def":"A modifier that removes the semantics of all child nodes and replaces them with only the properties you set."},{"term":"stateDescription","def":"A semantics property that describes a control’s current state in words, such as \"On\" or \"72 percent\"."},{"term":"Role","def":"A semantics property such as Role.Button or Role.Switch that tells accessibility services what kind of control an element is."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A <code>Row</code> has <code>semantics(mergeDescendants = true)</code> and holds two <code>Text</code>s and a <code>Switch</code> with its own <code>onCheckedChange</code>. How many focus stops?","options":["One","Two: the texts together, then the switch","Three"],"a":1,"why":"A child with its own action stays a separate stop. Move the action to the row with <code>toggleable</code> to get one stop."},{"q":"What does <code>clearAndSetSemantics { contentDescription = \"Battery\" }</code> do to the children’s semantics?","options":["Keeps them and adds a description","Removes them, so only \"Battery\" is read","Hides the composable from the screen"],"a":1,"why":"It clears everything below and uses only what you set in the block. The UI still draws as usual."},{"q":"Which property makes a custom slider say \"72 percent\"?","options":["<code>contentDescription</code>","<code>stateDescription</code>","<code>testTag</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>stateDescription</code> is for the current state. <code>contentDescription</code> names the thing itself."},{"q":"A cart total updates while TalkBack focus is on a product row. What makes TalkBack read the new total?","options":["<code>Modifier.semantics { liveRegion = LiveRegionMode.Polite }</code> on the total","<code>Modifier.semantics { heading() }</code> on the total","Requesting focus for the total on every change"],"a":0,"why":"Changes away from focus are silent by default. A polite live region is read once TalkBack finishes speaking. Moving focus would disorient the user."}]},{"id":"custom-actions","title":"Custom accessibility actions","tagline":"Give every hidden gesture a named action TalkBack and Switch Access can offer.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Every gesture-only action also needs a customActions entry with a clear label.","terms":[{"term":"CustomAccessibilityAction","def":"A semantics action with a label and a lambda. Accessibility services offer it in their actions menu."},{"term":"customActions","def":"The semantics property that holds an element’s list of CustomAccessibilityAction objects."},{"term":"Actions menu","def":"The TalkBack menu that lists the actions available on the focused element, including custom actions."},{"term":"Switch Access","def":"An Android accessibility service that lets people use the device with one or more switches instead of the touch screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"TalkBack is on. The user swipes left on a mail row that archives on swipe. What happens?","options":["TalkBack moves focus to the previous element","The mail is archived","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"TalkBack takes swipes for navigation. The archive gesture never reaches your row, which is why the custom action is needed."},{"q":"Which label is best for a custom action?","options":["\"Archive\"","\"Swipe left\"","\"Action 1\""],"a":0,"why":"Name what happens. The user is choosing a result from a menu, not a gesture."},{"q":"You added <code>customActions</code>. Should the swipe gesture stay?","options":["Yes, touch users still swipe","No, actions replace gestures","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"Custom actions are an extra way in for people who can’t use the gesture."},{"q":"Each article row has the summary, a bookmark button and a share button: three stops per row. Which change gives one stop with both actions available?","options":["<code>mergeDescendants = true</code> on the row, keeping the buttons as they are","Add both as <code>customActions</code> on the row and clear the buttons’ semantics with <code>clearAndSetSemantics { }</code>","Set <code>contentDescription = null</code> on both buttons"],"a":1,"why":"Children with their own clicks stay separate stops even when merged. Moving their actions to the row and clearing them leaves one stop with an actions menu."}]},{"id":"targets","title":"Touch targets and focus order","tagline":"48 dp to hit, and an order that makes sense when read aloud.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Make every target at least 48 dp. Group columns with isTraversalGroup so they are read top to bottom.","terms":[{"term":"minimumInteractiveComponentSize","def":"A Material 3 modifier that reserves at least 48 dp of layout space for an interactive element that would otherwise measure smaller."},{"term":"isTraversalGroup","def":"A semantics flag that makes accessibility services finish a container’s children before moving to the next container."},{"term":"traversalIndex","def":"A semantics value that reorders an element within its traversal group; lower values are visited first and the default is 0."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the minimum touch target size Android recommends?","options":["24 by 24 dp","36 by 36 dp","48 by 48 dp"],"a":2,"why":"48 dp is about 9 mm, roughly the size of a fingertip."},{"q":"Two plan cards sit side by side. Without traversal groups, how does TalkBack read \"Basic / $5\" and \"Pro / $12\"?","options":["Basic, $5, Pro, $12","Basic, Pro, $5, $12","Pro, $12, Basic, $5"],"a":1,"why":"Default order goes row by row. <code>isTraversalGroup</code> on each card fixes it."},{"q":"Which element is visited first?","options":["<code>traversalIndex = 1f</code>","<code>traversalIndex = 0f</code>","<code>traversalIndex = -1f</code>"],"a":2,"why":"Lower values come first within the traversal group. The default is 0."},{"q":"A row is built with <code>Modifier.pointerInput(Unit) { detectTapGestures { onOpen() } }</code>. What do keyboard and TalkBack users get?","options":["The same as touch users","Focus, but no ripple","No focus, no role and no click action; <code>clickable</code> adds all three"],"a":2,"why":"A raw tap detector handles touch only. clickable adds keyboard focus, Enter handling, the click action for accessibility services and an indication."}]},{"id":"keyboard-nav","title":"Keyboard and D-pad focus","tagline":"Tab walks a line, arrow keys jump to the nearest neighbour.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Tab follows screen order and wraps. Arrows go to the nearest element and stop at the edge.","terms":[{"term":"One-dimensional traversal","def":"Focus movement with Tab and Shift+Tab through focusable elements in their visual order. It wraps at the ends."},{"term":"Two-dimensional traversal","def":"Focus movement with arrow keys or a D-pad to the geometrically closest element in that direction. It does not wrap."},{"term":"focusProperties","def":"A Compose modifier that overrides where focus goes next in each direction, or blocks a direction with FocusRequester.Cancel."},{"term":"focusGroup","def":"A modifier that makes a set of children one navigation unit. Focus enters it, moves among its children, then leaves to the next element outside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Focus is on the last element. The user presses Tab. Where does focus go?","options":["The first element: Tab wraps","It stays on the last element","Nowhere, focus is cleared"],"a":0,"why":"One-dimensional traversal wraps from the end to the start. Shift+Tab goes the other way."},{"q":"Focus is on the bottom-most button. The user presses Down. Where does focus go?","options":["It stays on that button","The first element at the top","The next element in Tab order"],"a":0,"why":"Two-dimensional traversal does not wrap. There is nothing below, so nothing moves."},{"q":"The user clicks a field with the mouse, then presses Tab. Where does focus go?","options":["The first element on screen","The element after the clicked field","The element before it"],"a":0,"why":"A click clears keyboard focus, so Tab starts over from the first focus target."},{"q":"A carousel’s last card should not jump into an unrelated side panel on Right, but Down should work as usual. Which <code>focusProperties</code> fits?","options":["<code>right = FocusRequester.Cancel; down = FocusRequester.Default</code>","<code>right = FocusRequester.Default; down = FocusRequester.Cancel</code>","<code>canFocus = false</code> on the side panel"],"a":0,"why":"Cancel blocks the move in that direction. Default keeps the built-in search. Making the panel unfocusable would lock keyboard users out of it."},{"q":"You draw a focus ring from <code>onFocusChanged</code>, but it never appears. The chain is <code>.clickable { }.onFocusChanged { … }.border(…)</code>. What is wrong?","options":["border cannot read focus state","clickable elements are never focusable by keyboard","onFocusChanged must come before the modifier that makes the element focusable"],"a":2,"why":"onFocusChanged sees the focus of the focusable that follows it. Put it before <code>clickable</code> so it observes that element."}]},{"id":"text-scale","title":"Text scaling and contrast","tagline":"sp grows with the user’s font size. Colors need enough contrast to read.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Text in sp, everything else in dp. Give text minimum heights, never fixed ones. Body text needs 4.5:1 contrast.","terms":[{"term":"sp","def":"Scale-independent pixels: a dp multiplied by the user’s font scale, used for text sizes."},{"term":"Nonlinear font scaling","def":"Android 14 behavior where small text grows by the full font scale and large text grows less, up to 200%."},{"term":"Contrast ratio","def":"The WCAG measure of luminance difference between two colors, from 1:1 to 21:1; body text needs at least 4.5:1."},{"term":"@PreviewFontScale","def":"A Compose multipreview annotation that renders a composable at several font scales from 85% to 200%."}],"quiz":[{"q":"At 200% font scale, which grows more relative to its size?","options":["14sp body text","30sp headline","Both double"],"a":0,"why":"Android 14 scales nonlinearly. Body text nearly doubles while large headlines grow much less."},{"q":"What contrast ratio does WCAG AA require for normal body text?","options":["3:1","4.5:1","7:1"],"a":1,"why":"4.5:1 for body text. 3:1 is the bar for large text and icons, and 7:1 is the stricter AAA level."},{"q":"Which unit should an icon’s size use?","options":["sp, so it grows with text","dp"],"a":1,"why":"Font scale is for text. Icons and spacing use dp so the layout doesn’t blow up."},{"q":"A badge height is computed as <code>(16 * fontScale + 8).dp</code>. At 200% on Android 14 the badge is too tall for its text. Why?","options":["fontScale is capped at 1.5","dp values are scaled by fontScale too","Scaling is nonlinear there, so 16sp is drawn at about 28 dp, not 32; use <code>16.sp.toDp()</code>"],"a":2,"why":"Android 14+ uses a lookup curve for sp, so large text grows less than the linear factor. <code>toDp()</code> uses the same converter as the text."},{"q":"An email field shows an error only by turning its border red. Which fix meets the lesson’s rule?","options":["Use a brighter red with 4.5:1 contrast","Add an error icon and a message in words, so color is not the only signal","Make the border thicker"],"a":1,"why":"Many users cannot tell the red apart. Every error needs a second cue that does not depend on color, such as text and an icon."}]},{"id":"window-size","title":"Window size classes and adaptive layouts","tagline":"Pick a layout from the window’s width, and let list-detail do the rest.","level":"advanced","remember":"Compact under 600dp, Medium to 840dp, Expanded beyond. Size classes describe the window, not the device.","terms":[{"term":"Window size class","def":"A bucket for the app window’s width or height (Compact, Medium, Expanded and larger) used to choose a layout."},{"term":"currentWindowAdaptiveInfo","def":"A Compose function that returns the current window size class and posture, and updates when either changes."},{"term":"List-detail pane scaffold","def":"NavigableListDetailPaneScaffold, a Material 3 adaptive layout that shows a list and its detail side by side or one at a time."},{"term":"NavigationSuiteScaffold","def":"A Material 3 adaptive scaffold that shows a navigation bar, rail or drawer depending on the window."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A window is 700 dp wide. Which width size class is it?","options":["Compact","Medium","Expanded"],"a":1,"why":"Medium covers 600 to 839 dp."},{"q":"A tablet runs your app in split screen at 400 dp wide. What should you show?","options":["The tablet layout, since it is a tablet","The Compact layout"],"a":1,"why":"Size classes describe the window. A 400 dp window is Compact on any device."},{"q":"On a Compact window, what does <code>NavigableListDetailPaneScaffold</code> do when the user opens an item?","options":["Squeezes both panes in","Shows the detail pane alone, and back returns to the list","Opens a new Activity"],"a":1,"why":"With room for one pane it navigates between them and handles back for you."},{"q":"A phone in landscape is 780 dp wide and 400 dp tall. Which size classes apply?","options":["Expanded width, Medium height","Medium width, Compact height","Compact width, Compact height"],"a":1,"why":"600–839 dp is Medium width, and under 480 dp is Compact height. On such a short window, move navigation to a rail and collapse the top bar."},{"q":"A product card picks its wide layout when the window is Expanded. Inside a two-pane list-detail, it looks squeezed. What fixes it?","options":["Decide inside the card from the space it gets, for example with <code>BoxWithConstraints</code>","Use Medium instead of Expanded as the breakpoint","Hide the list pane on Expanded windows"],"a":0,"why":"Size classes describe the whole window and fit top-level structure. A component should measure its own space, because a pane is narrower than the window."}]},{"id":"foldables","title":"Foldables and large screens","tagline":"Postures, hinges and windows that change size while the app runs.","level":"advanced","remember":"Read the posture, keep state through every fold, and never lock orientation.","terms":[{"term":"Tabletop posture","def":"A foldable half open with a horizontal hinge, so the bottom half rests flat and the top half stands up."},{"term":"FoldingFeature","def":"A Jetpack WindowManager object that describes a fold or hinge: its bounds, orientation, state and whether it separates the screen."},{"term":"Multi-window mode","def":"Running two or more apps on screen at once, so your window can be resized while the app runs."},{"term":"Letterboxing","def":"What the system does to an app that locks orientation or aspect ratio on a large screen: it shows it in a smaller box with bars around it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A foldable is half open with the hinge running left to right, bottom half on the table. What posture is that?","options":["Book","Tabletop","Flat"],"a":1,"why":"A horizontal hinge with the device half open is tabletop."},{"q":"Your app targets API 36 and sets <code>screenOrientation=\"portrait\"</code>. What happens on a tablet with Android 16?","options":["It stays portrait with bars around it","The lock is ignored and the app fills the screen","It crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Android 16 ignores orientation and resizability limits on displays with a smallest width of 600 dp or more, for apps targeting API 36. Older targets get letterboxed."},{"q":"What happens to the Activity by default when the user unfolds the phone?","options":["Nothing","It is recreated because of a configuration change","It is moved to the background"],"a":1,"why":"The screen size changes, and by default that recreates the Activity. Keep state in a ViewModel or rememberSaveable."},{"q":"A flat, seamless foldable reports a <code>FoldingFeature</code> with <code>isSeparating = false</code>. How should a photo viewer lay out?","options":["Treat it as one continuous screen; content may run across the fold","Split the panes at the hinge bounds","Fall back to the phone layout"],"a":0,"why":"A non-separating fold does not divide the screen. Split at the hinge only when it separates, like half open or on a dual-screen device."},{"q":"A draft is kept in a ViewModel field. The user unfolds the phone, then leaves the app and the system kills the process. What survives?","options":["The draft survives both","The draft is lost on unfold already","The draft survives the unfold, but is lost after process death unless it is in SavedStateHandle"],"a":2,"why":"A ViewModel survives the configuration change but not process death. Only saved state, like SavedStateHandle or rememberSaveable, comes back."}]},{"id":"resizability","title":"Android 17: orientation locks are ignored on large screens","tagline":"Target API 37 and your portrait lock no longer holds on tablets and foldables.","level":"advanced","remember":"Targeting API 37: no orientation or resize locks on sw600dp+ screens, and no opt-out. Build layouts for any window.","terms":[{"term":"sw600dp","def":"A smallest width of at least 600dp: the line Android uses for large screens such as tablets and unfolded foldables."},{"term":"Pillarboxing","def":"Showing a narrow app in the middle of a wide screen with empty bars on the sides, as the system does for apps whose orientation lock is honored."},{"term":"Resizability opt-out property","def":"An Android 16 manifest property that temporarily opts an app out of ignored orientation and resizability limits. It has no effect once the app targets API 37."},{"term":"android:appCategory","def":"A manifest attribute that declares the app’s category. Apps that declare game are exempt from the large-screen rule."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app targets API 37 and sets <code>screenOrientation=\"portrait\"</code>. A user opens it on a landscape tablet. What happens?","options":["The lock is ignored and the app fills the screen in landscape","The app shows in a portrait box with bars","The app crashes on launch"],"a":0,"why":"On screens of sw600dp and larger, orientation locks are ignored for apps targeting API 37. There is no opt-out."},{"q":"Which app keeps its orientation lock on a tablet, even targeting API 37?","options":["One with <code>android:appCategory=\"game\"</code>","One with the Android 16 opt-out property","One with <code>resizeableActivity=\"false\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Games are exempt. The opt-out property only worked while targeting API 36, and resizeableActivity is one of the ignored attributes."},{"q":"Does the change affect a phone with a smallest width of 411dp?","options":["No, it only applies at sw600dp and above","Yes, on every device","Only in landscape"],"a":0,"why":"Screens smaller than sw600dp are exempt, so a phone keeps the lock."},{"q":"Your app targets API 36 today. You want to see the API 37 large-screen behavior before raising targetSdk. What do you run?","options":["Set <code>resizeableActivity=\"true\"</code> and rebuild","Turn on Don’t keep activities in Developer options","<code>adb shell am compat enable UNIVERSAL_RESIZABLE_BY_DEFAULT &lt;package&gt;</code> on a large-screen device"],"a":2,"why":"The app compatibility flag switches the behavior on for a debuggable build. The app is killed so the change applies on the next start."},{"q":"A portrait-only signup form now opens in landscape on a tablet. Fields stretch across the screen and the button falls off the bottom. Which fix fits?","options":["Add the opt-out property back to the manifest","Cap the content with <code>widthIn(max = …)</code>, center it and make the column scroll","Lock orientation in code with <code>setRequestedOrientation()</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Once you target API 37 the opt-out and orientation requests are ignored on sw600dp+ screens. The layout has to work in any window."}]},{"id":"insets","title":"Edge-to-edge and insets","tagline":"Draw behind the system bars, then pad content out of their way.","level":"advanced","remember":"enableEdgeToEdge() first, then safeDrawingPadding() or imePadding() where content must stay clear.","terms":[{"term":"enableEdgeToEdge","def":"An Activity extension that makes the system bars transparent and lets the app draw behind them on every Android version."},{"term":"WindowInsets","def":"The sizes of the screen edges covered by system UI such as the status bar, navigation bar, display cutout and keyboard."},{"term":"safeDrawing","def":"The WindowInsets type that combines system bars, display cutout and the keyboard: everywhere content could be covered."},{"term":"imePadding","def":"A modifier that pads content by the keyboard’s height and animates with it as the keyboard opens and closes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An app targets Android 15 and runs on Android 15, with no inset handling. What happens?","options":["The system adds padding for you","Content draws behind the status and navigation bars","The app falls back to the old non-edge-to-edge layout"],"a":1,"why":"Edge-to-edge is enforced for apps targeting API 35. Handling insets is up to you."},{"q":"Which insets include the keyboard?","options":["<code>WindowInsets.systemBars</code>","<code>WindowInsets.ime</code> and <code>WindowInsets.safeDrawing</code>","<code>WindowInsets.displayCutout</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>ime</code> is the keyboard alone. <code>safeDrawing</code> combines it with the bars and the cutout."},{"q":"Where should <code>enableEdgeToEdge()</code> go?","options":["In onCreate, before setContent","In onResume","Inside a composable"],"a":0,"why":"It configures the window, so call it once in onCreate before any content is set."},{"q":"Which line is the bug?<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><code>Box(\n    Modifier\n        .fillMaxSize()\n        .safeDrawingPadding()\n        .background(MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primaryContainer)\n)</code></pre>","options":["<code>.fillMaxSize()</code>: edge-to-edge screens must not fill","<code>.safeDrawingPadding()</code> comes before the background, so the bars show the window color instead of yours","<code>.background(…)</code>: backgrounds cannot sit behind system bars"],"a":1,"why":"Modifiers apply in order. Paint full bleed first, then pad the content with the insets."},{"q":"A Scaffold applies <code>Modifier.padding(innerPadding)</code>, and the input below uses <code>imePadding()</code>. With the keyboard open, a gap appears above it. What fixes it?","options":["Add <code>consumeWindowInsets(innerPadding)</code> after the padding","Remove imePadding()","Use <code>systemBarsPadding()</code> on the input too"],"a":0,"why":"Plain padding consumes no insets, so imePadding() adds the full keyboard height again on top of the navigation bar already covered. Consuming tells it what is used."}]}]},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing and quality","description":"Fast, trustworthy tests from ViewModels to Compose screens, plus measuring how fast the app starts.","requires":["arch"],"file":"tracks/testing.js","lessons":[{"id":"pyramid","title":"The testing pyramid","tagline":"Many fast tests at the bottom, a few slow ones on top.","level":"beginner","remember":"Most tests at the bottom: fast JVM unit tests. A few UI tests on top for the flows that must not break.","terms":[{"term":"Local unit test","def":"A test in src/test that runs on the JVM on your computer, with no device or emulator."},{"term":"Instrumented test","def":"A test in src/androidTest that runs on a device or emulator and can use real Android framework classes."},{"term":"Testing pyramid","def":"A guideline to write many fast, small tests and fewer slow, broad ones such as end-to-end UI tests."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which tests run on your computer without a device?","options":["<code>src/test</code>","<code>src/androidTest</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"Local unit tests in <code>src/test</code> run on the JVM. <code>androidTest</code> needs a device or emulator."},{"q":"Why should most of your tests be local unit tests?","options":["They give the most confidence that the real screen works","They are fast and stable enough to run on every change","Instrumented tests cannot use assertions"],"a":1,"why":"Milliseconds per test means you get feedback right away. The slower layers stay few."},{"q":"Which layer tells you most directly that a real user can finish checkout?","options":["A local unit test of the price formatter","A UI test that taps through the checkout screen"],"a":1,"why":"The UI test exercises the real screens together. That confidence is why you keep a few, even though they are slow."},{"q":"A DAO query returns the wrong rows in production. What is the lowest layer that can catch this bug?","options":["A local unit test with a fake repository","An integration test with an in-memory Room database","A UI test that taps through the screen"],"a":1,"why":"The bug lives in the SQL, so the test needs a real SQLite engine. A fake would skip the query entirely, and a UI test would be slower than needed."}]},{"id":"robolectric","title":"Robolectric: Android on the JVM","tagline":"Run tests that need Android classes on your computer, with no device.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Need Android classes but not a device? Robolectric runs them on the JVM. You pay the sandbox boot once per run.","terms":[{"term":"Robolectric","def":"A Google-maintained framework that runs Android tests on the JVM with a simulated Android framework."},{"term":"Shadow","def":"A Robolectric class that stands in for part of a framework class, such as ShadowLooper for Looper."},{"term":"Paused looper mode","def":"Robolectric’s default since 4.4: posted work waits in the main looper until the test idles it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do Robolectric tests live and run?","options":["<code>src/androidTest</code>, on a device","<code>src/test</code>, on the JVM","Only on a CI emulator"],"a":1,"why":"Robolectric is a local test runner. No device, no APK install."},{"q":"Why does the first Robolectric test in a run take seconds?","options":["It boots the simulated Android sandbox","It installs an APK","It waits for an emulator"],"a":0,"why":"The sandbox loads the framework once. The tests after it are fast."},{"q":"In paused looper mode, a Runnable posted to the main looper…","options":["runs right away","never runs","waits until the test calls idle() or idleFor()"],"a":2,"why":"Like on a real device, posting only queues the work. The test decides when the looper runs."},{"q":"In paused looper mode, code under test posts a Runnable to the main looper. Which test order is right?","options":["Trigger the code → shadowOf(getMainLooper()).idle() → assert","Trigger the code → assert → idle()","Trigger the code → Thread.sleep(500) → assert"],"a":0,"why":"Posted work waits in the queue until the test runs the looper. Asserting first sees nothing, and sleeping does not run the queue."},{"q":"A test is marked <code>@Config(sdk = [28, 34])</code>. How does it run?","options":["Once, at API 34","Twice, once per listed level","Once, at the app’s targetSdk"],"a":1,"why":"With several levels listed, Robolectric runs the test once for each. It is a cheap way to cover code that branches on SDK_INT."}]},{"id":"fakes","title":"Unit tests with fakes","tagline":"Swap the network for a small in-memory class.","level":"beginner","remember":"Depend on an interface, test with a fake. Given, When, Then.","terms":[{"term":"Test double","def":"Any object that stands in for a real dependency during a test, such as a fake, stub or mock."},{"term":"Fake","def":"A simple working implementation of an interface, often in memory, used in tests instead of the real one."},{"term":"Given / When / Then","def":"A test layout: set up the state, perform one action, then assert on the outcome."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What makes a fake different from a mock?","options":["A fake is a small working implementation; a mock is scripted per call","A fake can only be used in UI tests","There is no difference"],"a":0,"why":"A fake really stores and returns data. A mock answers only the calls you told it to expect."},{"q":"What has to be true about <code>NewsViewModel</code> so a test can pass it a fake?","options":["It creates its repository inside init","It takes a NewsRepository interface in its constructor","It is an object"],"a":1,"why":"Constructor injection of an interface lets the test choose the implementation."},{"q":"In Given / When / Then, where does <code>vm.load()</code> go?","options":["Given","When","Then"],"a":1,"why":"When is the one action under test. Given sets up the fake, Then asserts."},{"q":"A FakeNewsRepository is declared as an <code>object</code> and shared by all tests. A test passes alone but fails in the full suite. Why?","options":["Fakes cannot be objects","An object keeps its state for the whole test JVM, so another test left articles behind","The test needs Robolectric"],"a":1,"why":"Shared state makes results depend on run order. Create a new fake instance in each test."}]},{"id":"mockk","title":"Mocks with MockK","tagline":"Script an answer, then check the one call that matters.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Fakes for state, mocks for side effects. Verify the call that matters, not every call.","terms":[{"term":"Mock","def":"A test double that you program per call and that records its calls so the test can verify them."},{"term":"Stub","def":"A scripted answer for a call, such as every { repo.latest() } returns list."},{"term":"Verification","def":"A check that a call happened with certain arguments and a certain number of times, such as verify(exactly = 1)."},{"term":"Relaxed mock","def":"A MockK mock that returns default values for calls you did not script instead of throwing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When is a mock a better fit than a fake?","options":["To give a ViewModel data to show","To check a side effect with no state to read back, like an analytics event","In every unit test"],"a":1,"why":"A fake covers state. A mock shines when the call itself is the behavior."},{"q":"A plain <code>mockk&lt;Analytics&gt;()</code> gets a call you never scripted. What happens?","options":["It returns a default value","The call is ignored","It throws MockKException"],"a":2,"why":"Plain mocks are strict. Use relaxed or relaxUnitFun to change that, knowingly."},{"q":"Which pair scripts and checks suspend functions?","options":["coEvery and coVerify","every and verify","suspendEvery and suspendVerify"],"a":0,"why":"The co versions run their block as a coroutine, so suspend calls are allowed."},{"q":"<code>every { repo.load() } returns articles</code>, where load() is a suspend function. What happens?","options":["It works like any other function","It does not compile, because every { } is not a coroutine; use coEvery","It returns articles only on the main thread"],"a":1,"why":"A suspend call needs a coroutine. coEvery and coVerify are the coroutine versions with the same matchers."},{"q":"An Analytics mock has many Unit functions you don’t care about, plus <code>sessionId(): String</code>. You want unscripted Unit calls to pass but data calls to fail loudly. Which mock?","options":["mockk&lt;Analytics&gt;()","mockk&lt;Analytics&gt;(relaxed = true)","mockk&lt;Analytics&gt;(relaxUnitFun = true)"],"a":2,"why":"relaxUnitFun relaxes only Unit functions. A relaxed mock would quietly return an empty sessionId, and a strict one fails on every unscripted log call."}]},{"id":"coroutines","title":"Testing coroutines","tagline":"runTest skips delays with a virtual clock.","level":"intermediate","remember":"runTest skips delay() on a virtual clock. Standard queues until you advance; Unconfined runs at once.","terms":[{"term":"runTest","def":"A coroutine test builder that runs the body on a test scheduler whose virtual clock skips delays."},{"term":"StandardTestDispatcher","def":"A test dispatcher that queues new coroutines until the test advances the scheduler."},{"term":"UnconfinedTestDispatcher","def":"A test dispatcher that starts new coroutines immediately and runs them until their first suspension."},{"term":"MainDispatcherRule","def":"A common JUnit rule that calls Dispatchers.setMain with a test dispatcher before each test and resetMain after."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>runTest</code>, how long does <code>delay(10_000)</code> take on the wall clock?","options":["About 10 seconds","Almost no time; the virtual clock jumps ahead","It throws"],"a":1,"why":"The test scheduler advances virtual time instead of waiting."},{"q":"Main is a <code>StandardTestDispatcher</code>. Right after <code>vm.sync()</code>, has the launched coroutine run?","options":["Yes, up to its first delay","No, it waits in the queue until you advance"],"a":1,"why":"Standard queues new coroutines. Unconfined would start it right away."},{"q":"Why does a ViewModel test need <code>Dispatchers.setMain</code>?","options":["viewModelScope uses Dispatchers.Main, which the JVM does not have","It makes tests run in parallel","runTest requires it"],"a":0,"why":"Without a replacement, launching in viewModelScope fails because there is no Android main looper."},{"q":"A search runs 300 ms after the last keystroke. In the test you type, then call <code>advanceTimeBy(300)</code>. Has the search run?","options":["Yes","No, a task due exactly at the new time has not run yet; call runCurrent()","Only with UnconfinedTestDispatcher"],"a":1,"why":"advanceTimeBy runs tasks scheduled before the new time. runCurrent() then runs what is due right now."},{"q":"Inside runTest you write <code>launch { vm.state.collect { states += it } }</code>. What happens when the test body ends?","options":["The test passes","runTest waits for the collector, which never finishes, and fails on its timeout","The collector is cancelled automatically"],"a":1,"why":"runTest waits for every coroutine in its scope, and a StateFlow collector never completes. Launch it in backgroundScope, which runTest cancels at the end."}]},{"id":"flows","title":"Testing Flows","tagline":"Collect emissions into a queue, then assert one at a time.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Turbine queues every emission. awaitItem() takes one; leftovers fail the test.","terms":[{"term":"Turbine","def":"A small testing library by Cash App that collects a Flow into a queue so a test can await and assert each event."},{"term":"awaitItem()","def":"A Turbine call that suspends until the next emission arrives and returns it, failing after a timeout."},{"term":"backgroundScope","def":"A scope inside runTest for coroutines that should run alongside the test and be cancelled when it ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside Turbine’s <code>test { }</code>, you call <code>awaitItem()</code> and nothing is emitted. What happens?","options":["It returns null","It fails after the timeout, 3 seconds by default","It waits forever"],"a":1,"why":"Turbine fails with “No value produced” once the timeout passes."},{"q":"The block ends with one emission still in the queue. Result?","options":["The test passes","The test fails with unconsumed events"],"a":1,"why":"Turbine checks the queue is empty at the end. Use <code>cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()</code> if leftovers are fine."},{"q":"What is the first item a <code>StateFlow</code> gives a new collector?","options":["Its current value, right away","Nothing until the next change"],"a":0,"why":"StateFlow always has a value and replays it to every new collector."},{"q":"The ViewModel builds state with <code>stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(5_000), Loading)</code>. The test calls vm.load() and reads <code>vm.state.value</code> with no collector. What does it see?","options":["Success","Loading, forever","It throws"],"a":1,"why":"WhileSubscribed only runs the upstream while someone collects. Start an empty collector in backgroundScope first."},{"q":"The ViewModel sets Loading, then Success, with no suspension in between. The test collector runs on StandardTestDispatcher. What does it record?","options":["Loading, then Success","Only Success","Only Loading"],"a":1,"why":"StateFlow conflates. A queued collector runs later and reads only the newest value. Assert on the final state, not on every step."}]},{"id":"compose-ui","title":"Compose UI tests","tagline":"Find nodes in the semantics tree, act, assert.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Find one node in the semantics tree, perform an action, assert. Tags for things without text.","terms":[{"term":"Semantics tree","def":"The tree of meaning Compose builds alongside the UI, read by tests and accessibility services."},{"term":"createComposeRule","def":"A JUnit rule that hosts composable content in a test and synchronizes with Compose before each check."},{"term":"Modifier.testTag","def":"A modifier that puts a string tag in the semantics tree so tests can find the node with onNodeWithTag."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a Compose UI test read to find nodes?","options":["The rendered pixels","The semantics tree","The Kotlin source code"],"a":1,"why":"Finders match semantics properties like text, tags and content descriptions."},{"q":"<code>Button { Text(\"Refresh\") }</code>. What does <code>onNodeWithText(\"Refresh\")</code> match by default?","options":["The Button, because it merges its child Text","Only the inner Text node","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Finders use the merged tree. Pass <code>useUnmergedTree = true</code> to reach the inner Text."},{"q":"When is <code>Modifier.testTag</code> the right finder hook?","options":["For a node with no stable text, such as a list or an image","For every node, always","Never in production code"],"a":0,"why":"Prefer what the user sees, like text or content descriptions. Use tags when there is nothing user-visible to match."},{"q":"The ViewModel loads on a real Dispatchers.IO thread. <code>onNodeWithText(\"Kotlin\").assertIsDisplayed()</code> fails sometimes. Why?","options":["The rule only waits for Compose and idling resources, not your IO thread","Text finders are flaky","The semantics tree is not merged"],"a":0,"why":"The assertion can run before the data arrives. Use a fake repository that answers at once, or waitUntil the node exists."},{"q":"You want to check a card halfway through its expand animation. What do you do?","options":["Thread.sleep(150) after the click","Set rule.mainClock.autoAdvance = false, click, then advanceTimeBy(150)","Call waitForIdle() after the click"],"a":1,"why":"With autoAdvance on, the rule waits until animations end. Stopping the test clock lets you move time yourself and assert in the middle."}]},{"id":"screenshots","title":"Screenshot tests","tagline":"Save a picture of the UI and fail when it changes.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Record a reference, verify against it on the same machine, and read the diff before you record again.","terms":[{"term":"Reference image","def":"The approved screenshot a test compares against, also called a golden. It is committed with the code."},{"term":"Diff report","def":"The output of a failed screenshot test, showing the reference, the new render and the pixels that changed."},{"term":"Tolerance","def":"How much difference a screenshot comparison allows before it fails, such as a percentage of changed pixels."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A screenshot test fails after you made the title bigger on purpose. What next?","options":["Raise the tolerance until it passes","Check the diff, record new references, and let the reviewer approve them","Delete the test"],"a":1,"why":"An intended change means a new reference. The diff in the pull request is how the team approves it."},{"q":"Why record and verify on the same machine?","options":["Gradle refuses otherwise","It is faster","Fonts, OS and hardware change rendering slightly"],"a":2,"why":"Small rendering differences make pixel-perfect comparisons fail across machines."},{"q":"What is the risk of a high tolerance?","options":["A small real regression can pass","Tests run slower","More reference images"],"a":0,"why":"Anything under the threshold passes, including a missing icon or a 1 dp shift."},{"q":"Which change is a screenshot test most likely to catch that assertIsDisplayed() misses?","options":["A button that opens the wrong screen","Padding that shrank and a title that now wraps","A ViewModel that never loads"],"a":1,"why":"A screenshot compares the whole picture: colors, sizes, spacing and fonts. Behavior tests check that nodes exist and act right."},{"q":"You have 3 themes, 4 font scales, 5 widths and 6 languages. Which set of cases fits best?","options":["All 360 combinations","Default theme only","A few that break differently: dark theme, largest font scale, narrowest width, longest language"],"a":2,"why":"Each extra case adds images to review on every change. Pick the ones that can fail in their own way."}]},{"id":"hilt-testing","title":"Hilt in tests","tagline":"Swap a real binding for a fake in the graph the test app uses.","level":"advanced","remember":"@HiltAndroidTest plus HiltAndroidRule first. Replace modules with @TestInstallIn; use @UninstallModules only when one class needs something different.","terms":[{"term":"@HiltAndroidTest","def":"Marks a test class that uses Hilt, so Hilt generates components for it."},{"term":"HiltAndroidRule","def":"The JUnit rule that sets up the Hilt components for each test and injects the test’s @Inject fields."},{"term":"@TestInstallIn","def":"Marks a test module that replaces a production module for every test in the source set."},{"term":"@BindValue","def":"Binds a field of the test class into the Hilt graph under its declared type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Every test in <code>androidTest</code> should use <code>FakeNewsApi</code>. What do you write?","options":["@UninstallModules in each test class","One module with @TestInstallIn(replaces = [NetworkModule::class])","A @BindValue field in each class"],"a":1,"why":"One test module replaces the binding everywhere, with no extra components per class."},{"q":"Why prefer @TestInstallIn over @UninstallModules?","options":["Hilt generates new components for each class that uses @UninstallModules, which slows builds","@UninstallModules is deprecated","@TestInstallIn tests run faster on the device"],"a":0,"why":"The Hilt docs warn about the build time cost and recommend @TestInstallIn whenever possible."},{"q":"What does <code>hiltRule.inject()</code> do?","options":["Launches the Activity","Replaces the production modules","Fills the @Inject fields of the test class"],"a":2,"why":"Module replacement happens at compile time. inject() populates the test’s own fields."},{"q":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere\">@get:Rule(order = 0) val compose = createAndroidComposeRule&lt;MainActivity&gt;()\n@get:Rule(order = 1) val hilt = HiltAndroidRule(this)</pre>What is wrong?","options":["Nothing","The Activity launches before Hilt is set up; the Hilt rule needs order = 0","Rules cannot have an order"],"a":1,"why":"The Compose rule starts the Activity, which asks Hilt for its dependencies. HiltAndroidRule must run before every other rule."},{"q":"An instrumented @HiltAndroidTest crashes because the app runs your @HiltAndroidApp class. What fixes it?","options":["A custom test runner that creates HiltTestApplication, set as testInstrumentationRunner","Add @AndroidEntryPoint to the test","Call hiltRule.inject() earlier"],"a":0,"why":"Hilt tests must run in HiltTestApplication. For instrumented tests a small runner creates it; for Robolectric you set it with @Config."}]},{"id":"baseline","title":"Baseline Profiles and Macrobenchmark","tagline":"Compile the startup path ahead of time, then measure it.","level":"advanced","remember":"A Baseline Profile turns the startup path into AOT machine code at install. Prove it with Macrobenchmark on a release build.","terms":[{"term":"Baseline Profile","def":"A list of classes and methods shipped with the app that the system compiles ahead of time at install."},{"term":"Macrobenchmark","def":"A Jetpack library that measures real app behavior such as startup and scrolling by driving a release build on a device."},{"term":"Cold start","def":"A launch where the app process does not exist yet, so the system must create it before drawing the first frame."},{"term":"JIT vs AOT","def":"JIT compiles methods while the app runs; AOT compiles them to machine code before the app runs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a Baseline Profile contain?","options":["Machine code for the whole app","A list of classes and methods to compile ahead of time","Recorded frame timings"],"a":1,"why":"It is a rule list. The device compiles the listed code to machine code at install."},{"q":"What does <code>CompilationMode.None()</code> measure?","options":["Startup with no AOT compilation, the worst case","Startup with the Baseline Profile","Startup with everything compiled"],"a":0,"why":"It resets compilation, so code runs interpreted and JIT-compiled. Compare it with <code>Partial()</code> to see the profile’s gain."},{"q":"Why benchmark a non-debuggable release build?","options":["Debuggable builds skip optimizations, so the numbers are not real","Macrobenchmark only installs from Play"],"a":0,"why":"Users run release builds. Debuggable builds run slower in ways that hide or invent problems."},{"q":"The app is installed outside Play, so the profile is not applied at install. What still gets the Baseline Profile onto the device?","options":["Cloud Profiles","androidx.profileinstaller, which writes it on the first run","Nothing, the profile is lost"],"a":1,"why":"profileinstaller covers installs that skip install-time compilation, and Macrobenchmark uses it too. Keep it in the app."},{"q":"Why not use CompilationMode.Full() as the “with profile” side of a startup benchmark?","options":["Full() is slower than None()","Full() compiles the whole app, which no user’s device does, so it hides how good your profile is","Full() needs a debuggable build"],"a":1,"why":"Partial() installs your Baseline Profile like a real install would, so comparing it with None() shows what the profile gains."}]}]},{"id":"perf","title":"Performance and debugging","description":"Find out why an app janks, freezes, leaks or starts slowly, and fix it with the right tool.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/perf.js","lessons":[{"id":"frame","title":"The 16 ms frame","tagline":"Every frame has a deadline. Miss it and the user sees a stutter.","level":"intermediate","remember":"60 Hz = 16.7 ms, 120 Hz = 8.3 ms. The main thread is for UI. Everything else goes off it.","terms":[{"term":"Frame budget","def":"The time available to produce one frame: 1000 ms divided by the refresh rate, so 16.7 ms at 60 Hz."},{"term":"Jank","def":"A visible stutter caused by frames that miss their vsync deadline, so the screen repeats an old frame."},{"term":"Vsync","def":"The display refresh signal that marks each frame deadline; Choreographer schedules app frames on it."},{"term":"RenderThread","def":"A per-app thread that takes the drawing commands recorded on the main thread and sends them to the GPU."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the frame budget at 120 Hz?","options":["16.7 ms","11.1 ms","8.3 ms"],"a":2,"why":"1000 ms / 120 = 8.3 ms. High refresh screens leave less room for main-thread work."},{"q":"A frame takes 25 ms on a 60 Hz screen. What does the user see?","options":["The frame shows 9 ms late but smoothly","The previous frame stays up for an extra vsync, a stutter","The app shows an ANR dialog"],"a":1,"why":"The frame missed its deadline, so the display repeats the old one. An ANR takes seconds, not milliseconds."},{"q":"Which of these runs on the main thread?","options":["Compose composition and layout","Sending draw commands to the GPU","Work inside <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Composition, layout and recording draw commands are main-thread work. RenderThread talks to the GPU."},{"q":"Put one frame in order, from the vsync signal to the screen.","options":["Input, animation, traversal (measure, layout, draw) on main, then RenderThread issues GPU work, then SurfaceFlinger composites","Traversal on main, then input and animation, then RenderThread, then SurfaceFlinger composites","Input on main, then RenderThread draws pixels, then main runs layout, then SurfaceFlinger composites"],"a":0,"why":"Choreographer runs input, animation and traversal on the main thread. Draw only records display lists, RenderThread turns them into GPU work, and SurfaceFlinger puts the buffer on screen."},{"q":"A scroll on a 60 Hz phone has 95 frames at 8 ms and 5 frames at 60 ms. The average is 10.6 ms. What is the right reading?","options":["It is smooth, because the average is well under 16.7 ms","The user sees five hitches; look at P95 or P99 and frameOverrunMs, not the average","About 10% of the time is lost evenly across every frame"],"a":1,"why":"An average hides the slow end. Five frames far over budget are five visible stutters, which percentiles show and the mean does not."}]},{"id":"main-thread","title":"The main thread: Looper, Handler, MessageQueue","tagline":"One thread, one queue, one message at a time.","level":"intermediate","remember":"The main thread is a loop over one queue. Every message waits for the one before it, so keep each one short.","terms":[{"term":"Main thread","def":"The thread the system creates for your app at launch. It dispatches input, runs lifecycle callbacks and draws the UI."},{"term":"Looper","def":"Runs a message loop for one thread: it takes messages from the thread’s MessageQueue and handles them one by one."},{"term":"MessageQueue","def":"The time-ordered list of messages and Runnables waiting to run on a Looper’s thread."},{"term":"Handler","def":"Posts messages and Runnables to one Looper’s queue, now or after a delay, and can remove them again."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A 300 ms task is running on the main thread when the user taps. What happens to the tap?","options":["It interrupts the task","It waits in the queue until the task finishes","It is dropped"],"a":1,"why":"The Looper runs one message at a time. Everything behind a slow message waits."},{"q":"What does <code>postDelayed(r, 500)</code> promise?","options":["r runs exactly 500 ms from now","r runs within 500 ms","r runs no earlier than 500 ms from now, later if the queue is busy"],"a":2,"why":"After 500 ms the message is ready, but it still waits for whatever is running."},{"q":"Why is <code>Handler()</code> with no arguments deprecated?","options":["It picks the current thread’s Looper implicitly, which causes bugs","Handlers are replaced by coroutines","It leaks memory on every post"],"a":0,"why":"Passing the Looper, like <code>Looper.getMainLooper()</code>, makes the target thread explicit."},{"q":"This runs on the main thread inside an Activity. What is logged, in order?<pre class=\"code mini\">handler.post { log(\"A\") }\nlog(\"B\")\nlifecycleScope.launch { log(\"C\") }</pre>","options":["A, B, C","B, A, C","B, C, A"],"a":2,"why":"post() queues A behind the current message. lifecycleScope uses Dispatchers.Main.immediate, so C runs right away because you are already on the main thread."},{"q":"A HandlerThread has one message due now and one posted with a 5 second delay. You call <code>quitSafely()</code>. What happens?","options":["Both messages run, then the thread ends","The due message runs; the delayed one is dropped","Both are dropped and the thread ends at once"],"a":1,"why":"quitSafely() handles the messages that are already due and drops the delayed ones. quit() would drop everything."}]},{"id":"anr","title":"ANRs and StrictMode","tagline":"Block the main thread for 5 seconds and Android offers to kill your app.","level":"intermediate","remember":"No input handled for 5 s = ANR. StrictMode in debug catches disk and network on main first.","terms":[{"term":"ANR","def":"Application Not Responding: the system reports an app whose main thread failed to handle input or finish a component callback in time."},{"term":"Input dispatch timeout","def":"The 5 second limit for the main thread to handle a touch or key event before the system raises an ANR."},{"term":"StrictMode","def":"A developer tool that detects slow or risky calls, such as disk access on the main thread, and reports them with a stack trace."},{"term":"NetworkOnMainThreadException","def":"The exception Android throws when an app opens a network connection on the main thread."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How long can the main thread leave a tap unhandled before an ANR?","options":["16 ms","1 second","5 seconds"],"a":2,"why":"The input dispatch timeout is 5 seconds. 16 ms is the frame budget, which causes jank, not an ANR."},{"q":"Where should StrictMode penalties be turned on?","options":["Debug builds","Release builds, to catch users’ problems","Only in unit tests"],"a":0,"why":"StrictMode adds overhead and <code>penaltyDeath()</code> crashes on purpose. Use it while developing, and use Play Console vitals for release."},{"q":"You call <code>URL(url).readText()</code> on the main thread. What happens?","options":["It works but may jank","<code>NetworkOnMainThreadException</code> is thrown","An ANR after 5 seconds, always"],"a":1,"why":"Android blocks network on the main thread by default for apps targeting API 11 and up. Disk reads are allowed, which is why StrictMode exists."},{"q":"An ANR trace shows the main thread in state Blocked, waiting for a lock that a worker holds while it writes a file. StrictMode was on in that debug build and reported nothing. Why?","options":["StrictMode only reports network calls","The main thread never touched the disk itself; it only waited on a lock","StrictMode only reports problems that last longer than 5 seconds"],"a":1,"why":"StrictMode flags disk and network calls made on the main thread. Waiting on a lock is not one of them. Do the I/O first and take the lock only to swap in the result."},{"q":"Which line makes this screen prone to an ANR?<pre class=\"code mini\">override fun onCreate(s: Bundle?) {   // 1\n  super.onCreate(s)                    // 2\n  val u = runBlocking { repo.load() }  // 3\n  setContent { Profile(u) }            // 4\n}</pre>","options":["Line 2","Line 3","Line 4"],"a":1,"why":"runBlocking parks the main thread until the load finishes, so input and frames wait behind it. Load in a coroutine and show a loading state instead."}]},{"id":"leaks","title":"Memory leaks","tagline":"A destroyed Activity that something still points at can never be freed.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Long-lived things must not hold short-lived things. Register in onStart, unregister in onStop.","terms":[{"term":"Memory leak","def":"Memory the app no longer needs that the garbage collector can’t free because a reachable object still references it."},{"term":"GC root","def":"A starting point for garbage collection, such as a static field or a live thread; anything reachable from one stays in memory."},{"term":"LeakCanary","def":"A debug-only library that detects retained Activities, Fragments and ViewModels and prints the reference chain that keeps them alive."},{"term":"applicationContext","def":"The Context of the Application object, which lives as long as the process and is safe to keep in singletons."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a singleton holding an Activity leak?","options":["The singleton is reachable from a GC root, so the Activity it points at stays reachable too","Singletons copy the Activity on each rotation","The Activity holds the singleton"],"a":0,"why":"Reachability flows from the root through the singleton to the Activity, so the GC must keep it."},{"q":"What should a long-lived object keep when it needs a Context?","options":["The current Activity","<code>context.applicationContext</code>","A View’s context"],"a":1,"why":"The application context lives as long as the process, so holding it leaks nothing."},{"q":"In a LeakCanary trace, what does <code>Leaking: YES</code> on <code>MapActivity</code> mean?","options":["It is destroyed but still reachable","It is still on screen","It threw an exception"],"a":0,"why":"LeakCanary knows the Activity was destroyed, so being reachable means it leaked. Look up the trace for the reference to cut."},{"q":"An Activity and its presenter reference each other. After a rotation nothing else points at either of them. Is the old Activity leaked?","options":["Yes, the cycle keeps both alive","No, the GC traces from roots, so an unreachable cycle is freed","Only until LeakCanary forces a GC"],"a":1,"why":"ART does not count references. It marks what it can reach from GC roots and frees the rest, cycles included. A leak needs a path from a root."},{"q":"A Fragment keeps <code>private var binding: FeedBinding?</code> and goes onto the back stack. What prevents its old view tree from staying in memory?","options":["Set binding = null in onDestroyView()","Set binding = null in onDestroy()","Nothing, the Fragment clears view fields itself"],"a":0,"why":"A back-stack Fragment outlives its view. Clearing the field in onDestroyView lets the old view tree go now, not when the Fragment is finally destroyed."}]},{"id":"profiler","title":"Android Studio Profiler","tagline":"Measure first. The slow part is rarely where you guess.","level":"advanced","remember":"System trace for jank and startup, method trace for hot code, heap dump for memory. Profile release-like builds.","terms":[{"term":"System trace","def":"A Perfetto recording of all threads, CPU scheduling and frames over time, used to find jank and slow startup."},{"term":"Method trace","def":"A recording of which Java and Kotlin functions ran and for how long, shown as call charts and flame charts."},{"term":"Flame chart","def":"A chart that merges identical call stacks, with bar width showing total time, so the widest bars are the hottest code paths."},{"term":"Heap dump","def":"A snapshot of every object in the app heap with its size and references, used to find leaks and memory hogs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In a flame chart, what does a bar’s width show?","options":["When the function ran","Total time spent in that call stack","How many objects it allocated"],"a":1,"why":"Flame charts merge identical stacks and size them by time. Use a call chart to see order over time."},{"q":"Which recording shows dropped frames, every thread and waits on locks?","options":["System trace","Heap dump","Allocation tracking"],"a":0,"why":"A system trace shows the timeline of all threads and frames. Method traces only show function timing."},{"q":"Why profile a profileable release build instead of debug?","options":["Debug builds skip optimizations and add overhead, so timings are wrong","The Profiler can’t attach to debug builds","Release builds log more"],"a":0,"why":"Debuggable builds run slower in ways users never see. You end up fixing the wrong thing."},{"q":"In a system trace, a slow frame shows the main thread mostly Sleeping, not Running. What is the next step?","options":["Record a method trace to find the slow function","Find what main waits for, like a lock, a binder reply or a message","Switch to a sampled recording with a shorter interval"],"a":1,"why":"Sleeping means the thread waits for something. That is a waiting problem, and a method trace of CPU work will not show it."},{"q":"A traced recording says a one-line getter called 50,000 times takes 30% of the time. A sampled recording barely shows it. Which is closer to the truth?","options":["The traced one, because it counts every call exactly","The sampled one, because tracing adds a hook cost to every call and inflates tiny functions","Neither; the getter is inlined and should not appear at all"],"a":1,"why":"Traced recordings hook every entry and exit, so many tiny calls look much slower than they are. Confirm with a benchmark before you optimize."},{"q":"What is wrong with this trace section?<pre class=\"code mini\">suspend fun load() = trace(\"load\") {\n  val a = api.fetch()   // suspends\n  db.save(a)\n}</pre>","options":["trace { } cannot be used from Kotlin","After fetch() suspends, the coroutine may resume on another thread and close the wrong slice","The label has to be built with a string template"],"a":1,"why":"A normal section must begin and end on the same thread. Across a suspension point, use an async section, which is matched by name and cookie."}]},{"id":"compose-perf","title":"Compose performance debugging","tagline":"Count recompositions, read the compiler’s verdict, then fix the one that matters.","level":"advanced","remember":"Inspector counts show what runs too often. Compiler reports show why. key, derivedStateOf and stable types fix most of it.","terms":[{"term":"Layout Inspector","def":"An Android Studio tool that shows the live UI tree of a running app, including recomposition and skip counts per composable."},{"term":"Recomposition count","def":"How many times a composable has run again since it first appeared, as shown in the Layout Inspector."},{"term":"Compose compiler reports","def":"Text files from the Compose compiler that list each class’s stability and each composable’s skippable and restartable status."},{"term":"Composition tracing","def":"A runtime-tracing dependency that adds composable function names to system traces so you can see how long each one takes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you see per-composable recomposition and skip counts?","options":["Layout Inspector","Logcat","The APK Analyzer"],"a":0,"why":"The Layout Inspector shows both counts live while you use the app."},{"q":"A compiler report shows <code>unstable item: Item</code>. What does that mean for <code>ItemRow</code>?","options":["It skips only when it gets the same <code>Item</code> instance","It never compiles","It always skips"],"a":0,"why":"With strong skipping, unstable parameters are compared by instance. A new but equal object still triggers recomposition."},{"q":"When does <code>derivedStateOf</code> help?","options":["When the input changes much more often than the result","For every piece of state","Only for network results"],"a":0,"why":"Scroll position changes constantly, but “show the button” flips rarely. Readers only recompose when the derived value changes."},{"q":"<code>x</code> is an animated State that changes every frame. Which modifier runs only layout and draw, not composition, on each frame?","options":["<code>Modifier.offset(x = x.value.dp)</code>","<code>Modifier.offset { IntOffset(x.value.toInt(), 0) }</code>","<code>Modifier.padding(start = x.value.dp)</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The lambda version reads the state during layout, so composition is skipped. The other two read it in the composable body, which runs all three phases."},{"q":"Which line causes endless recomposition?<pre class=\"code mini\">@Composable fun Seen() {\n  var n by remember { mutableIntStateOf(0) } // 1\n  Text(\"Seen $n times\")                      // 2\n  n++                                        // 3\n}</pre>","options":["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3"],"a":2,"why":"This is a backwards write: composition writes the state it just read, which schedules it again, forever. Change state in event handlers or effects."}]},{"id":"startup","title":"App startup","tagline":"Cold, warm, hot, and everything Application.onCreate makes the user wait for.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Cold start pays for Application.onCreate. Init only what the first frame needs, the rest lazily.","terms":[{"term":"Warm start","def":"A launch where the app process is still alive but the Activity has to be created again."},{"term":"Hot start","def":"A launch where the Activity is still in memory and only needs to come back to the foreground."},{"term":"TTID","def":"Time to initial display: from launch until the first frame, printed in Logcat as Displayed."},{"term":"App Startup","def":"A Jetpack library that runs component initializers from one shared ContentProvider in a declared order."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The process is alive but the user backed out of the Activity. Next launch is a…","options":["Cold start","Warm start","Hot start"],"a":1,"why":"The process survives, so the Application is reused, but the Activity is created again."},{"q":"Where must <code>installSplashScreen()</code> be called?","options":["Before <code>super.onCreate()</code>","In <code>onResume()</code>","In <code>Application.onCreate()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It has to set up the splash theme before the Activity inflates its own."},{"q":"Which belongs in <code>Application.onCreate</code>?","options":["Crash reporting","Downloading the feed","Building every repository"],"a":0,"why":"Crash reporting must catch startup crashes. Everything else can wait for the screen that needs it."},{"q":"In a cold start, which order does your code run in on the main thread?","options":["attachBaseContext(), every ContentProvider.onCreate(), Application.onCreate(), then the Activity and its first frame","Application.onCreate(), every ContentProvider.onCreate(), attachBaseContext(), then the Activity","attachBaseContext(), Application.onCreate(), the Activity, then every ContentProvider.onCreate()"],"a":0,"why":"Providers, App Startup’s included, run after attachBaseContext and before Application.onCreate. All of it happens before the Activity draws."},{"q":"Your app has a sync service declared with <code>android:process=\":sync\"</code>. Analytics shows two app starts every time it syncs. Why?","options":["App Startup runs each initializer twice","The :sync process creates its own Application, so onCreate runs your startup again there","Hilt builds a second copy of every singleton on sync"],"a":1,"why":"Every process runs Application.onCreate. Check which process you are in and set up only what that process needs."}]},{"id":"r8","title":"Shrinking with R8","tagline":"Smaller, faster release builds, and the keep rules that stop them breaking.","level":"advanced","remember":"R8 removes and renames what it can’t see used. Reflection needs keep rules. Keep mapping.txt for every release.","terms":[{"term":"R8","def":"The Android compiler step that shrinks, optimizes and obfuscates release code while converting it to DEX."},{"term":"Keep rule","def":"A line in a ProGuard rules file that stops R8 from removing or renaming the classes and members it matches."},{"term":"Obfuscation","def":"Renaming classes, methods and fields to short meaningless names to make code smaller and harder to read."},{"term":"mapping.txt","def":"The file R8 writes for each build that maps obfuscated names back to the original ones, used to retrace crash stack traces."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>mapping.txt</code> for?","options":["Turning obfuscated stack traces back into real names","Listing which resources to keep","Signing the APK"],"a":0,"why":"It maps <code>a.b.c</code> back to your class and method names. Upload it with every release."},{"q":"Why does a class loaded with <code>Class.forName</code> need a keep rule?","options":["R8 can’t see reflection, so the class looks unused and is removed","Reflection only works in debug","Keep rules make reflection faster"],"a":0,"why":"R8 follows direct references from entry points. A class name in a string is invisible to it."},{"q":"Which build should you test before shipping?","options":["The minified release build","Debug is enough","Only unit tests"],"a":0,"why":"Missing keep rules only break the R8-processed build. Debug builds never run R8."},{"q":"Gson parses a JSON response fine in debug. In the minified release build every field comes back null or default. What happened?","options":["Gson does not run on release builds","R8 renamed the fields, so the JSON names no longer match them","isShrinkResources removed the JSON from the APK"],"a":1,"why":"Gson reads fields by name through reflection, which R8 cannot see. Keep the model’s field names, or use a codegen library like kotlinx.serialization."},{"q":"A class is used normally, so R8 keeps it anyway. You only need its field names to stay unrenamed. Which option is the narrowest fit?","options":["<code>-keep class Model { *; }</code>","<code>-keepclassmembers class Model { &lt;fields&gt;; }</code>","<code>-keepclasseswithmembers class Model { &lt;fields&gt;; }</code>"],"a":1,"why":"-keepclassmembers keeps the listed members only if the class survives shrinking anyway. -keep would also force the class itself to stay."}]}]},{"id":"media","title":"Media, camera and sensors","description":"Play audio and video with Media3, capture and analyze frames with CameraX, and read sensors and location without draining the battery.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/media.js","lessons":[{"id":"exoplayer","title":"Playing media with Media3 ExoPlayer","tagline":"Build, prepare, play, release. Four states in between.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Build, setMediaItem, prepare, play. Release when the screen stops.","terms":[{"term":"ExoPlayer","def":"The Media3 implementation of the Player interface that loads, decodes and plays audio and video."},{"term":"MediaItem","def":"A Media3 description of one piece of media to play, built from a URI plus optional metadata and settings."},{"term":"playWhenReady","def":"The player flag that says playback should run whenever the player is in STATE_READY; play() and pause() set it."},{"term":"Playback state","def":"One of STATE_IDLE, STATE_BUFFERING, STATE_READY or STATE_ENDED, reported to Player.Listener.onPlaybackStateChanged."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The state is <code>STATE_BUFFERING</code> and <code>playWhenReady</code> is true. What is <code>isPlaying</code>?","options":["true","false","It depends on the network"],"a":1,"why":"<code>isPlaying</code> needs STATE_READY too. Playback resumes on its own once enough data has loaded."},{"q":"Where should a screen’s player be released?","options":["In <code>onPause</code>","When the screen stops, such as <code>onStop</code> or <code>onStopOrDispose</code>","Never, the garbage collector handles it"],"a":1,"why":"Since Android 7 apps can be visible in multi-window while paused, so stop is the right moment. The garbage collector does not free codecs."},{"q":"Playback reached <code>STATE_ENDED</code>. Your button calls <code>player.play()</code>. What happens?","options":["It restarts from the beginning","playWhenReady becomes true but the state stays ENDED","It throws"],"a":1,"why":"Seek first, for example <code>seekTo(0)</code>. Media3’s <code>Util.handlePlayButtonAction()</code> does that for you."},{"q":"A playlist is 40 seconds into item 3. The user taps previous, which calls <code>seekToPrevious()</code>. What happens?","options":["Playback moves to item 2","Item 3 restarts from the beginning","Playback moves to item 1"],"a":1,"why":"seekToPrevious() goes back an item only near the start. More than 3 seconds in, it restarts the current item."},{"q":"onPlayerError() reports ERROR_CODE_IO_BAD_HTTP_STATUS for a 404. What should the app do?","options":["Call prepare() again until it plays","Show a message, because a retry will fail the same way","Call play(), since the player is still READY"],"a":1,"why":"A missing file stays missing. Retry buttons fit network errors like ERROR_CODE_IO_NETWORK_CONNECTION_FAILED, where prepare() can continue from the same position."}]},{"id":"session","title":"Background playback with MediaSessionService","tagline":"The service owns the player. Everything else talks to the session.","level":"advanced","remember":"Player and MediaSession live in the service. The UI holds a MediaController.","terms":[{"term":"MediaSession","def":"A Media3 object that exposes a player to the system, notifications, headsets and other devices so they can control it."},{"term":"MediaSessionService","def":"A Media3 service base class that hosts MediaSessions, shows the media notification and manages its own foreground state."},{"term":"MediaController","def":"A Player implementation that sends commands to a MediaSession in another component or process."},{"term":"SessionToken","def":"A handle that identifies a MediaSession or MediaSessionService so a MediaController can connect to it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user pauses, then presses Home. What does Media3 do with the service?","options":["Keeps it in the foreground forever","Takes it out of the foreground; the notification stays and can be dismissed","Destroys it at once"],"a":1,"why":"A paused service does not need foreground priority. The system may stop it later, so keep state you need to resume."},{"q":"How does a Bluetooth headset’s play button reach your player?","options":["A BroadcastReceiver you write","Through the MediaSession","Through the Activity’s onKeyDown"],"a":1,"why":"Media buttons go to the active MediaSession, which calls play or pause on its player."},{"q":"What should the Activity use to control playback?","options":["A second ExoPlayer","A <code>MediaController</code> built from a <code>SessionToken</code>","Static access to the service’s player field"],"a":1,"why":"The controller talks to the session, so the UI and the system share one player."},{"q":"Which lifecycle fits a MediaController in an Activity?","options":["onStart: buildAsync(), use it when the future completes; onStop: MediaController.releaseFuture(future)","onCreate: buildAsync() and get() the future; onDestroy: release the player","onStart: buildAsync(); onStop: nothing, the service cleans up controllers"],"a":0,"why":"A connected controller keeps the service bound. Release it in onStop, or every visit leaves one more controller keeping the service and its player alive."},{"q":"After a phone restart, the user taps play on your app in the Quick Settings media controls. You never implemented <code>onPlaybackResumption()</code>. What happens?","options":["Media3 restores the last queue and position by itself","Nothing plays, because the default implementation fails","The system opens your Activity instead"],"a":1,"why":"The session remembers nothing across a restart. Save the queue and position as you play, and return them from onPlaybackResumption()."},{"q":"You add a “forward 15 s” CommandButton to the media notification and allow it in onConnect. Where does the press arrive?","options":["onCustomCommand on the session callback","A BroadcastReceiver you register for the button","Player.Listener.onEvents on the controller"],"a":0,"why":"Media3 routes the SessionCommand to onCustomCommand. The same command works from the notification, Android Auto and your own UI."}]},{"id":"audiofocus","title":"Audio focus and audio attributes","tagline":"Pause for calls, duck for directions, stop when headphones come out.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Turn on handleAudioFocus and becoming-noisy handling, and ExoPlayer pauses, ducks and resumes for you.","terms":[{"term":"Audio focus","def":"The Android system that decides which app should be heard and tells the others to pause, duck or stop."},{"term":"Ducking","def":"Lowering your playback volume for a short sound from another app, such as a navigation prompt, then restoring it."},{"term":"AudioAttributes","def":"A description of a sound’s usage and content type that the system uses for routing, volume and audio focus decisions."},{"term":"Becoming noisy","def":"The ACTION_AUDIO_BECOMING_NOISY broadcast sent when audio output is about to move to the speaker, for example when headphones are unplugged."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone call comes in during playback. <code>handleAudioFocus</code> is true. What happens?","options":["Playback continues quietly","It pauses, then resumes when the call ends","It pauses and stays paused"],"a":1,"why":"A call is a transient loss. ExoPlayer suppresses playback and resumes on AUDIOFOCUS_GAIN."},{"q":"Another music app starts playing. What does ExoPlayer do?","options":["Ducks to 20%","Pauses and waits for the user to press play","Keeps playing"],"a":1,"why":"A permanent loss sets playWhenReady to false. Resuming later would surprise the user."},{"q":"A podcast player uses <code>AUDIO_CONTENT_TYPE_SPEECH</code>. A navigation prompt asks other apps to duck. What does ExoPlayer do?","options":["Ducks the volume","Pauses briefly instead of ducking","Ignores it"],"a":1,"why":"Lowered speech under another voice is hard to follow, so ExoPlayer pauses speech instead of ducking it."},{"q":"An alarm clock app plays its ringtone with <code>USAGE_MEDIA</code>. At night the user turned media volume down to zero. What happens at 07:00?","options":["It rings at alarm volume anyway","It stays silent, because it follows the media volume","It only vibrates"],"a":1,"why":"The usage picks the volume slider. USAGE_ALARM would follow the alarm volume and still sound when media is muted."}]},{"id":"camerax","title":"CameraX basics","tagline":"Pick use cases, bind them to a lifecycle, let CameraX open and close the camera.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Build use cases, bindToLifecycle, and the lifecycle opens and closes the camera.","terms":[{"term":"ProcessCameraProvider","def":"The CameraX singleton that binds use cases to a lifecycle and manages the cameras for the whole process."},{"term":"Use case","def":"A CameraX building block for one job, such as Preview, ImageCapture, ImageAnalysis or VideoCapture."},{"term":"CameraSelector","def":"A CameraX filter that picks which camera to use, such as DEFAULT_BACK_CAMERA or DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA."},{"term":"CameraXViewfinder","def":"The camera-compose composable that displays the frames of a Preview use case from its SurfaceRequest."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The viewfinder is open and the user presses Home. Use cases were bound to the Activity’s lifecycle. What happens to the camera?","options":["It stays open until you close it","CameraX closes it on stop and reopens it on start","The app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Binding to a lifecycle means CameraX follows ON_START and ON_STOP for you."},{"q":"How do the frames from <code>Preview</code> reach <code>CameraXViewfinder</code>?","options":["Through a Bitmap you post to state","Preview’s SurfaceProvider receives a SurfaceRequest, which the viewfinder draws into","The viewfinder opens the camera itself"],"a":1,"why":"The SurfaceRequest is the handoff. Keep it in state and pass it to the composable."},{"q":"Which is the right way to switch from the back to the front camera?","options":["Call bindToLifecycle again with the front selector","<code>unbindAll()</code>, then bindToLifecycle with the front selector","Create a second ProcessCameraProvider"],"a":1,"why":"A use case can only be bound to one camera at a time. Unbind first, then bind to the new lens."}]},{"id":"video-capture","title":"Recording video with CameraX","tagline":"VideoCapture, a Recorder, and a stream of events that ends in Finalize.","level":"intermediate","remember":"prepareRecording, start, and wait for Finalize. Check hasError() there before you tell the user the video is saved.","terms":[{"term":"VideoCapture","def":"The CameraX use case for video. It feeds camera frames to a video output such as a Recorder."},{"term":"Recorder","def":"The CameraX video output that encodes audio and video and writes the file. It runs one recording at a time."},{"term":"PendingRecording","def":"A prepared recording with its output chosen. Nothing is recorded until you call start(), which returns a Recording."},{"term":"VideoRecordEvent","def":"Events sent to the recording listener: Start, Status, Pause, Resume and Finalize."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>prepareRecording()</code> give you?","options":["A PendingRecording that records nothing until start()","A Recording that is already running","The finished file"],"a":0,"why":"Preparing only picks the output and options. start() returns the active Recording."},{"q":"The user presses Home while recording (not a persistent recording). What does the listener get?","options":["Pause, and recording resumes later","Finalize with ERROR_SOURCE_INACTIVE, and a file with the frames so far","Nothing until the app returns"],"a":1,"why":"The camera closes on ON_STOP, so the recording ends. The frames before that are saved."},{"q":"You call <code>start()</code> while another recording on the same Recorder is still active. What happens?","options":["Both record to separate files","The first one stops on its own","It throws IllegalStateException"],"a":2,"why":"A Recorder handles one recording at a time. Stop the first one before you start another."},{"q":"Which order records a video with sound?","options":["prepareRecording(output), withAudioEnabled(), start(listener), later stop(), then Finalize arrives","start(listener), prepareRecording(output), withAudioEnabled(), then Finalize arrives","prepareRecording(output), start(listener), withAudioEnabled(), later stop()"],"a":0,"why":"withAudioEnabled() is set on the PendingRecording before start(). start() returns the active Recording, and Finalize tells you the file is done."},{"q":"Finalize reports ERROR_FILE_SIZE_LIMIT_REACHED. What should you do with the file?","options":["Delete it, since any error means a broken file","Keep it: the data recorded before the limit is saved","Start a new recording into the same file to finish it"],"a":1,"why":"Size and duration limits still leave a usable video. ERROR_NO_VALID_DATA is the one where the file is not valid and should be deleted."}]},{"id":"analysis","title":"CameraX ImageAnalysis","tagline":"Thirty frames a second, one slow analyzer. Something has to give.","level":"advanced","remember":"KEEP_ONLY_LATEST keeps results fresh. Always close the ImageProxy.","terms":[{"term":"ImageAnalysis","def":"The CameraX use case that delivers camera frames to your code for processing instead of to the screen."},{"term":"ImageProxy","def":"A CameraX wrapper around one camera frame; it must be closed so the buffer can be reused."},{"term":"Backpressure strategy","def":"The ImageAnalysis setting that decides whether frames are dropped (KEEP_ONLY_LATEST) or queued (BLOCK_PRODUCER) when the analyzer is slow."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The analyzer takes 100 ms and the camera runs at 30 fps with <code>KEEP_ONLY_LATEST</code>. What do you get?","options":["All 30 frames, 3 seconds late","About 10 frames a second, each the newest one","Nothing, the camera stops"],"a":1,"why":"Frames that arrive while you are busy are dropped. When you finish, you get the latest one."},{"q":"What happens if the analyzer never calls <code>image.close()</code>?","options":["Nothing, the garbage collector closes it","CameraX stops delivering new frames to the analyzer","The preview turns black"],"a":1,"why":"The image buffer is not returned, so no new frame can be handed over."},{"q":"When does <code>STRATEGY_BLOCK_PRODUCER</code> make sense?","options":["A live barcode scanner","When you need consecutive frames and can accept growing delay","Always, it is faster"],"a":1,"why":"Queued frames keep their order, but every queued frame adds latency. Live UI usually wants KEEP_ONLY_LATEST."},{"q":"Text recognition finds nothing while the phone is held upright, but works when it is held sideways. What is the likely cause?","options":["The analysis resolution is too high","The frames arrive sideways and rotationDegrees is not passed to the detector","KEEP_ONLY_LATEST drops the frames that contain text"],"a":1,"why":"The sensor is mounted in landscape and CameraX does not rotate pixels. Pass image.imageInfo.rotationDegrees to the detector."},{"q":"Which line stops the analyzer from getting new frames?<pre class=\"code mini\">setAnalyzer(exec) { image -&gt;\n  val buf = image.planes[0].buffer   // 1\n  if (!ready) return@setAnalyzer     // 2\n  detect(buf)                        // 3\n  image.close()                      // 4\n}</pre>","options":["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3"],"a":1,"why":"The early return skips close(), so CameraX holds back the next frame. Close in a finally block, or on every path."},{"q":"Your own model needs RGB pixels as a Bitmap. Which setup fits best?","options":["Keep YUV_420_888 and write your own conversion","Ask for OUTPUT_IMAGE_FORMAT_RGBA_8888 and call toBitmap()","Take a photo with ImageCapture for every frame"],"a":1,"why":"CameraX converts each frame to RGBA for you, at some cost per frame. Keep the YUV default when ML Kit is the consumer."}]},{"id":"sensors","title":"Sensors","tagline":"Register when the screen shows, unregister when it goes. Sample as slowly as you can.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Unregister when the UI stops, and pick the slowest rate that looks right.","terms":[{"term":"SensorManager","def":"The system service that lists device sensors and registers listeners for their readings."},{"term":"Sampling period","def":"The requested delay between sensor events, such as SENSOR_DELAY_UI; the system treats it as a hint."},{"term":"Accelerometer","def":"A sensor that reports acceleration along the device’s x, y and z axes in m/s², including gravity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone lies flat on a table, screen up. What does the accelerometer read?","options":["x 0, y 0, z 0","x 0, y 0, z about 9.81","x 9.81, y 0, z 0"],"a":1,"why":"The accelerometer includes gravity, and z points out of the screen."},{"q":"You navigate from the tilt screen to another screen. The listener was registered with no unregister. What happens?","options":["Events stop on their own","Events keep coming and the sensor keeps drawing power","The app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"A registered listener keeps the sensor running. Unregister when the UI leaves."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 12 and asks for <code>SENSOR_DELAY_FASTEST</code> without <code>HIGH_SAMPLING_RATE_SENSORS</code>.","options":["It throws SecurityException","The rate is capped at 200 Hz","It falls back to NORMAL"],"a":1,"why":"Without the permission, listeners registered with SensorManager are limited to 200 Hz."},{"q":"A phone lies flat and still on a table. What does TYPE_LINEAR_ACCELERATION report on z?","options":["About 9.81 m/s²","About 0 m/s²","About -9.81 m/s²"],"a":1,"why":"Linear acceleration has gravity removed, so a still phone reads about 0. TYPE_GRAVITY would show the 9.81."},{"q":"A tilt game reads x as left and right. It works in portrait, but in landscape the ball rolls the wrong way. Why?","options":["The sampling rate is too slow for landscape","Sensor axes stay fixed to the natural orientation; remap them using the display rotation","Landscape mode needs TYPE_GRAVITY instead of the accelerometer"],"a":1,"why":"The screen rotates but the axes do not. Read the display rotation and remap, or use remapCoordinateSystem() for rotation matrices."}]},{"id":"location","title":"Location","tagline":"Ask for the accuracy you need, as often as you need it, only while you need it.","level":"advanced","remember":"Lowest priority that works, longest interval that works, foreground unless you truly need background.","terms":[{"term":"Fused location provider","def":"The Google Play services location API that combines GPS, Wi-Fi, cell and sensors to balance accuracy and battery."},{"term":"Approximate location","def":"A location fix granted by ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION, deliberately blurred to an area of a few square kilometers."},{"term":"Background location","def":"Location access while the app is not visible and runs no location foreground service; it needs ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION."},{"term":"Geofence","def":"A circular region the system watches for you, sending an enter or exit event to a PendingIntent when the device crosses it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You request FINE and COARSE together on Android 12. The user picks “Approximate”. What do you get?","options":["Precise location anyway","Only ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION, so fixes are approximate","Nothing, the request fails"],"a":1,"why":"The user decides the accuracy. Build features that still work with approximate location."},{"q":"Your app has while-in-use permission only, no foreground service, and the user presses Home. What happens to <code>requestLocationUpdates</code>?","options":["Updates continue as before","The app stops receiving updates while in the background","Updates switch to PASSIVE"],"a":1,"why":"While-in-use access ends when the app is no longer visible, unless a foreground service of type location is running."},{"q":"<code>addGeofences()</code> fails with <code>GEOFENCE_<wbr>INSUFFICIENT_<wbr>LOCATION_<wbr>PERMISSION</code>. Fine location is granted. What is missing?","options":["ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION","ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION","POST_NOTIFICATIONS"],"a":1,"why":"Geofences fire while the app is not running, so they need background location access."},{"q":"Right after a phone restart, <code>lastLocation</code> returns null. Why?","options":["The restart revoked the location permission","No fix has been computed since the restart; ask for a fresh one with getCurrentLocation()","lastLocation only works with ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"],"a":1,"why":"lastLocation returns a cached fix and is null when nothing has asked for location yet. getCurrentLocation() computes a new one."},{"q":"A run tracker sets a 10 second interval and <code>setMaxUpdateDelayMillis</code> of 2 minutes. How does the callback run?","options":["Every 10 seconds with one location","About every 2 minutes with about 12 locations, oldest first","About every 2 minutes with only the newest location"],"a":1,"why":"The provider collects fixes and delivers them together in result.locations. The track is the same and the app wakes far less often."},{"q":"Permission is granted, but the user switched off Location for the whole device. What should the app do?","options":["Request the location permission again","Call checkLocationSettings() and launch the ResolvableApiException resolution","Fall back to PRIORITY_PASSIVE"],"a":1,"why":"With device location off, requests succeed but no fixes arrive. The resolution shows a system dialog that turns Location on without leaving your app."}]}]},{"id":"push","title":"Notifications and push","description":"Notification channels, styles and actions, FCM tokens and messages, and deep links and App Links that land the user on the right screen.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/push.js","lessons":[{"id":"channels","title":"Notifications and channels","tagline":"The channel decides how loud it is. The user decides after that.","level":"beginner","remember":"You pick a channel’s importance once. After that it belongs to the user.","terms":[{"term":"NotificationChannel","def":"A named category of notifications (Android 8.0+) that carries the importance, sound and vibration the user can change in Settings."},{"term":"Notification importance","def":"A channel level from IMPORTANCE_NONE to IMPORTANCE_HIGH that decides sound, status bar icon and heads-up display."},{"term":"POST_NOTIFICATIONS","def":"The runtime permission an app needs on Android 13+ to post notifications to the shade."},{"term":"Heads-up notification","def":"A banner that briefly floats over the current app, shown only for IMPORTANCE_HIGH channels."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You shipped a channel as <code>IMPORTANCE_LOW</code>. The next release creates the same id with <code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code>. What do existing users get?","options":["HIGH: the new code wins","LOW: once created, importance belongs to the user","A crash on startup"],"a":1,"why":"Recreating an existing channel only updates its name and description. Importance and sound stay as they are."},{"q":"Which importance shows a heads-up banner?","options":["<code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code>","<code>IMPORTANCE_DEFAULT</code>","<code>IMPORTANCE_LOW</code>"],"a":0,"why":"DEFAULT makes a sound but does not pop up. Only HIGH shows the banner."},{"q":"On Android 14 the user denied <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>. Your code calls <code>notify()</code>. What happens?","options":["The notification shows anyway","Nothing is posted and no exception is thrown","It throws <code>SecurityException</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The system drops it quietly. Check the permission yourself so the UI can tell the user."}]},{"id":"styles","title":"Notification styles and actions","tagline":"A style decides what the expanded notification shows. Actions let the user answer without opening the app.","level":"intermediate","remember":"A style for the content, up to three actions, none that repeat the tap.","terms":[{"term":"NotificationCompat.Style","def":"The expanded layout of a notification, such as BigTextStyle, BigPictureStyle, InboxStyle or MessagingStyle, set with setStyle()."},{"term":"Notification action","def":"A button added with addAction() that fires its own PendingIntent; a notification shows up to three."},{"term":"RemoteInput","def":"An input attached to an action that lets the user type a reply in the notification; the text arrives in the action’s Intent."},{"term":"Live Update","def":"An Android 16 promoted ongoing notification shown at the top of the shade, on the lock screen and as a status bar chip."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You add five lines with <code>InboxStyle.addLine()</code>, then two more. How many show when expanded?","options":["All seven","Six","Five"],"a":1,"why":"InboxStyle shows up to six lines. Put a \"+1 more\" in the summary text if it matters."},{"q":"You set <code>setContentTitle(\"Maya\")</code> on a <code>MessagingStyle</code> notification. What does the user see?","options":["\"Maya\" as the title","The title is ignored; the style decides what shows","Both the title and the conversation"],"a":1,"why":"MessagingStyle ignores setContentTitle and setContentText. Senders come from the Person on each message."},{"q":"A download notification on a sound-making channel is updated ten times. How do you make it alert only once?","options":["Post each update with a new id","<code>setOnlyAlertOnce(true)</code>","Use <code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code>"],"a":1,"why":"setOnlyAlertOnce lets only the first post make sound or vibrate. New ids would stack ten cards."},{"q":"Inline replies arrive with no text. Which line is the bug?<pre class=\"code mini\">val pi = PendingIntent.getBroadcast(\n  ctx, chatId,                    // 1\n  replyIntent,                    // 2\n  PendingIntent.FLAG_IMMUTABLE)   // 3\nbuilder.addRemoteInput(input)     // 4</pre>","options":["Line 1","Line 3","Line 4"],"a":1,"why":"The system writes the reply into the action’s Intent, so the reply PendingIntent must be FLAG_MUTABLE and explicit. An immutable one cannot be filled in."},{"q":"Your receiver reads the inline reply and sends it, but the reply box in the notification keeps spinning. What is missing?","options":["setOnlyAlertOnce(true) on the original notification","Posting the notification again with the same id","Calling cancelAll() on the NotificationManager"],"a":1,"why":"The spinner stays until you update the notification. Post it again with the same id, for example with the sent message added."}]},{"id":"fcm","title":"Firebase Cloud Messaging basics","tagline":"A token per install, kept fresh on your server.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Every onNewToken goes to your server. Every 404 UNREGISTERED gets deleted.","terms":[{"term":"FCM registration token","def":"An id FCM gives one app install so a server can address messages to that device."},{"term":"onNewToken","def":"The FirebaseMessagingService callback that fires when a token is first created and whenever it changes. Deprecated since firebase-messaging 25.1.0 in favor of onRegistered."},{"term":"onMessageReceived","def":"The FirebaseMessagingService callback that receives data messages, and notification messages while the app is in the foreground."},{"term":"FCM HTTP v1 API","def":"The current server API for sending FCM messages, authorized with a short-lived OAuth 2.0 token from a service account."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these does <em>not</em> give the app a new token?","options":["The user clears app data","The app is reinstalled","The app is restored on a new phone","The app updates from the Play Store"],"a":3,"why":"A normal update keeps the token. Clearing data, reinstalling and restoring to a new device all create a new one."},{"q":"Your server sends to a stored token and FCM answers <code>404 UNREGISTERED</code>. What should it do?","options":["Retry with backoff","Delete the token","Ignore it and try again tomorrow"],"a":1,"why":"The install behind that token is gone or has a new token. Retrying never works."},{"q":"Why also send the token at login, not only in <code>onNewToken</code>?","options":["onNewToken only fires when the token changes, so a new user on the same install would never be linked","getToken is faster","onNewToken is deprecated"],"a":0,"why":"The token belongs to the install, not the user. Link it again whenever the signed-in user changes."},{"q":"Your server stores tokens with an auto-increment row id and no unique key on the token. Bob signs in on Ada’s old phone. What happens?","options":["Only Bob gets notifications on that phone","Two rows point at the phone, so Ada’s pushes keep landing on it","FCM rejects the second registration"],"a":1,"why":"A token belongs to one user at a time. Make the token the unique key and upsert on it, so Bob’s sign-in moves the row."},{"q":"sendEach returns UNAVAILABLE for token A and UNREGISTERED for token B. What should the server do?","options":["Delete both tokens","Retry A with exponential backoff, delete B","Retry both right away with the same request"],"a":1,"why":"UNAVAILABLE is temporary, so retry with backoff and honor Retry-After. UNREGISTERED means the token will never work again."}]},{"id":"messages","title":"Notification vs data messages","tagline":"Who handles the push depends on the payload and the app state.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Background plus a notification block means the system shows it and your code never runs.","terms":[{"term":"Notification message","def":"An FCM message with a notification block that the SDK displays automatically while the app is in the background."},{"term":"Data message","def":"An FCM message with only custom key-value data, always handed to onMessageReceived for your code to process."},{"term":"FCM message priority","def":"The android.priority field, high or normal, that decides whether FCM may wake a dozing device to deliver now."},{"term":"Doze","def":"An Android power mode for idle devices that defers network access and jobs to periodic maintenance windows."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A message has both <code>notification</code> and <code>data</code>. The app is in the background. Does <code>onMessageReceived</code> run?","options":["Yes, with both payloads","No: the system shows it, and the data goes into the launcher intent extras on tap","No, and the data is lost"],"a":1,"why":"In the background the notification block takes the system path. The data rides along in the launcher Activity’s intent."},{"q":"A <code>normal</code> priority data message reaches a device in Doze. What happens?","options":["Delivered at once","Held until a maintenance window or until the device wakes","Dropped"],"a":1,"why":"Only high priority can wake a dozing device. Normal waits."},{"q":"The user force-stopped your app in Settings. A high priority data message arrives. What runs?","options":["onMessageReceived","Nothing until the user opens the app again","The system shows it in the tray"],"a":1,"why":"Android does not deliver broadcasts, FCM included, to a force-stopped app."},{"q":"The phone is offline. You send five live score updates with the same collapse_key. When it reconnects, what arrives?","options":["All five, in order","Only the newest one","Nothing; onDeletedMessages() runs instead"],"a":1,"why":"FCM keeps just the newest message per collapse key. Non-collapsible messages would all be kept, up to 100."},{"q":"Your launcher Activity is singleTask and already running. The user taps a background notification message. You read the extras only in onCreate. What happens?","options":["The article opens as expected","The app comes forward but the article does not open, because the tap arrives in onNewIntent","The app crashes with a missing extra"],"a":1,"why":"With singleTop or singleTask, a running Activity gets the new Intent in onNewIntent. Handle it there too."}]},{"id":"deeplinks","title":"Pending intents and deep links","tagline":"A tap on a notification should land on the right screen with a sane Back.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Immutable, a unique request code, and a built back stack. Then the tap lands right.","terms":[{"term":"PendingIntent","def":"A token that lets another process, such as the system UI, perform an Intent later with your app’s identity."},{"term":"FLAG_IMMUTABLE","def":"A PendingIntent flag that stops the receiver from filling in the wrapped Intent; one of it or FLAG_MUTABLE is required on Android 12+."},{"term":"TaskStackBuilder","def":"A helper that builds a synthetic back stack of parent Activities so Back works after opening a deep screen from outside."},{"term":"Android App Links","def":"Verified https deep links (autoVerify plus assetlinks.json) that open your app directly instead of the browser."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app targets Android 14. You call <code>PendingIntent.getActivity(ctx, 0, intent, FLAG_UPDATE_CURRENT)</code>. What happens?","options":["It works","It throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>: specify FLAG_IMMUTABLE or FLAG_MUTABLE","It returns null"],"a":1,"why":"Since Android 12, every PendingIntent must declare its mutability."},{"q":"A notification opens <code>ArticleActivity</code> through a plain <code>getActivity()</code>. The user presses Back. Where do they land?","options":["On your home screen Activity","On the device home screen","On the previous article"],"a":1,"why":"The new task holds only ArticleActivity. Use TaskStackBuilder or a Navigation deep link to put your main screen under it."},{"q":"Your App Link filter has <code>autoVerify</code>, but <code>assetlinks.json</code> is missing. A user taps the link in a chat app on Android 13. What opens?","options":["Your app","A chooser dialog","The browser"],"a":2,"why":"Since Android 12, unverified web links go to the default browser."},{"q":"Notifications for articles 7 and 9 use the same action, request code 0 and <code>FLAG_UPDATE_CURRENT or FLAG_IMMUTABLE</code>. The id is only in the extras. Article 9 is posted second. The user taps article 7. What opens?","options":["Article 7","Article 9, because both share one PendingIntent and the second call swapped in its extras","Nothing, because the first PendingIntent was cancelled"],"a":1,"why":"PendingIntents are matched without extras, so both posts got the same one. Put the id in the data URI or use a distinct request code."}]},{"id":"app-links","title":"Verified App Links","tagline":"Your website vouches for your app, so https links open it without a chooser.","level":"intermediate","remember":"autoVerify in the manifest, assetlinks.json on every host, no redirects, the Play signing fingerprint.","terms":[{"term":"Android App Link","def":"An https deep link whose domain is verified for your app, so it opens the app without a chooser."},{"term":"autoVerify","def":"An intent-filter attribute that asks the system to verify the hosts in your web intent filters when the app is installed."},{"term":"assetlinks.json","def":"The Digital Asset Links file at /.well-known/ on your domain that names the package and certificate fingerprints allowed to handle its links."},{"term":"Domain verification state","def":"The per-host result shown by pm get-app-links; only \"verified\" means the app is approved for that domain."}],"quiz":[{"q":"On Android 13, your filter has <code>autoVerify</code> but assetlinks.json returns 404. A user taps your link in a chat app. What opens?","options":["Your app","A chooser dialog","The default browser"],"a":2,"why":"Since Android 12, a web link opens an app only if it is approved for that domain."},{"q":"Your app uses Play App Signing. Which fingerprint belongs in assetlinks.json?","options":["The upload key from keytool","The app signing key shown in Play Console","Any key, the package name is enough"],"a":1,"why":"Users install an APK signed with Play’s app signing key. Its SHA-256 must be in the file."},{"q":"<code>pm get-app-links</code> shows <code>news.example.com: none</code> right after install. What does it most likely mean?","options":["Verification failed for good","The verifier hasn’t finished yet","The user turned links off"],"a":1,"why":"<code>none</code> means nothing is recorded yet. Wait, re-run verification, and check again."},{"q":"Your filters list news.example.com and m.news.example.com. assetlinks.json is served only on news.example.com. On Android 13, a user taps a link to m.news.example.com. What opens?","options":["The app, since one verified host covers the others","The browser, because each host is checked on its own","A dialog asking which app to use"],"a":1,"why":"Verification is per host. Serve the file on every host your filters list."},{"q":"On Android 15 with Google services, dynamic_app_link_components lists first <code>/promo/*</code> with <code>\"exclude\": true</code>, then <code>/*</code>. A user taps /promo/sale. What opens?","options":["The app, because /* matches","The browser, because the first match wins and it excludes","The app, because dynamic rules widen the manifest"],"a":1,"why":"Rules are checked in order and the first match decides. Dynamic rules can only narrow what the manifest declares."}]}]},{"id":"background","title":"Background work","description":"WorkManager, Doze and standby buckets, foreground services, broadcast receivers and alarms: the rules that decide when your code gets to run.","requires":["android","coroutines"],"file":"tracks/background.js","lessons":[{"id":"workmanager","title":"WorkManager","tagline":"Work that must finish, run when the conditions are right.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Constraints decide when, backoff decides how often, unique work decides how many.","terms":[{"term":"CoroutineWorker","def":"A WorkManager worker whose doWork is a suspend function, cancelled when the work is stopped."},{"term":"Constraints","def":"Conditions such as network type, charging or storage that must hold before WorkManager runs a request."},{"term":"BackoffPolicy","def":"LINEAR or EXPONENTIAL growth of the delay before WorkManager retries work that returned Result.retry()."},{"term":"ExistingWorkPolicy","def":"What enqueueUniqueWork does when work with the same name exists: KEEP, REPLACE, APPEND or APPEND_OR_REPLACE."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Exponential backoff at 30 seconds. A worker returns <code>retry()</code> three times. What are the waits?","options":["30 s, 30 s, 30 s","30 s, 60 s, 120 s","1 s, 2 s, 4 s"],"a":1,"why":"Exponential doubles the delay after each attempt, up to a 5 hour cap."},{"q":"<code>enqueueUniqueWork(\"sync\", KEEP, r)</code> runs while an earlier \"sync\" is still ENQUEUED. What happens?","options":["Both run","The new request is ignored","The old one is cancelled"],"a":1,"why":"KEEP keeps the existing pending or running work. REPLACE would cancel it."},{"q":"What is the shortest repeat interval for a <code>PeriodicWorkRequest</code>?","options":["1 minute","15 minutes","1 hour"],"a":1,"why":"Shorter intervals are raised to 15 minutes."},{"q":"<code>beginWith(compress).then(upload).then(notify)</code> runs, and compress returns <code>Result.failure()</code>. What happens to upload and notify?","options":["They run with empty input data","They are marked FAILED without running","They are marked CANCELLED"],"a":1,"why":"A chain stops at the first failure and marks every later worker FAILED. CANCELLED is what you get when you cancel the chain."},{"q":"An upload worker’s file reached the server, then the process died before doWork() returned. What happens, and what protects you?","options":["WorkManager marks it succeeded, so nothing is needed","It runs again from the start; an idempotency key lets the server spot the repeat","It is marked failed, and you must enqueue it again yourself"],"a":1,"why":"WorkManager never got a result, so it runs the work again. Send a key like the work id so the second attempt finds the first result."}]},{"id":"doze","title":"Doze and App Standby buckets","tagline":"When the phone sleeps or your app goes unused, the system decides when your code runs.","level":"advanced","remember":"Doze defers almost everything to maintenance windows. Only user-facing work should cut through.","terms":[{"term":"Doze","def":"A power mode for an unplugged, still phone with the screen off that defers network, jobs, syncs and normal alarms."},{"term":"Maintenance window","def":"A short break from Doze where pending jobs, syncs and alarms run and apps get network access."},{"term":"App Standby bucket","def":"One of active, working set, frequent, rare or restricted (Android 9+); lower buckets get fewer jobs and alarms."},{"term":"Restricted bucket","def":"The lowest bucket (Android 12+): jobs once a day in a 10-minute batch and one alarm a day, even on a charger."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The phone is in Doze. Your <code>CoroutineWorker</code> has a network constraint that is met. When does it run?","options":["Right away","In the next maintenance window, or when Doze ends","Never, until the app is opened"],"a":1,"why":"Jobs, and so WorkManager work, don’t run in Doze. They run in the next window."},{"q":"Which of these fires on time while the phone is in Doze?","options":["<code>setExact()</code>","<code>setWindow()</code>","<code>setAlarmClock()</code>"],"a":2,"why":"The system leaves Doze shortly before an alarm clock alarm. setExact and setWindow wait for a window."},{"q":"Your app is in the restricted bucket. How many alarms can it fire a day?","options":["One","Ten","No limit while charging"],"a":0,"why":"Restricted apps get one alarm per day, and the limits stay even on a charger."},{"q":"To sync at 03:00 you set a <code>setExact()</code> alarm and take a partial wake lock. The phone is in Doze. What happens?","options":["The sync runs on time","The alarm waits for a maintenance window, and the wake lock is ignored","The alarm fires on time, but the sync fails without network"],"a":1,"why":"Doze defers setExact() and ignores wake locks. Let WorkManager run the sync in the next maintenance window."},{"q":"You test your sync on a phone plugged into your laptop and it always runs on time. Users report delays. Why?","options":["A charging phone never dozes and bucket limits are lifted","USB debugging turns off WorkManager’s scheduler","Debug builds always stay in the active bucket"],"a":0,"why":"Doze needs the phone unplugged, and buckets apply only on battery. Simulate battery power, or force idle with adb."},{"q":"An app is on the battery optimization exemption list. It sets a regular <code>set()</code> alarm, and the phone enters Doze. Does it fire on time?","options":["Yes, the exemption covers alarms","No: the exemption allows network and partial wake locks, not regular alarms","Only while the screen is on"],"a":1,"why":"The exemption only partly lifts Doze. Regular alarms still wait, which is why it is not a fix."}]},{"id":"fgs","title":"Foreground services","tagline":"Long work the user can see, with a type and a deadline.","level":"advanced","remember":"Declare the type, start from the foreground, call startForeground right away.","terms":[{"term":"Foreground service","def":"A service that shows an ongoing notification while it does work the user is aware of."},{"term":"foregroundServiceType","def":"The manifest attribute naming what a foreground service does, such as mediaPlayback or location; required for apps targeting Android 14+."},{"term":"Background start restriction","def":"The Android 12+ rule that an app in the background can’t start a foreground service unless an exemption applies, such as a high priority FCM message."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app is in the background. A <code>normal</code> priority FCM message arrives and your code calls <code>startForegroundService()</code>. Target is Android 14.","options":["The service starts","It throws <code>ForegroundService<wbr>StartNotAllowed<wbr>Exception</code>","It waits until the app is opened"],"a":1,"why":"Only some cases are exempt from the background start ban. A high priority message is one of them, normal priority is not."},{"q":"<code>onStartCommand</code> launches a coroutine that downloads for a minute and only then calls <code>startForeground()</code>. What happens?","options":["Nothing special","The app crashes with <code>ForegroundService<wbr>DidNotStartInTime<wbr>Exception</code>","It quietly becomes a background service"],"a":1,"why":"The service has only a few seconds after startForegroundService(). Go foreground first, then do the work."},{"q":"Targeting Android 14, the <code>&lt;service&gt;</code> has no <code>foregroundServiceType</code>. <code>startForeground()</code> is called.","options":["It works as before","It throws <code>MissingForeground<wbr>ServiceType<wbr>Exception</code>","The type defaults to dataSync"],"a":1,"why":"Android 14 requires a declared type for every foreground service."},{"q":"Targeting Android 14, a high priority FCM message lets you start a foreground service from the background. You start one of type location. The user granted only while-in-use location. What happens?","options":["It starts and gets location fixes","It throws SecurityException, because while-in-use types need the app in front","It starts, then onTimeout() stops it after 3 minutes"],"a":1,"why":"Camera, microphone and location services rely on while-in-use permissions. Start them while the user is looking at your app."},{"q":"Targeting Android 15, a dataSync service uses up its 6 hour budget. The system calls onTimeout() and your code ignores it. What happens?","options":["It keeps running in the background","The app crashes with RemoteServiceException","The system quietly turns it into a normal service"],"a":1,"why":"After onTimeout the service has a few seconds to stop. A dataSync service that keeps going crashes the app."}]},{"id":"receivers","title":"Broadcast receivers and system events","tagline":"Who hears a broadcast depends on how the receiver was registered.","level":"advanced","remember":"Manifest receivers wake you up for a short list. Context receivers hear more, only while registered.","terms":[{"term":"BroadcastReceiver","def":"A component whose onReceive runs on the main thread when a matching broadcast Intent is sent."},{"term":"Implicit broadcast","def":"A broadcast that names no package or class, which manifest receivers mostly can’t receive since Android 8."},{"term":"RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED","def":"A registerReceiver flag that hides a context-registered receiver from other apps; it or RECEIVER_EXPORTED is required on Android 14+ for non-system broadcasts."},{"term":"goAsync","def":"A BroadcastReceiver call that keeps the broadcast alive after onReceive returns, for a short piece of async work."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app targets Android 15 and declares a manifest receiver for <code>ACTION_POWER_CONNECTED</code>. The user plugs in. What happens?","options":["onReceive never runs","onReceive runs","The app crashes"],"a":0,"why":"It is an implicit broadcast and not on the exempt list. Register it from code instead."},{"q":"Targeting Android 14, you call <code>registerReceiver</code> for a custom action without an exported flag. What happens?","options":["It defaults to exported","It throws <code>SecurityException</code>","It defaults to not exported"],"a":1,"why":"For non-system broadcasts, apps targeting Android 14 must choose RECEIVER_EXPORTED or RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED."},{"q":"Which thread does <code>onReceive()</code> run on by default?","options":["A background thread","The main thread","A coroutine on Dispatchers.IO"],"a":1,"why":"That is why long work there causes ANRs. Use goAsync for a few seconds of work, or WorkManager."},{"q":"onReceive() launches a coroutine on GlobalScope for an 8 second upload and returns at once. Nothing else in your process is active. What is the risk?","options":["None; the coroutine keeps the process alive","The system can kill the process after onReceive returns, and the upload is lost","An ANR, because onReceive took 8 seconds"],"a":1,"why":"Once onReceive returns, the receiver counts as finished. Use goAsync() for a few seconds of work, or WorkManager for work that must not be lost."},{"q":"Only apps signed with your key should be able to send to your exported receiver. How?","options":["Register it with RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED","Define a permission with protectionLevel=\"signature\" and require it when you register","Check intent.getPackage() inside onReceive"],"a":1,"why":"Only apps signed with your key can hold a signature permission. NOT_EXPORTED would block your own other apps too, and the package field is set by the sender."},{"q":"On Android 14 your app is cached. A system broadcast for your context-registered receiver arrives three times. The app then comes back to the foreground. How many times does onReceive run?","options":["Exactly three","Possibly fewer, since queued copies can be merged; read the current state instead of counting","Zero, because broadcasts to cached apps are dropped"],"a":1,"why":"The system queues broadcasts for cached apps and may merge copies. Treat a broadcast as a signal to read state, not as a counter."}]},{"id":"alarms","title":"Alarms and exact timing","tagline":"AlarmManager runs your code at a time, even when the app is closed. Exact time costs a permission.","level":"advanced","remember":"Inexact by default. Exact only for what the user set, and only after canScheduleExactAlarms().","terms":[{"term":"AlarmManager","def":"The system service that fires a PendingIntent at a set time, even when the app isn’t running."},{"term":"SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM","def":"Special app access, shown as Alarms & reminders, that the user grants and can revoke; needed for exact alarms."},{"term":"USE_EXACT_ALARM","def":"An install-time permission for exact alarms (Android 13+), allowed on Google Play only for alarm clock and calendar apps."},{"term":"setAlarmClock","def":"The most visible exact alarm: the system never moves it and leaves Doze shortly before it fires."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app targets Android 14 and is freshly installed on an Android 14 phone. It is not an alarm clock or calendar app. Is <code>SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM</code> granted?","options":["Yes, at install","No, the user must allow it in Alarms & reminders","Only after a reboot"],"a":1,"why":"Since Android 14 it is denied by default for new installs of apps targeting Android 13 or higher."},{"q":"On Android 12+, <code>set(RTC_WAKEUP, 07:00, pi)</code>. When can it fire, battery savers aside?","options":["Exactly at 07:00","Between 07:00 and about 08:00","Any time before 07:00"],"a":1,"why":"Inexact alarms never fire early, and on Android 12+ they fire within an hour of the time given."},{"q":"The user revokes Alarms & reminders for your app. What happens to exact alarms already set?","options":["They still fire","They become inexact","The app is stopped and future exact alarms are cancelled"],"a":2,"why":"Revoking the permission stops the app and cancels its future exact alarms."},{"q":"A reminder should fire “in 30 minutes”, even if the user changes the time zone meanwhile. Which alarm type?","options":["RTC_WAKEUP","ELAPSED_REALTIME_WAKEUP","RTC"],"a":1,"why":"ELAPSED_REALTIME counts time since boot and ignores clock and time zone changes. RTC follows the wall clock, which fits “at 07:00”."},{"q":"<code>canScheduleExactAlarms()</code> returns false for a reminder app. What is the best flow?","options":["Call setExact() anyway inside try/catch and ignore the error","Explain why in your UI, open the Alarms & reminders settings screen, keep an inexact fallback, and check again in onResume()","Ask for SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM with the runtime permission dialog"],"a":1,"why":"SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is special access granted in settings, not through a dialog. Some users say no, so the fallback keeps the feature working."},{"q":"The user sets a reminder, then the phone restarts. What happens to the alarm?","options":["AlarmManager restores it after boot","It is gone; set it again from a BOOT_COMPLETED receiver using your stored reminders","It fires right away when the phone boots"],"a":1,"why":"All alarms are cleared on shutdown. Keep reminders in storage and reschedule them at boot."}]}]},{"id":"security","title":"Security and privacy","description":"Protect keys, data and traffic on devices you do not control, and collect only what you need.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/security.js","lessons":[{"id":"threats","title":"Threat model for mobile apps","tagline":"Your app runs on the attacker’s phone. Plan for that.","level":"intermediate","remember":"The APK is public and the phone may belong to the attacker. Decisions that matter run on the server.","terms":[{"term":"Threat model","def":"A short list of what you protect, who might attack it, what they can do, and which defenses address each risk."},{"term":"Rooted device","def":"A phone where the owner has gained superuser access, so they can read any app’s files and change running code."},{"term":"Frida","def":"A dynamic instrumentation toolkit that injects scripts into a running app to read or replace what its functions do."},{"term":"Decompiler","def":"A tool such as jadx that turns the dex bytecode in an APK back into readable Java or Kotlin-like source."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where should the check “is this user allowed to download this file?” live?","options":["In the app, behind R8 obfuscation","On the server, for every request","In EncryptedSharedPreferences"],"a":1,"why":"Code on the device can be patched or hooked. Only the server sees a request it can trust its own answer about."},{"q":"What can the owner of a rooted phone do to your app?","options":["Only read public files","Read your private files and change what your functions return","Nothing, because of the app sandbox"],"a":1,"why":"Root removes the sandbox for that user. Tools like Frida can hook any method while the app runs."},{"q":"Which of these stays protected even on a rooted phone?","options":["A string constant in your APK","Private key material in a hardware-backed Keystore","A boolean in DataStore"],"a":1,"why":"Hardware-backed keys can’t be copied out. The attacker can still ask your app to use the key while it runs, which is why server checks still matter."}]},{"id":"keystore","title":"Android Keystore","tagline":"Keys that your app can use but never read.","level":"advanced","remember":"Keystore keys never leave secure hardware. Your app sends data in and gets results out.","terms":[{"term":"Android Keystore","def":"The system service that generates and stores cryptographic keys outside the app process and performs operations with them on the app’s behalf."},{"term":"TEE","def":"Trusted Execution Environment: an isolated secure area of the main processor where Keystore keys are stored and used."},{"term":"StrongBox","def":"A Keystore backed by a separate tamper-resistant security chip, available on many devices since Android 9."},{"term":"Auth-bound key","def":"A Keystore key that only works after the user authenticates, either per use or for a set number of seconds."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>secretKey.encoded</code> return for a Keystore key?","options":["The 32 key bytes","<code>null</code>","A base64 string"],"a":1,"why":"The key material is not exportable. Your app holds a reference, and the operation runs inside the TEE or StrongBox."},{"q":"Where does the IV come from when you encrypt with a Keystore AES-GCM key?","options":["You pass a fixed one","The Keystore generates a random one; read it from <code>cipher.iv</code>","GCM does not use an IV"],"a":1,"why":"Randomized encryption is required by default. Store the IV with the ciphertext; it is not secret."},{"q":"A key was made with <code>setUserAuthenticationParameters(30, AUTH_BIOMETRIC_STRONG)</code>. The user unlocked a minute ago. What happens on <code>cipher.init</code>?","options":["It works","It throws <code>UserNotAuthenticatedException</code>","The key is deleted"],"a":1,"why":"The 30 second window has passed. Show a BiometricPrompt, then try again."},{"q":"Version 2 of your app adds a 30 second auth timeout to the KeyGenParameterSpec. <code>getOrCreateKey()</code> looks the alias up first. What do existing users get?","options":["The new rules apply on the next use","The old key with its old rules, until you delete it and make a new one","A crash on cipher.init because the spec changed"],"a":1,"why":"The rules are sealed into the key and checked by secure hardware. To change them, delete the key and create a new one, losing what it sealed."},{"q":"A key needs a biometric for every use and was built with default settings. The user enrolls a new fingerprint. What happens on the next cipher.init?","options":["It works as before","KeyPermanentlyInvalidatedException; delete the alias, make a new key and get the data again","UserNotAuthenticatedException; just show the prompt again"],"a":1,"why":"By default a new enrollment invalidates per-use biometric keys, so a finger added by someone else can’t open your data. There is no repair."},{"q":"This runs on every app launch. What goes wrong?<pre class=\"code mini\">val g = KeyGenerator.getInstance(\n  \"AES\", \"AndroidKeyStore\")\ng.init(spec(\"token\"))\nval key = g.generateKey()</pre>","options":["AndroidKeyStore does not support AES","It replaces the key under the alias each launch, so earlier data can’t be decrypted","generateKey() returns null for Keystore keys"],"a":1,"why":"Generating under an existing alias replaces the key. Look the alias up first and generate only when it is missing."}]},{"id":"at-rest","title":"Encrypting data at rest","tagline":"Tink does the crypto. The Keystore guards Tink’s key.","level":"advanced","remember":"EncryptedSharedPreferences is deprecated. Tink Aead with a Keystore master key, stored in DataStore.","terms":[{"term":"Tink","def":"Google’s open-source crypto library that offers safe, high-level primitives such as Aead and manages keys as keysets."},{"term":"AEAD","def":"Authenticated encryption with associated data: encryption that also detects any change to the ciphertext or to the extra data bound to it."},{"term":"Keyset","def":"Tink’s container of one or more keys with ids, which lets you rotate keys while still decrypting old data."},{"term":"Envelope encryption","def":"Encrypting data with a data key and then encrypting that data key with a master key kept somewhere safer."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should replace <code>EncryptedSharedPreferences</code> in new code?","options":["Plain SharedPreferences, the disk is encrypted anyway","Tink Aead with a Keystore-wrapped keyset, storing the result in DataStore or a file","Base64-encoding the values"],"a":1,"why":"<code>security-crypto</code> is deprecated. Tink is the library it used inside, and you can call it directly. Base64 is an encoding, not encryption."},{"q":"One byte of a sealed value changes on disk. What does <code>aead.decrypt</code> do?","options":["Returns slightly wrong text","Throws <code>GeneralSecurityException</code>","Returns the original text"],"a":1,"why":"AEAD checks an authentication tag. Any change, even one bit, fails the check."},{"q":"Someone copies the app’s files to a laptop. Why can’t they read the token?","options":["The files are hidden","The keyset is wrapped by a master key that stays in this phone’s Keystore","Tink deletes them"],"a":1,"why":"The copied keyset is itself ciphertext. The key to unwrap it can’t leave the secure hardware of the original phone."},{"q":"A token is sealed with associated data <code>\"refresh\"</code>. An attacker copies the ciphertext into the access token field, which your code decrypts with <code>\"access\"</code>. What happens?","options":["It decrypts fine, because the associated data is stored inside","decrypt throws, because the associated data does not match","It decrypts to random bytes"],"a":1,"why":"Associated data is not stored in the ciphertext, but decryption checks it. That binds each value to where it belongs."},{"q":"Which order rotates a Tink key safely?","options":["Add a new key and make it primary, re-encrypt old records, then disable the old key","Disable the old key, add a new primary, then re-encrypt old records","Add a new key, disable the old key, then make the new key primary"],"a":0,"why":"Each ciphertext names the key that sealed it. The old key must stay enabled until no record points at it."},{"q":"You need to encrypt a 200 MB video export. Which fits?","options":["Aead.encrypt on the whole file as one byte array","StreamingAead with its own keyset","A Keystore Cipher used directly on the file"],"a":1,"why":"StreamingAead encrypts in segments through normal streams and detects a file cut short. Aead needs the whole plaintext in memory."}]},{"id":"network","title":"Network security","tagline":"HTTPS only, the right trust anchors, and pinning only if you can maintain it.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Cleartext is blocked, user CAs are ignored. Pin only with a backup pin and an expiry date.","terms":[{"term":"Network security config","def":"An XML file referenced from the manifest that sets cleartext rules, trusted CAs and certificate pins per domain."},{"term":"Man-in-the-middle","def":"An attack where someone between the app and the server reads or changes traffic, usually by presenting their own certificate."},{"term":"Certificate pinning","def":"Accepting a server only if its certificate chain contains a public key whose hash the app already knows."},{"term":"Trust anchor","def":"A root certificate authority the app trusts; every server certificate must chain up to one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app targets API 35 and calls <code>http://api.example.com</code>. What happens by default?","options":["It works","The request fails: cleartext is not permitted","Android upgrades it to HTTPS"],"a":1,"why":"Since API 28 the default is <code>cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\"</code>. OkHttp throws <code>UnknownServiceException</code>."},{"q":"A user installs a proxy’s CA certificate in Settings. Does your release app trust it?","options":["Yes, it’s in the device store","No, apps targeting API 24+ trust only system CAs unless you opt in","Only for HTTP"],"a":1,"why":"User CAs need an explicit <code>&lt;certificates src=\"user\"/&gt;</code>. Put it in <code>debug-overrides</code> to keep release builds safe."},{"q":"Why ship a backup pin?","options":["To make pinning faster","So you can rotate the server key without breaking installed apps","Android rejects a single pin"],"a":1,"why":"Old app versions stay installed for years. If the only pinned key changes, every one of them fails to connect."},{"q":"Your app fails with “Trust anchor not found” for api.example.com, but a desktop browser loads the site fine. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The certificate has expired","The server does not send its intermediate certificate","The certificate names a different host"],"a":1,"why":"An unknown root usually means a broken chain, often a missing intermediate. An expired certificate and a wrong name fail with different errors."}]},{"id":"secrets","title":"Secrets in the app","tagline":"If it ships in the APK, it is public.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Anything in the APK is public. Secrets stay on the server; the app sends a session token and a Play Integrity token.","terms":[{"term":"BuildConfig","def":"A class generated at build time whose constant fields are compiled into the APK as readable values."},{"term":"Play Integrity API","def":"A Google Play service that returns a signed verdict about whether a request comes from your genuine app on a genuine Android device."},{"term":"Request hash","def":"A digest of the request you pass when asking for an integrity token, so the server can check the token belongs to that exact request."},{"term":"Key restriction","def":"A Cloud Console rule that limits an API key to a package name and signing certificate, or to specific APIs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Does R8 hide a secret stored in <code>BuildConfig</code>?","options":["Yes, it obfuscates it","No, R8 renames code but leaves string constants readable","Only in release builds"],"a":1,"why":"Obfuscation changes names like <code>BuildConfig.PAY_KEY</code> to <code>a.b</code>. The value <code>\"sk_live_...\"</code> is still in the dex for anyone to grep."},{"q":"Which key is fine to ship in the app?","options":["A payment provider’s secret key","A Maps SDK key restricted to your package name and signing certificate","Your database password"],"a":1,"why":"Some keys are designed to be public and are limited by restrictions on the provider’s side. Keys that grant power must stay on a server."},{"q":"Who decodes a Play Integrity token?","options":["The app, before sending it","Your server, through Google’s API","Nobody, it’s just a random value"],"a":1,"why":"The app treats the token as opaque. The server sends it to Google’s decode endpoint and checks the verdict and the request hash."},{"q":"Your app gets one Play Integrity token at launch and attaches it to every payment request. What is the problem?","options":["Play rejects a token that is sent twice","It is not bound to any request, so a captured one can be replayed with a different body","Nothing, as long as it is less than an hour old"],"a":1,"why":"Ask for a fresh token per sensitive action, bound to a hash of that request body. The server then checks requestHash."}]},{"id":"credential-manager","title":"Sign-in with Credential Manager and passkeys","tagline":"One request, one sheet: passkeys, saved passwords and Sign in with Google.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Ask for every credential type in one getCredential() call, and let the server verify what comes back. The phone proves, the server decides.","terms":[{"term":"Credential Manager","def":"The Jetpack API (androidx.credentials) that gets and saves passkeys, passwords and federated sign-in credentials through one system UI."},{"term":"Passkey","def":"A WebAuthn key pair for one account on one domain. The private key stays with the credential provider and signs server challenges; the server stores only the public key."},{"term":"Relying party ID (rpId)","def":"The domain a passkey belongs to, such as news.example. The provider only offers the passkey to that domain and to apps the domain vouches for."},{"term":"Digital Asset Links","def":"An assetlinks.json file on your domain that lists your app’s package name and signing certificate fingerprint. Required for passkeys."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does your server store after a user creates a passkey?","options":["The private key, encrypted","The public key and the credential ID","A hash of the user’s screen lock PIN"],"a":1,"why":"The private key never leaves the credential provider. The server only needs the public key to check signatures, so a database leak reveals nothing useful."},{"q":"<code>getCredential()</code> throws <code>NoCredentialException</code> on a new user’s phone. What should the app do?","options":["Call getCredential() again in a loop","Show the normal sign-in or sign-up screen","Crash, the phone is misconfigured"],"a":1,"why":"Nothing is saved for this app yet. Show your regular form, and offer to create a passkey after the user is signed in."},{"q":"Your app still calls Smart Lock for Passwords. What happens when you update to <code>play-services-auth</code> 21.0.0?","options":["Nothing, it still works the same","The build fails: the Smart Lock API was removed, so migrate to Credential Manager","Passwords are deleted from users’ phones"],"a":1,"why":"Smart Lock was deprecated in 2022 and removed from the SDK. Versions already in the Play Store keep working, but new builds with 21.0.0 can’t use it."},{"q":"Which flow fits Sign in with Google through Credential Manager?","options":["setFilterByAuthorizedAccounts(true) first; on NoCredentialException ask again with false; send the ID token to the server to verify","setFilterByAuthorizedAccounts(false) first; then true; verify the ID token on the phone","setFilterByAuthorizedAccounts(true); on NoCredentialException show an error; trust the ID token in the app"],"a":0,"why":"The first pass signs in returning users, the second shows every account for sign-up. Only the server can safely verify the token and its nonce."},{"q":"After the user signs out, the next sign-in sheet auto-selects the previous account. What did sign-out miss?","options":["clearCredentialState()","Deleting the user’s passkey","Revoking access through AuthorizationClient"],"a":0,"why":"clearCredentialState() tells every provider to forget the current session, so the next sheet shows all options."}]},{"id":"biometric","title":"Biometric authentication","tagline":"A fingerprint should unlock a key, not just a callback.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Pass a CryptoObject with BIOMETRIC_STRONG. A match unlocks the key, not just a callback.","terms":[{"term":"BiometricPrompt","def":"The AndroidX API that shows the system authentication sheet for fingerprint, face or device credential and reports the result to a callback."},{"term":"BIOMETRIC_STRONG","def":"Class 3 biometrics: sensors that meet Android’s strictest spoofing limits and can unlock Keystore keys."},{"term":"CryptoObject","def":"A wrapper around a Cipher, Signature or Mac from an auth-bound key that BiometricPrompt authorizes for one operation."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why pass a <code>CryptoObject</code> instead of just checking <code>onAuthenticationSucceeded</code>?","options":["It makes the prompt faster","The secure hardware unlocks the key only after a real match, so a hooked callback gets nothing useful","It allows face unlock"],"a":1,"why":"A callback is just code and can be called by a hook. A Keystore operation authorized by the TEE can’t be faked from the app process."},{"q":"You call <code>authenticate(info, CryptoObject(cipher))</code> with <code>BIOMETRIC_WEAK</code>. What happens?","options":["It works with face unlock","It throws: crypto-based auth is not supported for Class 2","It silently upgrades to STRONG"],"a":1,"why":"Class 2 sensors are not trusted to unlock keys. Use <code>BIOMETRIC_STRONG</code>, or drop the CryptoObject for convenience-only checks."},{"q":"The user touches the sensor with the wrong finger. Which callback runs?","options":["<code>onAuthenticationError</code>","<code>onAuthenticationFailed</code>, and the prompt stays open","None"],"a":1,"why":"A non-match is not an error. Errors are for cancel, lockout or a missing sensor, and they close the prompt."},{"q":"A phone has Class 2 face unlock and no fingerprint enrolled. What do <code>canAuthenticate(BIOMETRIC_WEAK)</code> and <code>canAuthenticate(BIOMETRIC_STRONG)</code> say?","options":["Both succeed","WEAK succeeds, STRONG does not","Both fail, because face unlock is not a biometric"],"a":1,"why":"The answer is for one exact set of authenticators. Ask with the same set you will pass to the prompt."},{"q":"onAuthenticationError reports ERROR_LOCKOUT_PERMANENT. What should the app do?","options":["Reopen the prompt in a loop until a match","Say what happened and offer the password, or a prompt that allows DEVICE_CREDENTIAL","Wait 30 seconds and show the biometric prompt again"],"a":1,"why":"Biometrics stay off until the user unlocks with their PIN, pattern or password. Waiting only helps with the temporary ERROR_LOCKOUT."}]},{"id":"privacy","title":"Privacy and permissions hygiene","tagline":"Ask for less, keep less, log nothing personal.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Smallest access that works: picker over storage, coarse over fine. No personal data in logs.","terms":[{"term":"Approximate location","def":"A location estimate accurate to about 3 square kilometers, granted by ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION or chosen by the user in the dialog."},{"term":"Data safety section","def":"The Play Store form and listing section where an app declares what user data it collects and shares, and why."},{"term":"PII","def":"Personally identifiable information: data such as a name, email, phone number or precise location that points to a specific person."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app lets users set a profile photo. Which permission do you need?","options":["<code>READ_MEDIA_IMAGES</code>","<code>READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE</code>","None, use the photo picker"],"a":2,"why":"The photo picker gives you just the chosen image, without access to the rest of the library."},{"q":"You request <code>ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION</code> and <code>ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION</code> on Android 12+. What can the user do?","options":["Only allow or deny","Grant approximate location only","Nothing, fine is granted automatically"],"a":1,"why":"The dialog lets the user pick approximate. Your feature has to handle that answer gracefully."},{"q":"An analytics SDK in your app collects the device location. Does it go in your Data safety section?","options":["No, it’s the SDK’s data","Yes, you declare everything your app and its SDKs collect","Only if you read it yourself"],"a":1,"why":"Play holds the app responsible for the data its SDKs collect and share."},{"q":"The user picked “Only this time” for location. You saved <code>hasLocation = true</code> in prefs. The next day the feature trusts that flag. What happens?","options":["It works, because the grant lasts until the user revokes it","Location calls fail, because the one-time grant was revoked after the app went to the background","The prefs flag grants the permission again"],"a":1,"why":"A one-time grant lasts only while the app is in use. Check the current permission state each time the feature needs it."},{"q":"Which line leaks personal data?<pre class=\"code mini\">fun onLogin(u: User) {\n  Log.i(\"auth\", \"login ${u.email}\") // 1\n  analytics.track(\"login\", u.opaqueId) // 2\n  nav.toHome() // 3\n}</pre>","options":["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3"],"a":0,"why":"Logcat output can end up in bug reports and crash tools. Log an opaque id or nothing, never an email."}]}]},{"id":"build","title":"Build and release","description":"How Gradle turns your modules into a signed App Bundle and how that bundle reaches users through Google Play.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/build.js","lessons":[{"id":"gradle","title":"Gradle basics","tagline":"Settings, build scripts, tasks and the three phases of a build.","level":"beginner","remember":"Scripts configure, tasks execute. Work in a build script runs on every build; work in doLast runs only when needed.","terms":[{"term":"Gradle task","def":"A unit of build work, such as compiling Kotlin or packaging an APK, with declared inputs and outputs so Gradle can skip it when nothing changed."},{"term":"Configuration phase","def":"The build phase where Gradle runs every build script to create and wire up the task graph."},{"term":"Execution phase","def":"The build phase where Gradle runs the actions of only the tasks the requested command depends on."},{"term":"Gradle wrapper","def":"The gradlew script and its properties file that download and run one pinned Gradle version for everyone on the project."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You run <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code> twice without changing anything. What does the second run do?","options":["Recompiles everything to be safe","Configures, then marks the tasks UP-TO-DATE and skips their work","Skips all three phases"],"a":1,"why":"Gradle compares each task’s inputs and outputs with the last run. Nothing changed, so every task is up to date. Configuration still runs unless the configuration cache is reused."},{"q":"Where does <code>include(\":feature:news\")</code> belong?","options":["<code>app/build.gradle.kts</code>","<code>settings.gradle.kts</code>","<code>gradle.properties</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The settings file decides which projects are part of the build. It is read in the initialization phase."},{"q":"What does the configuration cache let Gradle skip?","options":["The execution phase","The configuration phase, when build scripts and their inputs are unchanged","Downloading dependencies"],"a":1,"why":"Gradle stores the configured task graph and reuses it. Tasks still run or get skipped as usual."},{"q":"A custom task has a doLast action but declares no outputs. You run the build twice and change nothing. What does Gradle do with that task on the second run?","options":["Marks it UP-TO-DATE and skips it","Runs it again, because a task with no outputs can never be up to date","Restores it FROM-CACHE"],"a":1,"why":"The up-to-date check compares declared inputs and outputs with the last run. With no outputs there is nothing to compare, so the action runs on every build."},{"q":"You build on <code>main</code>, switch to a feature branch, build, then switch back to <code>main</code>. Tasks that were UP-TO-DATE before now run again. Which setting lets Gradle reuse the earlier outputs?","options":["<code>org.gradle.configuration-cache=true</code>","<code>org.gradle.parallel=true</code>","<code>org.gradle.caching=true</code>, the build cache"],"a":2,"why":"UP-TO-DATE only compares with the last run in this folder. The build cache stores outputs by an input key, so when the main-branch inputs come back the outputs are copied in as FROM-CACHE."}]},{"id":"catalogs","title":"Version catalogs","tagline":"One TOML file for every library and plugin version.","level":"beginner","remember":"Versions live in libs.versions.toml. Dashes become dots: androidx-room-runtime is libs.androidx.room.runtime.","terms":[{"term":"Version catalog","def":"A TOML file, usually gradle/libs.versions.toml, that declares library and plugin coordinates and versions and generates type-safe libs accessors."},{"term":"version.ref","def":"A catalog key that points a library or plugin at a shared entry in the [versions] section."},{"term":"Catalog bundle","def":"A named group of catalog libraries that you add with one line, such as implementation(libs.bundles.room)."},{"term":"Plugin alias","def":"A catalog entry under [plugins] that you apply with alias(libs.plugins.name) instead of id plus a version string."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which accessor does the entry <code>androidx-lifecycle-runtime-ktx</code> generate?","options":["<code>libs.androidx_lifecycle_runtime_ktx</code>","<code>libs.androidx.lifecycle.runtime.ktx</code>","<code>libs[\"androidx-lifecycle-runtime-ktx\"]</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Dashes, underscores and dots in alias names all become dot-separated accessor segments."},{"q":"You misspell an accessor: <code>libs.androidx.room.runtme</code>. When do you find out?","options":["When the build script compiles, or earlier in the IDE","Only after Gradle searches every repository","At runtime on the device"],"a":0,"why":"Accessors are generated Kotlin code. A name that does not exist is an unresolved reference in the build script."},{"q":"Why declare plugins in the root build file with <code>apply false</code>?","options":["To disable them","To load each plugin version once, so every module uses the same one","It is required by the catalog format"],"a":1,"why":"The root build puts the plugin on the classpath with one version. Modules then apply it without repeating a version."},{"q":"The catalog says <code>coroutines = \"1.8.1\"</code>. You add an SDK that was built against coroutines 1.10.2. Which version does the app run?","options":["1.8.1, because the catalog wins","1.10.2, because conflict resolution picks the highest requested version","Neither: the build fails with a version conflict"],"a":1,"why":"A catalog version is a request, not a lock. Gradle picks the highest version in the graph, and <code>./gradlew :app:dependencies</code> shows it as <code>1.8.1 -> 1.10.2</code>."},{"q":"You add the Compose BOM to the catalog. How should <code>androidx-compose-material3</code> be declared?","options":["With <code>version.ref = \"composeBom\"</code>","With its own version number, to be safe","With no version, and the BOM added with <code>platform(libs.androidx.compose.bom)</code>"],"a":2,"why":"The BOM lists one tested version for every member. Members without a version take it from the BOM, so upgrading the whole family is one line."}]},{"id":"api-levels","title":"API levels and compatibility","tagline":"compileSdk, minSdk and targetSdk answer three different questions.","level":"intermediate","remember":"compileSdk lets you see APIs, minSdk decides who can install, targetSdk decides how Android treats you. Guard every call above minSdk.","terms":[{"term":"compileSdk","def":"The Android SDK your code compiles against. It decides which platform classes and constants exist at compile time. It is not stored in the APK."},{"term":"minSdk","def":"The lowest API level that may install the app. Devices below it get INSTALL_FAILED_OLDER_SDK and Play does not offer the app to them."},{"term":"targetSdk","def":"The API level you tested against. Behavior changes gated on a target level apply only when targetSdk is that level or higher."},{"term":"Core library desugaring","def":"A build step where D8/R8 rewrites calls to newer Java library APIs, such as java.time, to a copy bundled in your app, so they work below the API level that added them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You raise <code>compileSdk</code> from 35 to 36 and change nothing else. What changes for users?","options":["Android 16 behavior changes now apply to the app","Nothing at runtime: you can now call Android 16 APIs and see new lint warnings","Android 15 phones can no longer install the app"],"a":1,"why":"<code>compileSdk</code> only decides what your code can see while compiling. Runtime behavior follows <code>targetSdk</code>, and who can install follows <code>minSdk</code>."},{"q":"Your app targets API 35 and runs on an Android 16 phone. Which Android 16 changes apply?","options":["All of them","Only the changes for all apps. The target-gated ones stay off until you target 36","None, the app runs as Android 15"],"a":1,"why":"Some changes apply to every app on the new version. Changes gated on targetSdk stay off while your target is lower, so the app runs in a compatibility mode for those."},{"q":"Since August 31, 2026, what must a new app update on Google Play target (phone app)?","options":["API 34 or higher","API 35 or higher","API 36 or higher"],"a":2,"why":"New apps and updates must target Android 16 (API 36). Wear OS and Automotive need 35, TV and XR need 34. An extension until November 1, 2026 could be requested."},{"q":"<pre>// minSdk = 24\nval v = Build.VERSION.RELEASE\nif (v.toInt() >= 8) {\n  createChannel()\n}</pre>Which line is the bug?","options":["<code>val v = Build.VERSION.RELEASE</code>","<code>if (v.toInt() >= 8) {</code>","<code>createChannel()</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>RELEASE</code> is a display string such as \"8.1.0\", so <code>toInt()</code> throws. Check <code>Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 26</code>, or use <code>NotificationManagerCompat</code>."},{"q":"You mark a function <code>@RequiresApi(26)</code>. A caller calls it with no version check, and the app runs on an Android 7 phone (API 24). What happens?","options":["The annotation skips the call on older phones","The function throws a clear \"API too low\" exception","Lint flags the caller, but nothing checks at runtime: the call reaches the missing API and crashes"],"a":2,"why":"<code>@RequiresApi</code> only moves the lint duty to the caller. It adds no runtime check, so an unguarded call still fails on old phones."},{"q":"With <code>minSdk = 24</code>, you want <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> and <code>NotificationChannel</code>. What does core library desugaring give you?","options":["Both work on every device with no checks","<code>LocalDate</code> works everywhere; <code>NotificationChannel</code> still needs a version check or a Compat class","Neither, you must raise minSdk to 26"],"a":1,"why":"Desugaring bundles a copy of parts of the Java library, such as java.time. It cannot add Android platform APIs like notification channels."}]},{"id":"variants","title":"Build types and product flavors","tagline":"How you build, times what you build, gives build variants.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Flavors times build types equals variants. The most specific source set wins.","terms":[{"term":"Build type","def":"A Gradle setting group, such as debug or release, that controls how the app is built: debuggability, shrinking and signing."},{"term":"Product flavor","def":"A version of the app inside a flavor dimension, such as free or paid, with its own ID suffix, values and source files."},{"term":"Build variant","def":"One combination of product flavors and a build type, such as paidRelease, which Gradle builds with its own set of tasks."},{"term":"buildConfigField","def":"A Gradle call that adds a typed constant to the generated BuildConfig class for a build type or flavor."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Flavor dimensions <code>tier</code> (free, paid) and <code>env</code> (staging, prod), plus debug and release. How many variants?","options":["4","6","8"],"a":2,"why":"2 tiers x 2 envs x 2 build types = 8, like <code>freeStagingDebug</code>."},{"q":"Both <code>src/main/res/values/strings.xml</code> and <code>src/paid/res/values/strings.xml</code> define <code>title</code>. Which does <code>paidRelease</code> use?","options":["The one in src/main","The one in src/paid","The build fails with a duplicate"],"a":1,"why":"Resources merge by priority. A flavor source set overrides <code>main</code>."},{"q":"What is <code>applicationIdSuffix = \".debug\"</code> on the debug build type for?","options":["It makes the build faster","It lets the debug app install next to the release app on one phone","Play requires it"],"a":1,"why":"A different application ID is a different app to Android, so both can live on the same device."},{"q":"Flavors <code>staging</code> and <code>prod</code> with debug and release. A staging release build makes no sense. What removes its tasks completely?","options":["Set <code>variant.enable = false</code> for it in <code>androidComponents.beforeVariants</code>","Set <code>isMinifyEnabled = false</code> on release","Never run <code>assembleStagingRelease</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A variant disabled in beforeVariants is never created, so it gets no tasks, no configuration cost and no IDE entry."},{"q":":app adds a build type <code>benchmark</code>. The library :core:billing has only debug and release. What happens when you build the benchmark variant?","options":["Gradle quietly uses the library’s release variant","Dependency resolution fails until you add <code>matchingFallbacks += listOf(\"release\")</code>","AGP creates a benchmark build type in the library for you"],"a":1,"why":"Variants are matched by name across modules. When the library has no matching build type, matchingFallbacks tells AGP which one to try instead."}]},{"id":"modules","title":"Modularization","tagline":"Feature and core modules, api vs implementation, and what recompiles.","level":"intermediate","remember":"implementation hides, api leaks. Every api edge widens what recompiles when that module changes.","terms":[{"term":"implementation","def":"A Gradle dependency configuration that puts a library on your module’s compile classpath without exposing it to modules that depend on you."},{"term":"api","def":"A Gradle dependency configuration, from the java-library or Android library plugin, that also exposes the dependency to your consumers’ compile classpath."},{"term":"Convention plugin","def":"A Gradle plugin you write, usually in a build-logic included build, that applies shared build setup to many modules."},{"term":"Compile avoidance","def":"Gradle and Kotlin skipping recompilation of dependent modules when a change does not alter a module’s public API."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You change only a private function body in <code>:core:network</code>. What recompiles?","options":["Every module",":core:network only. :app still repackages",":core:network and everything above it"],"a":1,"why":"The public API did not change, so dependents keep their compiled output. Only packaging reruns to pick up the new bytecode."},{"q":"When should a module use <code>api</code> for a dependency?","options":["Always, so consumers get everything","When its public signatures use that dependency’s types","Never, it is deprecated"],"a":1,"why":"If consumers must see a type to call you, expose it with <code>api</code>. Otherwise <code>implementation</code> keeps rebuilds small."},{"q":"What is a convention plugin for?","options":["Sharing build setup like compileSdk and minSdk across modules","Running tests faster","Publishing to Maven Central"],"a":0,"why":"It moves repeated Gradle setup into one place. Each module then applies one plugin ID."},{"q":":feature:news needs to open settings, and :feature:settings needs a link back to news. Which setup works?","options":["news depends on settings, and settings depends on news","Merge both features into one module","Each feature depends on the other’s small <code>:api</code> module, and :app wires in the real screens"],"a":2,"why":"Two features that depend on each other form a cycle, which Gradle refuses. Small :api modules hold only routes or interfaces, so the graph stays acyclic."},{"q":"You split code into ten modules where each depends on the one below, like a chain, and turn on <code>org.gradle.parallel=true</code> on an 8-core machine. Why is the build barely faster?","options":["Each module must wait for the one below it, so build time follows the longest path, not the module count","Parallel builds only help on CI","Gradle runs only one Kotlin compile at a time"],"a":0,"why":"A module can start only when its dependencies are done. In a chain there is never more than one module ready, so the extra workers sit idle. Keep the graph wide and shallow."}]},{"id":"signing","title":"Signing","tagline":"Debug key, upload key, app signing key, and keeping them safe.","level":"intermediate","remember":"You sign with the upload key, Google signs with the app key. Lose the upload key and you can reset it. Never commit either one.","terms":[{"term":"Keystore","def":"A password-protected file, usually .jks, that holds one or more private keys and their certificates."},{"term":"Upload key","def":"The key you use to sign app bundles before uploading them to Play, which Play can reset if it is lost or leaked."},{"term":"App signing key","def":"The key that signs the APKs installed on phones, which every future update must also be signed with."},{"term":"Play App Signing","def":"A Google Play service that stores your app signing key and signs the APKs it generates from your uploads."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your laptop with the upload keystore is stolen. What now?","options":["The app can never be updated again","Request an upload key reset in Play Console, register a new upload key, keep shipping","Publish the app under a new package name"],"a":1,"why":"With Play App Signing, Google holds the app signing key. Only the upload key is lost, and Play can replace it."},{"q":"You upload a bundle signed with the debug keystore. What happens?","options":["Play rejects it because it was signed in debug mode","Play re-signs it and publishes it","It installs only on test devices"],"a":0,"why":"Play refuses uploads signed in debug mode. Sign release builds with your upload key."},{"q":"Which key do users’ phones check when an update installs?","options":["The upload key","The app signing key","The debug key"],"a":1,"why":"Play delivers APKs signed with the app signing key. The upload key only proves to Play that the upload came from you."},{"q":"Google sign-in works on your debug build and on the release APK you install from your laptop. For users who install from Play, it fails. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Play strips Google sign-in from release builds","The upload key has expired","The fingerprint of Play’s app signing key is not registered with the service"],"a":2,"why":"Copies from Play are signed with the app signing key, not your upload key. Copy its SHA-1 and SHA-256 from the App signing page in Play Console and register them."},{"q":"Inside <code>signingConfigs</code> in app/build.gradle.kts:<pre>create(\"upload\") {\n  val p = System.getenv(\"KS\")!!\n  storeFile = file(p)\n}</pre>A new contributor clones the repo and runs <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code>. It fails. Which line is to blame?","options":["<code>create(\"upload\") {</code>","<code>val p = System.getenv(\"KS\")!!</code>","<code>storeFile = file(p)</code>"],"a":1,"why":"This block runs during configuration for every build, even debug. Without the secret, <code>!!</code> throws. Attach the signing config only when the secrets exist."}]},{"id":"bundles","title":"App Bundles and Play delivery","tagline":"Upload one .aab, and each phone downloads only its split APKs.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Upload one bundle. Play cuts it into base plus ABI, density and language splits, so each phone downloads only what it uses.","terms":[{"term":"Android App Bundle","def":"The .aab publishing format that contains all of an app’s code and resources and lets Google Play generate optimized APKs per device."},{"term":"Split APK","def":"One of several APKs, such as a base APK or an ABI, density or language split, that install together as a single app."},{"term":"Dynamic feature module","def":"A module built with the com.android.dynamic-feature plugin whose code Play can deliver later, on demand or by condition."},{"term":"bundletool","def":"Google’s command-line tool that builds the APK set from an .aab, the same way Play does, so you can test it locally."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A French-speaking user on an arm64 phone with an xxhdpi screen installs your app. What does Play send?","options":["A universal APK with every ABI, density and language","The base APK plus arm64-v8a, xxhdpi and fr config splits","Only the base APK"],"a":1,"why":"Play picks the splits that match the device configuration and installs them together."},{"q":"What is a dynamic feature module good for?","options":["Code most users don’t need at install, like a rarely used scanner","Making every screen load faster","Replacing Gradle modules"],"a":0,"why":"Delivering it on demand keeps the first download small. Users who never open it never download it."},{"q":"Your app has an in-app language picker. Why might you turn off the language split?","options":["Language splits are not allowed with pickers","The phone only gets its system languages, so other languages would be missing until downloaded","It makes the app smaller"],"a":1,"why":"With language splits on, only device languages install. Turn the split off, or request extra languages with SplitInstallManager."},{"q":"You run bundletool build-apks, then <code>adb install</code> only the base APK on a recent Android phone. What usually happens?","options":["The install is refused with <code>INSTALL_FAILED_MISSING_SPLIT</code>","It installs and works, because the base has everything","adb installs the missing splits automatically"],"a":0,"why":"The base declares that it needs its splits. Use <code>bundletool install-apks</code> so the matching splits go in together, or build a universal APK for testers."},{"q":"An AR mode works only on phones with ARCore support. How should its dynamic feature module ship?","options":["Install-time for everyone, so it is always there","Conditional delivery on the AR device feature, with on-demand as a fallback","Put it in :app, since dynamic features are deprecated"],"a":1,"why":"Conditional delivery installs the module only on phones that match, so others never download it. On-demand covers the case where a user needs it later."}]},{"id":"tracks","title":"Play Console release tracks","tagline":"Internal to production, staged rollouts, halting, and versionCode rules.","level":"advanced","remember":"Roll out in stages, halt on a spike, fix forward. You can’t roll back, so versionCode only goes up.","terms":[{"term":"Release track","def":"A Play Console channel, such as internal, closed, open or production, that decides which users can install a given release."},{"term":"Staged rollout","def":"A production release that Play offers to a chosen percentage of users, which you raise step by step."},{"term":"Halted rollout","def":"A staged rollout you stopped in Play Console, so no new users get it while existing installs keep it."},{"term":"versionCode","def":"A positive integer, at most 2100000000, that orders app versions; each upload needs a new one and updates need a higher one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your rollout is at 20% and crashes spike. You halt it. What happens to the 20%?","options":["Play rolls them back to the previous version","They keep the new version; only new users stop getting it","Their app is uninstalled"],"a":1,"why":"Play never downgrades installed apps. Halting only stops further distribution, so you fix forward."},{"q":"You try to upload a fix with the same <code>versionCode</code> as the halted release. What does Play say?","options":["It replaces the halted release","Version code has already been used","It accepts it on a test track only"],"a":1,"why":"Every upload needs a versionCode the app has never used. Bump it."},{"q":"Which track gets a build to up to 100 testers fastest?","options":["Internal testing","Open testing","Production at 1%"],"a":0,"why":"Internal testing is meant for quick team checks and is usually available within minutes."},{"q":"Production is on versionCode 251. You publish 249 to closed testing. Who gets 249?","options":["Closed testers","Nobody, because every tester is also eligible for production’s higher 251","Everyone, because the newest upload wins"],"a":1,"why":"Play gives each user the highest versionCode across all tracks they are eligible for. Testers already get 251, so a lower test build reaches no one."},{"q":"You compute <code>versionCode = major * 100 + minor * 10 + patch</code>. Which two versions get the same code?","options":["1.2.3 and 1.3.2","2.9.0 and 2.9.1","2.10.0 and 3.0.0"],"a":2,"why":"2.10.0 gives 200 + 100 + 0 = 300, the same as 3.0.0. A part above 9 spills into the next digit, so Play refuses the second upload or orders releases wrongly."},{"q":"You start a flexible in-app update. It finishes downloading, but users stay on the old version for days. What is missing?","options":["A call to <code>completeUpdate()</code>, usually after the user taps Restart","A higher update priority in Play Console","Nothing, Play installs it on the next reboot"],"a":0,"why":"A flexible update downloads and then waits. Play does not restart the app for it, so the app must ask the user and call completeUpdate()."}]}]},{"id":"kmp","title":"Kotlin Multiplatform","description":"Write the logic once in Kotlin and ship it to Android, iOS and desktop, with shared or native UI.","requires":["kotlin","arch"],"file":"tracks/kmp.js","lessons":[{"id":"shares","title":"What KMP shares","tagline":"One codebase, split into source sets, compiled per target.","level":"beginner","remember":"commonMain goes everywhere. androidMain becomes JVM bytecode. iosMain becomes native code in a framework.","terms":[{"term":"source set","def":"A group of Kotlin files with its own dependencies that is compiled for a specific set of targets."},{"term":"commonMain","def":"The source set compiled for every target in a KMP module; it can only use multiplatform APIs."},{"term":"default hierarchy template","def":"The Kotlin Gradle plugin rule that creates intermediate source sets such as iosMain and appleMain from the targets you declare."},{"term":"Kotlin/Native","def":"The Kotlin compiler backend that uses LLVM to produce native binaries, such as iOS frameworks, without a virtual machine."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where can you call <code>android.content.Context</code>?","options":["<code>commonMain</code>","<code>androidMain</code>","Any source set"],"a":1,"why":"Only <code>androidMain</code> compiles against the Android SDK. <code>commonMain</code> must compile for iOS too."},{"q":"What does the iOS build of a KMP module produce?","options":["A JAR that runs on a JVM inside the app","A native framework compiled by Kotlin/Native","JavaScript run by a WebView"],"a":1,"why":"Kotlin/Native compiles to machine code through LLVM. Xcode links the framework like any other."},{"q":"You target <code>iosArm64</code> and <code>iosSimulatorArm64</code>. Where does code for both go?","options":["Copy it into both target source sets","<code>iosMain</code>","<code>androidMain</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The default hierarchy template creates <code>iosMain</code> as the shared parent of both iOS targets."},{"q":"commonMain has <code>import java.util.UUID</code>. The Android app builds. What happens when you build the iOS framework?","options":["It fails: java.util is an unresolved reference for iOS","It works: UUID is mapped to NSUUID","It builds but crashes when the function runs"],"a":0,"why":"commonMain is compiled for every target, and java.util exists only on the JVM. Use a multiplatform API or put the JVM code in androidMain."},{"q":"You declare <code>iosArm64()</code> and <code>iosX64()</code>. On an Apple silicon Mac, the app does not link for the simulator. What fixes it?","options":["Remove <code>iosArm64()</code>","Add <code>iosSimulatorArm64()</code>","Add <code>macosArm64()</code>"],"a":1,"why":"iosX64 is the simulator on Intel Macs. The simulator on Apple silicon needs the iosSimulatorArm64 binary, and iosArm64 is for real devices."}]},{"id":"expect","title":"expect and actual","tagline":"Declare it once in common, implement it per platform.","level":"beginner","remember":"expect promises it in common. Each target keeps the promise with actual, or that target fails to build.","terms":[{"term":"expect","def":"A modifier on a commonMain declaration that has no body there and must be implemented by every target."},{"term":"actual","def":"A modifier on the platform implementation of an expect declaration, with the same signature."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An <code>expect fun</code> has an <code>actual</code> in <code>androidMain</code> only. The module targets Android and iOS. What happens?","options":["iOS uses the Android version","The iOS compilation fails","It returns null on iOS"],"a":1,"why":"Every target needs its own <code>actual</code>. Android still compiles. iOS does not."},{"q":"Your Android implementation needs a <code>Context</code>. What fits best?","options":["An <code>expect fun</code> that reads a global static Context","An interface in <code>commonMain</code>, an Android class that takes Context in its constructor, wired with DI","Pass Context through <code>commonMain</code> function parameters"],"a":1,"why":"An interface keeps common code free of Android types, and the platform class gets its dependencies the normal way."},{"q":"An expect fun has one actual in iosMain and another in iosArm64Main. What happens to the iosArm64 target?","options":["The iosArm64Main actual wins because it is more specific","The iosMain actual wins because it is higher","The build fails: walking up from the target finds two actuals"],"a":2,"why":"For each target the compiler must find exactly one actual on the path to commonMain. None fails, and two fail too."},{"q":"<pre>// commonMain\nexpect fun price(\n  cents: Long,\n  cur: String,\n): String\n\n// androidMain\nactual fun price(\n  cents: Long,\n  cur: String = \"EUR\",\n): String = ...</pre>Which part is the bug?","options":["The default value <code>= \"EUR\"</code> on the actual","The <code>Long</code> type on the expect","expect functions cannot return String"],"a":0,"why":"Common callers only see the expect signature, so defaults belong there. The compiler rejects default values on an actual."},{"q":"commonMain/Platform.kt holds an expect fun, and androidMain/Platform.kt holds its actual. The Android build fails. Why?","options":["expect and actual must be in the same file","Both files compile to a JVM class named PlatformKt, which is a duplicate","Android does not allow top-level functions"],"a":1,"why":"On the JVM, top-level functions live in a class named after the file. Add a platform suffix, such as Platform.android.kt."}]},{"id":"data","title":"Sharing data and logic","tagline":"Networking, JSON, a database and settings, all in commonMain.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Ktor fetches, serialization decodes, Room or SQLDelight caches. Only the engine and file paths are per platform.","terms":[{"term":"HttpClientEngine","def":"The Ktor component that does the actual network I/O, such as OkHttp on Android or Darwin on iOS."},{"term":"@Serializable","def":"A kotlinx.serialization annotation that makes the compiler plugin generate a serializer for the class, with no reflection."},{"term":"BundledSQLiteDriver","def":"A SQLite driver that ships its own SQLite build, used by Room KMP so every platform runs the same database engine."},{"term":"multiplatform-settings","def":"A library that wraps SharedPreferences on Android and NSUserDefaults on iOS behind one key-value API."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which part of the Ktor setup has to differ between Android and iOS?","options":["The JSON configuration","The engine, such as OkHttp or Darwin","The request URLs"],"a":1,"why":"The client config is shared. Only the engine that does the I/O is platform-specific."},{"q":"Why does kotlinx.serialization work on iOS where reflection-based JSON libraries do not?","options":["It uses Objective-C runtime reflection","A compiler plugin generates the serializers at build time","iOS apps only use XML"],"a":1,"why":"The code is generated during compilation, so Kotlin/Native needs no runtime reflection."},{"q":"Where does a Room KMP database file path usually get decided?","options":["In <code>commonMain</code>, hard-coded","In each platform source set, which creates the builder","Room picks it on its own"],"a":1,"why":"Android uses <code>context.getDatabasePath</code>, iOS uses its documents directory. Common code takes the builder and finishes the setup."},{"q":"<pre>@Serializable\ndata class Article(\n  val id: Long,\n  val summary: String = \"\",\n)\nval json = Json {\n  ignoreUnknownKeys = true\n}</pre>The server sends <code>\"summary\": null</code>. What happens?","options":["summary becomes \"\"","summary becomes \"null\"","Decoding throws a SerializationException"],"a":2,"why":"A default only helps when the key is missing. An explicit null for a non-null type throws, unless you turn on coerceInputValues."},{"q":"<pre>suspend fun load(): List&lt;Item&gt; {\n  val c = HttpClient(engine)\n  return c.get(URL).body()\n}</pre>What is the problem?","options":["A new client, with its own connection pool and threads, is made on every call and never closed","body() cannot decode lists","HttpClient cannot be used in a suspend function"],"a":0,"why":"Create one client for the app and inject it. Each client holds resources, and this one leaks every time."}]},{"id":"viewmodels","title":"Shared ViewModels and coroutines","tagline":"One ViewModel, observed by Compose and by SwiftUI.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Write the ViewModel once. Android collects the StateFlow directly; Swift needs SKIE or KMP-NativeCoroutines to for-await it.","terms":[{"term":"lifecycle-viewmodel (KMP)","def":"The AndroidX ViewModel artifact published for Android, iOS, desktop and web, so ViewModel and viewModelScope work in commonMain."},{"term":"SKIE","def":"A Kotlin compiler plugin from Touchlab that generates Swift-friendly APIs: flows as AsyncSequence, suspend as async, sealed types as Swift enums."},{"term":"KMP-NativeCoroutines","def":"A library that exposes annotated Kotlin flows and suspend functions to Swift as async/await, Combine or RxSwift."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to coroutines in <code>viewModelScope</code> when the ViewModel is cleared on iOS?","options":["They keep running until the app exits","They are cancelled, same as on Android","viewModelScope does not exist on iOS"],"a":1,"why":"The KMP lifecycle library cancels the scope on every platform when the ViewModel is cleared."},{"q":"With no bridge library, what can Swift easily do with a Kotlin <code>StateFlow</code>?","options":["<code>for await</code> over it","Read its current <code>value</code>; collecting needs a hand-written collector","Bind it with <code>@Published</code> automatically"],"a":1,"why":"Swift sees an Objective-C protocol. Reading <code>value</code> works; observing changes needs a <code>FlowCollector</code> or a tool like SKIE."},{"q":"With SKIE, how does SwiftUI observe <code>viewModel.state</code>?","options":["<code>for await state in viewModel.state</code>","<code>viewModel.state.observe(owner)</code>","<code>viewModel.state.sink { }</code> only"],"a":0,"why":"SKIE exposes Kotlin flows as Swift <code>AsyncSequence</code>s, and cancels the Kotlin collection when the Swift task is cancelled."},{"q":"<pre>viewModelScope.launch {\n  while (true) {\n    try {\n      delay(5_000)\n      _state.value = repo.poll()\n    } catch (e: Exception) {\n      log(e)\n    }\n  }\n}</pre>Swift calls clear() on the holder. What happens?","options":["The loop stops, because the scope is cancelled","The catch swallows the CancellationException from delay, so the loop keeps spinning","The app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Cancellation is delivered as an exception at the next suspension point. Catching every Exception eats it. Catch only what you expect, or rethrow CancellationException."},{"q":"Swift awaits a Kotlin suspend function through Kotlin’s own export, with no bridge library. The SwiftUI view disappears and its task is cancelled. What happens to the coroutine?","options":["It stops at its next suspension point","It runs to the end, and the result is thrown away","The app crashes with a CancellationException"],"a":1,"why":"The plain export does not pass Swift’s cancellation on to Kotlin. SKIE and KMP-NativeCoroutines connect the two, so cancelling the task cancels the coroutine."}]},{"id":"compose-mp","title":"Compose Multiplatform","tagline":"One composable, drawn on Android, iOS and desktop.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Write the UI once in commonMain. Each platform only hosts it: setContent, ComposeUIViewController, Window.","terms":[{"term":"Compose Multiplatform","def":"The JetBrains framework that runs Compose UI code on Android, iOS, desktop and web from one codebase."},{"term":"Res","def":"The class Compose Multiplatform generates from composeResources, with typed accessors like Res.string.title and Res.drawable.logo."},{"term":"ComposeUIViewController","def":"The iOS function that wraps a composable in a UIViewController so UIKit or SwiftUI can show it."},{"term":"UIKitView","def":"A composable that embeds a native UIKit view, such as a map, inside Compose UI on iOS."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does a shared <code>strings.xml</code> go?","options":["<code>androidMain/res/values</code>","<code>commonMain/composeResources/values</code>","In the Xcode project"],"a":1,"why":"Files under <code>composeResources</code> are packaged for every target and exposed through <code>Res</code>."},{"q":"How does an iOS app show a shared composable?","options":["It wraps it with <code>ComposeUIViewController</code> and shows the UIViewController","It converts it to SwiftUI views at build time","It runs Android inside the iOS app"],"a":0,"why":"Compose draws into a UIViewController. SwiftUI hosts it with <code>UIViewControllerRepresentable</code>."},{"q":"On iOS, what draws a Compose Multiplatform <code>Button</code>?","options":["A UIKit <code>UIButton</code>","Compose itself, rendering with Skia","WebKit"],"a":1,"why":"Compose renders its own pixels with Skia. Use <code>UIKitView</code> when you need a real native view."},{"q":"The phone is set to Spanish. <code>values-es/strings.xml</code> has no entry for <code>share</code>, but <code>values/strings.xml</code> does. What does <code>stringResource(Res.string.share)</code> show?","options":["The text from values/strings.xml","The key name \"share\"","It throws because the Spanish string is missing"],"a":0,"why":"Compose picks the closest match and falls back to the folder without a qualifier when the qualified one has no entry."},{"q":"A shared ViewModel needs to show \"Could not load\". What fits best?","options":["Call <code>stringResource(Res.string.load_failed)</code> in the ViewModel","Hard-code the English text in the ViewModel","Keep the <code>StringResource</code> in state and resolve it in the UI with stringResource"],"a":2,"why":"stringResource is a composable, so a ViewModel cannot call it. Resolving in the UI also updates the text when the language changes."}]},{"id":"swift","title":"Interop with Swift and iOS","tagline":"What your Kotlin API looks like from the Swift side.","level":"advanced","remember":"Swift sees Kotlin through Objective-C: Int is Int32, object is .shared, suspend is async, and only @Throws exceptions survive.","terms":[{"term":"@Throws","def":"A Kotlin annotation that lists exceptions to turn into Swift errors; undeclared exceptions crash when they reach Swift."},{"term":"Objective-C export","def":"The Kotlin/Native step that generates an Objective-C header for the framework, which is how Swift sees Kotlin types today."},{"term":"XCFramework","def":"An Apple bundle that holds one framework per platform and architecture, such as device and simulator, for Xcode to link."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Kotlin has <code>fun count(): Int</code>. What does Swift get?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>Int32</code>","<code>NSNumber</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Kotlin <code>Int</code> is always 32-bit, so the header uses <code>int32_t</code>, which Swift imports as <code>Int32</code>."},{"q":"A Kotlin function throws an exception it did not declare with <code>@Throws</code>, and Swift called it. What happens?","options":["Swift receives an <code>Error</code>","The app crashes","It returns nil"],"a":1,"why":"Only declared exceptions become Swift errors. Undeclared ones terminate the program."},{"q":"Without SKIE, how does Swift check which <code>UiState</code> it has?","options":["An exhaustive <code>switch</code> over enum cases","<code>as?</code> casts or <code>is</code> checks, with a fallback case","It cannot see sealed classes"],"a":1,"why":"Swift sees a class hierarchy, so the compiler cannot prove every case is handled. SKIE adds <code>onEnum(of:)</code> for that."},{"q":"Kotlin has <code>fun ids(): List&lt;Int&gt;</code>. What type does Swift see?","options":["<code>[Int]</code>","<code>[Int32]</code>","<code>[KotlinInt]</code>"],"a":2,"why":"Numbers inside generic types must be objects in Objective-C, so they are boxed. KotlinInt is a subclass of NSNumber."},{"q":"The iOS app links two Kotlin frameworks, News and Profile. Passing an Article from one to the other does not compile. What fixes it?","options":["One umbrella module that depends on both with api and lists them with export(), built as a single framework","Make Article an interface","Add @ObjCName to Article in both modules"],"a":0,"why":"Each framework carries its own copy of the runtime and of every type, so the two Articles are unrelated. One framework gives Swift one copy."},{"q":"<pre>@Throws(IOException::class)\nsuspend fun load(id: String) =\n  api.fetch(id)</pre>What happens?","options":["It works, and Swift gets IOException as an Error","It compiles, but cancellation crashes the app","The build fails: a suspend @Throws must list CancellationException or a superclass"],"a":2,"why":"Kotlin/Native requires cancellation to reach Swift as an error too. List CancellationException next to IOException."}]}]},{"id":"launch","title":"After launch","description":"Keep a released app healthy and earning: crash reports and Android vitals, analytics, feature flags, in-app updates and reviews, Play Billing and the Play policies that block releases.","requires":["build"],"file":"tracks/launch.js","lessons":[{"id":"crashes","title":"Crash reporting and Android vitals","tagline":"Crashlytics tells you why the app crashed. Android vitals tells you whether Play thinks it crashes too often.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Play judges user-perceived crashes and ANRs per day per user: stay under 1.09% and 0.47% overall, and under 8% on every phone model.","terms":[{"term":"User-perceived crash rate","def":"Share of daily active users who had at least one crash while an activity was visible or a foreground service was running."},{"term":"ANR","def":"Application Not Responding: the main thread was blocked too long, so the system offered to close the app."},{"term":"Bad behavior threshold","def":"The Android vitals limit (for example 1.09% for user-perceived crashes) above which Play may reduce an app’s visibility."},{"term":"Non-fatal","def":"An exception your code caught and reported with recordException(). The app kept running."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A crash happens inside a BroadcastReceiver while no activity is on screen. Which number goes up?","options":["Only the user-perceived crash rate","Only the crash rate (all crashes)","Both"],"a":1,"why":"User-perceived crashes need a visible activity or a running foreground service. A background crash raises the plain crash rate only."},{"q":"Your overall user-perceived crash rate is 0.9%, but one phone model is at 10%. Is that a problem for Play?","options":["No, the overall rate is under 1.09%","Yes, the per-model threshold is 8%","Only if the model is a Pixel"],"a":1,"why":"Android vitals also has a per-phone-model threshold of 8%. Play can act for that model even when the average looks fine."},{"q":"Why does a test crash only show up in Crashlytics after you open the app again?","options":["The report is written to disk and sent on the next launch","Crashlytics waits 24 hours","Debug builds never send reports"],"a":0,"why":"A dying process is a bad place for network calls. The report is saved locally and uploaded when the app starts again."},{"q":"Stack traces from your release build only show names like <code>a.b.c()</code>. What most likely went wrong?","options":["Crashlytics does not support R8","<code>mappingFileUploadEnabled = false</code> is set on the release variant, so Crashlytics has no mapping file","The phones run a debug build"],"a":1,"why":"R8 renames classes and writes a mapping file for each build. Crashlytics needs it uploaded to turn the short names back. Turn upload off only for debug-like variants."},{"q":"<pre>val c = Firebase.crashlytics\nc.setCustomKey(\"screen\", \"news\")\nc.setUserId(user.email)\nc.log(\"prepare started\")</pre>Which line should not ship?","options":["<code>c.setCustomKey(\"screen\", \"news\")</code>","<code>c.setUserId(user.email)</code>","<code>c.log(\"prepare started\")</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Crash data leaves the device and your whole team sees it. Use an opaque internal ID instead of an email, and keep it in line with your Data safety answers."}]},{"id":"analytics","title":"Analytics events that answer questions","tagline":"Start from the question, then log the few events and parameters that answer it.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Question first, then events. Event names say what the user did; the details go in parameters.","terms":[{"term":"Event","def":"A named thing the user did, such as share or select_content, logged with logEvent()."},{"term":"Parameter","def":"A key and value attached to an event (method = whatsapp) that lets you split the event in reports."},{"term":"Funnel","def":"A sequence of events where each step should follow the last. The drop between steps shows where users stop."},{"term":"Consent mode","def":"Google Analytics settings (analytics_storage, ad_storage and so on) that record what the user agreed to."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You want to know which share target is popular. Where does \"whatsapp\" go?","options":["In the event name: share_whatsapp","In a parameter: method = whatsapp","In a user property"],"a":1,"why":"One <code>share</code> event with a <code>method</code> parameter can be split by target in reports, and it costs one event type."},{"q":"Why route all events through one class such as ArticleAnalytics?","options":["It makes events send faster","Names and parameters stay consistent across screens","Firebase requires it"],"a":1,"why":"Event names are case-sensitive. One place that logs them stops two screens from inventing two spellings for the same action."},{"q":"Where do you set the default consent state before the user has chosen?","options":["In AndroidManifest.xml meta-data","In Remote Config","In the Play Console"],"a":0,"why":"Keys such as <code>google_analytics_default_allow_analytics_storage</code> set the defaults. <code>setConsent()</code> updates them later."},{"q":"The article screen logs <code>\"Share\"</code> and the gallery logs <code>\"share\"</code>. What do you see in Analytics?","options":["Two separate events, because event names are case-sensitive","One event, because names are lowercased","An error, because the same name was used twice"],"a":0,"why":"Share and share are different names, so the data splits in two. Routing every event through one class keeps names from drifting."},{"q":"<pre>// a = Firebase.analytics\na.setDefaultEventParameters(\n  bundleOf(\"edition\" to \"us\")\n)\na.logEvent(\"share\") {\n  param(\"edition\", \"uk\")\n}</pre>Which edition does the share event carry?","options":["\"us\", because defaults always win","Both values","\"uk\", because a value passed to logEvent wins over the default"],"a":2,"why":"Default parameters fill in what an event does not set itself. A value passed to logEvent overrides it, and passing null clears it."},{"q":"Which order matches the lesson’s advice for adding analytics?","options":["Write the question, then the event and parameters, check which decision it drives, then log it through one class like ArticleAnalytics","Log every button tap, look at the dashboard, then pick questions","Name events after the buttons, ship, then write the tracking plan"],"a":0,"why":"A row with no decision is dropped before any code exists. One class then keeps names and parameters consistent."}]},{"id":"flags","title":"Remote Config and feature flags","tagline":"Ship the code turned off, turn it on for a few users, and keep a switch to turn it off again.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Every flag has an in-app default, every risky feature has a kill switch, and every finished flag gets deleted.","terms":[{"term":"Feature flag","def":"A boolean or value, read at runtime, that turns a piece of shipped code on or off."},{"term":"Kill switch","def":"A flag you can set to false from the server to turn off a broken feature without a new release."},{"term":"Activate","def":"The Remote Config step that makes the last fetched values the ones getBoolean() and friends return."},{"term":"Rollout","def":"Turning a feature on for a growing share of users while watching crashes and analytics."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A feature behind a flag crashes for 20% of the users who have it. What is the fastest safe fix?","options":["Publish a hotfix to Play","Set the flag to false in Remote Config","Ask users to clear app data"],"a":1,"why":"The kill switch turns the code path off for everyone on their next fetch, or right away with real-time updates, without a release."},{"q":"What is the default minimum fetch interval for Remote Config?","options":["1 minute","1 hour","12 hours"],"a":2,"why":"The default is 12 hours. You can lower it for development, but frequent fetches in production can get throttled."},{"q":"Why ship in-app defaults for every parameter?","options":["Remote Config refuses to fetch without them","The app must behave sensibly before any fetch succeeds","They make fetches faster"],"a":1,"why":"First launch and offline launches have no fetched values. The defaults are what the app runs with until a fetch is activated."},{"q":"<pre>val task = rc.fetch()\ntask.addOnSuccessListener {\n  rc.getBoolean(\"new_ui\")\n}</pre>The server value of <code>new_ui</code> just changed to true. What does that getBoolean call return?","options":["true, because fetch() downloaded it","The last active value, or the in-app default, because fetch() does not activate","It throws until activate() runs"],"a":1,"why":"fetch() only downloads. getBoolean() reads the active layer, which changes only when you call activate() (or use fetchAndActivate())."},{"q":"A kill switch has to reach phones within minutes, not hours. What fits?","options":["Set the minimum fetch interval to 0 in production","Publish a hotfix with the new default","A real-time config update listener that activates right away when the key changes"],"a":2,"why":"Fetching very often gets throttled. A real-time listener hears about published changes and which keys they touched, and you can activate immediately."}]},{"id":"updates","title":"In-app updates","tagline":"Ask Play to update the app from inside it: in the background for most releases, full screen only when it is critical.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Flexible by default, immediate only when the old version cannot work. Check again in onResume().","terms":[{"term":"Flexible update","def":"An in-app update that downloads in the background while the app stays usable; you call completeUpdate() to install it."},{"term":"Immediate update","def":"A full-screen in-app update; Play installs it and restarts the app before the user can continue."},{"term":"Update priority","def":"A number from 0 to 5 set per release through the Play Developer API and read with updatePriority()."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which flow lets the user keep using the app while the update downloads?","options":["Immediate","Flexible","Both"],"a":1,"why":"Flexible updates download in the background. Immediate updates take over the screen."},{"q":"After a flexible update reaches DOWNLOADED, what must the app do?","options":["Nothing, Play restarts it","Call completeUpdate(), usually after the user taps Restart","Start an immediate update"],"a":1,"why":"Play does not restart the app for a flexible update. <code>completeUpdate()</code> installs it."},{"q":"An immediate update was interrupted when the user left the app. What do you look for in onResume()?","options":["UPDATE_NOT_<wbr>AVAILABLE","DEVELOPER_<wbr>TRIGGERED_<wbr>UPDATE_<wbr>IN_<wbr>PROGRESS","RESULT_<wbr>CANCELED"],"a":1,"why":"That availability value means an update your app started is still in progress, so you resume it."},{"q":"The rule: immediate if priority is 4 or more, flexible if the update has been available for 3 days or more, otherwise do nothing. A release has priority 2 and has been available for 1 day. What does the app do?","options":["Starts an immediate update","Starts a flexible update","Nothing yet"],"a":2,"why":"Neither branch matches: the priority is below 4 and the update is only 1 day old. The app checks again later and starts a flexible flow from day 3."},{"q":"The user cancelled an update flow. Later you call <code>startUpdateFlowForResult</code> again with the same <code>AppUpdateInfo</code> object. What is wrong?","options":["Nothing, the info can be reused","Each AppUpdateInfo can start a flow only once; ask <code>appUpdateInfo</code> for a fresh one","You must switch to an immediate update"],"a":1,"why":"An AppUpdateInfo is used up once it starts a flow. Request a new one before you retry."}]},{"id":"review","title":"In-app reviews","tagline":"Ask for a rating at a good moment, and accept that Play decides whether the card shows up.","level":"beginner","remember":"The review API may show nothing and never tells you what happened: call it at a good moment and carry on.","terms":[{"term":"ReviewInfo","def":"A short-lived token from requestReviewFlow() that you pass to launchReviewFlow()."},{"term":"Review quota","def":"Play’s undocumented, time-bound limit on how often one user can see the review card."}],"quiz":[{"q":"launchReviewFlow completed. What do you know?","options":["The user rated the app","The card was shown","Only that the flow is over"],"a":2,"why":"The API does not say whether the card appeared or whether a rating was given. Continue your normal flow."},{"q":"Where should a \"Rate us\" item in Settings lead?","options":["launchReviewFlow()","The app’s Play Store listing","A custom 5-star dialog"],"a":1,"why":"The quota can make the review API show nothing. A button the user taps must always do something visible."}]},{"id":"billing","title":"Play Billing","tagline":"Connect, load products, launch the purchase, verify on your server, then acknowledge within three days.","level":"advanced","remember":"Grant only PURCHASED, verify on your server, acknowledge within three days, and check queryPurchasesAsync() on every start.","terms":[{"term":"Purchase token","def":"The globally unique string that identifies one purchase; your server stores it and verifies it with Google."},{"term":"Acknowledge","def":"Telling Play you granted the purchase. Without it, Play refunds the purchase after three days."},{"term":"Pending purchase","def":"A purchase waiting for payment, such as cash at a store. Grant nothing until it becomes PURCHASED."},{"term":"Consume","def":"Marking a one-time product as used so it can be bought again; it also acknowledges the purchase."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to a purchase that is never acknowledged?","options":["Nothing","Play refunds it after three days and revokes the entitlement","It is acknowledged automatically"],"a":1,"why":"Unacknowledged purchases are refunded automatically after three days."},{"q":"The purchase state is PENDING. What should the app do?","options":["Grant and acknowledge","Grant, but acknowledge later","Wait until it becomes PURCHASED"],"a":2,"why":"Pending means no money has moved yet. Grant and acknowledge only once it is PURCHASED."},{"q":"Which value makes a good primary key for purchases in your database?","options":["orderId","purchaseToken","productId"],"a":1,"why":"The purchase token is globally unique. Not every purchase has an orderId."},{"q":"Which order is right for a one-time \"remove ads\" purchase?","options":["Grant, acknowledge, then send the token to your server","Get PURCHASED, send the token to your server, verify it with the Play Developer API, grant, then acknowledge","Acknowledge right away, then verify when the server has time"],"a":1,"why":"The server decides before anything is granted. Acknowledging within three days keeps Play from refunding a purchase you have verified."},{"q":"A user pays with cash at a store. The payment clears two days later while your app is closed. How does the app find out?","options":["<code>queryPurchasesAsync()</code> after connecting and in onResume() returns it","<code>onPurchasesUpdated()</code> fires on the next launch by itself","Play shows the app a system notification it can read"],"a":0,"why":"onPurchasesUpdated() only fires while a connection is open. Querying purchases on every start catches anything that finished while you were not looking."},{"q":"A user buys 100 coins and should be able to buy them again. What completes the purchase?","options":["Acknowledge it, then consume it a day later","Acknowledge it only","Consume it, which also counts as acknowledging and lets the product be bought again"],"a":2,"why":"Consumables are consumed instead of acknowledged. The server must still refuse a token it has already granted, since a consume call can fail."}]},{"id":"policy","title":"Play policy essentials","tagline":"A few rules decide whether Play accepts your next update: target API level, the Data safety form, the billing library, and a verified developer.","level":"beginner","remember":"Before each release: targetSdk meets this year’s rule, the billing library is current, and the Data safety form matches every SDK you added.","terms":[{"term":"targetSdk","def":"The Android version your app says it was built and tested for; it opts the app into that version’s behavior changes."},{"term":"Data safety form","def":"The Play Console declaration of what user data your app and its SDKs collect and share, shown on the store listing."},{"term":"Developer verification","def":"Android’s requirement that apps on certified devices come from a registered, verified developer."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your app collects no user data at all. Do you need to fill in the Data safety form?","options":["No","Yes, every app on Play does","Only if it has ads"],"a":1,"why":"All apps published on Google Play must complete the form, even when the answer is \"no data collected\"."},{"q":"Crashlytics sends crash logs from your app. Whose data is that in the Data safety form?","options":["Google’s, so you skip it","Your app’s, so you declare it","Nobody’s, crash logs are exempt"],"a":1,"why":"Data collected through third-party SDKs in your app counts as your app’s data."},{"q":"Since August 31, 2026, which Play Billing Library versions can new apps and updates use?","options":["Any version","Version 8 or later","Only version 9.1.0"],"a":1,"why":"Version 8 or later is required (extension possible to November 1, 2026). 9.1.0 is simply the latest."},{"q":"Your app sends a search query to your server, uses it in memory for that one request and stores nothing. What goes in the Data safety form?","options":["Nothing, it is never stored","Declare it; ephemeral data is still declared, but it is not shown on the store listing","Declare it as shared with a third party"],"a":1,"why":"Data that leaves the device counts as collected, even if it is only handled in memory. Ephemeral handling is still declared; it just is not shown to users."},{"q":"The target API deadline is August 31. When should you plan the targetSdk bump?","options":["Early in the summer, so the extension stays a backup","The week before the deadline","Ask for the extension first, then plan"],"a":0,"why":"Raising targetSdk turns on new behavior that you need to test on a device. Starting early leaves time to fix what breaks."}]}]},{"id":"cs","title":"Data structures and algorithms","description":"Big-O, the core data structures and the classic algorithm patterns, in Kotlin, to write faster code and solve problems with confidence.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/cs.js","lessons":[{"id":"big-o","title":"Big-O: how cost grows","tagline":"Count how the work grows with the input, not the milliseconds.","level":"beginner","remember":"Count the loops that depend on n. Nested loops multiply, loops in a row add, and halving gives log n.","terms":[{"term":"Big-O","def":"A description of how an algorithm’s work grows with input size, keeping only the fastest-growing term."},{"term":"Time complexity","def":"How the number of steps grows as the input grows, for example O(n) or O(n²)."},{"term":"Space complexity","def":"How much extra memory an algorithm needs as the input grows, including the call stack."},{"term":"O(log n)","def":"Growth where doubling the input adds only one more step, typical of halving the problem each time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A function loops over a list, and inside that loop it loops over the same list again. What is its time complexity?","options":["O(n)","O(2n)","O(n²)"],"a":2,"why":"For each of the n items it does n more steps: n × n."},{"q":"Binary search halves the range on every step. About how many steps for 1,000,000 sorted items?","options":["About 20","About 1,000","About 500,000"],"a":0,"why":"2²⁰ is about 1,048,576, so 20 halvings get from a million down to one."},{"q":"<code>val copy = list.sorted()</code>. How much extra space does it use?","options":["O(1)","O(n), because it returns a new list"],"a":1,"why":"<code>sorted()</code> leaves the original alone and builds a new list of n items. <code>sort()</code> on a mutable list sorts in place."},{"q":"a and b are Lists with n and m items. What is the cost of <code>a.filter { it in b }</code>?","options":["O(n), one loop","O(n × m), because <code>in</code> on a List scans it","O(n + m)"],"a":1,"why":"The filter runs n times and each <code>in</code> walks b. Build <code>b.toHashSet()</code> once to make it O(n + m)."},{"q":"<code>fun sumTo(n: Int): Long = if (n == 0) 0 else n + sumTo(n - 1)</code>. How much extra memory does it use?","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n), one stack frame per call that has not returned yet"],"a":2,"why":"Each call waits for the next one, so n frames sit on the call stack at once. The loop version uses O(1)."}]},{"id":"arrays-lists","title":"Arrays and lists","tagline":"Reading by index is instant. Inserting at the front moves everything.","level":"beginner","remember":"Index and append are cheap. Insert or remove at the front shifts everything: O(n).","terms":[{"term":"Amortized O(1)","def":"An operation that is sometimes slow but cheap on average over many calls, like ArrayList.add."},{"term":"Capacity","def":"How many slots a list’s backing array has, which can be more than the number of items in it."},{"term":"Boxing","def":"Wrapping a primitive like an Int in an object so it can live in a generic collection."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is <code>add()</code> at the end of an ArrayList called O(1) amortized?","options":["It never copies","It copies rarely, so the average cost per add stays constant","The JVM caches it"],"a":1,"why":"A full array is copied into a bigger one, but the growth is by a factor, so total copies stay proportional to the number of adds."},{"q":"Which call is O(n) on an ArrayList of n items?","options":["list[n / 2]","list.add(x)","list.add(0, x)"],"a":2,"why":"Inserting at index 0 shifts every item one slot right."},{"q":"You need cheap adds and removes at both ends. Which type?","options":["ArrayList","ArrayDeque","IntArray"],"a":1,"why":"<code>ArrayDeque</code> is a ring buffer: both ends are O(1) amortized."},{"q":"<pre>var i = 0\nwhile (i &lt; list.size) {\n  if (list[i] &lt; 0)\n    list.removeAt(i)\n  else i++\n}</pre>What is the worst-case cost, and what does it in one pass?","options":["O(n²); <code>list.removeAll { it &lt; 0 }</code>","O(n); it is already fine","O(n log n); sort the list first"],"a":0,"why":"Every removeAt shifts the rest of the list, so many removals cost O(n²). removeAll does the job in one pass."},{"q":"You read <code>linked[5_000]</code> on a java.util.LinkedList of 10,000 items. What does it cost?","options":["O(1), like an array","O(n), it walks node by node from one end","O(log n)"],"a":1,"why":"A linked list has no index math. It follows pointers from the nearer end, and the scattered nodes make the walk slow too."}]},{"id":"hashing","title":"Hash maps and sets","tagline":"Turn a key into a bucket number, and lookups stop depending on size.","level":"intermediate","remember":"equals and hashCode go together. Equal objects must have equal hash codes, or the map looks in the wrong bucket.","terms":[{"term":"Hash code","def":"An Int computed from an object, used by hash maps and sets to pick a bucket."},{"term":"Bucket","def":"One slot in a hash table’s array, holding every entry whose hash maps to that index."},{"term":"Collision","def":"Two different keys landing in the same bucket; equals() is used to tell them apart."},{"term":"Load factor","def":"How full a hash table may get before it grows; the JVM HashMap default is 0.75."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two keys land in the same bucket. What does HashMap use to tell them apart?","options":["hashCode()","equals()","toString()"],"a":1,"why":"The hash code only picks the bucket. Inside it, keys are compared with <code>equals()</code>."},{"q":"Which of these keeps insertion order when you iterate?","options":["hashMapOf()","mutableMapOf()","HashSet()"],"a":1,"why":"<code>mutableMapOf()</code> returns a <code>LinkedHashMap</code>."},{"q":"Why is HashMap.get O(1) only on average?","options":["Because of garbage collection","If many keys collide in one bucket, the map has to search that bucket","Because Strings are immutable"],"a":1,"why":"With a good hash, buckets hold one or two keys. With a bad one, many keys pile into one bucket."},{"q":"<pre>data class Tag(var name: String)\nval tags = hashSetOf(Tag(\"a\"))\nval t = tags.first()\nt.name = \"b\"\nprintln(t in tags)</pre>What does it print?","options":["true","false","It throws ConcurrentModificationException"],"a":1,"why":"t is filed under the hash it had with name \"a\". The lookup uses the new hash and searches another bucket, so the entry is lost. Keys should be immutable."},{"q":"<pre>class P(val x: Int, val y: Int)\nval s = HashSet&lt;P&gt;()\ns.add(P(1, 2))\ns.add(P(1, 2))\nprintln(s.size)</pre>What does it print?","options":["2","1","It does not compile"],"a":0,"why":"A plain class inherits identity equals and hashCode, so the two points are different keys. A data class would print 1."}]},{"id":"stacks-queues","title":"Stacks and queues","tagline":"Last in, first out for undo and brackets. First in, first out for BFS and jobs.","level":"beginner","remember":"ArrayDeque for both: addLast + removeLast is a stack, addLast + removeFirst is a queue.","terms":[{"term":"Stack (LIFO)","def":"A collection where the last element added is the first one removed."},{"term":"Queue (FIFO)","def":"A collection where the first element added is the first one removed."},{"term":"Deque","def":"A double-ended queue that supports adding and removing at both ends."},{"term":"Ring buffer","def":"An array used in a circle: indexes wrap around from the end to the start, so nothing has to shift."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You push A, then B, then C onto a stack and pop once. What comes out?","options":["A","B","C"],"a":2,"why":"Last in, first out: C was pushed last."},{"q":"Which structure does BFS use to pick the next node?","options":["A stack","A queue","A hash map"],"a":1,"why":"A queue hands out nodes in the order they were found, which is the order of their distance."},{"q":"Why prefer ArrayDeque over LinkedList for a queue?","options":["It is thread-safe","Its elements sit in one array: less memory, faster walks, still O(1) at both ends","It keeps elements sorted"],"a":1,"why":"Neither is thread-safe. <code>ArrayDeque</code> avoids one node object per element."},{"q":"The bracket checker pushes openers and pops on closers. What does it return for <code>([)]</code>?","options":["true, every opener has a closer","false, at the end, because the stack is not empty","false, at <code>)</code>, because the top of the stack is <code>[</code>"],"a":2,"why":"On a closer the checker pops and compares. <code>)</code> pops <code>[</code>, which does not match, so it stops right there."},{"q":"With <code>compileSdk = 35</code> and <code>minSdk = 24</code>, you call <code>history.removeLast()</code> on a MutableList. What happens on an Android 14 phone?","options":["It works, Kotlin provides removeLast()","It returns null","NoSuchMethodError, because the call resolved to the Java method Android 15 added"],"a":2,"why":"With compileSdk 35, Kotlin picks the new Java List.removeLast(), which older devices lack. Use removeLastOrNull() or removeAt(lastIndex); lint’s NewApi flags it."}]},{"id":"two-pointers","title":"Two pointers and sliding window","tagline":"Move two indexes instead of trying every pair, and O(n²) becomes O(n).","level":"intermediate","remember":"Each pointer only moves in one direction, so the whole scan is O(n).","terms":[{"term":"Two pointers","def":"A technique that moves two indexes through an array by a rule, instead of checking every pair."},{"term":"Sliding window","def":"A range [left, right] that moves through an array, growing on the right and shrinking on the left."},{"term":"Complement","def":"The value still needed to reach a target, target − x, often looked up in a hash map."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two pointers from both ends rely on what?","options":["The array is sorted","The array has no duplicates","The array size is even"],"a":0,"why":"Sorting is what tells you which pointer to move when the sum is too small or too big."},{"q":"Why is a sliding window O(n) even with a while loop inside the for loop?","options":["The while loop runs at most once","Each item enters the window once and leaves at most once","Kotlin optimizes the loop"],"a":1,"why":"<code>left</code> never moves back, so all the inner loop runs add up to at most n steps."},{"q":"Sorted array, pointers at both ends, the sum is too small. What moves?","options":["left moves right","right moves left","both move"],"a":0,"why":"Only a bigger left value can raise the sum."},{"q":"<code>maxSumOfK(intArrayOf(2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2), k = 3)</code>. What does it return?","options":["8","9","11"],"a":1,"why":"The windows sum to 8, 7, 9 and 6. Each step adds the item entering and subtracts the one leaving, and the best is 9 (5 + 1 + 3)."},{"q":"An unsorted array, and you need the original indexes of two numbers that add up to a target. Which tool fits?","options":["Two pointers from both ends","Sort first, then two pointers","One pass with a hash map from each value you need to its index"],"a":2,"why":"The two-pointer rule needs sorted data, and sorting loses the original indexes. A map of what you have seen finds the pair in O(n)."}]},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary search","tagline":"Halve a sorted range on every check: a million items take about 20.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Pick what lo and hi mean, keep it true on every step, and make sure every branch shrinks the range.","terms":[{"term":"Binary search","def":"Finding a value in sorted data by checking the middle and dropping half of the range each time."},{"term":"Insertion point","def":"The index where a missing value would go to keep a list sorted."},{"term":"Lower bound","def":"The first index whose value is greater than or equal to the target."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>listOf(10, 20, 30).binarySearch(25)</code> returns?","options":["-1","-3","2"],"a":1,"why":"25 would go at index 2, so the result is −2 − 1 = −3."},{"q":"What does binary search need from the data?","options":["Sorted in the same order you search with","Stored in a HashMap","No duplicates"],"a":0,"why":"Duplicates are fine. Without sorting, dropping half the range is not safe."},{"q":"Inclusive bounds <code>lo..hi</code>, and <code>a[mid] &gt; target</code>. Which update is right?","options":["hi = mid","hi = mid - 1","lo = mid"],"a":1,"why":"<code>mid</code> is already ruled out and everything right of it is bigger still."},{"q":"<pre>var lo = 0\nvar hi = a.lastIndex\nwhile (lo &lt;= hi) {\n  val mid = (lo + hi) ushr 1\n  if (a[mid] == t) return mid\n  if (a[mid] &lt; t) lo = mid + 1\n  else hi = mid\n}</pre>Which line is the bug?","options":["<code>var hi = a.lastIndex</code>","<code>while (lo &lt;= hi)</code>","<code>else hi = mid</code>"],"a":2,"why":"With inclusive bounds, <code>hi = mid</code> can leave the range the same size when lo == hi, so the loop never ends. Use <code>hi = mid - 1</code>."},{"q":"<code>lowerBound(intArrayOf(10, 20, 20, 30), 20)</code>. What does it return?","options":["1","2","3"],"a":0,"why":"lowerBound returns the first index whose value is at least the target. The first 20 is at index 1."},{"q":"Why can binary search find the smallest truck capacity that ships every parcel in 5 days?","options":["The weights are sorted","The answer to \"can capacity c do it?\" is no up to some point and yes from there on","There are fewer than 1,000 parcels"],"a":1,"why":"Binary search works on any yes/no question that flips once. Each check is O(n), and the search takes O(log(range)) checks."}]},{"id":"sorting","title":"Sorting","tagline":"Use the stdlib sort, know that it is stable, and build comparators that read well.","level":"intermediate","remember":"sorted* is O(n log n) and stable for objects. Build comparators with compareBy and thenBy, not by hand.","terms":[{"term":"Stable sort","def":"A sort that keeps items with equal keys in their original relative order."},{"term":"Comparator","def":"An object that decides the order of two items, built in Kotlin with compareBy and thenBy."},{"term":"TimSort","def":"The merge-sort-based, stable sort the JVM uses for objects; it is fast on data that is already partly sorted."},{"term":"Partition","def":"The quick sort step that puts smaller items left of a pivot and bigger items right of it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “stable” mean for <code>sortedBy</code>?","options":["It never throws","Items with equal keys keep their original order","It runs in O(n)"],"a":1,"why":"Stability is about ties: equal items do not swap places."},{"q":"Which sort is O(n log n) in the worst case and stable?","options":["Quick sort","Merge sort","Selection sort"],"a":1,"why":"Merge sort always splits in half and merges with ties going left. Quick sort can hit O(n²) and is not stable."},{"q":"You only need the biggest number in a list. Best call?","options":["sortedDescending().first()","maxOrNull()","sorted().last()"],"a":1,"why":"One pass, O(n), and no new list."},{"q":"<code>listOf(\"Ken\" to 2, \"Omar\" to 1, \"Lena\" to 2).sortedBy { it.second }</code>. What is the order of names?","options":["Omar, Ken, Lena","Omar, Lena, Ken","Ken, Lena, Omar"],"a":0,"why":"sortedBy is stable. Ken and Lena both have 2, so they keep their input order: Ken before Lena."},{"q":"The quick sort from the lesson always picks the last item as the pivot. You give it an array that is already sorted. What happens?","options":["It finishes in O(n), since nothing moves","It stays O(n log n)","It degrades to O(n²), because every partition splits off only one item"],"a":2,"why":"The pivot is the biggest item every time, so each partition leaves one side empty. That is n levels of O(n) work."}]},{"id":"trees","title":"Recursion and trees","tagline":"A tree is a node plus smaller trees, so recursive code follows its shape.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Base case first, then recurse on smaller pieces. DFS uses the call stack; BFS uses a queue.","terms":[{"term":"Recursion","def":"A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller versions of it."},{"term":"Base case","def":"The input a recursive function answers directly, without calling itself, so the recursion stops."},{"term":"DFS","def":"Depth-first search: follow one branch to the bottom before backing up, using recursion or a stack."},{"term":"Binary search tree","def":"A binary tree where every node’s left subtree holds smaller values and its right subtree bigger ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In-order traversal of a binary search tree gives?","options":["The values sorted ascending","The values level by level","The root last"],"a":0,"why":"Left subtree (smaller), then the node, then right subtree (bigger), at every level."},{"q":"What does every recursive function need?","options":["A loop","A base case that stops it","A global variable"],"a":1,"why":"Without one it never stops calling itself."},{"q":"Which order records the root first?","options":["Pre-order","In-order","Post-order"],"a":0,"why":"Pre-order records each node before its children, and the root is visited first."},{"q":"You build a binary search tree by inserting 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in that order. What does <code>contains(5)</code> cost?","options":["O(n), because the tree has become a line","O(log n), because it is a binary search tree","O(1)"],"a":0,"why":"Search costs O(height). Sorted inserts make every node a right child, so the height is n."},{"q":"A tree can be 100,000 levels deep and each node has two children. Which fix does NOT avoid the StackOverflowError?","options":["Keep your own stack in an ArrayDeque","Mark the function <code>tailrec</code>","Wrap it in DeepRecursiveFunction"],"a":1,"why":"tailrec only works when the recursive call is the very last thing the function does. With two children, one call always has to come back first."}]},{"id":"graphs","title":"Graphs","tagline":"Nodes and edges. Store them as an adjacency list, then walk them with BFS or DFS.","level":"advanced","remember":"Graphs have cycles, so keep a visited set. BFS gives shortest paths when every edge costs the same.","terms":[{"term":"Adjacency list","def":"A graph stored as a map from each node to the list of its neighbours."},{"term":"Visited set","def":"The nodes a search has already seen, kept so cycles don’t make it loop forever."},{"term":"Unweighted shortest path","def":"The path with the fewest edges; BFS finds it because it explores in order of distance."},{"term":"Back edge","def":"In a DFS, an edge to a node that is still on the current path; in a directed graph it means a cycle."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does BFS find the shortest path in an unweighted graph?","options":["It visits nodes in order of their distance from the start","It tries every possible path","It uses recursion"],"a":0,"why":"The queue hands out all distance-1 nodes before any distance-2 node, so the first time it reaches a node is along a shortest path."},{"q":"How much memory does an adjacency list need for V nodes and E edges?","options":["O(V²)","O(V + E)","O(E²)"],"a":1,"why":"One entry per node plus one list item per edge (two for undirected edges)."},{"q":"In DFS cycle detection, you reach a node that is still gray. What does it mean?","options":["There is a cycle","It is a dead end","It is the shortest path"],"a":0,"why":"Gray nodes are on the current path, so you have walked back onto your own path."},{"q":"Each road has a driving time in minutes. You need the fastest route. Which algorithm fits?","options":["BFS, it finds shortest paths","Dijkstra with a priority queue","DFS with a visited set"],"a":1,"why":"BFS counts edges, but a route with more roads can still take fewer minutes. Dijkstra always expands the cheapest node found so far."},{"q":"<pre>val node = queue.removeFirst()\nif (!seen.add(node)) continue\nfor (n in g[node].orEmpty())\n  queue.addLast(n)</pre>The results are right, but it is slow on a dense graph. Why?","options":["It should use a stack","removeFirst() is O(n)","Nodes are marked when taken out, so the same node can sit in the queue many times"],"a":2,"why":"Mark a node the moment you add it to the queue. Then each node is queued once."},{"q":"A social graph has 1,000,000 users who follow about 200 accounts each. How do you store it?","options":["An adjacency list: O(V + E) memory","An adjacency matrix: O(1) edge checks are worth it","A list of all edges, scanned each time"],"a":0,"why":"A matrix would need 10¹² cells for a graph that has about 200 million edges. Sparse graphs fit an adjacency list."}]},{"id":"dp","title":"Dynamic programming","tagline":"Solve each small problem once, store the answer, and build the big one from it.","level":"advanced","remember":"Say what dp[i] means in one sentence, find how it builds from smaller i, set the base case, then fill in order.","terms":[{"term":"Dynamic programming","def":"Solving a problem by combining stored answers to smaller, overlapping subproblems."},{"term":"Memoization","def":"Top-down DP: recursion plus a cache, so each subproblem is computed once."},{"term":"Tabulation","def":"Bottom-up DP: filling a table from the base case upward in a loop."},{"term":"Overlapping subproblems","def":"The same smaller problem showing up many times inside a recursion, which is what makes DP pay off."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Memoization vs tabulation?","options":["Memoization is top-down recursion with a cache; tabulation fills a table bottom-up","They are the same thing","Tabulation always uses recursion"],"a":0,"why":"Both store subproblem answers. They differ in direction and in whether recursion drives the order."},{"q":"Why is the plain recursive <code>ways(n)</code> slow?","options":["Recursion is slow on Android","It solves the same subproblems many times","It uses Long"],"a":1,"why":"The number of calls grows exponentially because <code>ways(k)</code> is recomputed for every path that reaches it."},{"q":"Coins 1, 3 and 4. Fewest coins for 6?","options":["2","3","6"],"a":0,"why":"3 + 3. Greedy picks 4 + 1 + 1, which is 3 coins."},{"q":"<pre>fun ways(n: Int): Long {\n  val memo = HashMap&lt;Int,Long&gt;()\n  if (n &lt;= 1) return 1\n  return memo.getOrPut(n) {\n    ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)\n  }\n}</pre>What is wrong?","options":["It returns the wrong answer","Each call makes a new empty memo, so it is as slow as plain recursion","getOrPut cannot be called recursively"],"a":1,"why":"The answers are right, but nothing is ever found in a fresh map. Pass one shared memo through all calls."},{"q":"Which order does the lesson give for solving a DP problem?","options":["Write the code, then find the base case, then name the state","Fill the table, then define the transition, then the state","Define the state, then the transition, then the base case, then the fill order"],"a":2,"why":"Say what dp[i] means first. The transition and base case follow from it, and the fill order makes sure every value you read is already there."},{"q":"<code>gridPaths(3, 3)</code>: paths from the top-left to the bottom-right of a 3 × 3 grid, moving only right or down. What does it return?","options":["6","4","9"],"a":0,"why":"The first row and column are 1. Then dp[1][1] = 2, dp[1][2] = 3, dp[2][1] = 3, and dp[2][2] = 3 + 3 = 6."}]},{"id":"interview","title":"Solving a problem step by step","tagline":"Clarify, try examples, start simple, improve, then test out loud.","level":"beginner","remember":"Clarify, examples, brute force, optimize, test. Say each step out loud before you code it.","terms":[{"term":"Brute force","def":"The simplest correct solution, usually trying every option, used as a starting point."},{"term":"Edge case","def":"An input at the boundary, like empty, one item, or the maximum size, where bugs tend to hide."},{"term":"Trade-off","def":"Gaining one thing, like speed, by giving up another, like memory or simplicity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should you do first when you get a problem?","options":["Write the optimal code","Clarify the inputs, outputs and edge cases","Pick the fastest language"],"a":1,"why":"Questions about size, empty input and the no-answer case shape everything after."},{"q":"Why say the brute force solution out loud?","options":["It gives a correct baseline and a Big-O to improve on","Slow code is the goal","It is always the final answer"],"a":0,"why":"A working baseline shows you can solve it, and its cost points at the repeated work to remove."},{"q":"The input is sorted and you need a pair with a given sum. Which tool fits?","options":["Two pointers","Dynamic programming","A stack"],"a":0,"why":"Sorted input lets you move one pointer or the other based on the sum, in O(n)."},{"q":"Which order is the five-step routine?","options":["Brute force, clarify, optimize, examples, test","Clarify, examples, brute force, optimize, test","Examples, optimize, clarify, test, brute force"],"a":1,"why":"Clarifying first can change the problem, examples check your understanding, and the brute force gives a baseline to improve before you test."},{"q":"<code>fun maxGap(a: IntArray) = (1 until a.size).maxOf { a[it] - a[it - 1] }</code>. What does <code>maxGap(intArrayOf(5))</code> do?","options":["Returns 0","Returns 5","Throws NoSuchElementException"],"a":2,"why":"With one item the range 1 until 1 is empty, and maxOf throws on an empty range. One-item and empty inputs are the first tests to try."}]}]},{"id":"capkotlin","title":"Project: a budget tracker","description":"Build one small Kotlin program step by step: it reads expenses, adds them up, saves them and warns you about your budget.","requires":["kotlin","oop","solid"],"kind":"project","builds":"A budget tracker in plain Kotlin: it reads lines like \"12 food lunch\", skips bad ones, shows totals per category, saves to a text file and loads it back, warns when a category goes over its limit, and checks itself with small hand-made tests.","file":"tracks/cap-kotlin.js","lessons":[{"id":"plan","title":"What we’ll build","tagline":"Write down what the program must do, then cut the work into small steps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn an idea into a short list of checkable requirements","Cut a program into small steps that each end with code that runs","Decide what to leave out of the first version"],"remember":"Write down what the program must do as checkable sentences, then build it in small steps that each end with a program that runs.","terms":[{"term":"requirement","def":"A short sentence about what a program must do, written so that anyone can check it: “Show the total for each category.”"},{"term":"project scope","def":"What a project includes now, and what it leaves out on purpose (not the same as a variable’s scope)."},{"term":"build step","def":"A small piece of work that ends with a program that runs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a good requirement?","options":["Show the total for each category","Make the code good","Use a lot of Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"You can run the program and check whether it shows the totals. The others can’t be checked."},{"q":"Why should every step end with a program that runs?","options":["If something breaks, the mistake is in the code you just added","Kotlin can only run short programs","Then you never need to test"],"a":0,"why":"The last version worked, so a new problem comes from the new lines."},{"q":"The plan says amounts are whole numbers. Why leave cents out?","options":["To keep the first version small; cents can be a later step","Kotlin has no decimal numbers","Nobody ever needs cents"],"a":0,"why":"Project scope keeps a project finishable. A first version that works beats a big one that doesn’t."}],"refs":["kotlin.lists","kotlin.list-loop","git.commits"]},{"id":"model","title":"The data model","tagline":"Category, Expense and Tracker: the three types the rest is built on.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe an expense with a data class and its category with an enum","Keep the list of expenses inside a class, behind a private property","Create, print and copy expenses"],"remember":"Category is an enum, Expense is a data class, and Tracker keeps its list private behind add and all().","terms":[{"term":"data model","def":"The types a program uses to describe its world. Here: <code>Category</code>, <code>Expense</code> and <code>Tracker</code>."},{"term":"List<Expense>","def":"The type of a list of expenses. Through this type you can read the list but not add to it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the category an enum and not a String?","options":["Only the five categories can exist, so a typo won’t compile","An enum prints faster","A data class can’t hold a String"],"a":0,"why":"The enum is the full list of allowed values. Kotlin rejects anything else."},{"q":"<code>val dinner = lunch.copy(note = \"dinner\")</code>. What is <code>lunch.note</code> now?","options":["<code>\"lunch with Sam\"</code>, as before","<code>\"dinner\"</code>","An error: <code>note</code> is a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>copy</code> makes a new object. The original keeps its values."},{"q":"Outside the <code>Tracker</code> class, which line won’t compile?","options":["<code>tracker.add(lunch)</code>","<code>tracker.all().size</code>","<code>tracker.expenses.add(lunch)</code>"],"a":2,"why":"<code>expenses</code> is private. Outside code goes through <code>add</code> and <code>all()</code>."}],"refs":["kotlin.data-classes","kotlin.enums","kotlin.encapsulation-basics","kotlin.mutable-lists","oop.domain-modeling"]},{"id":"input","title":"Reading input","tagline":"Turn a typed line like “12 food lunch” into an Expense, and skip lines that make no sense.","level":"beginner","goals":["Cut a line of text into parts with <code>split</code>","Turn each part into the right type, or <code>null</code> when it doesn’t fit","Skip bad lines without crashing"],"remember":"split(\" \", limit = 3) cuts the line; each part becomes a value or null; a null anywhere means the line is skipped.","terms":[{"term":"split","def":"Cuts text into a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> at a separator: <code>\"a b\".split(\" \")</code> is <code>[a, b]</code>."},{"term":"limit","def":"In <code>split</code>, the most parts to make. The last part keeps the rest of the text."},{"term":"lowercase()","def":"The same text in small letters: <code>\"Food\".lowercase()</code> is <code>\"food\"</code>."},{"term":"parse","def":"To read text and turn it into a value of some type, or say that it can’t be done."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>\"9 travel bus to Oslo\".split(\" \", limit = 3)</code>?","options":["<code>[9, travel, bus to Oslo]</code>","<code>[9, travel, bus, to, Oslo]</code>","<code>[9 travel bus to Oslo]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Three parts at most. The third keeps the rest of the line, spaces included."},{"q":"<code>parseExpense(\"abc food\")</code> gives <code>null</code>. Why doesn’t the program crash?","options":["<code>toIntOrNull()</code> gives <code>null</code> instead of throwing, and the line is skipped","Kotlin skips bad lines by itself","<code>split</code> removes the letters"],"a":0,"why":"<code>toInt()</code> would crash on <code>\"abc\"</code>. <code>toIntOrNull()</code> gives <code>null</code>, which the code checks."},{"q":"Why does <code>parseExpense</code> return <code>Expense?</code> and not <code>Expense</code>?","options":["Some lines make no sense, and <code>null</code> says “no expense here”","Every expense might be empty","Data classes must be nullable"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>?</code> makes the caller deal with a missing answer, which is how bad lines get skipped."}],"refs":["kotlin.null-safety","kotlin.when","kotlin.lists","kotlin.if-expression","kotlin.default-args"]},{"id":"reports","title":"Reports","tagline":"Add up what you spent, in total and per category.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add up amounts with <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>sum</code>","Write the totals as extension functions on <code>List&lt;Expense&gt;</code>","Go through every category with <code>Category.entries</code>"],"remember":"filter narrows, map picks the amount, sum adds; Category.entries lists every category for the report.","terms":[{"term":"report","def":"Numbers worked out from the data, like a total per category."},{"term":"entries","def":"A list of all the entries of an enum, in the order they are written: <code>Category.entries</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>listOf(Expense(5, Category.FUN, \"\"), Expense(7, Category.FUN, \"\")).total()</code>?","options":["<code>12</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>[5, 7]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> gives <code>[5, 7]</code>, and <code>sum()</code> adds them."},{"q":"What does <code>Category.entries</code> hold?","options":["All five categories, in the order they are written","Only the categories you have spent on","The number 5"],"a":0,"why":"It lists every entry of the enum, used or not. That is why <code>printReport</code> checks <code>spent &gt; 0</code>."},{"q":"Inside <code>fun List&lt;Expense&gt;.total()</code>, what is <code>this</code>?","options":["The list <code>total()</code> was called on","One <code>Expense</code>","The <code>Tracker</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The value before the dot is the receiver, and <code>this</code> is the receiver."}],"refs":["kotlin.collection-ops","kotlin.extensions","kotlin.lambdas","kotlin.enums"]},{"id":"storage","title":"Saving to a file","tagline":"Keep the expenses when the program stops, and load them next time.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write lines to a text file and read them back with <code>File</code>","Hide where data is kept behind a <code>Storage</code> interface","Swap the file for a list in memory without changing the Tracker"],"remember":"One line per expense, written with writeText and read back with readLines; the Tracker only knows the Storage interface.","terms":[{"term":"import","def":"Makes a class from somewhere else usable in this file: <code>import java.io.File</code>."},{"term":"File","def":"A file on disk, found by its name or path: <code>File(\"expenses.txt\")</code>."},{"term":"writeText / readLines","def":"<code>writeText(text)</code> writes text into the file, replacing what was there. <code>readLines()</code> reads it back as a list with one item per line."},{"term":"\\n","def":"Inside quotes, a new line. <code>\"a\\nb\"</code> prints <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> on two lines."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>Expense(800, Category.RENT, \"\").toLine()</code> give?","options":["<code>\"800 RENT \"</code>, ending with a space","<code>\"800 RENT\"</code>","<code>\"Expense(amount=800, category=RENT, note=)\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The template puts a space before the note, and the note is empty. <code>split</code> later reads it back as an empty note."},{"q":"Why does <code>saveTo</code> take a <code>Storage</code> and not a <code>FileStorage</code>?","options":["So any storage works: a file, memory for tests, later a database","An interface runs faster","A class can’t be a parameter type"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker only needs the promise: save lines, load lines. Who keeps them is a detail it doesn’t depend on."},{"q":"On the first run, <code>expenses.txt</code> doesn’t exist. What does <code>FileStorage.load()</code> give?","options":["An empty list: <code>readLines()</code> throws, and <code>catch</code> returns <code>listOf()</code>","The program crashes","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> turns the missing file into “nothing saved yet”."}],"refs":["kotlin.interfaces","kotlin.exceptions","kotlin.extensions","solid.dip"]},{"id":"rules","title":"Budget rules","tagline":"Set a limit per category, and get a clear answer: no limit, within it, or over.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep a limit per category in a <code>Map</code>","Answer with one of a closed set of results, using a sealed interface","Turn each result into text with a <code>when</code> that needs no <code>else</code>"],"remember":"Budget looks up the limit and answers NoLimit, Within(left) or Over(amount); describe handles all three with no else.","terms":[{"term":"Map<Category, Int>","def":"The type of a map whose keys are categories and whose values are <code>Int</code>s."},{"term":"validation","def":"Checking values against rules: is this line a real expense, is this spending within its limit."},{"term":"budget limit","def":"The most you plan to spend in one category."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The limits map has no RENT key. What is <code>limits[Category.RENT]</code>?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"A missing key gives <code>null</code>. <code>status</code> turns that into <code>NoLimit</code>."},{"q":"Why does the <code>when</code> in <code>describe</code> need no <code>else</code>?","options":["<code>BudgetStatus</code> is sealed, so Kotlin knows all three kinds are handled","<code>else</code> is not allowed with objects","Because it returns a String"],"a":0,"why":"All members of a sealed interface are known, so Kotlin can check that each has a branch."},{"q":"You add <code>class Near(val left: Int) : BudgetStatus</code>. What happens?","options":["<code>describe</code> stops compiling until it handles <code>Near</code>","Near statuses print nothing","Nothing changes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>when</code> no longer covers every member, and Kotlin points you to it."}],"refs":["kotlin.maps","kotlin.sealed","kotlin.classes","oop.encapsulation"]},{"id":"tests","title":"Testing it","tagline":"Small checks that tell you, after every change, that the program still works.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a check as a name plus a true or false answer","Check parsing, totals, budget rules and saving","Use a failing check to find a bug fast"],"remember":"A test compares what the code gives with a value you wrote by hand; run them all after every change.","terms":[{"term":"test","def":"A small piece of code that checks one thing the program must do and reports PASS or FAIL."},{"term":"expected value","def":"What the result should be, written by hand in the test."},{"term":"regression","def":"A bug in something that used to work. Running the tests after each change catches it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a test write <code>parseExpense(\"800 rent\") == Expense(800, Category.RENT, \"\")</code>?","options":["Expense is a data class, so <code>==</code> compares the values","Kotlin compares any two objects by their values","Because the note is empty"],"a":0,"why":"A data class gets <code>equals</code>, so two expenses with the same values are equal."},{"q":"Why does the saving check use <code>MemoryStorage</code>?","options":["It keeps the <code>Storage</code> promise without a file, so the check runs anywhere, in Run it too","Files can’t be tested","It is faster to type"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker only needs a <code>Storage</code>. Any class that keeps the promise can stand in."},{"q":"When should you run the tests?","options":["After every change","Once, when the project is done","Only when something looks broken"],"a":0,"why":"Then a failing check points at the change you just made."}],"refs":["kotlin.functions","kotlin.data-classes","kotlin.lambdas","solid.dip"]},{"id":"recap","title":"Putting it together","tagline":"The whole program in one file, one run from start to finish, and what to build next.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say what each part of the program does","Follow one run from typed lines to the saved file","Pick a next feature and plan it as one small step"],"remember":"Each part does one job: model, parse, report, store, rules, tests. runOnce wires them into one run.","terms":[{"term":"feature","def":"Something new the program can do, like dates or cents. Add each one as its own small step."},{"term":"end to end","def":"Following one run through every part, from the typed lines to the saved file."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which part decides that FUN is over its limit?","options":["<code>Budget</code>","<code>printReport</code>","<code>parseExpense</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Budget.status</code> holds the rule. <code>printReport</code> only prints what it answers."},{"q":"Later you want to keep expenses in a database. What has to change?","options":["Add a new class that implements <code>Storage</code>; the tracker stays the same","Rewrite <code>Tracker</code>","Change <code>Expense</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker depends only on the <code>Storage</code> promise, so a new kind of storage plugs in."},{"q":"You want to add dates. What is a good first move?","options":["Write it as a requirement and add a check for it to <code>runTests</code>","Rewrite the whole program at once","Delete the tests, since they will fail"],"a":0,"why":"A small, checkable step, the same way the whole project was built."}],"refs":["capkotlin.plan","solid.srp","oop.cohesion-coupling","git.commits"]}]},{"id":"capapp","title":"News reader","description":"Build an offline-first news reader, from an empty project to a working app.","requires":["compose","flow","arch"],"kind":"project","builds":"A Compose app that lists headlines, opens articles, refreshes from an API with pull-to-refresh, and keeps working offline from a Room cache, all wired with Hilt.","file":"tracks/cap-app.js","lessons":[{"id":"setup","title":"Project setup and version catalog","tagline":"One catalog, one app module, an empty screen that runs.","level":"beginner","remember":"Every version lives in libs.versions.toml. The Compose BOM picks the Compose versions.","terms":[{"term":"Compose BOM","def":"A Bill of Materials artifact, androidx.compose:compose-bom, that sets matching versions for every Compose library you add without a version."},{"term":"Built-in Kotlin","def":"AGP 9 behaviour where the Android Gradle plugin compiles Kotlin sources itself, so modules no longer apply org.jetbrains.kotlin.android."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>compose-material3</code> have no version in the catalog?","options":["The BOM, added with <code>platform(...)</code>, supplies it","Gradle picks the newest release on its own","Material 3 is part of the Android SDK"],"a":0,"why":"The BOM is a list of matching versions. Libraries from it can leave their version out."},{"q":"Which configuration adds Room’s compiler in this project?","options":["<code>implementation</code>","<code>ksp</code>","<code>kapt</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The compiler is a code generator, so it runs in KSP. It doesn’t ship inside the app."},{"q":"With AGP 9, what happens to the <code>org.jetbrains.kotlin.android</code> plugin?","options":["You still need it for Kotlin files","You remove it: AGP compiles Kotlin itself","It is renamed to <code>kotlin-compose</code>"],"a":1,"why":"AGP 9 turns on built-in Kotlin by default. Compiler plugins like Compose and serialization are still applied on their own."}],"refs":["build.catalogs","build.gradle","arch.layers"]},{"id":"screens","title":"List and detail screens with typed routes","tagline":"Two screens, two routes, sample data for now.","level":"beginner","remember":"Screens take data and lambdas. Routes carry ids, not objects.","quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>ArticleDetail</code> carry an id instead of the <code>Article</code>?","options":["Routes are saved with the back stack, so keep them small and look the article up by id","Navigation can only pass Strings","Data classes can’t be routes"],"a":0,"why":"The route is serialized into saved state. An id is small and always fresh. The detail screen loads the article itself."},{"q":"What does <code>key = { it.id }</code> give the list?","options":["Rows keep their identity and state when items move or new ones arrive","Faster JSON parsing","Sorting by id"],"a":0,"why":"Without keys, rows are matched by position. A new article at the top would shift every row’s state."},{"q":"<code>id</code> is a String. What happens with <code>navigate(ArticleDetail(id = 42))</code>?","options":["It compiles and converts to \"42\"","A compile error: argument type mismatch","A crash when you tap"],"a":1,"why":"Typed routes are plain Kotlin constructors, so the compiler checks every argument."}],"refs":["compose.lazy-keys","compose.hoisting","compose2.navigation"]},{"id":"viewmodel","title":"ViewModel and UiState with StateFlow","tagline":"The screen observes one state object that survives rotation.","level":"intermediate","remember":"One StateFlow<UiState> out of the ViewModel. The screen only collects and draws.","quiz":[{"q":"After a rotation, what does the list screen get first?","options":["The current <code>NewsUiState</code>, straight from the StateFlow","An empty state while the repository reloads","Nothing until the next emission"],"a":0,"why":"The same ViewModel comes back, and a StateFlow always holds its latest value."},{"q":"Why <code>WhileSubscribed(5_000)</code> and not <code>WhileSubscribed()</code>?","options":["A rotation unsubscribes for a moment, and the 5 seconds keep the upstream from restarting","It refreshes every 5 seconds","It is a network timeout"],"a":0,"why":"With no delay, the upstream stops the moment the old Activity stops collecting and restarts for the new one."},{"q":"Step 5 replaces <code>FakeNewsRepository</code>. What has to change in <code>uiState</code>?","options":["Nothing: it only knows the <code>NewsRepository</code> interface","Its type","Every operator"],"a":0,"why":"Depending on the interface lets you swap the data source without touching the ViewModel’s state pipeline."}],"refs":["android.viewmodel","arch.udf","flow.hot","flow.lifecycle"]},{"id":"network","title":"Retrofit and kotlinx.serialization","tagline":"Real headlines from the API, and what happens offline.","level":"intermediate","remember":"DTOs mirror the JSON. Map them to your own model at the edge.","terms":[{"term":"ignoreUnknownKeys","def":"A kotlinx.serialization Json setting that skips JSON keys the class doesn’t declare instead of throwing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server adds a new <code>author</code> key. What happens with <code>ignoreUnknownKeys = true</code>?","options":["The key is skipped and decoding works","Decoding throws a SerializationException","Retrofit retries the request"],"a":0,"why":"That is the point of the setting. Without it, the unknown key is an error."},{"q":"Why map <code>ArticleDto</code> to <code>Article</code>?","options":["So the rest of the app doesn’t depend on the server’s JSON shape","Retrofit requires it","To make requests faster"],"a":0,"why":"If the API renames a field, only the DTO and the mapper change."},{"q":"Offline, what does <code>OnlineNewsRepository</code> show at launch?","options":["An empty list: nothing from earlier is kept","Yesterday’s articles","A crash"],"a":0,"why":"The IOException is caught and turned into an empty list. There is no local copy yet."}],"refs":["data.net","flow.cold","coroutines.dispatchers"]},{"id":"cache","title":"Room cache as the single source of truth","tagline":"The screen reads Room. The network only writes to it.","level":"advanced","remember":"UI ← Room ← network. Writes go into the database, reads come out of it.","terms":[{"term":"Invalidation tracker","def":"The part of Room that records which tables a write touched and re-runs the observable queries that read them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>refresh()</code> just upserted 2 new rows. How do they reach the screen?","options":["Room re-runs <code>observeAll()</code> and the Flow emits the new list","<code>refresh()</code> returns them to the ViewModel","The screen polls the database"],"a":0,"why":"The invalidation tracker sees the write to <code>articles</code> and re-runs every Flow query on that table."},{"q":"Offline, the user reopens the app. What do they see?","options":["The articles saved by the last successful refresh","An empty list","A crash"],"a":0,"why":"Room is on disk. The query emits the saved rows, and the failed refresh only leaves them as they are."},{"q":"What did <code>NewsViewModel</code> need for the cache?","options":["Only an <code>init</code> block that calls <code>refresh()</code>","A new <code>uiState</code> pipeline","A reference to the DAO"],"a":0,"why":"It still reads <code>repository.observeArticles()</code>. Only the repository behind the interface changed."}],"refs":["data.room","arch.ssot","data.caching","flow.operators"]},{"id":"hilt","title":"Wire it together with Hilt","tagline":"One graph builds the API, the database and every ViewModel.","level":"advanced","remember":"Singletons for the API and the database, @Binds for the repository, hiltViewModel() for each screen.","terms":[{"term":"hiltViewModel()","def":"A Compose function from androidx.hilt that gets or creates a @HiltViewModel scoped to the current navigation entry or activity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is <code>NewsDatabase</code> a <code>@Singleton</code>?","options":["One instance means one invalidation tracker, so every Flow sees every write","Room requires the annotation to compile","It makes queries faster"],"a":0,"why":"Several instances on the same file don’t notify each other’s observers by default, and each one holds its own connections."},{"q":"Where does <code>ArticleViewModel</code> get the article id?","options":["From <code>SavedStateHandle</code>, with <code>toRoute&lt;ArticleDetail&gt;()</code>","From the list ViewModel","From a global variable"],"a":0,"why":"The navigation entry’s arguments are in the SavedStateHandle Hilt passes in. They also survive process death."},{"q":"You press back on the article. What happens to <code>ArticleViewModel</code>?","options":["Its entry leaves the back stack, so it is cleared","It lives until the app closes","Hilt keeps it as a singleton"],"a":0,"why":"<code>hiltViewModel()</code> scopes it to the navigation entry. Popping the entry clears its ViewModelStore."}],"refs":["arch.hilt","android.savedstate","compose2.navigation"]},{"id":"states","title":"Loading, error, empty and pull-to-refresh","tagline":"Every state the screen can be in, and a gesture to refresh.","level":"advanced","remember":"Saved articles beat error screens. Show the error over the content, not instead of it.","terms":[{"term":"PullToRefreshBox","def":"A Material 3 container that shows a refresh indicator when the user pulls its scrollable content down, and calls onRefresh."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Offline with 20 saved articles, the user pulls to refresh. What do they see?","options":["The list stays, and a snackbar says there is no connection","A full-screen error with Retry","An empty screen"],"a":0,"why":"Saved news is still useful. The error goes on top of it."},{"q":"Why is <code>isRefreshing.value = false</code> in <code>finally</code>?","options":["So the indicator stops even when refresh throws something unexpected or the coroutine is cancelled","finally runs faster","Kotlin requires it after try"],"a":0,"why":"<code>finally</code> runs on every exit path. Anywhere else, one uncaught exception leaves the spinner on forever."},{"q":"First launch, offline, nothing saved. Which branch draws?","options":["The loading spinner","The full-screen error with Retry","The empty state"],"a":1,"why":"Articles are empty, the refresh finished, and <code>errorMessage</code> is set, so the error branch matches before the empty one."}],"refs":["arch.ui-state-patterns","flow.operators","compose.effects"]},{"id":"recap","title":"You built it: the app end to end","tagline":"Every layer working together, online and off.","level":"advanced","remember":"Pull → ViewModel → repository → API → Room → Flow → UiState → screen.","quiz":[{"q":"The process was killed. The user reopens the app offline. Which layer brings the articles back?","options":["Room: the last refresh wrote them to disk","The ViewModel","SavedStateHandle"],"a":0,"why":"ViewModels die with the process. The database file doesn’t."},{"q":"During a rotation, which objects are new?","options":["The Activity and its composition. ViewModels and singletons stay.","Everything, including the database","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"ViewModels survive configuration changes, and Hilt singletons live as long as the process."},{"q":"A WorkManager sync job should call which function?","options":["<code>NewsRepository.refresh()</code>","<code>NewsViewModel.refresh()</code>","<code>ArticleListScreen</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Workers run without a screen or a ViewModel. The repository writes to Room, and any open screen updates on its own."}],"refs":["arch.layers","arch.ssot","android.viewmodel"]}]},{"id":"capquality","title":"Production Quality Pass","description":"Take the offline-first News Reader from working to production quality, one measured fix at a time.","requires":["capapp","testing","perf"],"kind":"project","builds":"The News Reader with ViewModel, Flow and UI tests, smooth scrolling, no leaks, an accessibility pass, a tablet layout and a measured faster cold start.","file":"tracks/cap-quality.js","lessons":[{"id":"unit-tests","title":"Unit-test the ViewModel with fakes","tagline":"A safety net before we change anything.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Fake behind the interface. Create the ViewModel after the Main rule, and collect uiState before you read it.","terms":[{"term":"Regression test","def":"A test that pins down behavior that already works, so a later change cannot break it without a failing build."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the test pass a <code>FakeNewsRepository</code> to <code>NewsViewModel</code>?","options":["Hilt swaps it in automatically","The ViewModel depends on the NewsRepository interface, not on OfflineFirstNewsRepository","Kotlin allows any class as a constructor argument"],"a":1,"why":"Depending on the interface is what lets the app bind the real implementation with <code>@Binds</code> while tests hand in a fake."},{"q":"A test reads <code>vm.uiState.value</code> and always gets the initial state. <code>uiState</code> uses <code>stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(5_000), …)</code>. What is missing?","options":["A collector, for example in <code>backgroundScope</code>","A call to <code>advanceUntilIdle()</code> only","A <code>Thread.sleep</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With <code>WhileSubscribed</code> the upstream combine only runs while there is a subscriber. Start one before you read <code>value</code>."},{"q":"In <code>refreshIsIgnoredWhileOneIsRunning</code>, how long does the test take in real time?","options":["About one second","Milliseconds: runTest skips the 1 s delay on its virtual clock","It never finishes"],"a":1,"why":"<code>delay</code> inside <code>runTest</code> advances virtual time, so <code>advanceUntilIdle()</code> runs the slow refresh instantly."}],"refs":["capapp.states","testing.fakes","testing.coroutines"]},{"id":"turbine","title":"Test the state stream with Turbine","tagline":"Check every state the screen goes through, in order.","level":"intermediate","remember":"test { } queues every state. awaitItem() takes them in order; leftovers fail the test.","terms":[{"term":"StateFlow conflation","def":"StateFlow keeps only its latest value and ignores a new value equal to the current one, so a slow collector can miss states in between."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>vm.uiState.test { }</code>, what does the first <code>awaitItem()</code> return?","options":["Nothing until something changes","The current value of the StateFlow","The last value the flow will ever emit"],"a":1,"why":"A StateFlow always hands a new collector its current value first, here <code>NewsUiState()</code> with <code>isLoading = true</code>."},{"q":"The fake emits the same list twice in a row. How many items does Turbine see?","options":["Two","One","Zero"],"a":1,"why":"StateFlow ignores a value equal to its current one, so only one emission happens."},{"q":"Why does the fake’s <code>refresh()</code> call <code>delay(refreshDelay)</code>?","options":["To slow the test down in real time","So “refreshing” is a separate state that lasts some virtual time and is not conflated away","Turbine requires every function to suspend"],"a":1,"why":"Without a suspension point, refreshing can flip true and back before the collector sees it. <code>runTest</code> skips the delay instantly."}],"refs":["testing.flows","flow.hot"]},{"id":"ui-tests","title":"Compose UI tests on the semantics tree","tagline":"Find nodes by meaning, act, assert.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Pass state in, find by text or tag, act, assert. Scroll lazy lists before you assert.","terms":[{"term":"useUnmergedTree","def":"A finder option that searches the semantics tree before merging, so each Text inside a merged row is its own node."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the test call <code>ArticleListScreen(state, …)</code> without a ViewModel?","options":["Compose tests mock ViewModels automatically","ArticleListScreen is stateless: it takes NewsUiState and lambdas","createComposeRule starts Hilt"],"a":1,"why":"State hoisting keeps the screen a plain function of its inputs, which is exactly what a UI test wants."},{"q":"A row is a <code>Card(onClick = …)</code> holding two Texts. What does <code>onNodeWithText(\"City council approves new bike lanes\")</code> find by default?","options":["The Text node only, which has no click action","The merged card node, which has the click action","Nothing, merged nodes can’t be found by text"],"a":1,"why":"A clickable card merges its children’s semantics. Finders use the merged tree by default, so the card node carries both texts and the click."},{"q":"Where do these tests live and how do they run?","options":["src/test, on the JVM","src/androidTest, on a device or emulator","In the release APK"],"a":1,"why":"Compose UI tests need the Android framework, so they are instrumented tests run with connectedAndroidTest."}],"refs":["testing.compose-ui","compose.hoisting","a11y.semantics"]},{"id":"recomposition","title":"Fix a recomposition hotspot","tagline":"One bookmark should redraw one row.","level":"advanced","remember":"Give each row only what it shows. derivedStateOf turns a per-frame value into a rare change.","quiz":[{"q":"Each <code>ArticleRow</code> takes the whole <code>bookmarkedIds: Set&lt;String&gt;</code>. Why do all rows recompose when one bookmark changes?","options":["The new set is a different object, and an unstable Set is compared by instance, so every row’s input looks changed","Bookmarks are written on the main thread","LazyColumn redraws everything on any change"],"a":0,"why":"Every row receives the same new set. Passing a Boolean means only the row whose value actually changed has a different input."},{"q":"With strong skipping on, how does Compose compare an unstable parameter?","options":["With equals()","By instance (===)","It never skips"],"a":1,"why":"Strong skipping lets composables with unstable parameters skip, but only when the same instance is passed again."},{"q":"Why does reading <code>listState.layoutInfo</code> in composition hurt while scrolling?","options":["It throws while scrolling","It is a new value on every scroll frame, so the reader recomposes every frame","It disables keys"],"a":1,"why":"Read it inside <code>derivedStateOf</code> (or use <code>firstVisibleItemIndex</code> there) so composition only hears about the change you care about."}],"refs":["compose2.stability","compose2.derived","perf.compose-perf","perf.frame"]},{"id":"leak","title":"Find and fix a memory leak","tagline":"Rotate five times, keep one Activity.","level":"advanced","remember":"Singletons outlive screens. Never hand them a UI object; expose a Flow and let the collector come and go.","terms":[{"term":"Retained object","def":"An object LeakCanary expected to be garbage collected, such as a destroyed Activity, that is still reachable after a GC."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What kept the old <code>MainActivity</code> alive?","options":["The ViewModel","A lambda in the singleton NetworkMonitor’s listener list that captured the Activity context","Room’s database"],"a":1,"why":"The singleton lives as long as the app. Anything it references, and everything those reference, cannot be collected."},{"q":"Why expose network status as a Flow from the data layer?","options":["Flows are faster than callbacks","Registration follows collection: awaitClose unregisters when the last collector stops","Hilt cannot inject listeners"],"a":1,"why":"<code>callbackFlow</code> ties the callback’s lifetime to the collector, so there is nothing to forget to remove."},{"q":"After rotation, does the new screen register a second network callback?","options":["Yes, every rotation adds one","No: the ViewModel survives rotation and WhileSubscribed(5_000) keeps the single upstream running","Only on tablets"],"a":1,"why":"The new screen collects the same StateFlow from the same ViewModel within 5 s, so the upstream never restarts."}],"refs":["perf.leaks","flow.callbackflow","flow.lifecycle"]},{"id":"a11y-pass","title":"Accessibility pass","tagline":"Labels, targets, announcements and 200% text.","level":"advanced","remember":"Label it, make it 48 dp, let text grow, give gestures a button, announce changes. Then lock it in with a test.","terms":[{"term":"enableAccessibilityChecks","def":"Turns on Accessibility Test Framework checks in a Compose UI test, so actions fail on problems such as missing labels or small touch targets."},{"term":"DeviceConfigurationOverride","def":"A Compose test API that overrides font scale, size, locale or other configuration for the content inside it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"At 200% font size, headlines are cut off. Which change fixes it?","options":["Use dp instead of sp for the title","Replace <code>height(72.dp)</code> with <code>heightIn(min = 72.dp)</code>","Set <code>maxLines = 1</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Text must be free to grow. A minimum height keeps the layout tidy at normal size without capping it."},{"q":"Why add a Refresh button when the list already has pull-to-refresh?","options":["Pull gestures are hard or impossible for TalkBack and Switch Access users; a button is reachable by everyone","PullToRefreshBox is deprecated","Buttons refresh faster"],"a":0,"why":"Any action that only exists as a gesture needs an accessible alternative, such as a button or a custom accessibility action."},{"q":"Why add <code>enableAccessibilityChecks()</code> to UI tests?","options":["It fixes the issues for you","Regressions such as a new unlabeled button fail the build instead of reaching users","It turns on TalkBack on the emulator"],"a":1,"why":"The checks run on test actions and fail on Accessibility Test Framework issues, so the pass stays done."}],"refs":["a11y.semantics","a11y.targets","a11y.text-scale"]},{"id":"adaptive","title":"Adaptive list-detail for tablets","tagline":"One pane on phones, two on wide screens.","level":"advanced","remember":"Decide panes by window width class, never by device type or orientation. The scaffold does it for you.","quiz":[{"q":"By default, at which window width class does the list-detail scaffold show two panes side by side?","options":["Compact (under 600 dp)","Medium (600 to 840 dp)","Expanded (840 dp and up)"],"a":2,"why":"The default directive allows two horizontal panes from expanded width. Below that it shows one pane at a time."},{"q":"Why does <code>ArticleViewModel</code> switch to an assisted factory?","options":["Assisted injection is faster","In a pane there is no navigation route, so the id can’t come from SavedStateHandle; it is passed in when the ViewModel is created","Hilt no longer supports SavedStateHandle"],"a":1,"why":"The detail pane knows the id from <code>contentKey</code> and hands it to the factory. Keying by id gives each article its own ViewModel."},{"q":"What happens to the selected article when a foldable unfolds from one pane to two?","options":["It is lost, the user starts over","The navigator keeps it as contentKey, so the detail pane shows it next to the list","The app restarts"],"a":1,"why":"The navigator state survives configuration changes. The same destination is shown with a different pane layout."}],"refs":["a11y.window-size","a11y.foldables","capapp.screens"]},{"id":"startup","title":"Baseline Profile and startup numbers","tagline":"Measure the cold start, then make it faster.","level":"advanced","remember":"Measure the release build cold, report fully drawn at real content, ship a Baseline Profile, measure again.","terms":[{"term":"TTFD","def":"Time to full display: from launch until the app reports it is fully drawn, for example when the first real content is on screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a Baseline Profile change on a fresh install?","options":["It removes unused code","ART compiles the listed startup methods ahead of time, so they don’t start in the interpreter","It makes network calls faster"],"a":1,"why":"Without a profile, code starts interpreted and is JIT-compiled as it runs. The profile lets install-time compilation cover the hot path."},{"q":"Why benchmark with <code>StartupMode.COLD</code> on a release build?","options":["Debug builds are faster","Cold starts include process creation and are what new users feel; debug builds are not optimized and give misleading numbers","Warm starts can’t be measured"],"a":1,"why":"Release builds run R8 and are what users install. Cold start is the slowest and most visible case."},{"q":"Why add <code>testTagsAsResourceId = true</code> at the root?","options":["It speeds up composition","UiAutomator can find Compose nodes by their test tags as resource ids","It is required by Hilt"],"a":1,"why":"Macrobenchmark and the profile generator drive the app with UiAutomator, which sees resource ids, not Compose test tags, unless you expose them."}],"refs":["testing.baseline","perf.startup","build.variants"]},{"id":"recap","title":"You shipped a production-quality app","tagline":"Everything, end to end, on one screen.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Measure, change one thing, measure again, and leave a test behind.","quiz":[{"q":"Which order did every step in this project follow?","options":["Rewrite, then hope","Measure, change one thing, measure again, keep a test","Add libraries until numbers improve"],"a":1,"why":"Each step showed a number before and after, and most left a test or benchmark behind to keep the gain."},{"q":"Which of these runs on the JVM without a device?","options":["The Turbine tests of NewsViewModel","The accessibility checks at 200% font","The startup benchmark"],"a":0,"why":"ViewModel and Flow tests with fakes are local unit tests. UI tests and Macrobenchmark need a device."}],"refs":["capapp.recap","testing.pyramid","perf.r8"]}]},{"id":"capship","title":"Ship the news reader","description":"Add breaking-news push to the offline-first news reader, harden it, and ship it to Play in stages.","requires":["capapp","push","background","build"],"kind":"project","builds":"A signed, staged Play release of the news reader where a breaking story travels from your server through FCM to a notification that opens the right article, already synced into Room.","file":"tracks/cap-ship.js","lessons":[{"id":"channels","title":"Channels and the permission ask","tagline":"Make room for alerts on the device before the first push arrives.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Create channels at startup, ask for POST_NOTIFICATIONS after a tap, and send people to Settings after the second no.","quiz":[{"q":"The app targets API 36 and runs on Android 14. Nobody has asked for the permission yet. What does <code>notify()</code> on the breaking channel do?","options":["Shows the alert","Posts nothing and throws nothing","Throws a SecurityException"],"a":1,"why":"On Android 13+ a new install starts without <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>. The notification is dropped silently, so check the permission before you rely on it."},{"q":"The user sets the breaking channel to Silent. Your next release creates it again with <code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code>. What happens?","options":["It goes back to high","It stays silent: the user owns it now","The app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Creating an existing channel can update its name and description, and can only lower an importance the user never touched. It can’t raise it or undo the user’s choice."},{"q":"The user tapped Don’t allow twice. What does <code>launcher.launch(POST_NOTIFICATIONS)</code> do now?","options":["Shows the dialog again","Returns false right away without a dialog","Opens Settings"],"a":1,"why":"Since Android 11 two denials stop the dialog. Only the app’s notification settings can turn it on, so link there."}],"refs":["push.channels","android.permissions"]},{"id":"tokens","title":"Tokens and topics","tagline":"Register each install with your server and let readers pick categories.","level":"intermediate","remember":"onNewToken enqueues the upload, the upload re-applies the topics, and the server keys rows by token.","terms":[{"term":"FCM topic","def":"A named group on FCM that app installs subscribe to, so one send from your server reaches every subscriber."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>onNewToken</code> enqueue a worker instead of calling the API itself?","options":["The callback runs on the main thread","The upload can fail offline; the worker waits for a network, retries with backoff and survives the process dying","FCM forbids network calls in the service"],"a":1,"why":"FirebaseMessagingService callbacks run on a background thread, but the process can die at any time. WorkManager makes the upload reliable."},{"q":"A reader turns Tech off. What should the app call?","options":["<code>unsubscribeFromTopic(\"news-tech\")</code>","<code>deleteToken()</code>","Nothing; the server filters"],"a":0,"why":"Unsubscribing removes this token from the topic. Deleting the token would stop every push to this install."},{"q":"Which message fits a topic best?","options":["Breaking news for everyone who follows Sports","A reply to the reader’s own comment","A sign-in code"],"a":0,"why":"Topics are for public messages to many installs. Personal or private messages go to that user’s tokens."}],"refs":["push.fcm","background.workmanager","data.datastore","arch.hilt"]},{"id":"messages","title":"Data messages for breaking news","tagline":"Send data, post the notification yourself, and know when your code runs.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Breaking news is a high-priority data message. Your code posts the alert, so it runs in every state except force-stopped.","terms":[{"term":"Collapse key","def":"An FCM option that keeps only the newest undelivered message with the same key for a device, up to four keys per device."},{"term":"Original priority","def":"The priority your server asked for, read with RemoteMessage.getOriginalPriority(); compare it with getPriority() to spot downgraded messages."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app is in the background. The server sends a message with a <code>notification</code> block and <code>article_id</code> in <code>data</code>. Where does <code>article_id</code> end up?","options":["In <code>onMessageReceived()</code>","In the launcher Activity’s intent extras, after the user taps","Nowhere, it is dropped"],"a":1,"why":"The SDK shows the notification itself. Its data rides along in the extras of the Intent that opens the launcher Activity."},{"q":"The reader swiped the app away from Recents. A high-priority data message arrives. What happens?","options":["Nothing until they open the app","FCM starts the process and <code>onMessageReceived()</code> runs","The system shows it without your code"],"a":1,"why":"Swiping away kills the process but is not a force stop. FCM can start the app to deliver. Only a force-stopped app gets nothing."},{"q":"An install keeps getting high-priority data messages that never lead to a notification. What can FCM do?","options":["Nothing changes","Deliver later messages to that install as normal priority","Unsubscribe it from the topic"],"a":1,"why":"FCM looks at recent behavior per install and may downgrade. Compare <code>getPriority()</code> with <code>getOriginalPriority()</code> to see it happen."}],"refs":["push.messages","push.fcm","push.channels"]},{"id":"deeplink","title":"A tap opens the article","tagline":"A deep link into the nav graph, with the feed underneath for Back.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Deep link the route, wrap the Intent in TaskStackBuilder, keep MainActivity standard. The feed ends up under the article.","terms":[{"term":"handleDeepLink","def":"The NavController method that matches an Intent’s URI against the graph’s deep links and builds the back stack for it; NavHost calls it for the launch Intent."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app was not running. The reader taps the alert. What is on the back stack?","options":["Only ArticleDetail, so Back exits","ArticleList, then ArticleDetail","Whatever screen was open last time, then ArticleDetail"],"a":1,"why":"For a deep link in a fresh task, Navigation adds the start destination under the target, so Back walks up to the feed."},{"q":"Two alerts for different articles both use request code 0. Do their taps open the right articles?","options":["No, the second PendingIntent replaces the first","Yes, their Intents have different data URIs, so they are different PendingIntents"],"a":1,"why":"PendingIntents match on action, data, type, component and categories. Different URIs make them distinct. Extras alone would not."},{"q":"Why doesn’t the alert need an intent filter in the manifest?","options":["The Intent names MainActivity explicitly, and NavHost matches the URI against the graph","Notifications skip the manifest","FLAG_IMMUTABLE replaces the filter"],"a":0,"why":"Filters are for implicit Intents, like a link tapped in the browser. An explicit Intent goes straight to the component."}],"refs":["push.deeplinks","compose2.navigation","android.backstack"]},{"id":"sync","title":"Prefetch with WorkManager","tagline":"The push starts an expedited job, so the article is in Room before the tap.","level":"advanced","remember":"Post the alert first, then let an expedited, unique worker pull the story into Room.","terms":[{"term":"Expedited work","def":"WorkManager work marked with setExpedited() that starts as soon as possible within a quota; an expedited job on Android 12+, a foreground service before that."},{"term":"HiltWorkerFactory","def":"The WorkerFactory from androidx.hilt that creates @HiltWorker classes with their dependencies; you set it in WorkManager’s Configuration."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why post the notification before the fetch finishes?","options":["The callback window is short; the fetch can take longer or wait for a network","WorkManager can’t post notifications","Room needs the notification id first"],"a":0,"why":"Rendering fits the window. Network work goes to WorkManager, which survives the service finishing."},{"q":"FCM delivers the same breaking message twice. What stops a second fetch?","options":["<code>enqueueUniqueWork(\"article-&lt;id&gt;\", KEEP, ...)</code>","<code>setExpedited()</code>","The network constraint"],"a":0,"why":"KEEP ignores a new request while one with that name is pending or running."},{"q":"On an Android 11 phone the expedited fetch fails with <code>IllegalStateException</code>. Why?","options":["getForegroundInfo() is not overridden","Expedited work needs Android 12","Network constraints are not allowed"],"a":0,"why":"Before Android 12, WorkManager runs expedited work as a foreground service and asks the worker for its notification. CoroutineWorker’s default throws."}],"refs":["background.workmanager","arch.ssot","arch.hilt","data.caching"]},{"id":"harden","title":"Security hardening","tagline":"Lock down the network and keep every secret on the server.","level":"advanced","remember":"HTTPS only, user CAs only in debug, the send credential only on the server, and every payload checked.","terms":[{"term":"Service account","def":"A Google Cloud identity for servers; its private key lets your backend get OAuth 2.0 tokens to call APIs such as FCM HTTP v1."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is fine to ship in the APK?","options":["The google-services.json API key, restricted to your package and signing certificate","The Firebase service account JSON","The news provider’s API key"],"a":0,"why":"Firebase API keys identify your project. The service account and the provider key grant power, so they stay on the server."},{"q":"A debug build trusts your proxy’s CA. Does the release build?","options":["Yes, the CA is installed on the phone","No, debug-overrides apply only to debuggable builds"],"a":1,"why":"Release builds ignore user-installed CAs unless the base config trusts them, which it shouldn’t."},{"q":"Why check <code>article_id</code> with a regex before building the URI?","options":["Payload text is input: a bad value could point the deep link somewhere unexpected","FCM corrupts long strings","Navigation requires it"],"a":0,"why":"A leaked credential or a buggy server can send anything. Validate the shape before it reaches an Intent."}],"refs":["security.network","security.secrets","security.threats"]},{"id":"release-build","title":"Dev and prod, signed for Play","tagline":"Two Firebase projects, one codebase, and an upload-signed bundle.","level":"advanced","remember":"One Firebase project per environment, one google-services.json per flavor, and the app signing key’s fingerprint wherever Play-installed builds are checked.","terms":[{"term":"SENDER_ID_MISMATCH","def":"The FCM error, HTTP 403, returned when a server sends to a registration token that a different Firebase project issued."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does the Google services plugin find the dev variant’s config?","options":["<code>app/src/dev/google-services.json</code>, or a more specific variant folder","Only <code>app/google-services.json</code>","<code>res/raw/</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The plugin checks variant, flavor and build type folders before <code>app/</code>, so each flavor can carry its own project."},{"q":"Your prod server sends to a token from a dev build. What does FCM answer?","options":["200, delivered","403 SENDER_ID_MISMATCH","404 UNREGISTERED"],"a":1,"why":"The token was issued by news-dev. Only that project’s credentials can send to it."},{"q":"Notification links open the browser for Play installs but your own release build opens the app. Which fingerprint is in <code>assetlinks.json</code>?","options":["The upload key’s","The app signing key’s","The debug key’s"],"a":0,"why":"Play-installed apps are signed with the app signing key. Add its SHA-256 from Play Console, and keep the upload key’s too if you also install builds you signed."}],"refs":["build.variants","build.signing","build.bundles"]},{"id":"ship","title":"Staged rollout and the whole path","tagline":"Roll out in steps, keep old versions working, and watch it all run.","level":"advanced","remember":"Ship in stages, fix forward, and keep every payload readable by the version before.","quiz":[{"q":"During a 20% rollout, version 42 adds a message type <code>\"live\"</code>. What do phones on version 41 do with it?","options":["Crash","Ignore it through the else branch","Show an empty alert"],"a":1,"why":"Version 41 shipped with <code>else -&gt; Unit</code>, so an unknown type is skipped."},{"q":"Why send <code>appVersion</code> when registering a token?","options":["So the server knows which installs can handle a new payload type","FCM needs it to route messages","It picks the Firebase project"],"a":0,"why":"FCM doesn’t know your app versions. Your token table does, so the server can target or hold back new types."},{"q":"A reader never granted <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>. What happens when breaking news is sent?","options":["Nothing reaches the phone","The data message still arrives and the sync runs; only the alert is skipped","<code>notify()</code> crashes the app"],"a":1,"why":"Delivery doesn’t depend on the permission. <code>BreakingNotifier</code> returns early and the worker still fills Room. Over time FCM may treat that install’s high-priority messages as normal."}],"refs":["build.tracks","push.messages","arch.ssot"]}]},{"id":"cloud","title":"Cloud and DevOps basics","description":"The servers, containers, pipelines, secrets and dashboards around your Android app, explained for app developers.","requires":["build"],"file":"tracks/cloud.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-cloud","title":"What \"the cloud\" is","tagline":"Someone else's computers, rented by the hour or by the request, in a place you pick.","level":"beginner","remember":"Pick the highest level that fits: managed or serverless first, VMs only when you need the control. You pay for what is switched on, not for what is used.","terms":[{"term":"Region / zone","def":"A region is a geographic area of data centers. Zones are separate failure areas inside one region."},{"term":"Managed service","def":"Something the provider runs for you (a database, a queue), including backups, patches and failover."},{"term":"Serverless","def":"You deploy code, the platform runs it per request or event, and it can scale to zero when idle."},{"term":"Cold start","def":"The delay while a new instance starts because none was running to take the request."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your API runs on one VM sized for the morning push burst. What happens at 03:00?","options":["It shuts down automatically","It keeps running and you keep paying for it","The provider moves it to a cheaper zone"],"a":1,"why":"A VM is billed while it is on. Nothing scales it down unless you set that up."},{"q":"Which trade-off fits serverless with scale to zero?","options":["No idle cost, but the first request after a quiet period is slower","Fastest possible first request, higher idle cost","Full control over the OS"],"a":0,"why":"With no instance running, a new request has to wait for one to start: a cold start."},{"q":"Why spread instances across zones in a region?","options":["Zones are cheaper","So one infrastructure failure does not take all copies down","Because Android apps need it"],"a":1,"why":"Zones are separate failure areas. Copies in two zones survive one zone failing."},{"q":"In the demo, why did the container service fall short at 09:00 even with autoscaling?","options":["It has a fixed size","Traffic grew faster than new instances could start","Containers cannot scale"],"a":1,"why":"Autoscalers react to load they already see. A sudden burst, like a push to millions of devices, arrives before new instances are ready."}]},{"id":"docker","title":"Containers with Docker","tagline":"Package the app with everything it needs, so it runs the same on your laptop, in CI and in production.","level":"beginner","remember":"Order the Dockerfile from rarely changes to always changes. A changed layer rebuilds itself and everything after it.","terms":[{"term":"Image","def":"An immutable package of layers: base OS, runtime, app and settings."},{"term":"Container","def":"An isolated running process started from an image, with its own thin writable layer."},{"term":"Layer cache","def":"Docker reuses a layer when its instruction and inputs are unchanged; a change rebuilds it and all layers after it."},{"term":"Volume","def":"Docker-managed storage whose contents live outside any one container's lifecycle."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You edit one Kotlin file. In the dependencies-first Dockerfile, which layers rebuild?","options":["All of them","COPY src and every layer after it","Only the FROM line"],"a":1,"why":"The COPY src layer's input changed, so it and all later layers run again. Earlier layers come from cache."},{"q":"The Dockerfile says <code>EXPOSE 8080</code>. You run <code>docker run headlines-api</code>. Can your browser open localhost:8080?","options":["Yes, EXPOSE published it","No, nothing is published without -p or -P","Only on Linux"],"a":1,"why":"EXPOSE documents the port. Publishing needs <code>-p 8080:8080</code> (or <code>-P</code> for all exposed ports)."},{"q":"You remove a Postgres container that had no volume and start a new one. Where is the data?","options":["In the new container","Gone, it lived in the removed container's writable layer","In the image"],"a":1,"why":"Without a volume, writes go to the container's own layer, which is deleted with it."},{"q":"Which is the main reason containers end \"works on my machine\" arguments?","options":["They are faster than VMs","The runtime, libraries and settings travel inside the image, so every environment runs the same thing","Docker fixes bugs automatically"],"a":1,"why":"The image carries the environment. CI, staging and production run the identical image."},{"q":"One image, five containers. How many copies of the image layers are on disk?","options":["Five","One shared set, plus a thin writable layer per container","None until a container writes"],"a":1,"why":"Read-only layers are shared. Each container only adds its own small writable layer."}]},{"id":"pipelines","title":"CI/CD pipelines","tagline":"Every push goes through the same build, test, sign and deploy stages, so releasing is boring.","level":"intermediate","remember":"One pipeline, same artifact, every stage: build, test, sign, deploy to staging, then a human approves production.","terms":[{"term":"Continuous delivery","def":"Every change that passes the pipeline is built, signed and deployed to at least one real environment automatically."},{"term":"Environment","def":"A named deploy target (dev, staging, prod) with its own config, secrets and protection rules."},{"term":"Artifact","def":"The built output that moves through the pipeline: a signed AAB or a container image."},{"term":"Smoke test","def":"A quick check after deploy (does /health answer?) that catches a dead deployment early."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Put the stages in order.","options":["Build → Sign → Test → Deploy","Build → Test → Sign → Deploy staging → Approve → Deploy prod","Deploy staging → Build → Test → Deploy prod"],"a":1,"why":"Test before you sign and ship anything, and let staging prove the deploy before a human approves production."},{"q":"The unit test stage fails. What happens to the deploy stages?","options":["They run anyway","They are skipped","Only production is skipped"],"a":1,"why":"A failed stage stops the stages that depend on it. That is the point of the pipeline."},{"q":"The workflow reads <code>${{ secrets.KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}</code>, but nobody created that secret. What does the step get?","options":["An error before the job starts","An empty string","The value from your laptop"],"a":1,"why":"GitHub evaluates an unset secret to an empty string, so the failure shows up later, when decoding or signing."},{"q":"Why must the first release of a new app be uploaded by hand in Play Console?","options":["Google wants a human to read the code","The Play Developer API cannot register a new app","CI cannot sign apps"],"a":1,"why":"Gradle Play Publisher's README says registering the app cannot be done through the API. After that, CI can take over."},{"q":"Staging passed with image <code>:a1b2c3</code>. Which trade-off fits \"rebuild from main for production\"?","options":["Safer, because it is fresh","Riskier: the new build may differ from what you tested","No difference"],"a":1,"why":"A rebuild can pick up different dependencies or base images. Promote the tested artifact."}]},{"id":"secrets","title":"Configuration and secrets","tagline":"Config lives in the environment, secrets live in a vault, and neither lives in git.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Config from the environment, secrets from a secret manager, nothing secret in git. A leaked secret gets rotated, not just deleted.","terms":[{"term":"Environment variable","def":"A named value the OS passes to a process at start. The usual way to hand config to a container."},{"term":"Secret manager","def":"A service that stores secrets with access control, versions and audit, and hands them to apps at run time."},{"term":"Rotation","def":"Replacing a secret with a new value and revoking the old one."},{"term":"Push protection","def":"GitHub secret scanning that blocks a push when it detects known credential formats."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Play service account JSON was pushed to a private repo last week. What do you do first?","options":["Delete the file in a new commit","Revoke the key and create a new one","Make the repo private again"],"a":1,"why":"The key is in history and in clones. Revoking it is the only thing that makes the leak harmless."},{"q":"Which of these is fine to ship inside the app?","options":["A Firebase API key restricted to Firebase APIs","A database password","A Play service account private key"],"a":0,"why":"Firebase says its keys only identify the project; Security Rules and App Check control access. The others grant real power."},{"q":"Which line is the problem? (1) <code>val url = System.getenv(\"API_BASE\")</code> (2) <code>ARG DB_PASSWORD</code> in a Dockerfile (3) <code>env: KEY: ${{ secrets.KEY }}</code>","options":["Line 1","Line 2","Line 3"],"a":1,"why":"Docker build args persist in the image. Pass secrets at run time or with a build secret mount."},{"q":"Why is Play App Signing helpful if the CI keystore leaks?","options":["It encrypts the APK","The leaked key is only the upload key, and Play can reset it","It deletes leaked keys automatically"],"a":1,"why":"Google holds the app signing key. You can request an upload key reset and keep updating the same app."}]},{"id":"baas","title":"Backend-as-a-service","tagline":"Firebase, Supabase or your own server: pick by your data, your team and how easily you can leave.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Start with a BaaS if it fits your data, but keep it behind your own interface. The cheapest migration is the one you planned for.","terms":[{"term":"BaaS","def":"Backend-as-a-service: hosted auth, database, storage and functions used through an SDK."},{"term":"Vendor lock-in","def":"How hard and costly it is to move your data and code to another provider."},{"term":"Row Level Security","def":"Postgres policies that decide, per row, which user can read or change it."},{"term":"Security Rules","def":"Firebase's rules language that decides who can read or write Firestore, Realtime Database and Storage data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your news app needs joins between articles, authors and tags, and the data must be on servers your company runs. Which trade-off fits?","options":["Firebase: fastest to start","Supabase self-hosted or your own server: SQL and you run it","Any of them, it does not matter"],"a":1,"why":"Relational data favors Postgres, and \"we run it\" rules out Firebase, which runs only on Google's infrastructure."},{"q":"You need a Cloud Function that runs when a document is written. Which Firebase plan?","options":["Spark","Blaze","Either"],"a":1,"why":"Firebase's pricing page lists Cloud Functions as Blaze only."},{"q":"Does choosing Supabase stop you from sending FCM push notifications?","options":["Yes, FCM needs Firebase as the database","No, any backend can call FCM","Only on Android 13+"],"a":1,"why":"FCM is a separate service. Your Supabase function or own server can send messages through its API."},{"q":"What does wrapping Firestore in a <code>HeadlineSource</code> interface buy you?","options":["Faster queries","One place to change if you switch backends, and an easy fake for tests","Offline support"],"a":1,"why":"The rest of the app depends on your interface, not the vendor SDK."}]},{"id":"observability","title":"Observability","tagline":"Logs, metrics and traces, joined by one request id, so a crash on a phone points to the server line that caused it.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Logs say what happened, metrics say how much, traces say where. One request id ties a phone's crash to the server's logs.","terms":[{"term":"Metric","def":"A number measured over time, such as error rate or p95 latency."},{"term":"Trace / span","def":"A trace is one request's path through the system; each span is one timed step in it."},{"term":"Correlation id","def":"An id sent with a request and logged by every service, so you can find all lines for one call."},{"term":"p95 latency","def":"The time under which 95% of requests finish. It shows the slow tail that averages hide."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Users say \"the app is slow sometimes.\" Which signal shows it best at a glance?","options":["A log search for ERROR","A p95 latency metric on a dashboard","One trace picked at random"],"a":1,"why":"A latency metric over time shows when and how much. Then traces explain the slow cases."},{"q":"You found the failing log line by request id. What do you open next to see which service spent the time?","options":["The trace for that request","The CPU metric of the database","The Play Console"],"a":0,"why":"The trace shows each span's duration, so the slow hop stands out."},{"q":"Which alert rule fits best?","options":["CPU above 50% for 10 seconds","Error rate above 2% for 5 minutes","Any ERROR log line"],"a":1,"why":"It tracks what users feel and ignores short blips, so it fires rarely and means something."},{"q":"What does the trace id in <code>traceparent</code> let services do?","options":["Encrypt the request","Join their spans into one trace","Skip authentication"],"a":1,"why":"Each service that receives the header continues the same trace instead of starting a new one."}]},{"id":"scaling","title":"Scaling basics","tagline":"More traffic: a bigger machine, or more machines behind a load balancer, and a CDN for what does not change.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Scale out, not up: stateless instances behind a load balancer, an autoscaler to add and remove them, and a CDN so most requests never reach them.","terms":[{"term":"Load balancer","def":"Spreads incoming requests across instances and routes around unhealthy ones."},{"term":"Stateless server","def":"An instance that keeps no user data between requests, so any instance can serve any request."},{"term":"Autoscaling","def":"Adding and removing instances automatically based on load, within a min and max."},{"term":"CDN","def":"A network of edge caches near users; a cache hit is answered without reaching your origin servers."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to sessions kept in instance memory when the autoscaler removes that instance?","options":["They move to another instance","They are lost and those users are logged out","The load balancer saves them"],"a":1,"why":"Memory dies with the instance. That is why horizontally scaled servers keep state in shared storage."},{"q":"Which trade-off fits vertical scaling?","options":["Simple, but there is a size ceiling and still one machine to fail","Unlimited, but requires stateless code","Free, but slow"],"a":0,"why":"A bigger machine needs no code changes, but it has a maximum size and stays a single point of failure."},{"q":"A push goes out and 60% of the requests are for article images. What cuts server load the most?","options":["A bigger database","A CDN in front of the images","More logging"],"a":1,"why":"Cache hits at the edge never reach your servers, so most of the spike disappears."},{"q":"Why can a brand-new instance not help with the first seconds of a spike?","options":["It has to start before it can take requests","Load balancers ignore new instances","It needs a new Docker image"],"a":0,"why":"Start time is the gap between \"autoscaler decided\" and \"instance serving\". Minimum instances or a CDN cover that gap."},{"q":"Which response must never be <code>public</code> in Cache-Control?","options":["/api/headlines","/api/me/saved","/images/812.webp"],"a":1,"why":"It is per-user. A shared cache would serve one user's data to others."}]}]},{"id":"concurrency","title":"Concurrency fundamentals","description":"Make a program do two jobs at once with threads, see how shared values go wrong, and fix it with locks, @Volatile and safer tools.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/concurrency.js","lessons":[{"id":"concurrency-parallelism","title":"Concurrency and parallelism","tagline":"Several jobs in progress at once: taking turns, or truly side by side.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start a second thread with <code>thread { }</code> and wait for it with <code>join()</code>","Tell concurrency (taking turns) from parallelism (at the same instant)","Expect the order of two threads to change from run to run"],"remember":"thread { } starts a new thread and join() waits for it. Taking turns is concurrency; running at the same instant on several cores is parallelism. The order is never up to you.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One worker inside a program that runs its own instructions in order. <code>thread { }</code> starts a new one."},{"term":"interleaving","def":"The way the steps of several threads get mixed together when they take turns."},{"term":"concurrency","def":"Several jobs in progress during the same time, even if only one runs at any instant."},{"term":"parallelism","def":"Several jobs running at the very same instant, on different cores."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone with one core runs two threads, and both make progress during the same second. What is this?","options":["Concurrency without parallelism","Parallelism","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Both jobs are in progress at once (concurrency), but one core runs only one of them at any instant, so there is no parallelism."},{"q":"What does <code>worker.join()</code> do?","options":["The current thread waits until <code>worker</code> has finished","It starts <code>worker</code>","It joins two threads into one"],"a":0,"why":"<code>thread { }</code> already started it. <code>join()</code> only waits."},{"q":"You run a program with two threads twice and get a different order of lines each time. Is something broken?","options":["No, the order of threads can change on every run","Yes, Kotlin has a bug","Yes, the phone is too slow"],"a":0,"why":"The operating system decides who runs when, and that changes from run to run. Good concurrent code is correct in every order."}]},{"id":"race-conditions","title":"Race conditions","tagline":"When two threads change the same value, updates can get lost.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>likes++</code> is really three steps","Spot shared state that two threads change","Recognize a lost update in a program’s output"],"remember":"likes++ is read, add 1, write. When two threads change the same variable, one thread’s update can be lost, and only on some runs.","terms":[{"term":"shared state","def":"A variable that more than one thread can read and change."},{"term":"race condition","def":"A bug where the result depends on the order in which threads happen to run."},{"term":"lost update","def":"Two threads read the same old value, and the second write wipes out the first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>likes</code> is 5. Ada’s thread reads 5. Sam’s thread reads 5 and writes 6. Ada’s thread adds 1 and writes. What is <code>likes</code>?","options":["6","7","5"],"a":0,"why":"Ada’s thread worked from the old 5, so it writes 6 over Sam’s 6. One like is lost."},{"q":"A two-thread counter printed the right total five runs in a row. Is it free of races?","options":["Not necessarily: a bad order can be rare but still happen","Yes, five runs prove it","Yes, if the phone has several cores"],"a":0,"why":"The order changes from run to run. A lost update needs only one unlucky order, maybe on someone else’s phone."},{"q":"Which of these is shared state?","options":["A <code>var</code> that two threads both change","A <code>var</code> inside a function that only one thread runs","A number written in the code, like <code>100</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Shared state is something more than one thread can change. If only one thread touches it, it cannot race."}]},{"id":"locks","title":"Locks","tagline":"Let only one thread at a time into the code that changes shared state.","level":"beginner","goals":["Guard shared state with <code>synchronized(lock) { }</code>","Explain what a critical section is","Make sure every thread uses the same lock"],"remember":"Put every read and change of shared state inside synchronized(lock) { }, with one shared lock object. Only one thread at a time gets in.","terms":[{"term":"lock","def":"An object that only one thread can hold at a time. <code>synchronized(lock) { }</code> holds it for the block."},{"term":"critical section","def":"Code that reads and changes shared state, which only one thread at a time may run."},{"term":"mutual exclusion","def":"The rule that while one thread is in the critical section, every other thread is kept out."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a critical section?","options":["Code that changes shared state and must run in one thread at a time","Code that runs first in <code>main</code>","Any code inside a lambda"],"a":0,"why":"It is the part where a second thread in the middle would break things, like the three steps of <code>likes++</code>."},{"q":"Ada’s thread uses <code>synchronized(lockA)</code> and Sam’s thread uses <code>synchronized(lockB)</code> around the same <code>likes++</code>. Is it safe?","options":["No, they never wait for each other","Yes, both used <code>synchronized</code>","Only if <code>likes</code> is a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Different locks are different doors. Both threads walk right in, so updates can still be lost."},{"q":"Two threads each call <code>synchronized(lock) { likes++ }</code> 1000 times, starting from 0. What does <code>println(likes)</code> print after both are joined?","options":["2000","Something between 1000 and 2000","1000"],"a":0,"why":"With one shared lock, the three steps of each <code>++</code> can no longer be split, so all 2000 likes count."}]},{"id":"deadlock","title":"Deadlock","tagline":"Two threads each hold what the other needs, and both wait forever.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how two locks can make threads wait in a circle","Prevent deadlock by always taking locks in the same order","Check whether a thread is still running with <code>isAlive</code>"],"remember":"A deadlock is threads waiting for each other’s locks in a circle. Take locks in the same order everywhere and the circle cannot form.","terms":[{"term":"deadlock","def":"Threads waiting for each other’s locks in a circle, so none of them can ever go on."},{"term":"lock ordering","def":"The rule that every thread takes several locks in the same fixed order, which prevents deadlock."}],"quiz":[{"q":"One thread runs <code>synchronized(a) { synchronized(b) { } }</code>, another runs <code>synchronized(b) { synchronized(a) { } }</code>. What can happen?","options":["Both wait forever","Kotlin refuses to compile it","The second thread always waits a little, then goes on"],"a":0,"why":"If each grabs its first lock before the other’s second, each holds what the other needs: a deadlock."},{"q":"Which change prevents that deadlock?","options":["Both threads take <code>a</code> first, then <code>b</code>","Add <code>Thread.sleep(100)</code> before the second lock","Use more threads"],"a":0,"why":"With one fixed order, the thread that waits holds nothing the other needs."},{"q":"What does a deadlocked app usually look like to the person using it?","options":["It freezes, with no error message","It shows a “deadlock” error and closes","It runs slowly but finishes"],"a":0,"why":"The threads are not broken, just waiting forever. Nothing crashes, so nothing reports a problem."}]},{"id":"memory-visibility","title":"Memory visibility","tagline":"A value one thread writes is not always seen by another, unless you ask for it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a thread may not see another thread’s write","Mark a shared flag with <code>@Volatile</code>","Know that <code>@Volatile</code> does not make <code>likes++</code> safe"],"remember":"A write on one thread is not sure to be seen by another. @Volatile makes a flag visible to all threads, but it does not make likes++ safe.","terms":[{"term":"cache","def":"A small, fast memory near each core that keeps copies of values, so it does not have to read main memory every time."},{"term":"visibility","def":"Whether a value one thread wrote can be seen by another thread."},{"term":"@Volatile","def":"An annotation that makes every read of a property get the latest value and every write visible to other threads."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which problem does <code>@Volatile</code> solve?","options":["A thread not seeing a value another thread wrote","Two threads losing updates in <code>likes++</code>","Two threads waiting for each other’s locks"],"a":0,"why":"It is about visibility. Lost updates need a lock, and deadlock needs a lock order."},{"q":"A class has the property <code>@Volatile var likes = 0</code>. Two threads each run <code>likes++</code> on it 100000 times. What does it print after both are joined?","options":["Often less than 200000","Always 200000","Always 100000"],"a":0,"why":"Each read and write is visible, but the three steps can still interleave, so updates are still lost."},{"q":"One thread sets the property <code>done</code> to <code>true</code> once; another checks <code>done</code> in a loop. Which property fits best?","options":["<code>@Volatile var done = false</code>","A plain <code>var done = false</code>","<code>val done = false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One writer, other readers: a volatile flag is exactly this case. A plain <code>var</code> may never be seen, and a <code>val</code> cannot change."}]},{"id":"lock-free-tools","title":"Safer tools","tagline":"Atomic counters, data that never changes, and data only one thread touches.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Count safely from many threads with <code>AtomicInteger</code>","Share data safely by making it immutable","Avoid sharing by keeping a value inside one thread"],"remember":"First try not sharing (confinement) or not changing (immutable data). For a shared counter use AtomicInteger. Use a lock for the rest.","terms":[{"term":"atomic","def":"Done as one step that cannot be split, so no other thread sees it half done. <code>incrementAndGet()</code> is atomic."},{"term":"immutable","def":"Can never change after it is made, like a data class with only <code>val</code> properties."},{"term":"confinement","def":"Keeping a value inside one thread, so no other thread can touch it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which can many threads read at the same time with no lock?","options":["An object of <code>data class Song(val title: String)</code>","A list from <code>mutableListOf()</code> that threads add to","A <code>var count</code> that threads increase"],"a":0,"why":"With only <code>val</code> properties, the object can never change, so there is nothing to race."},{"q":"What does <code>likes.incrementAndGet()</code> do?","options":["Adds 1 in one step that cannot be split, and gives back the new value","Reads the value without changing it","Takes a lock and keeps it"],"a":0,"why":"It is the atomic version of <code>++</code>: read, add and write as one step."},{"q":"A <code>var total = 0</code> is created inside one thread’s lambda, and only that thread uses it. Does it need a lock?","options":["No, it is confined to one thread","Yes, every var needs a lock","Yes, unless it is <code>@Volatile</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Races need at least two threads touching the same value. A confined value has only one."}]}]},{"id":"sweng","title":"Software engineering practice","description":"How teams plan, build and ship software together: life cycle, agile, estimation, design docs, code review, technical debt and working in a team.","requires":["kotlin","git"],"file":"tracks/sweng.js","lessons":[{"id":"sdlc","title":"How software gets built","tagline":"Every change goes through the same five phases, from an idea to users’ phones.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the five phases: requirements, design, build, test, release","Say what each phase hands to the next","Explain why a mistake found late costs more"],"remember":"Requirements, design, build, test, release: each phase hands something to the next, and a mistake costs less the earlier it is found.","terms":[{"term":"software development life cycle (SDLC)","def":"The phases every change goes through: requirements, design, build, test, release."},{"term":"requirement","def":"A short statement of what the software must do, written so anyone can check it."},{"term":"release","def":"Publishing a new version of the app so users can install it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which phase decides <em>what</em> a feature must do?","options":["Requirements","Design","Test"],"a":0,"why":"Requirements say what; design says how."},{"q":"A requirement was misunderstood. When is it cheapest to find out?","options":["While writing the requirements","During testing","After the release"],"a":0,"why":"Early, only a few sentences change. Later, the design, the code and the tests built on it change too."},{"q":"What does the build phase hand to testing?","options":["A list of what users asked for","Code that is written, reviewed and merged","A version in the app store"],"a":1,"why":"Each phase hands its result to the next. Build produces working code to test."}]},{"id":"agile","title":"Working in iterations","tagline":"Build a small piece, show it, learn, repeat.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why teams ship in short iterations","Use a backlog to choose what comes next","Give a short daily stand-up update"],"remember":"Work in short sprints: pull the top of the backlog, meet for 15 minutes a day, show the result, adjust.","terms":[{"term":"backlog","def":"The ordered list of tickets the team might work on, most important first. A <em>ticket</em> is a card for one piece of work."},{"term":"sprint","def":"One <em>iteration</em> (a short round of requirements, design, build, test and release on a small piece of work) with a fixed length, often two weeks. It starts with planning and ends by showing the finished work, then a retrospective."},{"term":"retrospective","def":"A short meeting at the end of a sprint about what to do differently next time."},{"term":"stand-up","def":"A daily meeting of about 15 minutes: yesterday, today, and anything that blocks you."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the backlog?","options":["The ordered list of work the team might do","The work finished last sprint","A list of bugs only"],"a":0,"why":"Ideas, requests and bugs all go in, most important first."},{"q":"Lin is stuck waiting for the rain data. When should she say so?","options":["At the next stand-up, or sooner","At the end of the sprint","Only if it lasts a week"],"a":0,"why":"A blocker said early can often be solved in minutes. Said late, it has cost days."},{"q":"Why do teams work in short iterations?","options":["Users see something early, and their feedback shapes the next piece","It skips the test phase","No planning is needed any more"],"a":0,"why":"Every phase still happens, on a small piece. The win is learning early."}]},{"id":"estimation","title":"Estimating work","tagline":"Break a job into small pieces, and answer with a range, not one number.","level":"beginner","goals":["Break a feature into small tasks you can picture","Give an estimate as a range","Spot the work that is easy to forget"],"remember":"Break the work into tasks of a day or less, count the work that isn’t code, and answer with a range you update as you learn.","terms":[{"term":"estimate","def":"A best guess of how long a piece of work will take, best given as a range."},{"term":"uncertainty","def":"What you don’t know yet about a piece of work. The more there is, the wider the range."},{"term":"breaking work down","def":"Splitting a feature into small tasks you can each picture from start to finish."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why break a feature into small tasks before estimating?","options":["Small tasks are easier to picture, and splitting turns up forgotten work","It makes the work itself go faster","So that each person gets exactly one task"],"a":0,"why":"You can guess a one-day task far better than a vague two-week one."},{"q":"Which answer is the most useful estimate?","options":["“3 to 6 days; I’ll know more once I see the data.”","“Exactly 4 days.”","“No idea.”"],"a":0,"why":"A range shows the uncertainty, and saying what would narrow it gives the team a plan."},{"q":"Which work is often left out of estimates?","options":["Tests, review comments and checks on different phones","Writing the main code","Choosing a ticket"],"a":0,"why":"It doesn’t feel like “the feature”, but it takes real days."}]},{"id":"design-doc","title":"Design documents","tagline":"Write the plan down, compare the options, and decide before you build.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the parts of a short design doc","Compare options by their trade-offs","Explain why the doc comes before the code"],"remember":"Before big work, write one page: problem, goals, non-goals, options with their trade-offs, and a decision with its reason.","terms":[{"term":"design doc","def":"A short written proposal, made before the code: problem, goals, non-goals, options, decision."},{"term":"non-goal","def":"Something the team deliberately won’t do in this piece of work, written down so the work doesn’t keep growing."},{"term":"trade-off","def":"What an option gains, and what it pays for it, such as more features for more time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When is a design doc most useful?","options":["Before the code, while changing the plan is cheap","After the release, to describe what was built","Only when something breaks"],"a":0,"why":"Its value is the cheap disagreement before the expensive work."},{"q":"What is a non-goal?","options":["Something the team deliberately won’t do in this work","A goal that failed","A bug nobody fixed"],"a":0,"why":"Writing it down settles “are we also doing this?” questions before they come up in review."},{"q":"What is a trade-off?","options":["What an option gains, and what it pays for it","Swapping two tickets in the backlog","Always choosing the cheapest option"],"a":0,"why":"Good designs compare trade-offs; the cheapest option is not always right."}]},{"id":"code-review","title":"Code review","tagline":"Comment on the code, not the person, and take comments as help.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write review comments that are kind, specific and say why","Mark how much a comment matters","Answer the comments on your own pull request"],"remember":"Review the code, not the person: say what, why and a fix, and label it Must, Suggestion or Nit. When you receive one, thank, fix and reply.","terms":[{"term":"review comment","def":"A note a reviewer leaves on a line of a pull request, ideally saying what, why and how to fix it."},{"term":"nit","def":"A tiny, optional point in a review, like a name or a space. Starting a comment with “Nit:” says it shouldn’t block the merge."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which comment follows the advice in this lesson?","options":["“Must: this reads one past the end of the list and crashes. Use <code>for (city in cities)</code>.”","“Wrong loop.”","“Why would you write it like this?”"],"a":0,"why":"It is about the code, says why, and suggests a fix."},{"q":"What does a comment that starts with “Nit:” mean?","options":["A tiny point that shouldn’t block the merge","A bug that must be fixed","The reviewer is not sure"],"a":0,"why":"Labels tell the author which comments are required and which are optional."},{"q":"A reviewer asks for a change you disagree with. What do you do?","options":["Reply with your reason and talk it through","Ignore it and merge","Close the pull request"],"a":0,"why":"The reviewer may know something you don’t, or you may know something they don’t. Talking finds out which."}]},{"id":"tech-debt","title":"Technical debt","tagline":"A shortcut saves time today and charges a little on every later change.","level":"beginner","goals":["Recognise a shortcut that becomes technical debt","Explain the “interest” it charges on later work","Pay it back in small steps, and write down what is left"],"remember":"Technical debt is a shortcut that makes later changes slower. Take it on purpose, write it down, and pay it back in small steps.","terms":[{"term":"technical debt","def":"A quicker, messier way of building something that saves time now and makes later changes slower."},{"term":"interest (technical debt)","def":"The extra time every later change costs because of the shortcut."},{"term":"refactoring","def":"Changing how code is built, without changing what it does, so it is easier to change next time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is technical debt?","options":["A shortcut that saves time now and makes later changes slower","Money the team owes for its tools","A bug that users reported"],"a":0,"why":"The “loan” is the time saved; the cost comes later."},{"q":"What is the “interest” on technical debt?","options":["The extra time every later change in that code costs","A fee paid every month","The time it took to write the shortcut"],"a":0,"why":"You pay it each time you touch the messy code, not on a calendar."},{"q":"There is no time to clean up before tomorrow’s release. What is a good move?","options":["Take the shortcut, and add a ticket to the backlog to pay it back","Take the shortcut and forget about it","Delay the release for a big rewrite"],"a":0,"why":"Debt taken on purpose and written down is a fair deal. Forgotten debt keeps growing."}]},{"id":"teamwork","title":"Working in a team","tagline":"Give every job one owner, ask early, and say what you tried.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give every task one owner, and close the loop when it is done","Ask for help before you lose a day","Write a message someone can act on: what, what happened, what you tried"],"remember":"Every task has one owner. Ask early, in the open, saying what you tried. Close the loop when it is done.","terms":[{"term":"owner","def":"The one person responsible for seeing a task through, shown as its assignee."},{"term":"close the loop","def":"When a task is done, tell the person who asked for it, and close the ticket."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Three people read a bug report and each thinks someone else has it. What prevents this?","options":["One named owner, visible on the ticket","More people reading it","Waiting for the next sprint"],"a":0,"why":"When the ticket shows one name, nobody has to guess."},{"q":"You have been stuck for 30 minutes. What is a good next step?","options":["Ask the team, saying what you tried","Keep trying alone for two more days","Start another task without telling anyone"],"a":0,"why":"A teammate who knows the code may answer in minutes."},{"q":"The bug is fixed and released. What does “closing the loop” mean here?","options":["Telling the person who reported it, and closing the ticket","Deleting the branch","Starting the next sprint"],"a":0,"why":"The person who asked shouldn’t have to wonder whether anything happened."}]}]},{"id":"runtime","title":"How code runs","description":"What happens between your Kotlin and the phone’s chip: compilers, bytecode, DEX files, the Android Runtime, memory clean-up and app start.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/runtime.js","lessons":[{"id":"source-to-machine","title":"From source to machine code","tagline":"The phone’s chip doesn’t read Kotlin. Here is how your code gets translated.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a compiler and an interpreter do","Say what bytecode is and why a VM runs it","Follow one line of Kotlin from your file to the chip"],"remember":"The CPU runs only machine code. A compiler translates before the program runs, an interpreter while it runs; bytecode is the in-between form a VM runs.","terms":[{"term":"compiler","def":"A program that translates a whole program into another form, such as bytecode or machine code, before it runs."},{"term":"interpreter","def":"A program that reads code while it runs and carries out each step directly."},{"term":"bytecode","def":"Simple steps written for a virtual machine instead of a real chip. Kotlin compiles to bytecode."},{"term":"virtual machine (VM)","def":"A program that acts like a simple computer and runs bytecode. On Android it is ART."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the only thing a CPU understands?","options":["Kotlin","Bytecode","Machine code"],"a":2,"why":"Everything else has to be translated into machine code before the chip can run it."},{"q":"You misspell <code>println</code>. Who stops you before the program runs?","options":["The compiler","The CPU","Nobody: it crashes later"],"a":0,"why":"The compiler checks the whole program before anything runs. That check is what a compile error is."},{"q":"Why compile to bytecode instead of straight to machine code?","options":["Bytecode is shorter to type","The same bytecode runs on any device that has the VM, whatever its chip","Bytecode never needs translating again"],"a":1,"why":"Phones use different chips. The VM on each phone turns the same bytecode into that phone’s machine code."}]},{"id":"kotlin-compile","title":"How Kotlin compiles","tagline":"Look inside the files kotlinc writes, and see what a property really is.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Compile a Kotlin file with <code>kotlinc</code> and find its <code>.class</code> file","Read what <code>javap</code> shows inside a class file","Explain what a <code>val</code>, a <code>var</code> and a custom getter compile to"],"remember":"kotlinc writes one .class file per class. A val is a private field plus a getter; a var adds a setter.","terms":[{"term":"kotlinc","def":"The Kotlin compiler as a terminal command. It writes <code>.class</code> files full of bytecode."},{"term":"javap","def":"A viewer that comes with Java. <code>javap -p</code> lists what is inside a <code>.class</code> file."},{"term":"field","def":"The private box inside an object that stores a property’s value."},{"term":"getter / setter","def":"The small functions a property compiles to: <code>getName()</code> reads it, <code>setAge()</code> changes it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>class Box(val size: Int)</code> compile to for <code>size</code>?","options":["A private field and <code>getSize()</code>","A getter and a setter","Nothing: properties exist only in Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>val</code> is read-only: a private field plus a getter. A <code>var</code> would add <code>setSize</code>."},{"q":"Your file <code>Notes.kt</code> has a function outside any class. Which class file holds it?","options":["<code>Notes.class</code>","<code>NotesKt.class</code>","None: it stays in the .kt file"],"a":1,"why":"Bytecode keeps every function inside a class, so the compiler makes one named after the file plus <code>Kt</code>."},{"q":"<code>val isEmpty: Boolean get() = count == 0</code>. What appears in the class file for it?","options":["A field and a getter","Only the method <code>isEmpty()</code>","A setter <code>setEmpty()</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Its value is computed each time, so nothing is stored. A name starting with <code>is</code> keeps that name for its getter."}]},{"id":"dex","title":"DEX files","tagline":"Android runs its own kind of bytecode, packed into classes.dex.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why an Android build converts <code>.class</code> files to DEX","Say what D8 does when you build","Know what happens when an app outgrows one DEX file"],"remember":"D8 turns every .class file into DEX. One DEX file refers to at most 65,536 methods; past that, the build adds classes2.dex.","terms":[{"term":"DEX","def":"Dalvik Executable: the bytecode format Android runs, stored in <code>classes.dex</code> files inside the APK."},{"term":"D8","def":"The build tool that converts <code>.class</code> files into DEX. Android Studio runs it for you."},{"term":"method","def":"Bytecode’s name for a function, including the getters and setters properties compile to."},{"term":"multidex","def":"Splitting an app’s code over several DEX files: <code>classes.dex</code>, <code>classes2.dex</code> and so on."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which files does Android run?","options":["<code>.kt</code> files","<code>.class</code> files","DEX files"],"a":2,"why":"kotlinc writes <code>.class</code> files, and D8 converts them to DEX, the format Android runs."},{"q":"What does D8 do?","options":["Converts <code>.class</code> files into DEX","Checks your Kotlin for typos","Installs the app on the phone"],"a":0,"why":"Typos are the Kotlin compiler’s job. D8 comes after it and turns its output into DEX."},{"q":"Your app passes 65,536 method references and has <code>minSdk = 24</code>. What must you do?","options":["Remove libraries until it fits","Nothing: the build writes <code>classes2.dex</code> and ART loads it","Turn the app into two apps"],"a":1,"why":"With <code>minSdk</code> 21 or higher, multidex is on by default."}]},{"id":"art","title":"Android Runtime","tagline":"Interpreted, compiled while running, or compiled ahead: how ART runs your code.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three ways ART can run a method","Explain why a fresh install starts slower","Say what a profile and a Baseline Profile do"],"remember":"Interpreter first, JIT for hot methods, AOT for what the profile lists. A Baseline Profile gives every user that list from launch 1.","terms":[{"term":"ART","def":"The Android Runtime: Android’s VM. It runs DEX code with an interpreter, a JIT compiler and AOT-compiled code."},{"term":"JIT compilation","def":"Compiling hot methods into machine code while the app runs. The code is kept in memory."},{"term":"AOT compilation","def":"Compiling methods into machine code before the app runs, done on the phone by <code>dex2oat</code>."},{"term":"profile","def":"A list of the methods that matter, which ART compiles ahead of time. A <em>Baseline Profile</em> is one shipped inside the app."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A fresh install has no profile and no Baseline Profile. How do its first launches run?","options":["Fully compiled ahead of time","In the interpreter, with hot methods JIT-compiled","Not at all until dex2oat finishes"],"a":1,"why":"Without a profile there is nothing to compile ahead of time, so ART starts with the interpreter and the JIT."},{"q":"Why doesn’t ART compile every method of every app ahead of time?","options":["It costs time and storage for code that rarely runs","AOT code is slower than the interpreter","Android doesn’t allow it"],"a":0,"why":"The profile points ART at the code that matters, so it compiles only that."},{"q":"What does shipping a Baseline Profile change?","options":["The app gets smaller","ART compiles the listed methods at or soon after installation, so the first launches are faster","The JIT is switched off"],"a":1,"why":"The profile is inside the app, so it is there from the first install instead of after the first idle-and-charging night."}]},{"id":"gc","title":"Garbage collection","tagline":"Objects stay in memory while something can reach them. The rest is cleaned up for you.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what makes an object reachable","Follow one mark-and-sweep collection step by step","Predict which objects a collection frees"],"remember":"Reachable from a GC root means kept. Everything else is freed, even objects that only point at each other.","terms":[{"term":"garbage collector (GC)","def":"The part of ART that finds objects the app can no longer reach and frees their memory."},{"term":"GC root","def":"Something surely in use, such as a local variable of a running function or a Kotlin <code>object</code>."},{"term":"reachable","def":"An object you can get to by following references from a GC root. Reachable objects are kept."},{"term":"mark and sweep","def":"Mark every object reachable from the roots, then free (sweep) all the others."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>A</code> and <code>B</code> point at each other, and nothing else points at either. What does the GC do?","options":["Keeps both, because each has a reference","Frees both, because neither is reachable from a root","Frees one and keeps the other"],"a":1,"why":"The only question is “reachable from a root?”. A pair cut off from the roots is garbage."},{"q":"A function ends. What happens to an object only its local variable pointed at?","options":["It is freed that same moment","It becomes unreachable, and a later collection frees it","It stays forever"],"a":1,"why":"The local variable is gone when the function ends. The object waits until the GC runs and finds it unreachable."},{"q":"You add an object to a list inside a Kotlin <code>object</code>. When can the GC free it?","options":["When the function that made it ends","Not while it is in that list","At the next collection"],"a":1,"why":"The <code>object</code> is a GC root, and the list inside it points at your object, so it stays reachable."}]},{"id":"references","title":"Strong and weak references","tagline":"A normal reference keeps an object alive. A WeakReference doesn’t.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how a reference can keep a closed screen in memory","Hold an object with <code>WeakReference</code> and read it with <code>get()</code>","Decide when a weak reference helps and when to let go instead"],"remember":"A strong reference keeps an object alive; a WeakReference doesn’t, and its get() can return null. Never let something long-lived hold a screen strongly.","terms":[{"term":"strong reference","def":"A normal reference. While it exists, the object it points at can’t be freed."},{"term":"WeakReference","def":"A reference the GC ignores. <code>get()</code> returns the object, or <code>null</code> after it has been freed."},{"term":"memory leak","def":"An object the app no longer needs that is still reachable, so the GC can never free it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Only weak references point at an object. What can happen at the next GC?","options":["Nothing: weak references keep it too","It is freed, and <code>get()</code> then returns <code>null</code>","It turns into a strong reference"],"a":1,"why":"The GC ignores weak references, so the object is unreachable."},{"q":"Why is <code>ref.get()</code> read with <code>?.</code>?","options":["It returns <code>null</code> once the object has been freed","Kotlin requires <code>?.</code> after every function call","It makes the reference strong"],"a":0,"why":"The object may already be gone. <code>?.</code> skips the call instead of crashing."},{"q":"An Activity adds itself to a list in a Kotlin <code>object</code> in <code>onCreate</code>. What fixes the leak most directly?","options":["Removing it from the list in <code>onDestroy</code>","Rotating the phone less","Making the list a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"If the Activity leaves the list when it is destroyed, no root reaches it and the GC can free it."}]},{"id":"startup","title":"What happens at app start","tagline":"From tapping the icon to the first frame: each step, and where the time goes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List the steps of a cold start in order","Explain why work in <code>Application.onCreate</code> delays every launch","Move heavy set-up out of the start with <code>by lazy</code>"],"remember":"Cold start: process, your code, Application.onCreate, the first Activity, the first frame, one after another on the main thread. Do less there, or do it later.","terms":[{"term":"cold start","def":"A start when the app isn’t running at all, so every step from creating the process to the first frame happens."},{"term":"process","def":"A running program with its own memory. Android creates one for your app."},{"term":"Zygote","def":"A process started when the phone boots, with Android’s code loaded. Every app process is a copy of it."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The one line of work that runs your start-up code and also draws the screen and answers taps."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which order is right for a cold start?","options":["Activity.onCreate → Application.onCreate → process","Process → Application.onCreate → Activity.onCreate → first frame","First frame → process → Application.onCreate"],"a":1,"why":"The process comes first, the <code>Application</code> before any Activity, and drawing last."},{"q":"Why does heavy work in <code>Application.onCreate</code> hurt so much?","options":["It runs on the main thread before the first screen, on every cold start","It runs only once, at install","It makes the APK bigger"],"a":0,"why":"Every cold start waits for it, and the person sees only a blank window meanwhile."},{"q":"A Baseline Profile speeds up which steps of a cold start?","options":["Creating the process from Zygote","The steps that run your app’s code","None of them"],"a":1,"why":"The profile changes how your DEX code runs. Creating the process is Android’s own work."}]}]},{"id":"db","title":"Databases","description":"Keep an app’s data in a database with SQLite: make tables, ask them questions in SQL, link them with keys, and keep the data fast, safe and up to date as the app grows.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/db.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-db","title":"What a database is","tagline":"A place where an app keeps its data in tidy tables.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what a table, a row and a column are","Explain why apps keep data in a database instead of a text file","Make a table with <code>CREATE TABLE</code> in <code>sqlite3</code>"],"remember":"A table holds rows of one kind of thing; every column has a name and a type. CREATE TABLE makes one.","terms":[{"term":"database","def":"A program that keeps data safe and finds it fast. SQLite is one."},{"term":"table, row, column","def":"A table is a grid of data. Each row is one thing (one book); each column is one kind of value (the title)."},{"term":"SQL","def":"The language you use to talk to a database, with commands like <code>CREATE TABLE</code>."},{"term":"sqlite3","def":"A terminal program for SQLite: <code>sqlite3 library.db</code> opens that database file."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In Ada’s <code>books</code> table, what is one row?","options":["One book","Every title, top to bottom","The whole file"],"a":0,"why":"A row is one thing. Every title, top to bottom, is a column."},{"q":"Which column type fits a year like 2019?","options":["<code>TEXT</code>","<code>INTEGER</code>"],"a":1,"why":"A year is a whole number, so <code>INTEGER</code>."},{"q":"Why does a text file get hard to use as an app’s data grows?","options":["Text files cannot hold numbers","The program must read and cut apart every line itself to find anything","A computer can open only one text file"],"a":1,"why":"A database does the finding for you, and keeps the data safe while it saves."}]},{"id":"sql-select","title":"Reading data with SQL","tagline":"Ask a table a question and get back only the rows you need.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read columns from a table with <code>SELECT</code> and <code>FROM</code>","Keep only the rows that match with <code>WHERE</code>","Sort with <code>ORDER BY</code> and keep the first few with <code>LIMIT</code>"],"remember":"SELECT columns FROM table WHERE test ORDER BY column LIMIT n, in that order. Text goes in single quotes.","terms":[{"term":"query","def":"A question you ask the database, like <code>SELECT title FROM books;</code>. The answer is a small table."},{"term":"SELECT … FROM","def":"<code>SELECT</code> names the columns you want, <code>FROM</code> names the table. <code>*</code> means all columns."},{"term":"WHERE","def":"Keeps only the rows that pass a test, like <code>WHERE year &gt; 2018</code>."},{"term":"ORDER BY, DESC, LIMIT","def":"<code>ORDER BY</code> sorts the answer (<code>DESC</code> for biggest first); <code>LIMIT</code> keeps the first few rows."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>SELECT title FROM books WHERE author = 'mia hart';</code> print?","options":["Rain at Dawn and Night Train","Nothing: no row matches","An error"],"a":1,"why":"Text must match exactly, small and capital letters included."},{"q":"Which part keeps only some of the rows?","options":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>WHERE</code>","<code>ORDER BY</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>SELECT</code> picks columns; <code>WHERE</code> picks rows."},{"q":"How do you get only the oldest book?","options":["<code>ORDER BY year LIMIT 1</code>","<code>ORDER BY year DESC LIMIT 1</code>","<code>LIMIT 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>DESC</code> the smallest year comes first. <code>LIMIT 1</code> alone gives no promised order."}]},{"id":"sql-write","title":"Changing data with SQL","tagline":"Add rows, change them and remove them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add rows with <code>INSERT INTO</code> and <code>VALUES</code>","Change rows with <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>SET</code>","Remove rows with <code>DELETE FROM</code>, aimed by <code>WHERE</code>"],"remember":"INSERT adds rows, UPDATE changes them, DELETE removes them. UPDATE and DELETE hit every row unless WHERE aims them.","terms":[{"term":"INSERT INTO … VALUES","def":"Adds a row: <code>INSERT INTO books VALUES (6, 'Blue Hills', 'Ines Costa', 2023, 1);</code>"},{"term":"UPDATE … SET","def":"Changes columns in the rows that pass the <code>WHERE</code> test."},{"term":"DELETE FROM","def":"Removes the rows that pass the <code>WHERE</code> test. Without <code>WHERE</code>, all of them."},{"term":"NULL","def":"SQL’s “no value”, like <code>null</code> in Kotlin. A column you leave out of an <code>INSERT</code> holds <code>NULL</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>DELETE FROM books;</code> remove?","options":["Nothing: it needs a <code>WHERE</code>","Every row; the empty table stays","The whole table, columns and all"],"a":1,"why":"It removes all the rows. The table itself is still there, empty."},{"q":"After <code>INSERT INTO books (id, title) VALUES (9, 'Mist');</code>, what is in that row’s <code>year</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","Nothing: it stays empty (<code>NULL</code>)","An error stops the INSERT"],"a":1,"why":"Columns you leave out hold <code>NULL</code>. <code>sqlite3</code> prints the row as <code>9|Mist|||</code>."},{"q":"Which command changes Night Train’s year to 2020?","options":["<code>UPDATE books SET year = 2020 WHERE id = 3;</code>","<code>INSERT INTO books VALUES (3, 2020);</code>","<code>UPDATE books WHERE id = 3 SET year = 2020;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>SET</code> comes before <code>WHERE</code>. <code>INSERT</code> adds a new row instead of changing one."}]},{"id":"keys","title":"Keys and relations","tagline":"Give every row its own name, and let one table point at another.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a column that names each row with <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>","Point from one table to another with <code>REFERENCES</code>","Turn on SQLite’s link checks with <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON</code>"],"remember":"A primary key names each row; a foreign key points at another table’s primary key. In SQLite, run PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON first.","terms":[{"term":"primary key","def":"A column whose value is different in every row, so it names exactly one row: <code>id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code>."},{"term":"foreign key","def":"A column that holds another table’s primary key, like <code>book_id INTEGER REFERENCES books(id)</code>."},{"term":"relation","def":"The link between two tables made by a foreign key."},{"term":"PRAGMA","def":"Reads or changes a setting of SQLite, like <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>id</code> is the primary key of <code>books</code>, and a book with id 2 exists. What does another <code>INSERT</code> with id 2 do?","options":["Replaces the old row","Fails: <code>UNIQUE constraint failed: books.id</code>","Keeps both rows"],"a":1,"why":"A primary key value can belong to one row only."},{"q":"A loan has <code>book_id</code> 4. What is <code>book_id</code>?","options":["The loan’s primary key","A foreign key: it points at book 4 in <code>books</code>","A copy of the book’s title"],"a":1,"why":"It holds another table’s primary key, so it is a foreign key."},{"q":"You open <code>sqlite3</code> again tomorrow. Are the foreign key checks on?","options":["Yes, the file remembers","No: run <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;</code> again"],"a":1,"why":"The setting lasts only until the session ends."}]},{"id":"joins","title":"Joining tables","tagline":"Put rows from two tables side by side in one answer.","level":"beginner","goals":["Combine two tables in one query with <code>JOIN</code> and <code>ON</code>","Name a column by its table, like <code>books.title</code>","Explain why rows without a partner drop out"],"remember":"FROM a JOIN b ON a.foreign_key = b.id puts matching rows side by side. Rows without a partner drop out.","terms":[{"term":"join","def":"Combining rows of two tables into one answer: <code>FROM loans JOIN books ON ...</code>."},{"term":"ON","def":"In a join, the rule for which rows belong together, like <code>loans.book_id = books.id</code>."},{"term":"ambiguous column","def":"A column name that more than one table in the query has. Write <code>table.column</code> instead."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Small Rooms missing from the joined loans?","options":["No loan points at it","Its id is the biggest","A join always skips the last row"],"a":0,"why":"A join keeps only rows that found a partner."},{"q":"What does <code>ON loans.book_id = books.id</code> say?","options":["Copy <code>book_id</code> into <code>books</code>","Which loan row goes with which book row","Sort the answer by id"],"a":1,"why":"<code>ON</code> is the rule for pairing rows."},{"q":"SQLite says <code>ambiguous column name: id</code>. What fixes it?","options":["Write <code>books.id</code> or <code>loans.id</code>","Add <code>DESC</code>","Remove the <code>ON</code> part"],"a":0,"why":"Naming the table makes clear which <code>id</code> you mean."}]},{"id":"normalization","title":"Designing tables","tagline":"Keep each fact in one place, so it can’t disagree with itself.","level":"beginner","goals":["Spot a fact that is copied into many rows","Split a wide table into tables that each describe one kind of thing","Use the normal forms as a plain-words checklist"],"remember":"Write each fact once: one table per kind of thing, linked by keys, and joins to put them back together.","terms":[{"term":"normalization","def":"Designing tables so each fact is stored once, with keys linking the tables."},{"term":"normal forms","def":"Checklist steps for normalization. In plain words: one value per cell, and every column describes what its table’s key names."},{"term":"anomaly","def":"A problem caused by copies: an update that misses a copy, a delete that erases a fact you still need, or a fact you can’t store yet."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A table copies each member’s city into every loan. What can go wrong?","options":["Changing the city in one row leaves other rows saying something else","The table runs out of columns","SQLite refuses text it has seen before"],"a":0,"why":"Every copy must change together, and it is easy to miss one."},{"q":"In Ada’s design, where does a member’s city belong?","options":["In <code>members</code>","In <code>loans</code>","In <code>books</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A city describes a member, so it sits next to the member’s key."},{"q":"What is first normal form, in plain words?","options":["One value per cell","Every table has one row","Use only one table"],"a":0,"why":"No lists like “Night Train, Paper Birds” packed into a single cell."}]},{"id":"indexes","title":"Indexes","tagline":"A sorted lookup list that lets the database jump straight to a row.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a search in a big table is slow without an index","Make an index with <code>CREATE INDEX</code> and check it with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code>","Name the cost: every change also updates the index"],"remember":"An index is a sorted list that turns a SCAN of every row into a quick SEARCH. It costs space and slows every write a little.","terms":[{"term":"full scan","def":"Reading every row to answer a query. <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> shows it as <code>SCAN</code>."},{"term":"index","def":"A sorted list of one column’s values, each pointing at its row, made with <code>CREATE INDEX</code>."},{"term":"query plan","def":"How SQLite will run a query. <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> prints it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The plan says <code>SCAN books</code>. What does SQLite do?","options":["Reads every row","Jumps to the row through an index","Sorts the table"],"a":0,"why":"A scan reads the whole table."},{"q":"What does an index cost?","options":["Nothing","Space, plus extra work on every <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>","It makes every <code>SELECT</code> slower"],"a":1,"why":"The sorted list has to be kept up to date on every write."},{"q":"There is an index on <code>title</code> only. Which search can use it?","options":["<code>WHERE title = 'Night Train'</code>","<code>WHERE author = 'Mia Hart'</code>","<code>WHERE year = 2021</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An index helps searches on the column it sorts."}]},{"id":"transactions","title":"Transactions","tagline":"Several changes that happen all together, or not at all.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Group changes with <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code>","Throw away a half-done group with <code>ROLLBACK</code>","Explain ACID in plain words"],"remember":"BEGIN, the changes, then COMMIT to keep them all or ROLLBACK to drop them all.","terms":[{"term":"transaction","def":"A group of changes that count as one: all of them happen, or none do."},{"term":"COMMIT, ROLLBACK","def":"<code>COMMIT</code> makes every change since <code>BEGIN</code> permanent; <code>ROLLBACK</code> throws them all away."},{"term":"ACID","def":"Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (rules still hold), Isolated (nobody sees it halfway), Durable (a commit survives a crash)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>ROLLBACK;</code> undo?","options":["Only the last command","Every change since <code>BEGIN</code>","Every change ever made"],"a":1,"why":"It drops the whole open transaction."},{"q":"Which letter of ACID means “all of it or none of it”?","options":["A, atomic","D, durable","I, isolated"],"a":0,"why":"Atomic: the group can’t be split."},{"q":"Ada runs <code>COMMIT;</code> with no <code>BEGIN</code> before it. What does <code>sqlite3</code> say?","options":["Nothing","<code>Runtime error: cannot commit - no transaction is active</code>","It commits the last command again"],"a":1,"why":"Without <code>BEGIN</code>, each command was already final, so there is nothing to commit."}]},{"id":"nosql","title":"Other kinds of databases","tagline":"Not every database is made of tables.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe a key-value store and a document store","Say what each is good at, and when tables are better","Pick a fitting kind of store for a piece of app data"],"remember":"Tables for linked data that must agree, key-value for lookups by key, documents for data read as one piece.","terms":[{"term":"NoSQL","def":"A name for databases that don’t keep data in tables, such as key-value and document stores."},{"term":"key-value store","def":"Stores values under keys, like a Kotlin <code>Map</code> saved to disk. Finds things only by key."},{"term":"document store","def":"Stores each item as one nested document, so it can be read in one go."},{"term":"JSON","def":"A text format for data: names and values in curly braces, lists in square brackets."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which fits the app’s dark-mode setting best?","options":["A key-value store","Three tables and a join","One document per book"],"a":0,"why":"One value, found by one key."},{"q":"Documents copy a book’s title into each member who borrowed it. What is the risk?","options":["A correction must reach every copy, or they disagree","Documents can’t hold text","Each read needs a join"],"a":0,"why":"The same problem as copies in a wide table."},{"q":"What can a key-value store find quickly?","options":["The value for one key","Every key whose value is <code>dark</code>","Rows joined with another table"],"a":0,"why":"It is built to look things up by key, and only by key."}]},{"id":"schema-evolution","title":"Changing a schema","tagline":"Change the shape of a database that already holds people’s data.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add a column to an existing table with <code>ALTER TABLE</code> … <code>ADD COLUMN</code>","Number schema versions and write one migration per step","Keep old code working by adding instead of renaming"],"remember":"A migration changes the schema one version at a time and keeps the rows. Add new things; remove old ones only when nothing uses them.","terms":[{"term":"schema","def":"The shape of a database: its tables and their columns."},{"term":"migration","def":"A script that moves the schema from one version to the next and keeps the data."},{"term":"user_version","def":"A number SQLite keeps in the file for you, used as the schema version: <code>PRAGMA user_version;</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do old rows hold in a column added with <code>ADD COLUMN</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>NULL</code>: no value","A copy of the column before it"],"a":1,"why":"Nothing was ever stored there, so it is <code>NULL</code>."},{"q":"A phone is at <code>user_version</code> 1, and the new app expects 3. What should run?","options":["Only migration 2→3","Migration 1→2, then 2→3","Delete the database and start again"],"a":1,"why":"Each step builds on the one before, so they run in order."},{"q":"Why prefer adding a column over renaming one?","options":["Old code that uses the old name keeps working","SQL has no way to rename","Adding is faster to type"],"a":0,"why":"Renaming is possible, but it breaks every query that uses the old name."}]}]},{"id":"backend","title":"Backend with Kotlin","description":"Build the server side of your own app in the language you already know: HTTP, Ktor, REST design, PostgreSQL, auth, tests, deployment and the Android client that talks to it.","requires":["coroutines","data"],"file":"tracks/backend.js","lessons":[{"id":"what","title":"What a backend does","tagline":"A request comes in, routing picks a handler, the handler talks to a database, and a response goes back.","level":"beginner","remember":"Request → routing → handler → database → response. Never trust what the client sends.","terms":[{"term":"Backend","def":"The server-side program that receives requests from your apps, applies the rules and owns the shared data."},{"term":"Handler","def":"The function that runs for one matched route and produces the response."},{"term":"Routing","def":"Choosing which handler runs, from the request’s method and path."},{"term":"Stateless","def":"Each request carries everything needed to answer it. The server keeps no per-client memory between requests."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app sends <code>GET /api/nope</code> and no route matches. Which stages run?","options":["Routing, then the response (404). No handler, no database.","All five stages, with an empty database result","None: the request is dropped silently"],"a":0,"why":"Routing finds no match, so no handler code runs and Ktor answers 404 directly."},{"q":"Which of these belongs on the server, not in the app?","options":["The colour of the like button","Checking that a user may delete a comment","Showing a loading spinner"],"a":1,"why":"Permission checks must happen where the user cannot change the code. The app can hide a button, but only the server can refuse the request."},{"q":"Your server runs on your laptop at port 8080. What URL does the Android emulator use?","options":["http://localhost:8080","http://10.0.2.2:8080","http://127.0.0.1:8080"],"a":1,"why":"Inside the emulator, localhost and 127.0.0.1 point at the emulator itself. 10.0.2.2 is the emulator’s alias for the host machine."}]},{"id":"http","title":"HTTP in depth","tagline":"Methods say what you want, status codes say what happened, headers carry everything else.","level":"beginner","remember":"GET is safe. PUT and DELETE are idempotent. POST is neither, so retries need care.","terms":[{"term":"Safe method","def":"A method that only reads: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS. Calling it must not change server state."},{"term":"Idempotent","def":"Sending the request several times has the same effect on the server as sending it once. GET, PUT and DELETE are; POST and PATCH are not guaranteed to be."},{"term":"Content negotiation","def":"Picking the response format from the client’s Accept header and the formats the server supports."},{"term":"Status code","def":"The three-digit number at the start of a response. The first digit gives its family: 2xx success, 4xx client error, 5xx server error."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app sends <code>DELETE /api/bookmarks/42</code> twice. The first returns 204, the second 404. Is DELETE still idempotent here?","options":["No, the responses differ","Yes: the server state after both is the same as after one","Only if the server returns 204 both times"],"a":1,"why":"Idempotency is about the effect on the server, not the response. After one or two deletes, bookmark 42 is gone either way."},{"q":"A token is missing from the request. Which status fits?","options":["401 Unauthorized","403 Forbidden","400 Bad Request"],"a":0,"why":"401 means the server does not know who you are. 403 is for a known user who lacks permission."},{"q":"A request times out on a train. Which method can the app safely retry without extra work?","options":["POST /comments","PUT /profile/name","Both"],"a":1,"why":"PUT replaces the value, so repeating it sets the same name again. A repeated POST may create a second comment."},{"q":"The client sends <code>Accept: application/xml</code> to a JSON-only API. What may the server answer?","options":["406 Not Acceptable","415 Unsupported Media Type, because of the Accept header","500 Internal Server Error"],"a":0,"why":"406 says the server cannot produce any format the client accepts. 415 is about the format of the request body (Content-Type)."},{"q":"Which response tells the app that retrying later might work?","options":["404 Not Found","400 Bad Request","503 Service Unavailable"],"a":2,"why":"5xx means the server failed, not the request. A 4xx will fail the same way until the client changes the request."}]},{"id":"ktor","title":"Your first Ktor server","tagline":"An engine, a module function, plugins, and a routing tree of handlers.","level":"beginner","remember":"Module installs plugins and routes. Handlers read from call and answer with call.respond.","terms":[{"term":"Ktor","def":"JetBrains’ Kotlin framework for building HTTP servers and clients, built on coroutines."},{"term":"Engine","def":"The part that listens on a port and speaks HTTP, such as Netty or CIO."},{"term":"Plugin (Ktor)","def":"A reusable piece installed with install(...) that adds behaviour to every call, like ContentNegotiation or StatusPages."},{"term":"call","def":"The current request and response pair inside a handler."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Routes: <code>route(\"/api\") { route(\"/articles\") { get(\"/{id}\") { } } }</code>. Which path reaches the handler?","options":["/articles/7","/api/articles/7","/api/{id}"],"a":1,"why":"Each nested route adds one path segment, so the full path is /api/articles/{id}."},{"q":"Which plugin turns <code>call.respond(article)</code> into a JSON body?","options":["StatusPages","ContentNegotiation with json()","Routing"],"a":1,"why":"ContentNegotiation picks a converter from the Accept header; json() registers kotlinx.serialization."},{"q":"A handler throws <code>ArticleNotFound</code>. What turns it into a 404?","options":["Ktor does it by name","A StatusPages exception&lt;ArticleNotFound&gt; handler","The client"],"a":1,"why":"StatusPages catches exceptions from handlers and lets you map each type to a status and body."},{"q":"Put these in the order Ktor uses them for one request.","options":["Engine receives → plugins and routing → handler → response","Handler → routing → engine → response","Routing → engine → plugins → handler"],"a":0,"why":"The engine accepts the connection, the pipeline runs plugins and resolves the route, your handler runs, and the response goes back out through the same pipeline."}]},{"id":"rest","title":"Designing a REST API","tagline":"Nouns for paths, methods for verbs, one error shape, and pages the app can load one by one.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Nouns in paths, a version prefix, one error shape, and every list paginated.","terms":[{"term":"Resource","def":"A thing the API exposes, named by a noun path such as /v1/articles/7."},{"term":"Cursor pagination","def":"Paging by a marker of the last item seen (“after id 42”) instead of a row offset."},{"term":"API versioning","def":"Keeping old contracts alive under one prefix (/v1) while new ones ship under another (/v2)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which path fits REST style for “comments on article 7”?","options":["GET /getCommentsForArticle?id=7","GET /v1/articles/7/comments","POST /v1/comments/list"],"a":1,"why":"A sub-collection under the parent resource, read with GET."},{"q":"Users scroll a feed while new articles arrive at the top. Offset paging shows duplicates. Why does a cursor fix it?","options":["Cursors are cached","The cursor marks “older than id 42”, so new rows at the top do not shift the next page","Cursors return fewer items"],"a":1,"why":"An offset counts rows from the top, which moves when rows are added. A cursor is anchored to an item."},{"q":"You need to rename <code>title</code> to <code>headline</code> in responses. Old app versions are still installed. What do you do?","options":["Rename it in /v1 and force-update","Add headline to /v2, keep /v1 returning title","Return both and remove title next week"],"a":1,"why":"Installed apps keep calling /v1 for a long time. Breaking changes go into a new version."},{"q":"Why give every error a stable <code>code</code> like <code>validation_failed</code> as well as a message?","options":["Messages are translated and may change; the code is what client logic can switch on","Codes are shorter","HTTP requires it"],"a":0,"why":"The app maps codes to behaviour and its own localized text. Messages are for people and logs."}]},{"id":"sql","title":"SQL with PostgreSQL","tagline":"Tables, the four statements you use daily, joins, indexes, and calling it all from Kotlin.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Filter and join on indexed columns. JDBC blocks, so run it on Dispatchers.IO.","terms":[{"term":"Primary key","def":"The column (or columns) that uniquely identifies each row."},{"term":"Foreign key","def":"A column whose value must match a primary key in another table, such as articles.user_id → users.id."},{"term":"Index","def":"A sorted lookup structure on columns that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table, at a small cost on writes."},{"term":"JOIN","def":"Combines rows from two tables where a condition matches, such as a.user_id = u.id."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The articles table has 2 million rows and no index on user_id. What does <code>WHERE user_id = 2</code> do?","options":["Reads only the matching rows","Reads every row to find the matches","Fails without an index"],"a":1,"why":"Without an index the only option is a sequential scan. An index on user_id lets it jump to the matches."},{"q":"You add an index to every column “to be safe”. What gets worse?","options":["Nothing","Every INSERT and UPDATE, because each index must be updated too","SELECT queries"],"a":1,"why":"Indexes speed up reads on the columns you search, but every write has to maintain every index. Add the ones your queries use."},{"q":"Which query lists each article with its author name?","options":["SELECT * FROM articles, users;","SELECT a.title, u.name FROM articles a JOIN users u ON a.user_id = u.id;","SELECT title, name FROM articles WHERE users;"],"a":1,"why":"The JOIN condition pairs each article with exactly its author. The first option pairs every article with every user."},{"q":"Why wrap Exposed’s blocking <code>transaction { }</code> in <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code> inside a handler?","options":["Exposed requires it to compile","JDBC blocks the thread; IO keeps the engine threads free for other requests","It makes the query faster"],"a":1,"why":"The query takes as long either way. The point is not to park one of the server’s request threads while it waits."}]},{"id":"auth","title":"Authentication on the server","tagline":"Hash passwords, hand out short access tokens and long refresh tokens, and verify Google sign-in on the server.","level":"advanced","remember":"Short access token, rotating refresh token in the database, slow password hash, and verify every third-party token on the server.","terms":[{"term":"Access token","def":"A short-lived credential sent on every API call, often a signed JWT."},{"term":"Refresh token","def":"A long-lived secret the app trades for a new access token. Stored hashed on the server so it can be revoked."},{"term":"JWT","def":"JSON Web Token: a base64url header, a payload of claims and a signature. Anyone can read it; only the key holder can create a valid one."},{"term":"Argon2id","def":"A slow, memory-hard password hashing algorithm. OWASP’s first choice for storing passwords."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The access token lasts 15 minutes. The user signs out at minute 2 and the server deletes the refresh token. A copy of the access token leaked earlier. How long can it be used?","options":["Not at all","Until it expires at minute 15","For 30 days"],"a":1,"why":"A JWT is checked by signature, not by a database lookup, so it works until exp. That is why access tokens are kept short."},{"q":"A refresh token that was already used is presented again. What should the server do?","options":["Issue a new pair, it is probably a retry","Revoke every token in that family and answer 401","Ignore it"],"a":1,"why":"With rotation, a reused token means two parties hold it. Revoking the family stops the attacker; the real user signs in again."},{"q":"Which order fits Google sign-in from an Android app?","options":["App gets ID token → sends to server → server verifies aud, iss, exp, signature → server issues its own tokens","App reads email from the ID token → sends email to server → server signs in by email","Server asks Google for the user’s password"],"a":0,"why":"The server must verify the token itself and then give the app your own access and refresh tokens."},{"q":"You need instant “sign out everywhere” and run on one server with a database. Which trade-off fits best?","options":["Server sessions: revocation is deleting rows","Long JWTs with no refresh token","Store the password on the phone"],"a":0,"why":"Sessions are checked on every request, so deleting them takes effect at once. JWTs trade that for lookup-free checks."},{"q":"Which is a reason to cap password length with bcrypt?","options":["bcrypt reads at most 72 bytes of input","Long passwords are insecure","The database column is small"],"a":0,"why":"Bytes after the 72nd are ignored by most bcrypt implementations, so a cap avoids surprising behaviour."}]},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing the server","tagline":"testApplication runs your whole module in memory, and a fresh database per test keeps results honest.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Test at the HTTP edge with testApplication. Every test gets its own clean data.","terms":[{"term":"testApplication","def":"Ktor’s test entry point. It runs your modules in memory and gives you a client wired straight into them."},{"term":"Flaky test","def":"A test that passes or fails without code changes, often because of shared state, timing or test order."},{"term":"Testcontainers","def":"A library that starts real services such as PostgreSQL in Docker for the length of a test run."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Test 4 passes alone but fails when the whole suite runs. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Ktor is slower in suites","Another test leaves rows in a shared database","The JIT compiler"],"a":1,"why":"Order-dependent results almost always mean shared state. Give each test clean data."},{"q":"What does testApplication avoid compared with starting a real server?","options":["Routing","Opening a real network port","Running plugins"],"a":1,"why":"The full pipeline runs, plugins and routes included, but requests go through memory instead of a socket."},{"q":"Your queries use a PostgreSQL-only feature. Where should repository tests run?","options":["H2 in-memory","A real PostgreSQL, for example via Testcontainers","Production"],"a":1,"why":"Only the same database engine proves the SQL works. H2 is fine for plain queries but differs in dialect."}]},{"id":"deploy","title":"Deploying","tagline":"Config from the environment, a fat JAR or image from the build, a host that checks health before it switches traffic.","level":"intermediate","remember":"One build, config from the environment, secrets never in git, health check before traffic.","terms":[{"term":"Fat JAR","def":"A single JAR that contains your code and all its dependencies, runnable with java -jar."},{"term":"Container image","def":"A packaged file system plus start command that a host can run the same way everywhere."},{"term":"Health check","def":"An endpoint the host calls to decide whether an instance is ready for traffic."},{"term":"Environment variable","def":"A named value set by the host for a process. The standard way to pass config and secrets to a server."}],"quiz":[{"q":"JWT_SECRET is missing on the host and the config has no default. What happens with a host that health-checks?","options":["v2 starts with an empty secret","v2 fails to start, never passes the health check, and v1 keeps serving","Both versions go down"],"a":1,"why":"The config cannot resolve <code>${JWT_SECRET}</code>, so the server fails at startup. The host never routes traffic to an instance that is not healthy."},{"q":"Which Gradle task from the Ktor plugin makes a single runnable JAR?","options":["buildFatJar","assembleRelease","shadowRun"],"a":0,"why":"buildFatJar writes build/libs/*-all.jar with every dependency inside."},{"q":"Put the deploy in order.","options":["tests → build artifact → image → deploy → health check → traffic","deploy → tests → health check → build","build → traffic → tests → health check"],"a":0,"why":"Nothing reaches users before tests pass and the new instance reports healthy."},{"q":"Where should the production database password live?","options":["application.conf in git","The host’s secret settings, read through an environment variable","A comment in the Dockerfile"],"a":1,"why":"Secrets stay out of the repository and the image, and can be rotated without a code change."}]},{"id":"client","title":"Connecting the Android app","tagline":"Share the models, call your own server, and turn every failure into a message the user understands.","level":"advanced","remember":"Shared DTOs, one HTTP client, and errors translated at every layer: status → exception → AppError → user text.","terms":[{"term":"DTO","def":"Data transfer object: a plain class that describes exactly what goes over the wire."},{"term":"Shared module","def":"A Kotlin Multiplatform module whose commonMain code is compiled into both the server and the app."},{"term":"expectSuccess","def":"Ktor client setting that makes non-2xx responses throw: ClientRequestException for 4xx, ServerResponseException for 5xx."},{"term":"Error mapping","def":"Translating a failure into the vocabulary of each layer, from HTTP status to domain error to user-facing text."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server answers 400 with <code>{\"code\":\"validation_failed\",\"fields\":{\"text\":\"must not be blank\"}}</code>. What should the screen show?","options":["“HTTP 400”","A message in the user’s language under the text field, from AppError.Invalid(fields)","Nothing, and retry"],"a":1,"why":"The field map tells the UI where the problem is. The user sees their language, not a status code."},{"q":"Where should <code>ArticleDto</code> live if you own both the server and the app?","options":["Copied into each project","In a shared KMP module both depend on","Only on the server"],"a":1,"why":"One definition means a rename or type change breaks the build instead of breaking users’ apps at runtime."},{"q":"With <code>expectSuccess = true</code>, the server returns 503. Which exception does the Ktor client throw?","options":["ClientRequestException","ServerResponseException","IOException"],"a":1,"why":"ServerResponseException covers 5xx. ClientRequestException is 4xx; IOException is a network failure with no response at all."},{"q":"The access token expired. With the Auth plugin’s bearer provider, what happens on the next call?","options":["The call fails and the user signs in again","The server answers 401, the plugin calls refreshTokens, then retries the call once with the new token","The plugin refreshes before every call"],"a":1,"why":"The bearer provider reacts to 401: it refreshes and replays the request. Only if refresh fails do you send the user to sign-in."},{"q":"Which line is the bug? <code>catch (e: Exception) { Result.failure(e) }</code> in a suspend function called from viewModelScope.","options":["It hides CancellationException, so cancelled work looks like a failure","Result cannot hold exceptions","Nothing is wrong"],"a":0,"why":"Cancellation is a normal signal in coroutines. Rethrow CancellationException before a broad catch."}]}]},{"id":"net","title":"Computer networks","description":"What happens between your app and a server, step by step: packets, addresses, DNS, TCP and UDP, HTTP, HTTPS, HTTP versions, speed and finding what broke.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/net.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-network","title":"What a network is","tagline":"Computers passing small packets of data, one asking and one answering.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what a network and a packet are","Tell the client from the server in a conversation","Ask a server for a page with <code>curl</code>"],"remember":"Data travels in small numbered packets. The client asks, the server answers.","terms":[{"term":"network","def":"Computers connected so they can send each other data. The internet is a network of networks."},{"term":"packet","def":"A small piece of data with a label saying where it goes and where it came from, usually with a number too."},{"term":"client","def":"The side that asks, such as an app or <code>curl</code>."},{"term":"server","def":"The side that waits for requests and answers them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada’s app asks for new messages, and a computer in a data centre (a building full of servers) sends them. Which one is the server?","options":["Ada’s app","The computer in the data centre","Both are servers"],"a":1,"why":"The server is the side that waits and answers. The app that asks is the client."},{"q":"Why do most packets carry a number?","options":["So the receiver can put the pieces back in order","To make packets travel faster","To count how much the data costs"],"a":0,"why":"Packets can arrive in any order. The numbers let the receiver rebuild the original data."},{"q":"Can a laptop be a server?","options":["No, servers are special machines","Yes: any computer that waits for requests and answers them is a server"],"a":1,"why":"Client and server are roles in a conversation, not kinds of machines."}]},{"id":"ip","title":"Addresses and routing","tagline":"Every device has an address, and routers pass packets along hop by hop.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read an IP address","Explain what a router does with a packet","List the hops to a server with <code>traceroute</code>"],"remember":"Every device has an IP address. Routers pass each packet one hop closer to its destination.","terms":[{"term":"IP address","def":"The number that identifies a device on a network, like <code>192.168.1.23</code>."},{"term":"router","def":"A device that passes packets on toward their destination. Your Wi-Fi box is one."},{"term":"hop","def":"One step of a packet’s trip. <code>traceroute</code> prints one line per hop, naming the router (or server) reached."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a router do with a packet?","options":["Reads the data inside and answers it","Looks at the destination address and passes it one hop closer","Stores it until the server asks for it"],"a":1,"why":"A router is a signpost: it only needs the destination address to choose the next hop."},{"q":"A traceroute ends with hop 12 being the server. How many routers did the packets pass before the server?","options":["11","12","13"],"a":0,"why":"Hops 1 to 11 are routers; hop 12 is the server itself."},{"q":"Which command lists the hops on Windows?","options":["<code>traceroute</code>","<code>tracert</code>","<code>curl</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Windows names it <code>tracert</code>. macOS and Linux use <code>traceroute</code>."}]},{"id":"dns","title":"Names to addresses","tagline":"DNS turns a name like example.com into an IP address, and remembers it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a DNS lookup does","Look up a name with <code>nslookup</code>","Say why the second lookup is faster"],"remember":"DNS turns a name into an IP address. Answers are cached for TTL seconds, so repeats are fast.","terms":[{"term":"DNS, DNS lookup","def":"The Domain Name System. A lookup asks it for the IP address that belongs to a name."},{"term":"resolver","def":"The DNS server your device asks, usually run by your router or internet provider."},{"term":"cache, TTL","def":"A cache is a short-term memory of answers. The TTL says how many seconds an answer may stay in it."},{"term":"NXDOMAIN","def":"The DNS answer for a name that does not exist."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An answer comes with a TTL of 300. What does that mean?","options":["The server is 300 km away","The answer may be kept in a cache for 300 seconds","The lookup took 300 ms"],"a":1,"why":"TTL means time to live: how long a cache may keep the answer before asking again."},{"q":"<code>nslookup</code> prints <code>NXDOMAIN</code>. What happened?","options":["The name does not exist","The server is down","The internet connection is broken"],"a":0,"why":"DNS answered, and the answer was “no such name”. Check the spelling."},{"q":"Why does a DNS lookup happen before the app sends its request?","options":["Packets need an IP address, not a name","To check the password","To make the request smaller"],"a":0,"why":"Routers only understand IP addresses, so the name must be turned into one first."}]},{"id":"tcp-udp","title":"TCP and UDP","tagline":"Two ways to send packets: carefully and complete, or fast and fire-and-forget.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what TCP adds: a connection, order and resending","Say when UDP is the better choice","Read the port number in an address"],"remember":"TCP: a connection, every packet confirmed and in order. UDP: sent once, fast. A port picks the program.","terms":[{"term":"TCP","def":"A way of sending that opens a connection, confirms every packet and resends lost ones."},{"term":"UDP","def":"A way of sending without a connection: each packet is sent once, nothing is resent."},{"term":"connection","def":"An agreement between two sides to exchange data, opened by a handshake."},{"term":"port","def":"A number that picks a program on a computer, such as 443 for secure web pages."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A video call loses one packet. Which way of sending fits a call better?","options":["TCP: wait until the packet is sent again","UDP: skip it and keep the call going"],"a":1,"why":"For live audio and video, late data is useless. UDP keeps things moving."},{"q":"What does a port number choose?","options":["The computer","The program on that computer","The router"],"a":1,"why":"The IP address finds the computer; the port finds the program on it."},{"q":"Why does TCP cost a little time before the first data moves?","options":["The handshake must go there and back first","TCP packets are bigger","The server must restart"],"a":0,"why":"The connection opens with a handshake, one trip there and back, before any data is sent."}]},{"id":"http","title":"HTTP","tagline":"The request and the response: how apps and servers talk.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the parts of an HTTP request and response","Read status codes like 200 and 404","Send a GET and a POST with <code>curl</code>"],"remember":"A request has a method, a path and headers. A response has a status code, headers and a body.","terms":[{"term":"HTTP, request, response","def":"HTTP is the language of the web. The client sends a request, the server sends back a response."},{"term":"method","def":"The first word of a request: <code>GET</code> to read, <code>POST</code> to send something new."},{"term":"status code","def":"The number at the start of a response: 2xx success, 4xx mistake in the request, 5xx problem on the server."},{"term":"header","def":"A line of extra information, <code>Name: value</code>, such as <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A response starts with <code>HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error</code>. Whose problem is it?","options":["The request was wrong","Something broke on the server","The network is down"],"a":1,"why":"5xx codes mean the server failed. The network worked: an answer came back."},{"q":"Which method fits “show me today’s weather”?","options":["<code>GET</code>","<code>POST</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Reading something is a GET. POST sends something new."},{"q":"What is <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>?","options":["A header","A status code","The body"],"a":0,"why":"It has the form <code>Name: value</code>, so it is a header. It describes the body."}]},{"id":"tls","title":"HTTPS and TLS","tagline":"Locking the conversation so only the two ends can read it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what encryption and a certificate do","Follow a TLS handshake in <code>curl -v</code>","Recognise certificate errors"],"remember":"HTTPS = HTTP inside TLS: encrypted, and the certificate proves who the server is.","terms":[{"term":"TLS, HTTPS","def":"TLS encrypts a connection and checks who the server is. HTTPS is HTTP sent inside TLS."},{"term":"encryption","def":"Scrambling data so only someone with the key can read it."},{"term":"certificate","def":"A server’s digital ID card: its name, an expiry date, and the signature of a trusted certificate authority."},{"term":"handshake","def":"The opening messages of a connection. In TLS they agree on keys and check the certificate."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada uses HTTPS on café Wi-Fi. What can the café’s router see?","options":["Everything, including her messages","Which server she talks to, but not what she sends or receives","Nothing at all"],"a":1,"why":"The router must see the server’s address to pass the packets on, but the contents are encrypted."},{"q":"What does a certificate prove?","options":["That the server is fast","That the server really is the name you asked for","That the data has no viruses"],"a":1,"why":"It is an ID card signed by someone your device trusts."},{"q":"When does the TLS handshake happen?","options":["After the TCP connection opens, before any HTTP","After the HTTP response","Before the DNS lookup"],"a":0,"why":"Order: DNS, TCP, TLS, then HTTP inside the encrypted connection."}]},{"id":"http-versions","title":"HTTP/1.1, 2 and 3","tagline":"Same requests, faster delivery: sharing one connection, then moving to QUIC.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why HTTP/1.1 makes requests wait in line","Describe multiplexing in HTTP/2","Say what HTTP/3 and QUIC change"],"remember":"HTTP/1.1: one request at a time per connection. HTTP/2: many at once on one TCP connection. HTTP/3: the same on QUIC.","terms":[{"term":"multiplexing","def":"Sending many requests and answers at the same time over one connection, each in its own stream."},{"term":"HTTP/2","def":"The HTTP version that multiplexes requests over a single TCP connection."},{"term":"HTTP/3, QUIC","def":"HTTP/3 runs on QUIC, a protocol built on UDP that resends lost packets per stream and sets up faster."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does multiplexing mean in HTTP/2?","options":["Many requests at once over one connection","Sending each request twice","Opening one connection per image"],"a":0,"why":"Each request gets its own stream, and all streams share the connection."},{"q":"HTTP/3 runs on which protocol?","options":["TCP","QUIC, built on UDP","DNS"],"a":1,"why":"QUIC brings its own resending per stream, on top of UDP."},{"q":"The server switches from HTTP/1.1 to HTTP/2. What changes in the requests you write?","options":["Every method gets a new name","Nothing: methods, headers and status codes stay the same","You must add a version header to each request"],"a":1,"why":"The version changes how requests travel, not what they mean."}]},{"id":"latency","title":"Latency and bandwidth","tagline":"Why a fast connection can still feel slow: round trips.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell latency from bandwidth","Measure a round trip with <code>ping</code>","Count the round trips before a first response"],"remember":"Bandwidth is how much per second; latency is how long each trip takes. Small requests wait on round trips.","terms":[{"term":"bandwidth","def":"How much data a connection can carry per second."},{"term":"latency","def":"How long data takes to travel from one end to the other."},{"term":"round trip, RTT","def":"A trip there and back. The RTT is its time, measured by <code>ping</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An app makes 30 tiny requests, one after another, on mobile data. What slows it down most?","options":["Bandwidth","Latency: 30 round trips","The size of the images"],"a":1,"why":"Tiny requests carry little data. The time goes into waiting for 30 trips there and back."},{"q":"What does <code>ping</code> measure?","options":["Bandwidth","Round-trip time","The number of hops"],"a":1,"why":"It times a tiny packet there and back."},{"q":"Why is a second request on an open connection faster than the first?","options":["It skips the DNS, TCP and TLS round trips","The server remembers the answer","It uses UDP"],"a":0,"why":"The connection is already set up, so only the request’s own round trip is left."}]},{"id":"debugging","title":"Seeing network traffic","tagline":"When a request fails, find out which step broke.","level":"beginner","goals":["Match a curl error to the step that failed","Tell a network failure from an HTTP error status","Explain what a debugging proxy shows"],"remember":"Find the failing step: DNS (6), TCP (7, 28), TLS (60). A status code means the server answered.","terms":[{"term":"timeout","def":"Giving up after waiting a set time for an answer that never came."},{"term":"proxy","def":"A program in the middle that requests pass through. A debugging proxy records them so you can read them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"curl prints <code>HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error</code>. Did the network work?","options":["No, the connection failed","Yes: DNS, TCP and TLS worked, and the server answered with an error"],"a":1,"why":"Any status code means a response arrived. The problem is inside the server."},{"q":"What does a debugging proxy do?","options":["Makes requests faster","Sits in the middle and records each request and response","Fixes broken certificates"],"a":1,"why":"All traffic passes through it, so you can read every request and response."},{"q":"<code>curl http://localhost:8080</code> gives error 7. What is the likely cause?","options":["A typo in the name","Nothing is running on port 8080","An expired certificate"],"a":1,"why":"<code>localhost</code> always resolves, and plain <code>http://</code> has no certificate. The computer refused the connection: no program listens on that port."}]}]},{"id":"os","title":"Operating systems","description":"What happens inside your phone when apps run: how it shares out time, memory, files and battery between them, and why apps freeze or close. Tried with simple terminal commands, then linked to Android.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/os.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-os","title":"What an operating system does","tagline":"The program that runs all the other programs.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an operating system does for the apps on your phone","Explain what the kernel is","Find out which kernel a computer runs with <code>uname</code>"],"remember":"Apps never touch the hardware. They ask the operating system, and its kernel does the work.","terms":[{"term":"operating system (OS)","def":"The program that runs all the other programs and shares the hardware between them, such as Android, Windows or Linux."},{"term":"kernel","def":"The heart of the OS: it controls the hardware and decides which program may do what. Android uses the Linux kernel."},{"term":"hardware","def":"The physical parts of a device: CPU, memory, storage, screen, camera."},{"term":"hardware abstraction, driver","def":"Apps make simple requests like “save this file”, and the OS turns them into work on the real parts. A driver is the code that knows one exact part."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A music app wants to play a sound. How does it reach the speaker?","options":["It asks the OS, which uses the speaker’s driver","It sends electricity to the speaker itself","It needs a different version for every phone model"],"a":0,"why":"Hardware abstraction: the app asks, and the OS and its driver handle the real part."},{"q":"Which part of the OS controls the hardware?","options":["The home screen","The kernel","The settings app"],"a":1,"why":"The home screen and settings are programs on top of the kernel."},{"q":"Android is built on which kernel?","options":["Windows","Linux","Darwin"],"a":1,"why":"Android uses the Linux kernel. Darwin is the kernel of macOS."}]},{"id":"processes","title":"Processes","tagline":"A running program, in its own walled-off space.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell a program from a process","Explain why one app crashing doesn’t crash the others","List running processes with <code>ps</code>"],"remember":"A process is a running program with its own memory and PID. A crash ends only that process.","terms":[{"term":"process","def":"A program that is running, with its own memory. Opening an app starts one."},{"term":"PID (process ID)","def":"The number the OS gives each process, such as <code>4821</code>."},{"term":"memory isolation","def":"The wall the OS builds between processes: one can’t read or change another one’s memory."},{"term":"process lifecycle","def":"The life of a process: created, running, often waiting, and finally ended."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a program and a process?","options":["A process is a program that is running","They are two words for the same thing","A program is a process that has crashed"],"a":0,"why":"The program is the stored file of instructions. Running it starts a process."},{"q":"A game crashes while a map app is open. What happens to the map app?","options":["It crashes too","It keeps running: each process has its own memory","It loses everything in its memory"],"a":1,"why":"Memory isolation keeps a crash inside the process where it happened."},{"q":"You close Music and open it again. Does the new process have the same PID?","options":["Yes, always","No: the OS starts a new process, with a new PID"],"a":1,"why":"Every start is a new process, with a new number and empty memory."}]},{"id":"threads","title":"Threads","tagline":"Several jobs at once inside one process.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a thread is","Say why an app freezes when its main thread is busy","List the threads of a process with <code>ps -T</code>"],"remember":"A thread is one line of work inside a process. Threads of a process share its memory; a busy main thread freezes the app.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One line of work inside a process, running instructions one after another. A process can have many."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The thread an app starts with. It draws the screen and answers taps, so slow work belongs elsewhere."},{"term":"SPID","def":"The number of one thread, shown by <code>ps -T</code>. The PID says which process it belongs to."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do threads of the same process share?","options":["Its memory","Nothing: they are walled off like processes","Only the screen"],"a":0,"why":"Memory isolation is between processes. Threads inside one process share its memory."},{"q":"An app freezes for a few seconds every time you tap Save. What is the likely cause?","options":["The main thread is doing slow work itself","The app has too many threads","The phone has no kernel"],"a":0,"why":"While the main thread works, it can’t draw or answer taps."},{"q":"In <code>ps -T</code> output, every row shows PID 5996. What does that tell you?","options":["They are threads of one process","They are separate processes","The command failed"],"a":0,"why":"The PID names the process. The SPID column tells the threads apart."}]},{"id":"scheduling","title":"Sharing the CPU","tagline":"Hundreds of threads, a few cores: the OS takes turns.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain how a few CPU cores run hundreds of threads","Describe a time slice and a context switch","Count cores with <code>nproc</code> and watch processes with <code>top</code>"],"remember":"A core runs one thread at a time. The scheduler gives out short time slices and switches so fast that everything seems to run at once.","terms":[{"term":"CPU core","def":"One part of the processor that runs one thread at a time. Phones have several."},{"term":"scheduler","def":"The part of the kernel that picks which thread runs next on each core."},{"term":"time slice","def":"The few milliseconds a thread may run before the scheduler gives another one a turn."},{"term":"context switch","def":"Pausing one thread and running another. The kernel saves where the first one was, so it can continue later."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the scheduler decide?","options":["Which thread runs next on each core","Which files an app may open","How bright the screen is"],"a":0,"why":"It hands out time slices on the CPU cores."},{"q":"<code>top</code> says 311 of 312 processes are sleeping. What are they doing?","options":["Waiting for something, without using the CPU","They are broken and need restarting","Running in secret"],"a":0,"why":"A sleeping process waits for a tap, a timer or data. It needs no turns until then."},{"q":"What happens in a context switch?","options":["The kernel saves where one thread was and runs another","The app starts again from the beginning","The phone switches to another network"],"a":0,"why":"Saving the paused thread’s place lets it continue later as if nothing happened."}]},{"id":"memory","title":"Memory","tagline":"Where a running program keeps its values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what RAM is and how it differs from storage","Tell the stack from the heap","Check how much RAM is free with <code>free -h</code>"],"remember":"RAM is fast working memory that is wiped when the power goes. Function values live on the stack, objects on the heap.","terms":[{"term":"RAM","def":"Fast working memory where running programs keep their values. It is wiped when the device switches off."},{"term":"stack","def":"Memory for the functions running right now: each call adds a frame with its local values, and returning removes it."},{"term":"heap","def":"Memory for objects such as lists, which can outlive the function that made them."},{"term":"virtual memory","def":"The private addresses each process sees. The kernel maps them to real places in RAM."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You switch the phone off and on again. What is gone?","options":["What was in RAM","Your photos in storage","The installed apps"],"a":0,"why":"RAM needs power to keep its contents. Storage keeps files without power."},{"q":"A function makes a local <code>val</code>, then returns. Where did the value live, and what happens to it?","options":["On the stack; it is removed when the function returns","On the heap; it stays forever","In storage; it is saved as a file"],"a":0,"why":"Local values live in the function’s stack frame, which goes away on return."},{"q":"Why can’t process A read process B’s memory?","options":["A’s virtual addresses don’t lead into B’s memory","B hides its memory with a password","RAM is split into two halves"],"a":0,"why":"The kernel maps each process’s addresses only to that process’s own places in RAM."}]},{"id":"low-memory","title":"When memory runs out","tagline":"Phones close apps you aren’t using to make room.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what happens when RAM is full","Predict which app a phone closes first","End a process yourself with <code>kill</code>"],"remember":"When RAM runs out, a phone kills the processes you are least likely to miss. A killed app starts over with empty memory.","terms":[{"term":"out of memory","def":"When programs need more RAM than is free."},{"term":"kill (a process)","def":"End a process on purpose. Phones do it to free RAM; in a terminal, <code>kill</code> and a PID does it."},{"term":"background","def":"An app is in the background when it is open but not on the screen, like music playing while you read."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You come back to an app and it opens on its first screen. What probably happened?","options":["The phone killed its process to free RAM","The app was deleted","The storage is full"],"a":0,"why":"A killed app starts again as a new process, with empty memory."},{"q":"Which app is the phone least likely to kill?","options":["A music app playing in the background","A chat app you left yesterday","A map you left an hour ago"],"a":0,"why":"You would notice the music stopping, so it is kept."},{"q":"What does <code>kill 6120</code> do?","options":["Ends the process with PID 6120","Deletes the file named 6120","Frees 6120 MB of RAM"],"a":0,"why":"<code>kill</code> takes a PID."}]},{"id":"ipc","title":"Processes talking","tagline":"Walled-off processes still pass messages.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why processes need the kernel to talk","Describe how a phone app asks the system to show a notification","Connect two commands with a pipe <code>|</code>"],"remember":"Processes talk by passing messages through the kernel. In the terminal, a pipe | passes one command’s output to the next.","terms":[{"term":"inter-process communication (IPC)","def":"Processes passing data to each other through the kernel, such as an app asking the system to show a notification."},{"term":"pipe","def":"The <code>|</code> between two commands: the kernel passes what the first prints into the second."},{"term":"wc","def":"Counts what it is given. <code>wc -l</code> counts lines."},{"term":"grep","def":"Keeps only the lines that contain a word, such as <code>grep sleep</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a phone app ask the system to show a notification, instead of drawing it itself?","options":["The top bar belongs to another process, the system","Apps can’t draw anything","Notifications are stored in a file"],"a":0,"why":"Memory isolation: only the system process may change what it owns, so the app sends it a request."},{"q":"In <code>ps -e | wc -l</code>, what does <code>|</code> do?","options":["Passes what <code>ps -e</code> prints into <code>wc</code>","Runs <code>ps</code>, then deletes its output","Means “or”, like <code>||</code> in Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"A pipe connects two processes: the output of the first becomes the input of the second."},{"q":"Who carries a message from one process to another?","options":["The kernel","The screen","Nobody: processes read each other’s memory"],"a":0,"why":"Only the kernel can reach both sides of the wall."}]},{"id":"files","title":"Files and storage","tagline":"How the OS keeps files in folders, and who may open them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a file system does","Tell an absolute path from a relative one","Read permissions like <code>-rw-r--r--</code> in <code>ls -l</code>"],"remember":"The file system keeps files in folders. Every file has an owner and permissions (r, w, x), and the kernel checks them.","terms":[{"term":"file system","def":"The part of the OS that turns storage into named files inside folders."},{"term":"absolute path, relative path","def":"An absolute path starts at <code>/</code> and works anywhere. A relative path, like <code>notes.txt</code>, starts from the folder you are in."},{"term":"permissions","def":"Who may read (<code>r</code>), write (<code>w</code>) or run (<code>x</code>) a file. <code>ls -l</code> shows them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is an absolute path?","options":["<code>/home/ada/notes.txt</code>","<code>notes.txt</code>","<code>ada/notes.txt</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An absolute path starts at the top, <code>/</code>."},{"q":"A file shows <code>-rw-r--r--</code>. What may other users do with it?","options":["Only read it","Read and change it","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"The last group, <code>r--</code>, is for everyone else: read, but no write."},{"q":"Why can’t one phone app open another app’s files?","options":["Each app has its own user and a private folder, and the kernel checks permissions","Files are hidden behind the screen","Apps can’t use storage"],"a":0,"why":"It is the same owner-and-permissions idea as on a shared computer."}]},{"id":"power","title":"Power and battery","tagline":"Why a phone sleeps, and why it limits apps that keep it awake.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why waiting costs almost no battery","Describe what a wake lock does","Say why phones limit apps in the background"],"remember":"A waiting CPU sleeps and saves power. A wake lock keeps it awake, so Android limits apps in the background.","terms":[{"term":"sleep state","def":"A low-power mode the CPU enters when nothing needs to run. It wakes up when something happens."},{"term":"wake lock","def":"An app’s request to the OS: “don’t let the CPU sleep yet”. Forgotten ones drain the battery."},{"term":"Doze","def":"Android’s mode for a phone left still with its screen off: background work waits for short, shared windows."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a sleeping CPU save battery?","options":["It uses very little power until something wakes it","It charges the battery","It closes all the apps"],"a":0,"why":"A sleep state is a low-power mode that ends when an event arrives."},{"q":"What does a wake lock ask the OS?","options":["Don’t let the CPU sleep yet","Lock the screen","Wake me up at 07:00"],"a":0,"why":"It keeps the CPU awake so the app can finish a job."},{"q":"<code>time sleep 2</code> shows <code>real 0m2.003s</code> and <code>user 0m0.002s</code>. What does that tell you?","options":["Waiting took real time but almost no CPU work","The computer is broken","<code>sleep</code> ran for only 2 milliseconds"],"a":0,"why":"A waiting process doesn’t use the CPU, which is why waiting is cheap."}]}]},{"id":"sysdesign","title":"Mobile system design","description":"How to design mobile systems, step by step: APIs, pagination, caching, offline sync, real-time, push at scale, media and reliability, with two full walkthroughs.","requires":["arch","data"],"file":"tracks/sysdesign.js","lessons":[{"id":"interview","title":"How to approach a system design","tagline":"Turn a vague prompt into a scoped design: clarify, estimate, sketch, deep-dive, weigh trade-offs.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Clarify, estimate, sketch, deep-dive, trade-offs. Say every assumption out loud, and put most of the depth into the client.","terms":[{"term":"Functional requirement","def":"What the product does: show a feed, open an article, bookmark it, receive breaking news."},{"term":"Non-functional requirement","def":"How well it must do it: works offline, opens fast, light on battery and data, handles 1M users."},{"term":"Back-of-the-envelope estimate","def":"A rough calculation of load (requests per second, bytes per day) used to pick the design, not to be precise."},{"term":"Deep dive","def":"The part of the design where you go into detail on one hard piece, such as sync or caching."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The prompt is \"Design a news app.\" What do you do in the first five minutes?","options":["Draw a load balancer, three servers and a database","Ask about scope, users, offline needs and platforms, and write the answers down","Write the Retrofit interface","Pick between SQL and NoSQL"],"a":1,"why":"Every later decision depends on the requirements. Asking first means you scope before you build."},{"q":"Which order fits a 45-minute design session?","options":["Sketch → Clarify → Deep dive → Estimate","Clarify → Estimate → Sketch → Deep dive → Trade-offs","Estimate → Deep dive → Clarify → Sketch","Deep dive → Sketch → Trade-offs → Clarify"],"a":1,"why":"Requirements decide what to estimate, the numbers shape the sketch, and the sketch shows which part deserves a deep dive."},{"q":"A stakeholder adds: \"People read on the metro with no signal.\" Which part of your design changes most?","options":["The CDN provider","The client: a local database as the source of truth, plus a sync job","The server language","The load balancer"],"a":1,"why":"Offline reading is a client problem. The screen must read from Room and the network only refreshes it."},{"q":"2M daily users each make 5 requests a day. About how many requests per second on average?","options":["About 12","About 116","About 1,160","About 10,000"],"a":1,"why":"2M × 5 = 10M requests a day. 10M / 86,400 is about 116 per second."}]},{"id":"api","title":"API design for mobile","tagline":"Design for screens, slow networks, and app versions you can't force to update.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Old app versions live for months. Only make additive changes, and parse leniently: ignore unknown keys, default everything optional.","terms":[{"term":"Backwards compatibility","def":"A new server response still works for app versions built before the change."},{"term":"Additive change","def":"Adding a new optional field or endpoint without removing or renaming anything. Safe for old clients that parse leniently."},{"term":"BFF (backend for frontend)","def":"A thin server layer that gives each screen exactly the data it needs in one call, built from several internal services."},{"term":"Protocol Buffers","def":"Google's binary data format used by gRPC. Smaller and faster to parse than JSON, with code generated from a schema."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server adds a field <code>liveScore</code> to every article. Your 3.0 app was built with plain <code>Json</code> and default settings. What happens?","options":["It ignores the field","Parsing throws for every article that has it","It shows the score"],"a":1,"why":"By default kotlinx.serialization rejects unknown keys. That is why <code>ignoreUnknownKeys = true</code> belongs in every mobile client."},{"q":"Which change is safe without a new API version?","options":["Renaming title to headline","Adding an optional field that clients default","Changing id from a number to a string","Removing a field old apps read"],"a":1,"why":"Adding is safe for lenient parsers. Renames, type changes and removals break versions already installed."},{"q":"On 3G, the article screen needs data from four internal services. What fits best?","options":["Four REST calls from the app, one after another","One screen-shaped endpoint (a BFF) or one GraphQL query","One WebSocket per service"],"a":1,"why":"Each round trip on a slow network can cost hundreds of milliseconds. Joining the data on the server sends one request instead of four."},{"q":"Why might a team keep the article endpoint as REST GET instead of moving it to GraphQL?","options":["GraphQL can't return JSON","GET URLs cache naturally at the CDN and in OkHttp","REST payloads are always smaller"],"a":1,"why":"A plain GET with Cache-Control is cached by every layer. GraphQL can be cached too, but it takes extra work like persisted queries sent as GET."}]},{"id":"pagination","title":"Pagination: offset vs cursor","tagline":"Load a live feed in pages without duplicates or gaps when new items arrive.","level":"intermediate","remember":"A live feed needs cursors: \"items after this one\", not \"skip N\". Offsets repeat or skip items when the list changes.","terms":[{"term":"Offset pagination","def":"Paging by position: skip N items, take M. Simple, allows page jumps, breaks when items are added or removed above."},{"term":"Cursor (keyset) pagination","def":"Paging by position in the sort order: take M items after this key. Stable under inserts and fast on an index."},{"term":"Opaque cursor","def":"A cursor the client treats as a plain string and never parses, so the server can change what is inside."},{"term":"Prefetch distance","def":"How many items from the end of the loaded list Paging starts fetching the next page."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You loaded 20 items with offset pagination. Two articles are published. You request <code>offset=20&amp;limit=20</code>. What do you get?","options":["20 new items, no repeats","Two items at the start repeat items you already have","Two items are skipped"],"a":1,"why":"The two new articles pushed everything down two places. The first two items after offset 20 are the last two items of the page you already have."},{"q":"With offset pagination, one article above your position is deleted before you load the next page. What happens?","options":["A duplicate appears","One item is skipped and never shown","Nothing changes"],"a":1,"why":"Everything shifts up one place, so the item that moved into position 19 is never requested."},{"q":"Why does a good cursor contain the id as well as <code>published_at</code>?","options":["To make the URL longer","Two articles can share a timestamp, and the id breaks the tie so none is skipped or repeated","Ids sort faster than timestamps"],"a":1,"why":"With \"older than this time\" alone, an article published in the same second as the cursor item falls through the gap. <code>(published_at, id)</code> is unique."}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching layers end to end","tagline":"Memory, disk, CDN, server cache: skip as many hops as you can, and know how each copy goes stale.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Every cache answers faster and goes stale. You can purge the CDN and the server, never the phones: choose max-age with that in mind.","terms":[{"term":"max-age","def":"A Cache-Control directive: how many seconds a response stays fresh and can be reused without asking the server."},{"term":"ETag","def":"A version tag for a response. The client sends it back in If-None-Match to ask \"has this changed?\""},{"term":"304 Not Modified","def":"The server's answer when the client's copy is still current. No body, so almost no bytes."},{"term":"CDN","def":"Content delivery network: cache servers close to users that answer repeated requests without reaching your servers."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The article came with <code>max-age=300</code>. The user opens it again 2 minutes later. What happens?","options":["A network request with If-None-Match","It is served from the disk cache, no network","It is served from the CDN"],"a":1,"why":"The copy is still fresh (120 s < 300 s), so OkHttp answers from disk without any request."},{"q":"Ten minutes later, the article has not changed. What does the network carry?","options":["A 200 with the full body","A 304 with no body; the stored copy is reused","Nothing; the app shows an error"],"a":1,"why":"The copy is stale, so OkHttp revalidates with the ETag. The server sees it still matches and sends 304."},{"q":"An editor fixes a headline and you purge the CDN. A phone fetched the article a minute ago with <code>max-age=300</code>. What does it show?","options":["The fix, at once","The old headline for up to 4 more minutes","An error"],"a":1,"why":"The phone's copy is fresh by its own rules and won't ask the server until max-age passes. Purges don't reach devices."},{"q":"Which header fits <code>GET /v1/me</code>, the signed-in user's profile?","options":["public, max-age=3600","private, no-cache","public, immutable"],"a":1,"why":"<code>private</code> keeps it out of the CDN, so one user's data is never served to another. <code>no-cache</code> means revalidate each time."},{"q":"An image URL contains a hash of its content, so a new image always gets a new URL. Which header?","options":["no-store","public, max-age=31536000, immutable","private, no-cache"],"a":1,"why":"The bytes behind that URL can never change, so every layer can keep it for a year. Updating means publishing a new URL."}]},{"id":"offline","title":"Offline-first and sync","tagline":"Read and write locally, queue changes in an outbox, and make retries safe with idempotency keys.","level":"advanced","remember":"Write to Room and the outbox in one transaction, sync later, and send the same idempotency key on every retry.","terms":[{"term":"Outbox","def":"A local table of changes not yet confirmed by the server. A sync job sends them in order and deletes each on success."},{"term":"Idempotency key","def":"A unique id sent with a write so the server can recognise a repeat and apply it only once."},{"term":"Last-write-wins","def":"A conflict rule: when two versions collide, keep the one written last. Simple, but it can drop the other side's changes."},{"term":"CRDT","def":"Conflict-free replicated data type: a data shape whose copies always merge to the same result, whatever order the changes arrive in."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user bookmarks an article with no connection. What should the screen show?","options":["An error: \"No connection\"","The bookmark at once, marked as pending","Nothing until the server confirms"],"a":1,"why":"The write goes to Room first, so the UI updates straight away. A small pending mark is honest about the sync."},{"q":"A request reached the server and was applied, but the response was lost. The worker retries with the same key. What should the server do?","options":["Apply it again","Return the first result without applying it again","Return 500 so the client stops"],"a":1,"why":"That is the point of the key: the server stored the result under it and replays the result for any repeat."},{"q":"Which order is right for a lazy write?","options":["Send to server → write Room → add outbox row","Write Room and the outbox row in one transaction → enqueue the worker → worker sends with the key → delete the row on success","Enqueue the worker → write Room → send without a key","Add an outbox row → delete it → write Room"],"a":1,"why":"Local first, in one transaction so a crash can't leave half a change. The row is deleted only after the server confirms."},{"q":"Last-write-wins on whole records. The phone edits a note's title offline; the tablet edits its tags and syncs later. What happens?","options":["Both edits are kept","The phone's title edit is lost, because the tablet's whole record replaced it","The server rejects both"],"a":1,"why":"Whole-record LWW keeps one version. Merging per field, or using operations instead of snapshots, keeps both."},{"q":"Why use server-side version numbers rather than phone clocks to decide which write is newer?","options":["Phone clocks can be wrong or changed by the user","Numbers take less space","Android has no clock API"],"a":0,"why":"A phone set five minutes ahead would win every conflict. A version the server increments has one order everyone agrees on."}]},{"id":"realtime","title":"Real-time updates","tagline":"Polling, long polling, SSE, WebSockets and push: freshness against battery and server cost.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Hold a socket only while the user is looking. In the background, let FCM push wake you.","terms":[{"term":"Long polling","def":"A request the server keeps open until it has news or a timeout passes. The client then sends the next one."},{"term":"Server-Sent Events","def":"A long-lived HTTP response in which the server writes a stream of text events. One way only, server to client."},{"term":"WebSocket","def":"A two-way connection that starts as HTTP and stays open, so both sides can send messages at any time (RFC 6455)."},{"term":"Heartbeat","def":"A small ping sent on an idle connection so both sides, and the networks between them, know it is still alive."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app polls every 10 seconds. A goal happens at second 11. When does the user see it?","options":["At second 11","At second 20, the next poll","At second 30"],"a":1,"why":"Polling delivers on its own schedule. The worst-case delay is the full interval, and most polls return nothing."},{"q":"The match screen shows a live score while it is open. What fits best?","options":["Only FCM push for every event","A WebSocket or SSE stream while the screen is visible, and push when the app is in the background","Poll every second"],"a":1,"why":"A stream gives instant updates while the user watches. Push covers the background without holding a connection."},{"q":"The client must also send \"typing…\" events to the server over the same connection. Which one?","options":["Server-Sent Events","WebSocket","Polling"],"a":1,"why":"SSE only carries server-to-client events. A WebSocket is two-way."},{"q":"What happens to your app's own socket when the device enters Doze?","options":["It keeps working as usual","App network access is suspended so it stalls; high-priority FCM messages can still wake the app","It switches to SSE"],"a":1,"why":"Doze defers app network access to save battery. FCM high-priority messages are the supported way to reach the app then."}]},{"id":"push-scale","title":"Push notifications at scale","tagline":"One event, a million phones: fan-out, topics, collapse keys, TTL and stale tokens.","level":"advanced","remember":"Push is best effort. Set a TTL that matches how long the news matters, collapse what supersedes itself, and prune dead tokens.","terms":[{"term":"Fan-out","def":"Turning one event into many deliveries, one per recipient device."},{"term":"Topic","def":"A named group in FCM that installs subscribe to. One send reaches every subscriber."},{"term":"Collapse key","def":"A label on a message. For an offline device, a newer message with the same key replaces an older undelivered one."},{"term":"TTL (time to live)","def":"How long FCM keeps trying to deliver a message to an offline device before dropping it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone is offline while three score updates are sent with the same collapse key. What does it get when it reconnects?","options":["All three, in order","Only the newest","None of them"],"a":1,"why":"For a disconnected device, FCM keeps only the last message per collapse key."},{"q":"A breaking-news alert is sent with the default TTL. A phone is switched off for three days. What happens?","options":["It never gets the alert","It gets a three-day-old alert when it turns on","It gets it one hour later"],"a":1,"why":"The default TTL is four weeks, so FCM delivers it late. For time-sensitive alerts, set a short TTL."},{"q":"FCM returns <code>UNREGISTERED</code> for a token. What should the server do?","options":["Retry it tomorrow","Delete the token from its database","Send the message to a topic instead"],"a":1,"why":"The token is invalid for good. Keeping it only adds failed sends to every fan-out."},{"q":"An app sends high-priority data messages that never show a notification. What can FCM do over time?","options":["Lower those messages to normal priority","Make them faster","Turn them into topics"],"a":0,"why":"FCM watches whether high-priority messages lead to visible notifications and may deprioritize them when they don't."}]},{"id":"media","title":"Images and media delivery","tagline":"Resize per screen, pick modern formats, load progressively, and upload in resumable chunks.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Request the pixels you will draw, in a modern format, from a CDN. Never ship the original to a thumbnail.","terms":[{"term":"Image variant","def":"A version of one original image at a given width, quality and format, made by the server or CDN on request."},{"term":"dp to px","def":"Pixels = dp × density. A 120 dp thumbnail on a density-3 screen needs 360 px."},{"term":"Progressive loading","def":"Showing something at once (a colour, a tiny preview) and refining it as better data arrives."},{"term":"Resumable upload","def":"An upload split into chunks, where the client can ask the server how much it has and continue from there after a failure."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A 4000×3000 photo is decoded as ARGB_8888. About how much memory does the bitmap take?","options":["About 12 MB","About 48 MB","About 3 MB, the file size"],"a":1,"why":"4000 × 3000 × 4 bytes = 48,000,000 bytes. The compressed file size says nothing about the decoded size."},{"q":"A 120 dp thumbnail on a screen with density 3. Which width should the app request?","options":["120 px","360 px","4000 px, and let the loader shrink it"],"a":1,"why":"120 × 3 = 360 pixels. Anything larger is wasted download and memory."},{"q":"A 40 MB video upload drops at 80%. With a resumable upload, what happens next?","options":["It starts again from 0","The app asks the server how much it has and sends the remaining 20%","The upload is lost for good"],"a":1,"why":"The server keeps the received bytes for the session. The client asks for the offset and continues from there."}]},{"id":"reliability","title":"Reliability: retries, backoff and degradation","tagline":"Timeouts, exponential backoff with jitter, circuit breakers, 429s and feature flags.","level":"advanced","remember":"Time out, retry only what is safe, back off exponentially with jitter, honour Retry-After, and degrade instead of failing.","terms":[{"term":"Exponential backoff","def":"Waiting twice as long after each failed attempt (1 s, 2 s, 4 s…), up to a cap."},{"term":"Jitter","def":"Randomness added to each wait so clients that failed together don't retry together."},{"term":"Circuit breaker","def":"A switch that stops calls to a failing dependency for a cool-down, then lets one test call through."},{"term":"Retry-After","def":"A response header on 429 or 503 telling the client how long to wait before trying again."},{"term":"Graceful degradation","def":"Keeping the core of the app working when a part fails, for example showing cached news without the comments."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff?","options":["To make retries faster","So clients that failed together don't retry together and overload the server again","To use less memory"],"a":1,"why":"Without jitter, every client that failed at second 0 retries at 1, 3, 7… at the same moments, in spikes."},{"q":"The server answers <code>429</code> with <code>Retry-After: 120</code>. What should the client do?","options":["Retry after 1 second","Wait at least 120 seconds before retrying","Never retry"],"a":1,"why":"The server is telling you its limit. Retrying sooner just gets more 429s and adds load."},{"q":"Which request is safe to retry automatically?","options":["POST /payments with no idempotency key","GET /v1/feed","POST /comments with no idempotency key"],"a":1,"why":"A GET doesn't change anything, so repeating it is harmless. A POST without a key may be applied twice."},{"q":"The circuit breaker for the comments service is open. What does the app do?","options":["Keeps calling the service as usual","Fails fast or shows cached comments, and lets one test call through after a cool-down","Restarts itself"],"a":1,"why":"Open means \"don't call\". After the cool-down, half-open lets one call test whether the service is back."},{"q":"The recommendations service is down. What should the article screen do?","options":["Show a full-screen error","Show the article without the \"related\" row","Spin until the service is back"],"a":1,"why":"The article is the core feature. Optional parts should disappear quietly when they fail."}]},{"id":"news-feed","title":"Design walkthrough: a news feed","tagline":"A news app with a million daily readers, from requirements to the boxes and back to Room.","level":"advanced","remember":"Read-heavy and the same for everyone: cache the shared feed at the CDN, keep Room as the screen's source, and use push for what can't wait.","terms":[{"term":"Fan-out on write","def":"Doing the work when content is published (rebuilding feed lists) so that reads are cheap lookups."},{"term":"Cache hit ratio","def":"The share of requests a cache answers without going to the origin. 95% means only 1 in 20 reaches your servers."},{"term":"Origin","def":"Your own servers behind the CDN, which answer whatever the CDN can't."},{"term":"Prefetch","def":"Downloading data before the user asks for it, at a cheap moment such as on Wi-Fi while charging."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which response is easiest to cache at the CDN?","options":["The shared category feed, the same for everyone","The user's bookmarks","The user's notification settings"],"a":0,"why":"One response serves every reader of that category. Personal data must stay private and can't be shared."},{"q":"About 116 feed requests per second arrive, and the CDN hit ratio is 95%. About how many reach the origin?","options":["About 6 per second","About 116 per second","About 1,000 per second"],"a":0,"why":"5% of 116 is about 6. That is why the origin for a news feed can be small."},{"q":"An editor fixes a typo and the CDN is purged. Why do some readers still see the typo for a few minutes?","options":["The CDN never purges","Phones keep their copy until its max-age runs out","Room is corrupted"],"a":1,"why":"Device caches follow the max-age they were given. Short max-age on articles keeps that window small."},{"q":"Morning, on the metro, no signal. Which order happens when the app opens?","options":["Network → Room → UI","Room → UI, then a network refresh into Room when possible","The UI waits for WorkManager"],"a":1,"why":"Offline-first: the screen reads Room at once. The refresh runs when it can and updates Room, which updates the screen."}]},{"id":"chat","title":"Design walkthrough: a chat app","tagline":"Delivery ticks, ordering, offline sending, push wake-ups and where encryption fits.","level":"advanced","remember":"Room first with a client id, server sequence for order, acks for ticks, push to wake an offline receiver.","terms":[{"term":"Delivery receipt","def":"An ack from the receiving device that it stored the message: the second tick."},{"term":"Sequence number","def":"A counter the server assigns per conversation. It gives every device the same order and makes gaps easy to spot."},{"term":"Client id","def":"An id the sender makes before sending. It identifies the message across retries, so the server stores it once."},{"term":"End-to-end encryption","def":"Only the participants' devices hold the keys. The server relays and stores ciphertext it can't read."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Alice sends a message while Bob's phone is switched off. What does Alice see?","options":["A clock forever","✓ sent, then ✓✓ once Bob's phone comes online and stores it","✓✓ at once"],"a":1,"why":"The server acks (✓) when it stores the message. Delivered (✓✓) needs Bob's device to receive it and ack."},{"q":"The server stored a message, but the connection dropped before the ack reached the sender. The app resends with the same client id. What should happen?","options":["Two copies appear","The server finds the client id and returns the existing sequence number: one copy","The message is lost"],"a":1,"why":"The client id makes the send idempotent. The resend only recovers the ack."},{"q":"Bob has messages up to sequence 41 and then receives 43. What should his app do?","options":["Show 43 and forget 42","Fetch the missing 42 from the server, then show them in order","Renumber 43 as 42"],"a":1,"why":"Sequence numbers make gaps visible. The client asks for what it is missing instead of silently skipping it."},{"q":"With end-to-end encryption, what can the server no longer do for users?","options":["Store messages","Send push wake-ups","Search the text of their messages on the server"],"a":2,"why":"The server holds only ciphertext. Search, previews and spam checks on content move to the device or go away."}]}]},{"id":"start","title":"First steps","description":"Never programmed before? Start here: what a program is, your first lines of code, and what to do when it goes wrong.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/start.js","lessons":[{"id":"program","title":"What a program is","tagline":"A list of steps that a computer follows exactly.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a program is","Break a job into small, exact steps","Put steps in the right order"],"remember":"A program is a list of exact instructions. The computer runs them in order and never guesses.","terms":[{"term":"program","def":"A list of instructions that a computer follows."},{"term":"instruction","def":"One step in a program, such as “move forward”."},{"term":"code","def":"The text of a program, written in a programming language."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A step in a program is unclear. What does the computer do?","options":["Guesses what you meant","Does exactly what the step says, or stops with an error","Skips it quietly and asks you later"],"a":1,"why":"Computers follow instructions literally. That is why programs must be exact."},{"q":"You swap the order of two steps. What happens?","options":["Nothing changes, the steps are the same","The program can now do something different","The computer puts them back in order"],"a":1,"why":"Order is part of the program. Steps run from top to bottom."}]},{"id":"first-program","title":"Your first program","tagline":"Write one line of Kotlin and see it print.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write and run a complete Kotlin program","Print a line of text with <code>println</code>","Know where a program starts: <code>fun main()</code>"],"remember":"A program starts at fun main(). println(\"...\") prints one line of text.","terms":[{"term":"Kotlin","def":"The programming language used in this course and in most Android apps."},{"term":"main","def":"Where a Kotlin program starts. Written <code>fun main() { }</code>."},{"term":"println","def":"Prints a line of text in the output."},{"term":"output","def":"Where a program shows its results, like the text a program prints."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does a Kotlin program start?","options":["At the first <code>println</code>","At <code>fun main()</code>","At the last line"],"a":1,"why":"<code>main</code> is the starting point. Its steps run top to bottom."},{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>println(\"Hi\")\nprintln(\"there\")</code></pre>","options":["<code>Hi there</code> on one line","<code>Hi</code>, then <code>there</code> on the next line","<code>there</code>, then <code>Hi</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Each <code>println</code> prints its own line, in order."}]},{"id":"comments","title":"Comments","tagline":"Notes in your code that the computer ignores.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write notes in code with <code>//</code> and <code>/* */</code>","Turn a line of code off by commenting it out"],"remember":"// and /* */ are notes for people. Kotlin ignores them, so they also turn code off.","terms":[{"term":"comment","def":"A note in code that the computer ignores. <code>// ...</code> or <code>/* ... */</code>."},{"term":"commenting out","def":"Turning a line of code off by making it a comment."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Kotlin do with a comment?","options":["Prints it","Ignores it","Runs it last"],"a":1,"why":"Comments are for people. The computer skips them."},{"q":"Which line is a comment?","options":["<code>println(\"// hi\")</code>","<code>// println(\"hi\")</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"In the first one, <code>//</code> is inside quotes, so it is just text to print."}]},{"id":"text","title":"Printing text","tagline":"Text goes in double quotes. Kotlin calls it a String.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write text in double quotes as a <code>String</code>","Print text exactly as written","Spot why text without quotes fails"],"remember":"Text goes in double quotes and prints exactly as written. That text is called a String.","terms":[{"term":"String","def":"A piece of text, written in double quotes: <code>\"like this\"</code>."},{"term":"empty string","def":"<code>\"\"</code>: text with nothing in it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(\"10 - 3\")</code> print?","options":["<code>7</code>","<code>10 - 3</code>","<code>\"10 - 3\"</code> with the quotes"],"a":1,"why":"Quotes make it text. The quotes themselves are not printed."},{"q":"What is a String?","options":["A piece of text","A whole number","A line of output"],"a":0,"why":"A String is text, written in double quotes."}]},{"id":"numbers","title":"Numbers and arithmetic","tagline":"Without quotes, Kotlin does the maths.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add, subtract, multiply and divide with <code>Int</code> numbers","Know which sums Kotlin works out first","Use brackets to choose the order"],"remember":"Numbers without quotes are calculated. * and / go before + and -, brackets go first of all.","terms":[{"term":"Int","def":"A whole number, like <code>3</code>, <code>0</code> or <code>-12</code>."},{"term":"operator","def":"A symbol that does something to values, like <code>+</code> or <code>*</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(10 - 2 * 3)</code> print?","options":["<code>24</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>10 - 2 * 3</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>2 * 3</code> is 6 first, then 10 − 6 is 4."},{"q":"Which line prints <code>9</code>?","options":["<code>println(\"4 + 5\")</code>","<code>println(4 + 5)</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"Only the version without quotes does the maths."}]},{"id":"decimals","title":"Decimal numbers","tagline":"Numbers with a point, and the surprise in whole-number division.","level":"beginner","goals":["Calculate with decimal numbers (<code>Double</code>)","Explain why <code>7 / 2</code> gives 3","Find the remainder with <code>%</code>"],"remember":"Int / Int drops the decimals. Use a Double like 7.0 to keep them. % gives the remainder.","terms":[{"term":"Double","def":"A number with a decimal point, like <code>3.5</code>."},{"term":"remainder","def":"What is left over after dividing. Written <code>%</code>: <code>7 % 2</code> is 1."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(9 / 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>4.5</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Whole divided by whole drops the decimals. Nothing is rounded up."},{"q":"What does <code>println(9 % 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>1</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>4.5</code>"],"a":0,"why":"2 fits into 9 four times, with 1 left over."},{"q":"Which gives <code>4.5</code>?","options":["<code>9 / 2</code>","<code>9.0 / 2</code>","<code>9 % 2</code>"],"a":1,"why":"With a decimal number involved, the answer keeps its decimals."}]},{"id":"errors","title":"When code doesn’t work","tagline":"Errors are normal. Here is how to read them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell a compile error from a crash","Read an error message to find the problem","Fix the first error first"],"remember":"Errors are normal. Compile errors stop the program before it starts; crashes stop it partway. Read the message, fix the first one.","terms":[{"term":"error","def":"A message saying something in the program went wrong."},{"term":"compile error","def":"A problem Kotlin finds before running, such as a typo. Nothing runs."},{"term":"crash","def":"A problem while the program runs, such as dividing by zero. It stops at that step."},{"term":"bug","def":"A mistake in a program that makes it do the wrong thing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You misspell <code>println</code> on the last line. What prints?","options":["Everything except the last line","Nothing: it is a compile error","Everything, with a warning"],"a":1,"why":"Compile errors are found before the program runs, so nothing runs."},{"q":"A program crashes on its third step. Did steps one and two run?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"A crash happens while running. The steps before it already happened."},{"q":"Your code shows three errors. Which do you fix first?","options":["The last one","The first one","The longest message"],"a":1,"why":"One mistake can cause the ones after it. Fixing the first often fixes the rest."}]}]}]);
